window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["gurugram-2025-mphil"] = {"id": "gurugram-2025-mphil", "title": "Gurugram 2025 M.Phil", "fullTitle": "Gurugram University 2025, M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper, official key)", "sub": "60 real questions · official answer key · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "The probability that the desired response will be performed is increased by taking away something undesirable whenever the desired response is made is called", "options": {"a": "Positive reinforcement", "b": "Negative reinforcement", "c": "Positive punishment", "d": "Negative punishment"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: taking away something unpleasant whenever the desired response occurs, so that the response becomes more likely, is negative reinforcement. In operant conditioning, learning through consequences, Edward Lee Thorndike (1898) first stated the law of effect: responses followed by satisfying outcomes are stamped in. Burrhus Frederic (B. F.) Skinner (1938) then sorted consequences on two questions. Does the behaviour increase (reinforcement) or decrease (punishment)? Is a stimulus added (positive) or removed (negative)? Positive and negative mean plus and minus, not good and bad. So reinforcement of either kind always strengthens behaviour, and punishment of either kind always weakens it. Negative reinforcement comes in two forms: escape, where the response ends an aversive stimulus already present, and avoidance, where the response stops it from arriving. In a Skinner box a rat presses a lever that switches off a mild shock, and lever pressing rises. Example: whenever Arjun starts his car it beeps, and the irritating beep stops only when he fastens his seatbelt. Removing the beep strengthens the seatbelt habit, so Arjun buckles up faster each time. The same logic explains why a headache pill gets taken again, and why avoidance keeps phobias alive: escaping the feared situation brings relief, which reinforces the escape. Option b is right because the stem has both defining features: the probability of the response goes up (reinforcement), and this happens by taking something undesirable away (negative). Exam trap: students hear 'negative' and think 'punishment'. Negative reinforcement is not punishment; it increases behaviour. Examiners also swap the halves: removing something pleasant to reduce behaviour (a fine, losing phone time) is negative punishment, not negative reinforcement.", "topic": "Negative reinforcement"}, {"q": "When the conditioned stimulus is presented before the unconditioned stimulus it is called", "options": {"a": "Forward conditioning", "b": "Backward conditioning", "c": "Spontaneous recovery", "d": "Simultaneous conditioning"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: when the conditioned stimulus (CS) is presented before the unconditioned stimulus (UCS), the arrangement is called forward conditioning. In classical conditioning, studied by Ivan Pavlov (1927) with salivating dogs, a neutral stimulus such as a bell is paired with a UCS such as food, which naturally produces an unconditioned response (salivation). After repeated pairings the bell alone produces salivation, now a conditioned response. Pavlov found that the timing of the pairing matters a great deal. In forward conditioning the CS comes first, so it works as a signal that the UCS is on its way. There are two forward forms. In delay conditioning the CS starts first and is still on when the UCS arrives; this is the most effective arrangement of all. In trace conditioning the CS starts and stops, and a gap follows before the UCS; it works but is weaker, because the learner must hold a memory trace across the gap. In simultaneous conditioning the CS and UCS start together, and in backward conditioning the UCS comes first; both are weak, backward the weakest, because the CS then predicts nothing. Robert Rescorla (1968) later showed that what matters is how well the CS predicts the UCS, which is why forward order works best. Example: Meera's phone buzzes (CS) a second before a message from her strict manager appears (UCS) and makes her anxious. Soon the buzz alone makes her tense. Option a is right because the stem defines exactly this order: CS first, UCS second. Exam trap: 'delay' conditioning sounds slow or weak, but it is the strongest forward form; trace is the one with the gap. Do not confuse backward conditioning (UCS before CS) with spontaneous recovery, which is not about timing at all.", "topic": "Forward conditioning and CS-UCS timing"}, {"q": "The internal or external locus of control theory was given by", "options": {"a": "Hans Eysenck", "b": "David McClelland", "c": "Herman Witkin", "d": "Julian Rotter"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: the theory of internal versus external locus of control was given by Julian Rotter (1966). Locus of control is a generalised expectancy about where the control of reinforcement lies. A person with an internal locus believes outcomes depend mainly on their own effort and ability. A person with an external locus believes outcomes depend on luck, fate, chance or powerful others. The idea grew out of Rotter's social learning theory (1954), in which behaviour potential depends on expectancy (how likely I think a reward is) and reinforcement value (how much I want it). Rotter measured the construct with his Internal-External (I-E) Scale, published in his 1966 monograph. It has 29 forced-choice items (23 scored plus 6 fillers), and a higher score means a more external orientation. Research links an internal locus with better academic achievement, health behaviour and coping, though extreme internality can bring self-blame. Hanna Levenson (1973) later split externality into two kinds: powerful others and chance. Example: after failing a test, Kabir says 'I did not revise enough', an internal attribution, while his friend Rohan says 'the paper was unfair and the examiner dislikes me', an external one. Kabir is more likely to change his study habits next time. Option d is right because Rotter coined the term, built the theory and wrote the standard scale for it. Exam trap: locus of control is often confused with Albert Bandura's self-efficacy (1977), which is belief in one's ability to perform a specific task, and with Bernard Weiner's attribution theory (1979), which uses locus as one dimension of causal attributions. Neither Bandura nor Weiner gave the internal-external locus of control theory.", "topic": "Locus of control (Rotter)"}, {"q": "Which of the following does not form a part of Gordon Allport's trait theory", "options": {"a": "Primary trait", "b": "Secondary trait", "c": "Cardinal trait", "d": "Central trait"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: 'primary trait' is not part of Gordon Allport's trait theory. Allport (1937) saw traits as real neuropsychic structures inside the person that make many situations functionally equivalent and so guide consistent behaviour. He later renamed individual traits 'personal dispositions' (1961) and arranged them on three levels according to how much of a life they govern. Cardinal traits are a single ruling passion that dominates almost everything a person does; few people have one, and those who do often lend it a name, such as Machiavellian or narcissistic. Central traits are the five to ten outstanding characteristics you would mention in a reference letter: honest, warm, ambitious. Secondary traits are narrower, less consistent preferences and attitudes that appear only in certain situations, such as a liking for spicy food or irritability when hungry. Example: Mahatma Gandhi's commitment to non-violence is often given as a cardinal trait; Priya's central traits might be kindness, punctuality and humour, and a secondary trait is that she becomes impatient in long queues. So options b, c and d are all genuine Allport levels, and 'primary' is the odd one out. It sounds right because it pairs so neatly with 'secondary', but Allport never used it. The word belongs elsewhere: Raymond Cattell (1946) called his 16 source traits the primary factors of the 16PF, and Louis Leon Thurstone (1938) proposed seven primary mental abilities. Option a is right because the stem asks for the item that does not belong, and primary trait is the only one not among Allport's cardinal, central and secondary traits. Exam trap: the pairing 'primary and secondary' is a natural guess, and examiners exploit it. Allport's three levels are cardinal, central and secondary; Cattell's distinction is surface versus source traits.", "topic": "Allport's levels of traits"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a part of Gardner's theory of multiple intelligence", "options": {"a": "Linguistic intelligence", "b": "Spatial intelligence", "c": "Interpersonal intelligence", "d": "Emotional intelligence"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: emotional intelligence is not one of Howard Gardner's multiple intelligences. Gardner (1983), in Frames of Mind, argued that intelligence is not one general ability (Charles Spearman's g, 1904) but several relatively independent abilities. He chose them by criteria rather than by factor analysis: each should be isolable by brain damage, show up in prodigies or savants, have its own developmental history and a distinctive core operation. His original seven were linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinaesthetic, interpersonal and intrapersonal. Naturalist intelligence was added in 1999, and existential intelligence was considered but not fully accepted. So linguistic (a), spatial (b) and interpersonal (c) are all on Gardner's list. Emotional intelligence came from a different line of work. Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990) defined it as the ability to perceive, use, understand and manage emotions, and Daniel Goleman (1995) popularised it in his bestseller. It overlaps with Gardner's interpersonal (understanding others) and intrapersonal (understanding oneself) intelligences, which is why examiners use it as a decoy, but it is a separate theory with its own tests, such as the MSCEIT. Example: Ananya, a poet, is strong in linguistic intelligence; her brother Vikram, a cricketer, in bodily-kinaesthetic intelligence; their friend Sara, who reads the mood of a classroom at once, shows interpersonal intelligence. Option d is right because emotional intelligence is the only option that was never among Gardner's intelligences; it belongs to Salovey, Mayer and Goleman. Exam trap: interpersonal and intrapersonal intelligence look like 'emotional intelligence' and tempt students to pick c, but they are genuine Gardner intelligences. Also do not credit Gardner with the triarchic theory; that is Robert Sternberg (1985).", "topic": "Gardner's multiple intelligences"}, {"q": "Syntax is", "options": {"a": "The Grammatical arrangement of words and sentences", "b": "The meaning of words and sentences", "c": "The smallest units of meaning", "d": "The smallest sound units."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: syntax is the set of rules for the grammatical arrangement of words into phrases and sentences. Language is built in layers. Phonemes are the smallest units of sound that change meaning, such as the /b/ and /p/ that separate 'bat' from 'pat'. Morphemes are the smallest units that carry meaning: 'unhappiness' has three (un, happy, ness). Semantics is the meaning of words and sentences. Syntax governs order and structure: who did what to whom. Pragmatics covers how language is used in social context. Noam Chomsky (1957), in Syntactic Structures, made syntax central to the study of language. He showed that a sentence can be perfectly grammatical yet meaningless ('Colourless green ideas sleep furiously'), which shows that syntax and semantics are separate systems. He also distinguished surface structure (the actual word order) from deep structure (the underlying meaning), linked by transformational rules. Syntax explains why word order changes meaning: 'The dog bit Rahul' and 'Rahul bit the dog' use identical words but say different things. Languages differ in syntax: English is mostly subject-verb-object, while Hindi is subject-object-verb. Example: when three-year-old Ishaan says 'Me want biscuit', his words and meaning are clear, but his syntax is still developing; this telegraphic speech keeps the content words and drops the grammatical ones. Option a is right because the grammatical arrangement of words and sentences is the definition of syntax. Exam trap: the four options list four layers of language, and each wrong answer is the real definition of a neighbouring term. Meaning is semantics, the smallest units of meaning are morphemes, and the smallest sound units are phonemes. Do not confuse syntax (structure) with semantics (meaning).", "topic": "Syntax and the levels of language"}, {"q": "Which type of memory is also called echoic memory", "options": {"a": "Visual memory", "b": "Kinesis", "c": "Haptic memory", "d": "Auditory memory"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: echoic memory is the auditory form of sensory memory. In the multi-store (modal) model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), information first enters a sensory register that holds a large amount of raw, unprocessed input for a very short time, with one store for each sense. Ulric Neisser (1967) named the visual store iconic memory and the auditory store echoic memory, because it holds an 'echo' of what was heard. Iconic memory lasts only about a quarter to half a second, as George Sperling (1960) showed with his partial-report method. Echoic memory lasts longer, about 2 to 4 seconds. Christopher Darwin, Michael Turvey and Robert Crowder (1972) showed this with their 'three-eared man' study: letters were played to the left ear, the right ear and both ears at once, and a cue said which channel to report. Partial report beat whole report for a few seconds, then the advantage faded. The longer echo is useful because speech unfolds over time; you need to hold the start of a word until it ends. Example: Neha is reading when her mother asks, 'Have you eaten?' Neha says 'What?' and then, before her mother can repeat it, answers 'Yes, I have'. The question was still in her echoic store, so she could replay it. Option d is right because echoic memory is, by definition, sensory memory for sounds, that is, auditory memory. Exam trap: iconic and echoic are easy to swap. Remember icon equals image (visual) and echo equals sound (auditory). Also note the durations: echoic lasts longer than iconic, so do not assume all sensory memory fades in under a second.", "topic": "Echoic (auditory) sensory memory"}, {"q": "Upon being asked to list various uses of a bedsheet Payal responds, \" a parachute\", \"A swing\", or \" for posing as a ghost\" . Here Payal is using", "options": {"a": "Metacognition", "b": "Crystallized intelligence,", "c": "Divergent thinking", "d": "transformational rules."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: listing many unusual uses for a bedsheet is divergent thinking. Joy Paul Guilford (1950), in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, called for research on creativity, and in his Structure of Intellect model (1967) he contrasted two kinds of production. Convergent thinking narrows down to the one correct answer, as in most intelligence and exam items. Divergent thinking spreads outwards to produce many possible answers to an open problem. Guilford's Alternate (Unusual) Uses Task asks people to list as many uses as they can for a common object such as a brick. Answers are scored for fluency (how many ideas), flexibility (how many different categories), originality (how rare the ideas are) and elaboration (how much detail is added). Ellis Paul Torrance (1966) built these scores into the Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking, and in India Baqer Mehdi (1973) built similar verbal and non-verbal tests of creative thinking. Payal's answers show all the signs. 'Parachute', 'swing' and 'ghost costume' are three ideas (fluency) from three different categories, safety, play and drama (flexibility), and none is a typical use like covering a bed (originality). Option c is right because the task is open-ended and Payal is generating many varied, original possibilities rather than searching for one correct answer. Exam trap: divergent thinking is swapped with its opposite, convergent thinking, or with metacognition, which sounds like a 'higher' skill. A good rule: one right answer means convergent; many possible answers means divergent. Note also that divergent thinking is a component of creativity, not the whole of it; real-world creativity also needs knowledge, motivation and the judgement to evaluate ideas.", "topic": "Divergent thinking (Guilford)"}, {"q": "The tendency to keep repeating solutions that that worked in other situations is called", "options": {"a": "Mental set", "b": "Functional fixedness", "c": "Schema", "d": "Heuristics"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the tendency to keep repeating a solution that worked before, even when a better one is available, is mental set. The classic evidence is Abraham Luchins's (1942) water-jar problems. Participants used three jars of different sizes (A, B and C) to measure out an exact amount of water. The first several problems could only be solved by the long formula B minus A minus 2C. Later problems could be solved by a much simpler route, such as A minus C, yet most participants who had been drilled on the long formula kept using it, and some failed problems the long formula could not solve. A control group that started on the later problems found the short route at once. Luchins called this Einstellung, German for 'attitude' or 'set'. A mental set is not always bad: it saves effort on familiar problems. It becomes an obstacle when the situation changes. Gestalt psychologists, who studied problem solving through insight, saw such sets as a barrier to restructuring a problem. Example: Kabir has always fixed his laptop by restarting it. When the screen freezes because a cable is loose, he restarts it again and again, never checking the cable, because the old solution has become his set. Option a is right because the stem describes reusing past solutions out of habit, which is the definition of mental set. Exam trap: mental set is easily confused with functional fixedness. Both are forms of fixation, but functional fixedness (Karl Duncker, 1945) is about seeing an object only in its usual function, as in the candle problem, whereas mental set is about repeating a strategy or method. Object, think fixedness; method, think set.", "topic": "Mental set (Einstellung)"}, {"q": "A researcher wishes to know whether there is any significant association between political choice and music choices. Which statistic should be employed to decide this question?", "options": {"a": "Chi-square", "b": "Independent subjects t", "c": "Within subjects t", "d": "Analysis of variance"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: to test whether two categorical variables, such as political choice and music choice, are associated, use the chi-square test. Karl Pearson (1900) introduced chi-square for frequency data. Nominal variables like 'party supported' or 'preferred music genre' have no means or standard deviations; you can only count how many people fall in each category. The chi-square test of independence arranges these counts in a contingency table (for example 3 parties by 4 music genres) and compares the observed frequency in each cell with the expected frequency, the count you would expect if the two variables were unrelated. The expected frequency for a cell equals the row total times the column total, divided by the grand total. Chi-square is the sum, across all cells, of (observed minus expected) squared divided by expected. A large value means the observed pattern departs from independence. It is tested against degrees of freedom equal to (rows minus 1) times (columns minus 1). Chi-square is non-parametric and needs independent observations, with expected counts of at least 5 in most cells; Cramer's V then shows how strong the association is. Example: Meera surveys 200 students in Delhi on which party they support and whether they prefer film, classical, rock or hip-hop music. If far more classical lovers support one party than chance predicts, chi-square will be significant. Option a is right because both variables are nominal and the question is about association between categories, which is exactly what chi-square tests. Exam trap: the words 'significant association' can pull students towards correlation or t-tests. Check the level of measurement first. Counts in categories mean chi-square; means of a continuous score mean a t-test or ANOVA.", "topic": "Chi-square test of association"}, {"q": "Which among the following measures of central tendency is most sensitive to outlying observation?", "options": {"a": "Mean", "b": "Median", "c": "Mode", "d": "Standard deviation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the mean is the measure of central tendency most sensitive to outlying observations. A measure of central tendency gives one typical value for a set of scores. The three main ones are the mean, the median and the mode. The mean is the arithmetic average: add every score and divide by the number of scores. Because every score enters the sum with its full value, one extreme score can pull the mean far towards it. The median is the middle score when the data are put in order; it depends only on rank position, so an extreme score at one end barely moves it. The mode is the most frequent score; an outlier, being rare, almost never changes it. This is why the median is preferred for skewed data such as incomes or house prices, and why, in a positively skewed distribution, the mean sits above the median, which sits above the mode. Example: five friends in Pune earn 20, 22, 25, 28 and 30 thousand rupees a month, so the mean and the median are both 25 thousand. Now Arjun's cousin, who earns 500 thousand, joins the group. The median moves only to 26.5 thousand, but the mean jumps to about 104 thousand, a figure that describes nobody in the group. Option a is right because the mean uses the actual size of every score, so a single outlier distorts it more than it distorts the median or the mode. Exam trap: standard deviation is also strongly affected by outliers, since it is built on squared deviations from the mean, so it looks tempting. But it is a measure of variability, not of central tendency, so it cannot answer a question about which average is most sensitive.", "topic": "Mean and outliers"}, {"q": "Which of the following is an assumption of Pearson's Product moment correlation", "options": {"a": "There should be homoscedasticity", "b": "There should be linear relation", "c": "Both a and b", "d": "Variables should be nominal"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: Pearson's product-moment correlation assumes both homoscedasticity and a linear relationship. Karl Pearson (1896) developed r, which measures the strength and direction of the straight-line relationship between two variables, running from minus 1 to plus 1. Its main assumptions are these. First, linearity: r captures only straight-line relationships. If the true relation is curved, such as the inverted U of the Yerkes-Dodson law (Robert Yerkes and John Dodson, 1908) linking arousal and performance, r can be near zero even though the variables are strongly related; the correlation ratio eta is used instead. Second, homoscedasticity: the spread of Y scores should be about the same at every level of X, so the scatterplot looks like an even, cigar-shaped band rather than a fan. When the spread widens at one end (heteroscedasticity), r misrepresents the relationship. Third, both variables should be measured on an interval or ratio scale, and for significance testing they should be roughly bivariate normal. Restriction of range lowers r, and outliers can inflate or deflate it, so the usual check is to draw a scatterplot before computing r. Example: Riya correlates hours of study with marks for 100 students. If the dots form a straight, evenly spread band, r is appropriate. If marks rise and then flatten, linearity fails; if marks vary widely only among heavy studiers, homoscedasticity fails. Option c is right because linearity and homoscedasticity are both standard assumptions of Pearson's r, so neither a nor b alone is complete. Exam trap: nominal variables are the opposite of what Pearson's r needs. For categories use the phi coefficient or the contingency coefficient; for ranks use Charles Spearman's (1904) rho.", "topic": "Assumptions of Pearson's r"}, {"q": "A normal distribution of 200 score has a mean of 30 and a standard deviation of 4. Rahul received a score of 38. which of the following is not true of his score", "options": {"a": "His score is equivalent to a z-score of +2", "b": "His score is 2 standard deviations from mean", "c": "Approximately 48% of the total scores lie between his score and the mean", "d": "Approximately 68% of the students scored lower than he did"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the statement that approximately 68% of students scored lower than Rahul is not true. Work it through with a z-score, which tells you how many standard deviations a score lies from the mean: z equals (score minus mean) divided by the standard deviation. Rahul's z equals (38 minus 30) divided by 4, which is plus 2. So statements a and b are true: his score equals a z of plus 2, and it lies 2 standard deviations above the mean. Now use the normal curve, described by Abraham de Moivre (1733) and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809). It is symmetrical, so 50% of scores lie below the mean. The empirical rule says about 68% of scores fall within 1 SD of the mean (34% on each side), about 95% within 2 SD (47.7% on each side) and about 99.7% within 3 SD. The area between the mean and z equals plus 2 is 47.72%, so statement c (about 48% between his score and the mean) is true. The percentage below Rahul is 50% plus 47.72%, about 97.7%. He outscored roughly 98% of the group, which puts him near the 98th percentile, so 68% is far too low. In concrete terms, of the 200 students about 195 scored below Rahul, not 136. Option d is right because it is the only false statement; the true figure for scores below z equals plus 2 is about 98%, not 68%. Exam trap: 68% is the famous figure for the area within plus or minus 1 SD, not the area below plus 2 SD. Examiners plant it because students remember the number without its meaning. Always add the 50% below the mean when you turn a positive z into a percentile.", "topic": "z-scores and normal curve areas"}, {"q": "A graphical representation of correlational data is called a", "options": {"a": "Bell curve", "b": "Scatterplot", "c": "Bimodal distribution", "d": "Skewed distribution"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a graphical representation of correlational data is a scatterplot, also called a scatter diagram. Correlation describes how two variables vary together. To draw a scatterplot, you put one variable on the X axis and the other on the Y axis, and each person becomes a single dot placed at their pair of scores. The pattern of dots then shows three things at a glance. Direction: dots rising from lower left to upper right show a positive correlation; dots falling from upper left to lower right show a negative one. Strength: the tighter the dots cluster around a straight line, the stronger the correlation; a shapeless cloud means r is near zero. Form: the dots reveal whether the relation is linear or curved, and whether there are outliers or uneven spread (heteroscedasticity). Francis Galton (1886) used such plots of parents' and children's heights to discover regression towards the mean, and Karl Pearson (1896) then formalised the correlation coefficient r. A line of best fit, the regression line, can be drawn through the dots to predict one variable from the other. Example: Aarav plots hours of sleep against exam marks for his 40 classmates. If the dots rise steadily, more sleep goes with higher marks, a positive correlation, though this does not prove that sleep causes better marks. Option b is right because a scatterplot is the graph designed to show the relationship between two variables measured on the same people. Exam trap: the other three options are all shapes of a frequency distribution of a single variable. A bell curve, a bimodal curve and a skewed curve each show how scores on one variable are spread, not how two variables relate to each other.", "topic": "Scatterplot for correlational data"}, {"q": "The variable whose effect is being studied and that the experimenter manipulates is –", "options": {"a": "Independent variable", "b": "Dependent variable", "c": "Confounding variable", "d": "Mediating variable"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the variable the experimenter manipulates, and whose effect is being studied, is the independent variable (IV). An experiment is a study in which the researcher deliberately changes one thing and watches what happens to another while holding everything else constant. The thing deliberately changed is the IV; its different values are called levels or conditions. The thing measured to see the effect is the dependent variable (DV), so called because its value depends on the IV. Robert Sessions Woodworth (1938), in his textbook Experimental Psychology, popularised this IV and DV language, and the logic goes back to John Stuart Mill's (1843) method of difference. If the IV is the only thing that differs between conditions, any difference in the DV can be put down to it, which is why only experiments can show cause and effect. Random assignment spreads other influences evenly across groups. A useful memory aid: the IV is the cause, what I change; the DV is the effect, the data I collect. Example: Dr Kavya Rao wants to know whether caffeine speeds up reaction time. She gives one group of students 200 mg of caffeine and another a placebo, then measures their reaction times. Caffeine (present or absent) is the IV; reaction time is the DV. Option a is right because manipulation by the experimenter is the defining feature of the IV. Exam trap: the stem says 'whose effect is being studied', and students sometimes pick the DV because it sounds like the 'effect'. But the IV is the one whose effect is studied; the DV is the outcome on which that effect shows. A confounding variable also changes alongside the IV, but by accident, not by the experimenter's design.", "topic": "Independent variable"}, {"q": "A study where the researcher does not intervene, is a type of –", "options": {"a": "Quazi experimental study", "b": "Experimental study", "c": "Naturalistic observation", "d": "Correlational study"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a study in which the researcher does not intervene at all is naturalistic observation. In naturalistic observation the researcher watches and records behaviour as it happens in its natural setting, such as a home, a classroom or a forest, without manipulating any variable, setting up any situation or interacting with the people observed. Its strength is ecological validity: behaviour is seen as it really occurs. Its weaknesses are reactivity (people may act differently when they know they are being watched), observer bias and lack of control, so it describes behaviour but cannot show cause. Researchers reduce reactivity by staying hidden or by letting those observed get used to them. The classic example is Jane Goodall (1960), who watched chimpanzees at Gombe in Tanzania for decades and discovered that they make and use tools. Example: Sana sits quietly at the edge of an Ahmedabad playground and records how often boys and girls share toys, never talking to the children or changing anything. Option c is right because naturalistic observation is defined by the absence of intervention: the researcher only observes. Exam trap: experimental and quasi-experimental studies both involve applying or manipulating an independent variable, so 'does not intervene' rules them out. The quasi-experiment, described by Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963), still applies a treatment; it only lacks random assignment. Key note: a correlational study also involves no manipulation, since variables are measured as they exist, so option d has support. The key takes naturalistic observation because there the researcher watches behaviour in its natural setting without intervening at all, whereas a correlational study usually still gives tests or questionnaires. The official key stands.", "topic": "Naturalistic observation"}, {"q": "When a researcher wants to compare the means of two groups, which type of significant test is best suited?", "options": {"a": "ANOVA", "b": "t-test", "c": "factorial design", "d": "regression"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: to compare the means of two groups, the best-suited test of significance is the t-test. The t-test was developed by William Sealy Gosset (1908), a chemist at the Guinness brewery in Dublin who published under the pen name 'Student', which is why it is called Student's t. He needed a test that worked with small samples, where the normal (z) distribution is not accurate. The t statistic is a ratio: the difference between the two means divided by the standard error of that difference. A large t means the difference is big relative to the variability within the groups, so it is unlikely to be due to chance. The value is checked against the t distribution at the right degrees of freedom (for independent groups, n1 plus n2 minus 2). There are two main forms: the independent-samples t-test for two separate groups, and the paired (dependent or within-subjects) t-test for the same people measured twice or for matched pairs. It assumes interval or ratio data, roughly normal distributions and, for independent groups, similar variances (homogeneity of variance). Example: Ritika compares the mean anxiety scores of 30 students who did a yoga programme with those of 30 who did not. A significant t shows that the two means differ by more than chance would produce. Option b is right because the t-test is designed for exactly this comparison: two means. Exam trap: ANOVA can also compare two means, and then F equals t squared, so it tempts students. But ANOVA (Ronald Fisher, 1925) is built for three or more groups, where running many t-tests would inflate the Type I error rate. For two groups, t is the standard answer.", "topic": "t-test for two means"}, {"q": "Which of the following can reduce experimenter bias?", "options": {"a": "Using control groups", "b": "Demand characteristics", "c": "Barnum effect", "d": "Double blinding"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: double blinding reduces experimenter bias. Experimenter bias, or the experimenter expectancy effect, is the way a researcher's expectations can unintentionally shape results: through subtle cues to participants, through how ambiguous responses are scored, or through how data are recorded. Robert Rosenthal (1966) made the effect famous. In Robert Rosenthal and Kermit Fode's (1963) study, students were told their rats were 'maze-bright' or 'maze-dull', though the rats had been assigned at random; the 'bright' rats learned faster, apparently because the students handled them differently. In a single-blind study the participants do not know which condition they are in, which controls their own expectations, such as placebo effects. In a double-blind study neither the participants nor the researchers who deal with them and record the data know who is in which condition. Because the experimenter does not know, their expectations cannot leak into the participants' behaviour or the scoring. Double-blind designs are standard in drug trials, where an independent person holds the code that links each participant to drug or placebo. Example: Dr Anil tests a new antidepressant. A pharmacist labels identical capsules with codes, and neither Dr Anil, who rates the patients' mood, nor the patients know which capsules hold the drug. The code is broken only after rating ends. Option d is right because double blinding removes the experimenter's knowledge of the conditions, which is the very source of experimenter bias. Exam trap: control groups are vital, but they control extraneous variables and placebo effects, not the experimenter's own expectations; an experimenter who knows which group is which can still bias the results. Demand characteristics and the Barnum effect are sources of bias, not cures for it.", "topic": "Double blinding and experimenter bias"}, {"q": "The Minnesota Multiphasic personality inventory yields score on how many clinical scales?", "options": {"a": "12", "b": "15", "c": "10", "d": "11"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) yields scores on 10 clinical scales. Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) built the MMPI at the University of Minnesota by empirical criterion keying: an item went onto a scale if patients with a given diagnosis answered it differently from normal people, whatever its content. The original test had 550 true-false items; the MMPI-2 (James Butcher and colleagues, 1989) has 567. The 10 clinical scales are known by number: 1 Hypochondriasis, 2 Depression, 3 Hysteria, 4 Psychopathic Deviate, 5 Masculinity-Femininity, 6 Paranoia, 7 Psychasthenia, 8 Schizophrenia, 9 Hypomania and 0 Social Introversion. Eight were keyed against psychiatric groups; scales 5 and 0 were built later from other criterion groups. Scales 1, 2 and 3 form the 'neurotic triad', and 6, 7, 8 and 9 the 'psychotic tetrad'. Scores are T scores with a mean of 50 and an SD of 10; on the MMPI-2 a T of 65 or more is clinically significant, and clinicians read the profile as a pattern, often by two-point code types. Separate validity scales, ? (cannot say), L (lie), F (infrequency) and K (correction), check for faking or careless answering. Example: Rakesh's profile peaks on scales 2 and 7, a 2-7 code type typical of anxious depression. Option c is right because the standard MMPI and MMPI-2 profile has exactly ten clinical scales. Exam trap: 13 is the number of 'basic scales', the 10 clinical scales plus the 3 validity scales L, F and K, and the MMPI-2-RF (Yossef Ben-Porath and Auke Tellegen, 2008) has 9 Restructured Clinical scales. None of 12, 15 or 11 is the clinical-scale count.", "topic": "MMPI clinical scales"}, {"q": "Which of the following indicates how well a person performed on an IQ test relative to her/his same-age peers?", "options": {"a": "Ratio IQ", "b": "Deviation IQ", "c": "Both a and b", "d": "Raw score"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the deviation IQ shows how well a person performed relative to same-age peers. David Wechsler (1939) introduced it in the Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scale to replace the older ratio IQ. A deviation IQ is a standard score. The test is given to a large norm sample at each age, and a person's raw score is compared with the distribution of scores of people of the same age. The scores are converted so that each age group has a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15 (the older Stanford-Binet used 16). An IQ of 115 therefore means one SD above the mean for your age, about the 84th percentile; 130 means two SD above, about the 98th percentile; 70 means two SD below. Because the meaning is the same at every age, a deviation IQ works for adults, whose mental age stops growing. The earlier ratio IQ was proposed by William Stern (1912) as mental age divided by chronological age, and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied it by 100 in the Stanford-Binet. It compares a person's mental age with their own chronological age. It breaks down after about age 16, because mental age levels off while chronological age keeps rising, and its spread differs from one age to another, so the same ratio IQ does not mean the same rank at different ages. Example: Tanvi, aged 25, scores 115 on the WAIS-IV, which means she did better than about 84% of adults her age in the norm group. Option b is right because the deviation IQ is defined by comparison with the score distribution of same-age peers. Exam trap: the ratio IQ also involves age, so 'both a and b' looks safe. But the ratio IQ compares mental age with chronological age, not a person with the spread of peers' scores.", "topic": "Deviation IQ versus ratio IQ"}, {"q": "A test's coverage of the particular skill or knowledge area that it is supposed to measure is", "options": {"a": "Content validity", "b": "Face validity", "c": "Convergent validity", "d": "Criterion validity"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: a test's coverage of the skill or knowledge area it is supposed to measure is its content validity. Validity is the extent to which a test measures what it claims to measure. Content validity asks whether the items are a fair, representative sample of the whole domain. It is built in during test construction rather than computed afterwards. The developer first defines the domain and draws up a test blueprint, or table of specifications, listing the topics and skills to be covered and how much weight each should get. Items are then written to match the blueprint, and subject experts judge whether the content is relevant and well balanced. Charles H. Lawshe (1975) proposed the content validity ratio to quantify how many experts rate each item as essential. Content validity matters most for achievement tests, licensing examinations and job knowledge tests. Example: Mr Iyer sets a Class 10 mathematics test, but 18 of its 20 questions are on algebra and only 2 on geometry, though the syllabus gives them equal weight. The test lacks content validity because it under-samples geometry, even if each question is well written. Option a is right because the stem's key word, 'coverage', is the defining idea of content validity: does the test cover the domain adequately? Exam trap: content validity is often confused with face validity. Face validity is only whether the test looks, to the people taking it, as if it measures the right thing; it is a matter of appearance and is not technical evidence. Content validity is a systematic judgement by experts against a defined domain.", "topic": "Content validity"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a type of reliability", "options": {"a": "Test-retest", "b": "Split-half", "c": "Alternate-form", "d": "Item difficulty"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: item difficulty is not a type of reliability; it is an index from item analysis. Reliability is the consistency of a test's scores. In classical test theory, set out by Charles Spearman (1904), every observed score equals a true score plus error, and reliability is the proportion of score variance that is true variance. The main types each check consistency in a different way, and options a, b and c are all genuine types. Test-retest reliability (a) gives the same test to the same people twice and correlates the two sets of scores; it measures stability over time. Alternate-form, or parallel-form, reliability (c) gives two equivalent versions of the test and correlates them; it measures equivalence. Split-half reliability (b) divides one test into two halves, usually odd versus even items, correlates them, and corrects the result with the Spearman-Brown formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910), because each half is only half as long as the full test. G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937) and Lee Cronbach (1951, coefficient alpha) extended this internal consistency idea. Item difficulty, by contrast, is the proportion of test takers who answer an item correctly, written p. An item that 80% pass has p equal to 0.80, an easy item; items near 0.50 usually discriminate best. It describes single items, not the consistency of total scores. Example: Dr Sharma builds a reasoning test. She uses item difficulty to drop items that nobody or everybody gets right, then checks split-half and test-retest reliability for the final version. Option d is right because item difficulty is an item-analysis statistic, while the other three are recognised methods of estimating reliability. Exam trap: item difficulty sounds technical, like a reliability term, but it belongs with item discrimination in item analysis.", "topic": "Types of reliability"}, {"q": "A test is constructed by including items that differentiate between addicted group and normals. The test therefore relies on the concept of –", "options": {"a": "Predictive validity", "b": "Construct validity", "c": "Concurrent validity", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: a test built from items that separate an addicted group from normals relies on concurrent validity. Criterion-related validity asks how well test scores relate to an external criterion, an independent measure of what the test is meant to show. It has two forms that differ only in timing. In concurrent validity the test and the criterion are measured at about the same time, here the person's present membership of an addicted or a normal group. In predictive validity the criterion is measured later, such as relapse a year after treatment. The method in the stem is the contrasted-groups, or empirical criterion-keying, approach: items are tried on two groups already known to differ, and only items that separate them are kept, whatever their content. Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) built the MMPI this way, and Craig MacAndrew (1965) built his alcoholism scale by comparing alcoholic and non-alcoholic psychiatric patients. Anne Anastasi (1954), in Psychological Testing, lists contrasted groups as criterion-related, concurrent evidence, because the criterion (current diagnosis) already exists at the time of testing. Example: Dr Fatima gives 300 items to 100 patients in a Bengaluru de-addiction centre and 100 people from the community, and keeps the 40 items the two groups answer most differently. Option c is right because the test is validated against a present, known criterion: current group membership. Exam trap: predictive validity sounds right because the test may later be used to predict, but nothing in the stem is measured in the future. Key note: other texts file the contrasted-groups (known-groups) method under construct validity, so option b has support. Following Anastasi, the key treats it as concurrent criterion evidence. The official key stands.", "topic": "Contrasted groups and concurrent validity"}, {"q": "Which of the following test results is based at least partly on Holland's model of occupational themes?", "options": {"a": "Rorschach inkblot test", "b": "Blacky pictures", "c": "Thematic Apperception test", "d": "Strong-Campbell interest inventory"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Strong-Campbell Interest Inventory is based at least partly on John Holland's model of occupational themes. Edward Kellogg Strong Jr. (1927) published the Strong Vocational Interest Blank, built by empirical keying: a person's likes and dislikes were compared with those of people already working happily in each occupation, such as engineers or teachers. In 1974 David Campbell revised it as the Strong-Campbell Interest Inventory and organised it around John L. Holland's (1959, set out fully 1973) theory of vocational choice. Holland proposed six personality types and matching work environments, known as RIASEC: Realistic (practical, hands-on), Investigative (scientific), Artistic (creative), Social (helping), Enterprising (persuading, leading) and Conventional (orderly, clerical). He arranged them on a hexagon: neighbouring types are similar, and opposite types such as Realistic and Social are least alike. People are expected to be most satisfied in environments that match their type. The Strong-Campbell reports six General Occupational Themes taken from RIASEC, along with Basic Interest Scales and Occupational Scales; today it is the Strong Interest Inventory. Holland's own Self-Directed Search (1971) also uses RIASEC. Example: Siddharth's Strong-Campbell profile is highest on Investigative and Artistic, so his counsellor suggests fields such as architecture, design research or psychology. Option d is right because it is the only interest inventory among the options, and it explicitly scores Holland's six occupational themes. Exam trap: the other three options are all projective personality tests, which aim at unconscious needs and conflicts, not vocational interests. Also do not confuse Strong's empirical occupational keying with the forced-choice Kuder Preference Record of G. Frederic Kuder (1939).", "topic": "Holland's RIASEC and the Strong-Campbell"}, {"q": "Which type of neurons id called efferent neurons", "options": {"a": "Sensory neuron", "b": "Motor neuron", "c": "Interneuron", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: efferent neurons are motor neurons. Neurons are classified by function into three types. Sensory neurons are afferent: they carry information from receptors in the skin, eyes, ears and organs towards the central nervous system (CNS). Motor neurons are efferent: they carry commands away from the CNS out to effectors, the muscles and glands, telling a muscle to contract or a gland to secrete. Interneurons lie wholly inside the brain and spinal cord and connect sensory to motor neurons; they are by far the most numerous. The words come from Latin: afferent means 'carrying towards' and efferent means 'carrying away'. A handy memory aid is SAME: Sensory Afferent, Motor Efferent. Charles Bell (1811) and Francois Magendie (1822) showed that the dorsal roots of spinal nerves carry sensory (afferent) fibres in and the ventral roots carry motor (efferent) fibres out, the Bell-Magendie law. The reflex arc shows all three types in order: receptor, sensory neuron, interneuron in the spinal cord, motor neuron, muscle. Example: Divya touches a hot tawa. Sensory neurons send the pain signal to her spinal cord, an interneuron relays it, and motor (efferent) neurons fire to her arm muscles, so her hand jerks away before she even feels the pain. Option b is right because a motor neuron carries signals away from the CNS to muscles and glands, which is exactly what efferent means. Exam trap: afferent and efferent differ by one letter and are easily swapped. Remember that afferent arrives at the CNS and efferent exits it. Interneurons are neither afferent nor efferent in the usual sense, because they stay inside the CNS.", "topic": "Efferent (motor) neurons"}, {"q": "Parasympathetic and sympathetic nervous system are a part of –", "options": {"a": "Central nervous system", "b": "Somatic nervous system", "c": "Autonomic nervous system", "d": "Brain"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems are the two main divisions of the autonomic nervous system (ANS). The nervous system has two major parts. The central nervous system (CNS) is the brain and spinal cord. The peripheral nervous system (PNS) is all the nerves outside them, and it has two branches. The somatic nervous system carries information from the senses and controls voluntary skeletal muscles. The autonomic nervous system controls involuntary functions: heart rate, breathing, digestion, sweating, pupil size and the glands. John Newport Langley (1898) coined the term 'autonomic', meaning self-governing, and later named its parasympathetic division. The sympathetic division prepares the body for action: it speeds the heart, widens the pupils, releases glucose and slows digestion, the 'fight-or-flight' response described by Walter Cannon (1915). Its nerves leave the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord. The parasympathetic division calms the body and conserves energy, 'rest and digest': it slows the heart, narrows the pupils and stimulates digestion. Its nerves leave the brainstem (through cranial nerves such as the vagus) and the sacral spinal cord. Most organs receive both divisions, which usually work in opposition. Example: when a dog suddenly runs at Rohit, his heart pounds and his mouth goes dry (sympathetic); once he is safely home, his heart slows and he feels hungry again (parasympathetic). Option c is right because both systems are divisions of the ANS, which is itself part of the PNS. Exam trap: the somatic system is the other half of the PNS, so students sometimes pick it. Somatic means voluntary skeletal muscle; autonomic means involuntary organs and glands.", "topic": "Divisions of the autonomic nervous system"}, {"q": "Which neurotransmitter is responsible for parasympathetic response in the bosy?", "options": {"a": "Acetylcholine", "b": "Dopamine", "c": "Serotonin", "d": "Epinephrine"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: acetylcholine (ACh) is the neurotransmitter responsible for the parasympathetic response. Autonomic pathways have two neurons in series: a preganglionic neuron leaves the CNS and synapses in a ganglion, and a postganglionic neuron runs on to the target organ. All preganglionic neurons, sympathetic and parasympathetic, release ACh. The divisions differ at the target. Parasympathetic postganglionic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic receptors, so the parasympathetic division is called cholinergic. Most sympathetic postganglionic neurons release norepinephrine (noradrenaline), so the sympathetic division is called adrenergic. The classic evidence is Otto Loewi's (1921) frog heart experiment. He stimulated the vagus nerve, a parasympathetic nerve, of one frog heart, which slowed it, then moved the fluid bathing it to a second heart, which also slowed. A chemical he called Vagusstoff ('vagus substance') had carried the message, and it was soon identified as acetylcholine, whose actions Henry Dale (1914) had already described; the two shared the 1936 Nobel Prize. This was the first proof of chemical transmission between nerve and organ. Example: after a heavy lunch, Aditya feels drowsy and his heart rate falls while his stomach gets busy digesting. Parasympathetic fibres in his vagus nerve are releasing ACh onto his heart and gut. Drugs that block ACh at muscarinic receptors, such as atropine, produce the opposite: a fast heart, dry mouth and dilated pupils. Option a is right because ACh is the transmitter that parasympathetic neurons release at their target organs, as Loewi's 'vagus substance' showed. Exam trap: epinephrine and norepinephrine go with the sympathetic 'fight-or-flight' response, not the parasympathetic one; epinephrine is mainly a hormone from the adrenal medulla. Dopamine and serotonin are mainly brain transmitters for reward, movement and mood.", "topic": "Acetylcholine and the parasympathetic system"}, {"q": "Reticular formation extends from –", "options": {"a": "Forebrain to mid brain", "b": "hind brain to fore brain", "c": "hind brain to mid brain", "d": "left hemisphere to right hemisphere"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the reticular formation extends from the hindbrain to the midbrain. The reticular formation is a net-like web of about 100 small nuclei and their connecting fibres (Latin reticulum, 'little net') that runs through the central core of the brainstem: the medulla and pons of the hindbrain and the tegmentum of the midbrain. It has descending and ascending parts. The descending part helps control muscle tone, posture and the cardiac and respiratory reflexes. The ascending part, the reticular activating system (RAS), sends fibres up through the thalamus to the cortex and regulates arousal, wakefulness, attention and consciousness. It filters incoming stimulation, which is why a parent can sleep through traffic noise yet wake at a baby's cry. The classic study is by Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun (1949). When they electrically stimulated the reticular formation of drowsy or lightly anaesthetised cats, their EEG changed from slow, synchronised waves to the fast, desynchronised pattern of alertness. Damage to the RAS causes coma, and general anaesthetics work partly by suppressing it. Example: Sneha is dozing in a late lecture when her name is suddenly called, and her reticular activating system snaps her cortex into alertness. Option c is right because the reticular formation's nuclei lie in the brainstem core, from the medulla through the pons to the midbrain, that is, from hindbrain to midbrain. Exam trap: the RAS projects upwards into the forebrain, so students confuse where its fibres go with where its nuclei sit. Key note: many texts say the reticular formation extends up into the thalamus of the forebrain, so option b has support. The key takes the brainstem core, hindbrain to midbrain, as its extent. The official key stands.", "topic": "Reticular formation and RAS"}, {"q": "Which of the following endocrine gland affects metabolism rate?", "options": {"a": "Adrenal gland", "b": "Hypothalamus", "c": "Ovaries", "d": "Thyroid gland"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the thyroid gland controls the body's metabolic rate. The endocrine system is a set of ductless glands that release hormones directly into the bloodstream. The thyroid is a butterfly-shaped gland at the front of the neck, below the larynx. It makes thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), both of which need iodine, and these set the basal metabolic rate: how fast cells burn fuel and produce heat. Edward Calvin Kendall (1914) first isolated thyroxine. The thyroid is run by a feedback chain, the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis: the hypothalamus releases TRH, which tells the pituitary to release TSH, which tells the thyroid to release T3 and T4. Too little thyroid hormone (hypothyroidism) brings sluggishness, weight gain, feeling cold, slowed thinking and low mood, so it can be mistaken for depression; in infants, untreated deficiency causes congenital hypothyroidism (cretinism) with intellectual disability. Too much (hyperthyroidism, as in Graves' disease) brings weight loss, a racing heart, heat intolerance, restlessness and anxiety. Iodine deficiency causes goitre, a swollen thyroid, which is why India iodises salt. Example: Lakshmi has felt tired, cold and low for months and has gained weight. Her psychiatrist orders a thyroid function test before starting antidepressants, and it shows hypothyroidism. Option d is right because thyroid hormones are the main regulators of metabolic rate. Exam trap: the adrenal glands also affect metabolism, since cortisol raises blood sugar, and the hypothalamus starts the thyroid chain, so both are tempting. But the gland whose hormones directly set the metabolic rate is the thyroid. For clinical work, remember that thyroid problems can mimic depression and anxiety.", "topic": "Thyroid and metabolic rate"}, {"q": "Who gave the opponent process theory of colour vision?", "options": {"a": "Young-Helmholtz", "b": "Ewald Hering", "c": "George Berkeley", "d": "J.J. Gibson"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the opponent-process theory of colour vision was given by Ewald Hering (1878). Hering proposed that colour is coded by three opposed pairs of processes: red versus green, blue versus yellow, and black versus white. Each pair works like a seesaw: activity for one member of a pair inhibits the other. This explains facts the earlier trichromatic theory could not. Nobody sees a 'reddish green' or a 'bluish yellow', because both members of a pair cannot be signalled at once. It also explains negative afterimages, in which a fatigued process lets its opponent rebound, and the fact that colour blindness often removes a whole pair, such as red-green. Leo Hurvich and Dorothea Jameson (1957) gave the theory quantitative support, and Russell De Valois (1966) found opponent cells in the lateral geniculate nucleus of monkeys. The modern dual-process view combines both theories: the trichromatic theory of Thomas Young (1802) and Hermann von Helmholtz (1852) holds at the level of the three cone types in the retina, while opponent processing holds in the ganglion cells and the thalamus beyond them. Example: Zoya stares at a bright red circle for 30 seconds, then looks at a white wall and sees a green circle floating there. Her red process has tired, so its opponent, green, briefly dominates. Option b is right because Hering is the author of the opponent-process theory of colour vision. Exam trap: Young and Helmholtz appear in the options because their trichromatic theory is the rival account of colour vision. Also do not confuse Hering's theory with the opponent-process theory of motivation and emotion by Richard Solomon and John Corbit (1974), which explains drug tolerance and withdrawal.", "topic": "Hering's opponent-process theory"}, {"q": "Stanley Milgram is a Pioneer in the field of –", "options": {"a": "Obedience", "b": "Conformity", "c": "Compliance", "d": "Social learning"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: Stanley Milgram is the pioneer of research on obedience to authority. Obedience is a form of social influence in which a person follows a direct order from someone seen as a legitimate authority. It differs from conformity (going along with a group when nobody gives an order) and from compliance (agreeing to a request from an equal). Stanley Milgram (1963), working at Yale University, told volunteers they were helping with a study of punishment and learning. Each volunteer, the 'teacher', had to give electric shocks of rising strength, from 15 to 450 volts, to a 'learner' (in fact an actor who received no shocks) every time he made a mistake. When the teacher hesitated, the experimenter in a grey lab coat used fixed prods such as 'The experiment requires that you continue'. About 65 per cent of participants went all the way to 450 volts, far more than psychiatrists had predicted. In later variations obedience fell when the experimenter gave orders by telephone, when the learner sat in the same room, and when two other 'teachers' refused. Milgram explained the results through the agentic state: people stop seeing themselves as responsible and act as agents of the authority. The study also started the modern debate on research ethics, because participants suffered real stress. Example: Arjun, a junior nurse, gives a patient a dose he thinks is too high simply because a senior doctor orders it; that is obedience, not conformity. Option a is right because obedience is the topic Milgram's experiments created and defined. Exam trap: examiners place Milgram next to Solomon Asch (1951), who is the pioneer of conformity with his line-judgement study, while Milgram is obedience. Remember 'Asch is the group, Milgram is the order'.", "topic": "Milgram and obedience"}, {"q": "The tendency that overtime the persuasive impact of a high credibility source decreases while the persuasive impact of a low credibility source increases is called", "options": {"a": "Reciprocity hypothesis", "b": "Social exchange theory", "c": "Sleeper effect", "d": "Placebo effect"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: the sleeper effect is the delayed rise in the persuasive impact of a message from a low-credibility source, while a high-credibility message loses some of its impact over time. Source credibility, meaning how expert and trustworthy a speaker seems, matters a lot at first: we believe a doctor more than a stranger. But memory for the source and memory for the message fade at different rates. Carl Hovland and Walter Weiss (1951) gave American students identical articles, credited either to a trusted source such as the physicist Robert Oppenheimer or to a distrusted source such as the Soviet newspaper Pravda. Straight after reading, the high-credibility version changed opinions much more. Four weeks later the difference had almost vanished: agreement with the trusted source had dropped and agreement with the distrusted source had risen. The usual explanation is the discounting cue hypothesis. The low-credibility source works as a cue that tells us to discount the message; when that cue is forgotten, or dissociated from the content, the arguments act on their own. Anthony Pratkanis and colleagues (1988) later found the effect is most reliable when the discounting cue comes after a strong message. Example: Kabir sees a forwarded WhatsApp claim about a herbal cure and dismisses it because it came from an unknown group. A month later he remembers the claim but not where it came from, and now he half believes it. Option c is right because the stem describes exactly this crossover over time: high-credibility impact falls while low-credibility impact rises. Exam trap: students pick 'placebo effect' because both involve belief without a real cause, but placebo is improvement after an inactive treatment, not persuasion changing with source credibility over time.", "topic": "Sleeper effect in persuasion"}, {"q": "If Sohail like Rajat, Rajat likes movies and Sohail hated movies, the relationship between them is in a state of", "options": {"a": "Balance", "b": "Imbalance", "c": "Semi-balance", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the triad is in a state of imbalance, because the product of its three relations is negative. Fritz Heider (1946, 1958) proposed balance theory, one of the first consistency theories of attitudes. It describes a triangle of three elements: a person (P), another person (O) and an object or issue (X). Each link is either positive (like, agree) or negative (dislike, disagree). To test a triad, multiply the three signs. If the product is positive, the system is balanced and feels comfortable. If it is negative, the system is imbalanced, it feels tense, and the person is motivated to change something. Here P is Sohail, O is Rajat and X is movies. Sohail likes Rajat (plus), Rajat likes movies (plus) and Sohail hates movies (minus). Plus times plus times minus gives minus, so the triad is imbalanced. Sohail can restore balance in three ways: start to enjoy movies, like Rajat less, or persuade Rajat that movies are a waste of time. Heider held that people usually choose the change that needs the least effort. A quick rule: a triad is balanced when it has zero or two negative links, and imbalanced when it has one or three. For example, if Meera dislikes Tara and Tara dislikes cricket, Meera liking cricket gives two negatives and is balanced, because disagreeing with someone you dislike feels fine. Balance theory led to the congruity theory of Charles Osgood and Percy Tannenbaum (1955) and to the cognitive dissonance theory of Leon Festinger (1957). Option b is right because one negative relation among three makes the sign product negative, which is the definition of imbalance. Exam trap: some students count 'two likes and one dislike' as mostly positive and call it balanced, or invent a middle state; Heider's model has only two states, balanced or imbalanced, decided by the sign product.", "topic": "Heider's balance theory"}, {"q": "Electra conflict occurs during which stage of development ?", "options": {"a": "Oral", "b": "Phallic", "c": "Genital", "d": "Anal"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Electra conflict occurs during the phallic stage, roughly ages 3 to 6. Sigmund Freud (1905) proposed that personality develops through psychosexual stages, each centred on a body zone where the libido (sexual energy) seeks pleasure: oral (birth to about 1), anal (about 1 to 3), phallic (about 3 to 6), latency (6 to puberty) and genital (puberty onwards). In the phallic stage the genitals become the focus, and the child forms an unconscious attraction to the opposite-sex parent along with rivalry towards the same-sex parent. For boys Freud called this the Oedipus complex, driven by castration anxiety. For girls he described penis envy: the girl blames her mother, turns towards her father and finally resolves the conflict by identifying with her mother, though in Freud's view less completely than boys do. Carl Gustav Jung (1913) named the girls' version the Electra complex, after the Greek heroine who helps to kill her mother; Freud himself rejected the term and spoke of the female Oedipus complex. Resolving the conflict through identification with the same-sex parent forms the superego and gender identity. Example: four-year-old Riya announces that she will marry her father and gets cross when her mother sits next to him; a Freudian reads this as the Electra phase, expected to fade as Riya starts copying her mother. Option b is right because the Electra and Oedipus conflicts are the defining events of the phallic stage. Exam trap: examiners offer 'genital' because the words sound alike; the genital stage begins at puberty and concerns mature adult sexuality, while the family triangle belongs to the phallic stage. Also note that 'Electra' is Jung's label, not Freud's, a favourite attribution question.", "topic": "Psychosexual stages: Electra complex"}, {"q": "The zone of proximal development was given by –", "options": {"a": "Erik Erickson", "b": "Jean Piaget", "c": "Lev Vygotsky", "d": "Sigmund Freud"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: the zone of proximal development (ZPD) was given by the Russian psychologist Lev Vygotsky. Lev Vygotsky (1896 to 1934) developed sociocultural theory, which holds that higher mental functions begin in social interaction and are then internalised by the child. His ideas reached English readers mainly through Mind in Society (1978). The ZPD is the gap between what a child can do alone (the actual developmental level) and what the child can do with guidance from an adult or a more capable peer (the potential developmental level). Teaching works best inside this zone, on tasks the child cannot yet do alone but can manage with help. If the task is too easy nothing new is learnt; if it is too hard, help cannot bridge the gap. David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976) later coined 'scaffolding' for the support a tutor gives and gradually withdraws as competence grows. Vygotsky also stressed private speech (children talking aloud to guide their own actions) and the role of language and culture in shaping thought. Example: Arjun, aged six, cannot finish a 30-piece jigsaw alone, but when his older sister suggests starting with the edges and sorting pieces by colour, he completes it. A week later he does it by himself. The jigsaw was in his ZPD, and his sister's hints were scaffolding. Option c is right because the ZPD is Vygotsky's central educational concept. Exam trap: Jean Piaget (1936) is the common wrong answer because both theorists studied cognitive development; Piaget saw the child as a lone scientist moving through fixed stages, while Vygotsky stressed social guidance. Also, the word 'scaffolding' is not Vygotsky's own; it came from Wood, Bruner and Ross.", "topic": "Zone of proximal development"}, {"q": "An important feature of cognition during the preoperational stage is –", "options": {"a": "Centration", "b": "Object permanence", "c": "Primary circular reaction", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: centration is an important feature of cognition in the preoperational stage. Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980) described four stages of cognitive development: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 onwards). In the preoperational stage the child can use symbols (words, images, pretend play) but cannot yet carry out 'operations', which are mental actions that can be reversed. Centration is the tendency to focus on one striking feature of a situation and ignore the others. It explains failure on conservation tasks. In the classic liquid task, water is poured from a short, wide glass into a tall, thin one, and a five-year-old says the tall glass now holds more, because the child centres on height and ignores width. The child also fails because of irreversibility (being unable to pour the water back mentally) and because of a focus on end states rather than on the transformation. Other preoperational features include egocentrism (shown in the three mountains task), animism (believing objects are alive) and transductive reasoning. Example: Meera, aged four, cries that her brother got 'more' biscuit because his was broken into four pieces and hers into two; she centres on the number of pieces, not the total amount. Decentration, attending to several features at once, arrives in the concrete operational stage. Option a is right because centration is a defining limit of preoperational thought. Exam trap: object permanence is the distractor because it is also a famous Piagetian term, but it is the main achievement of the earlier sensorimotor stage, usually secure by about 18 to 24 months, not a feature of preoperational thinking.", "topic": "Preoperational stage: centration"}, {"q": "Which among the following of Erickson's stage of conflict encompasses physiological revolution", "options": {"a": "Initiative vs guilt", "b": "Identity vs role confusion", "c": "Intimacy vs isolation", "d": "Industry vs inferiority"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: identity versus role confusion is the stage that Erik Erikson linked with the 'physiological revolution' of puberty. Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, proposed eight psychosocial stages across the lifespan. Each stage poses a crisis between two poles, and a good resolution adds a virtue to the ego. Stage five, identity versus role confusion, covers adolescence (about 12 to 18 years). Erikson wrote that young people, 'faced with this physiological revolution within them', meaning the rapid growth spurt and sexual maturation of puberty, must work out who they are while also facing the adult tasks ahead. Because body and feelings change so fast, the earlier sense of sameness and continuity is questioned again. The task is to integrate past identifications, new sexual feelings, abilities and social roles into a coherent identity. Success brings the virtue of fidelity; failure brings role confusion, an unclear sense of self and direction. Erikson also described the psychosocial moratorium, a period society allows for trying out roles. James Marcia (1966) later turned these ideas into four identity statuses: diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium and achievement. Example: Kabir, aged 15, has grown 10 centimetres in a year and feels awkward in his new body. He switches from wanting to be a cricketer to an engineer to a musician and keeps asking 'who am I?'. That is the identity crisis. Option b is right because the bodily upheaval of puberty is the trigger Erikson named for the adolescent identity crisis. Exam trap: students pick intimacy versus isolation because it involves sexuality, but that is the young-adult stage of forming close relationships after identity is settled; puberty itself belongs to identity versus role confusion.", "topic": "Erikson: identity versus role confusion"}, {"q": "The neonatal reflex wherein the infant's toes automatically spreads apart when the soles of their feet are stimulated is called –", "options": {"a": "Rooting", "b": "Babinski", "c": "Moro", "d": "Grasping"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Babinski reflex is the neonatal reflex in which the toes fan out when the sole of the foot is stroked. Newborns arrive with a set of reflexes, automatic responses to specific stimuli controlled mainly by the brainstem and spinal cord. Some are survival reflexes (rooting, sucking, breathing); others are primitive reflexes with no clear present use, which fade as the cerebral cortex matures. The Babinski reflex is named after the French neurologist Joseph Babinski (1896). When the sole is stroked firmly from the heel towards the toes, the big toe bends upwards and the other toes spread like a fan. It is normal until about 12 months, sometimes a little longer, and then gives way to the adult response, in which the toes curl downwards. Its disappearance shows that the corticospinal (pyramidal) tract has matured. So its return in an adult, called a positive Babinski sign, points to damage to the upper motor neurones, for example after a stroke, which makes it one of the most useful bedside tests in neurology. Paediatricians check a whole battery of reflexes, because both absence in infancy and persistence beyond the usual age can signal a nervous system problem. Example: at Meera's 3-month check-up, the paediatrician strokes the sole of her foot and her toes fan out, which is normal. If her 40-year-old uncle showed the same response, the doctor would order a brain scan. Option b is right because spreading of the toes when the sole is stimulated is the textbook description of the Babinski reflex. Exam trap: the grasping reflex is the near miss; in the plantar grasp the toes curl around a finger pressed at the base of the toes, which is the opposite of fanning out.", "topic": "Newborn reflexes: Babinski"}, {"q": "The term 'holophrasis' means", "options": {"a": "A toddler's use of a single word to express a complete thought", "b": "A toddler's use of a sentence to express a complete thought", "c": "A toddler's use of a single word to express an incomplete thought", "d": "A toddler's use of a sentence to express an incomplete thought"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: holophrasis means a toddler's use of a single word to express a complete thought. 'Holophrase' comes from Greek roots meaning 'whole phrase'. Language develops in a predictable order: cooing at about 2 months (vowel-like sounds), babbling at about 6 months (consonant-vowel strings such as 'ba-ba'), then the first words at about 12 months. In this one-word stage, roughly 12 to 18 months, a single word does the work of a whole sentence. Its meaning depends on context, tone of voice and gesture. 'Milk' can mean 'I want milk', 'The milk has spilled' or 'That is milk', and parents read the intended meaning from the situation. Around 18 to 24 months comes what Roger Brown (1973) called telegraphic speech: two-word or short utterances that keep the content words and drop the function words, like a telegram ('more juice', 'daddy go'). By 2 to 3 years children start overregularising grammar rules ('two foots', 'I goed'). The holophrase stage also shows that comprehension runs ahead of production: toddlers understand many more words than they can say. Example: 14-month-old Kabir points at the door and says 'Out!', meaning 'I want to go outside now'. The single word carries a full, complete thought. Option a is right because the defining feature of a holophrase is one word standing for a complete idea or sentence. Exam trap: the four options differ on two details, a single word versus a sentence, and a complete versus an incomplete thought. A sentence expressing a complete thought is simply mature speech, and 'incomplete thought' reverses the point, since the holophrase packs a whole idea into one word.", "topic": "Holophrases in early language"}, {"q": "Who gave the learned helpnessness theory of depression?", "options": {"a": "John Dollard", "b": "Neal Miller", "c": "Albert Ellis", "d": "Martin Seligman"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: Martin Seligman gave the learned helplessness theory of depression. Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967) placed dogs in a harness and gave them electric shocks. One group could stop the shock by pressing a panel; a yoked group received identical shocks but had no control; a third group received none. Later, in a shuttle box, the dogs could escape shock by jumping a low barrier. Most of the yoked dogs did not even try; they lay down and whimpered. The key was not the pain but the lack of control: the animals had learned that their actions did not matter. Seligman (1975) proposed that human depression works the same way, since helpless dogs showed passivity, weight loss and low motivation, much like depressed people. The original model could not explain why only some people become depressed, or why depressed people blame themselves. So Lyn Abramson, Martin Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) produced the attributional reformulation: people who explain bad events by internal ('it is me'), stable ('it will last') and global ('it ruins everything') causes are most at risk. This later grew into the hopelessness theory of Lyn Abramson, Gerald Metalsky and Lauren Alloy (1989). Seligman went on to study learned optimism and, in 1998, launched positive psychology. Example: Riya fails three maths tests despite studying hard, decides 'I am stupid, I will always fail, I fail at everything', and stops studying altogether; that pattern is learned helplessness. Option d is right because Seligman's dog experiments and his model of depression created the concept. Exam trap: Albert Ellis (1955) is a tempting wrong answer because he also wrote about the thinking of depressed people, but Ellis gave rational emotive behaviour therapy and irrational beliefs, not learned helplessness.", "topic": "Learned helplessness and depression"}, {"q": "A key feature of which is that the therapist might challenge an irrational belief that the client has, helping him/her recognize these beliefs and change them into more rational ones.", "options": {"a": "Behaviour therapy", "b": "Rational-emotive therapy", "c": "Cognitive-behaviour therapy", "d": "Psychoanalysis"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: actively challenging a client's irrational beliefs and turning them into rational ones is the key feature of rational-emotive therapy. Albert Ellis (1955) founded it as rational therapy, renamed it rational-emotive therapy in 1961 and rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT) in 1993. Its core is the ABC model. A is the activating event, B is the belief about the event, and C is the emotional and behavioural consequence. Ellis argued that it is B, not A, that causes C. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands, such as 'I must be loved by everyone', which he called 'musturbation'; they lead to awfulising ('this is the worst thing that could happen') and low frustration tolerance ('I can't stand it'). Therapy adds D, disputing, and E, the effective new belief. The therapist is direct and even confrontational, asking logical, empirical and practical questions such as 'Where is the evidence that you must be liked by everyone?' Homework and shame-attacking exercises help the client practise the new beliefs. Example: Arjun panics before presentations. A: his manager asks him to present. B: 'I must perform perfectly or I am worthless.' C: anxiety and avoidance. His therapist disputes the 'must' until Arjun reaches E: 'I would like to do well, but a mistake does not make me worthless.' Option b is right because 'irrational beliefs' and their direct disputation are the trademark of Ellis's approach. Exam trap: cognitive-behaviour therapy is the near miss, because Aaron Beck's (1967) cognitive therapy also tests thoughts; Beck targets automatic thoughts and cognitive distortions through gentle, collaborative Socratic questioning, while 'irrational beliefs' challenged head-on is Ellis's language. Key note: Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy and CBT in general also dispute unhelpful thoughts, so option c has some support. But 'irrational beliefs' is Albert Ellis's own term and the hallmark of REBT, so the official key b stands.", "topic": "Ellis's rational-emotive therapy"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a range of neurodevelopmental disorders characterized by impairment in social skills and communication skills as well as by repetitive behaviours ?", "options": {"a": "Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder", "b": "Manic depressive psychosis", "c": "Autism spectrum", "d": "Obsessive compulsive disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is the neurodevelopmental condition defined by impaired social and communication skills together with repetitive behaviours. Neurodevelopmental disorders begin in the developmental period, often before school age, and include intellectual disability, communication disorders, ASD, ADHD, specific learning disorder and motor disorders. Leo Kanner (1943) first described 'early infantile autism' in children who were aloof and insisted on sameness, and Hans Asperger (1944) described more verbal children with similar social difficulties. DSM-5 (2013) merged autistic disorder, Asperger's disorder, childhood disintegrative disorder and pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified into one spectrum. Diagnosis needs two domains. First, persistent deficits in social communication and interaction: poor back-and-forth conversation, reduced eye contact and gestures, and difficulty making and keeping friends. Second, restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests or activities: hand-flapping, lining up toys, insistence on routines, intense narrow interests, and over- or under-reaction to sensory input. Severity is rated at three levels of support needed. 'Spectrum' means the picture ranges from a non-verbal child with intellectual disability to a fluent, highly able adult. Example: four-year-old Kabir rarely looks at his parents, does not point to share interest, repeats lines from TV adverts and screams if his toy cars are moved out of their row. Features in both domains suggest ASD. Option c is right because the stem lists the two DSM-5 domains of autism, social-communication deficits plus repetitive behaviours, within the neurodevelopmental group. Exam trap: ADHD is also neurodevelopmental and children with it can struggle socially, but its core is inattention and hyperactivity-impulsivity, not repetitive behaviour. OCD has repetitive acts, but they are driven by obsessions and OCD is not a neurodevelopmental disorder.", "topic": "Autism spectrum disorder"}, {"q": "The term schizophrenia was coined by", "options": {"a": "Eugen Bleuler", "b": "David Rosenhan", "c": "Thomas Szasz", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: the term schizophrenia was coined by Eugen Bleuler. The Swiss psychiatrist Eugen Bleuler (1857 to 1939) introduced the word in a 1908 lecture and set it out fully in his 1911 book Dementia Praecox or the Group of Schizophrenias. Before him, Emil Kraepelin (1893, 1899) had grouped several pictures under 'dementia praecox' (premature dementia), stressing early onset and a deteriorating course. Bleuler disagreed on two points: not every patient deteriorates, and onset is not always early. He built 'schizophrenia' from the Greek schizein (to split) and phren (mind). The split he meant is a splitting of psychic functions, so that thought, emotion and behaviour lose their normal connection; it does not mean a split or multiple personality. He also wrote of a 'group' of schizophrenias, an early form of today's spectrum idea. Bleuler's fundamental symptoms are remembered as the four As: loosening of associations, disturbed affect, ambivalence and autism (withdrawal into a private inner world). He regarded hallucinations and delusions as accessory symptoms. Example: Rohan's relatives say he has a 'split personality' because of his schizophrenia. The clinician corrects them: Bleuler's split is between Rohan's thoughts and feelings, as when he smiles while describing his father's death. Option a is right because both the word and the idea of a group of schizophrenias come from Bleuler. Exam trap: Emil Kraepelin is the classic distractor because he described the illness first, as dementia praecox; Kraepelin named and classified it, and Bleuler renamed it. Also remember that 'split mind' never means dissociative identity disorder.", "topic": "Origin of the term schizophrenia"}, {"q": "False beliefs, discordant with reality that are maintained in spite of string evidence to the contrary are called –", "options": {"a": "Hallucinations", "b": "Delusions", "c": "Hypomania", "d": "Agoraphobia"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: false beliefs that clash with reality and are kept despite strong evidence to the contrary are called delusions. A delusion is a disorder of thought content, not of perception. DSM-5 (2013) defines delusions as fixed beliefs that are not amenable to change in the light of conflicting evidence. Karl Jaspers (1913), in General Psychopathology, gave three classic criteria: the belief is held with extraordinary conviction (certainty), it cannot be changed by experience or argument (incorrigibility), and its content is false or impossible. A further rule is cultural: a belief shared by the person's culture or religion, such as belief in karma or the evil eye, is not a delusion. Common types are persecutory (being plotted against), grandiose (special powers or identity), referential (ordinary events carry a special personal message), erotomanic (someone famous is in love with the person), nihilistic (organs are rotting or the world has ended) and somatic. Delusions are called bizarre when they are clearly impossible, such as aliens removing organs without leaving a scar. They are a positive symptom of schizophrenia and also occur in delusional disorder, mania and psychotic depression. Example: Vikram insists his neighbours have hidden cameras in his ceiling fan to read his thoughts. His family takes the fan apart to show him there is nothing inside, but he says the cameras were moved during the night. The belief is false, fixed and resistant to evidence. Option b is right because the stem gives the textbook definition of a delusion. Exam trap: hallucinations are the near miss because both are psychotic positive symptoms; a hallucination is a false perception (hearing a voice when no one is there), while a delusion is a false belief.", "topic": "Delusions: definition"}, {"q": "Tourette's disorder is characterized by", "options": {"a": "Multiple motor tics", "b": "One or more vocal ticks", "c": "Both a and b", "d": "Repetitive compulsion"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: Tourette's disorder is characterised by both multiple motor tics and one or more vocal tics. A tic is a sudden, rapid, recurrent, non-rhythmic movement or vocalisation. Motor tics range from simple (eye blinking, shoulder shrugging, grimacing) to complex (touching, jumping, copying other people's gestures). Vocal or phonic tics range from simple (throat clearing, sniffing, grunting) to complex (repeating words, echolalia and, in a minority, coprolalia, the involuntary utterance of obscenities). The French physician Georges Gilles de la Tourette (1885) described the syndrome. DSM-5 (2013) criteria for Tourette's disorder are: both multiple motor tics and one or more vocal tics have been present at some time during the illness, though not necessarily at the same time; the tics may wax and wane but have persisted for more than 1 year since the first tic; onset is before age 18; and the tics are not due to a substance or another medical condition. If a child has only motor or only vocal tics for over a year, the diagnosis is persistent (chronic) motor or vocal tic disorder; tics present for less than a year give provisional tic disorder. Tics are often preceded by a premonitory urge, can be held back for a short time, and commonly occur with ADHD and OCD. Habit reversal training is a first-line behavioural treatment. Example: eight-year-old Aarav blinks hard and jerks his neck, and several times an hour makes sniffing and barking sounds; this has gone on for 18 months. With both kinds of tic for over a year, he meets the criteria for Tourette's. Option c is right because Tourette's requires the combination of multiple motor tics and at least one vocal tic. Exam trap: coprolalia is the famous media image of Tourette's, but it occurs in only a minority and is not required for the diagnosis.", "topic": "Tourette's disorder criteria"}, {"q": "'Flat affect', which is a characteristic of schizophrenia is –", "options": {"a": "Severe reduction in the intensity of affect expression", "b": "A phase characterized by poor adjustment", "c": "Affect is discordant with the content of individual's speech or ideation", "d": "Virtually no signs of affect expression"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: flat affect means virtually no signs of affective expression. Affect is the outward expression of emotion that an observer sees in the face, voice and gestures; mood is the inner feeling the person reports. Clinicians grade reduced affect by degree. Restricted (constricted) affect shows a narrower range of emotion than usual. Blunted affect is a severe reduction in the intensity of expression. Flat affect is the extreme end: almost no expression at all, with an immobile face, little eye contact, few gestures and a monotonous voice. Flat affect is a negative symptom of schizophrenia, part of what DSM-5 (2013) calls diminished emotional expression, alongside alogia (poverty of speech), avolition, anhedonia and asociality. Eugen Bleuler (1911) counted disturbed affect among his fundamental four As. Negative symptoms predict poorer functioning and respond less well to medication than positive symptoms, and Nancy Andreasen (1982) introduced the Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms (SANS) to rate them. Flat affect does not always mean the person feels nothing inside; many report emotions they cannot show. Example: when Sunil is told his brother has been hurt in an accident, his face stays still and his voice keeps the same even tone he uses for the weather. He shows no visible emotion at all, which is flat affect. Option d is right because 'virtually no signs of affect expression' is the standard definition of flat affect. Exam trap: option a describes blunted affect, the step just before flat on the same scale; blunted is a severe reduction, flat is a near absence. Option c, emotion that does not match what the person is saying, is inappropriate affect, such as laughing while describing a death.", "topic": "Flat affect in schizophrenia"}, {"q": "Sometimes, a person with schizophrenia may invent new words. These new words are called –", "options": {"a": "Blunting", "b": "Neologisms", "c": "Prodromals", "d": "Catatonia"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: new words invented by a person with schizophrenia are called neologisms. The term comes from the Greek neos (new) and logos (word). Neologisms are a disorder of the form of thought and speech: the person creates a word that has no meaning in the shared language, or gives an ordinary word a private meaning, and uses it as though everyone should understand. They often arise by fusing two words or through sound associations. They are a sign of formal thought disorder, along with derailment (loosening of associations), tangentiality, incoherence or word salad, clang associations and thought blocking. In DSM-5 (2013) these come under disorganised speech, one of the positive symptoms. Eugen Bleuler (1911) saw loosening of associations as fundamental to schizophrenia, and neologisms are one visible form of that loosening. Neologisms also occur in Wernicke's (fluent) aphasia after damage to the left temporal lobe, where speech is fluent but empty and full of made-up words; there the cause is a brain lesion, not psychosis. Young children invent words in play too, but that is normal and not a symptom. Example: Ankit tells his psychologist that his neighbour is sending 'glorbitrons' into his room through the wall. Asked what a glorbitron is, he says it is obvious and everyone knows. The word is his own invention, a neologism. Option b is right because neologism is the exact technical term for a newly coined word in psychotic speech. Exam trap: catatonia and blunting are real schizophrenia terms, which makes them tempting, but catatonia is a motor syndrome and blunting is reduced emotional expression; neither concerns invented words.", "topic": "Neologisms in schizophrenia"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not true about the diagnosis of major depressive disorder", "options": {"a": "Atleast one week period of prominent and relatively persistent depressed mood", "b": "Loss of interest in all or almost all activities", "c": "Appetite and/or sleep disturbances", "d": "Feelings of worthlessness or excessive guilt"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: 'at least one week' of depressed mood is not true, because DSM-5 requires at least two weeks for a major depressive episode. DSM-5 (2013) diagnoses major depressive disorder (MDD) when a major depressive episode is present. That needs five or more of nine symptoms, present nearly every day during the same two-week period and marking a change from previous functioning, and at least one of the five must be either (1) depressed mood or (2) markedly diminished interest or pleasure in all, or almost all, activities (anhedonia). The other symptoms are (3) significant weight or appetite change, (4) insomnia or hypersomnia, (5) psychomotor agitation or retardation, (6) fatigue or loss of energy, (7) feelings of worthlessness or excessive or inappropriate guilt, (8) poor concentration or indecisiveness, and (9) recurrent thoughts of death or suicide. The symptoms must cause clinically significant distress or impairment, must not be due to a substance or medical condition, and there must never have been a manic or hypomanic episode. So options b, c and d are all true: b is criterion 2, c combines criteria 3 and 4, and d is criterion 7. Only a is false, because it halves the duration. Example: after losing her job, Neha feels low, sleeps badly and stops enjoying music for nine days, then gradually recovers. However painful, nine days does not meet the two-week rule, so this is not a major depressive episode. Option a is right because 'at least one week' is the wrong duration; the correct figure is at least two weeks. Exam trap: one week is the duration for a manic episode (or any length if hospitalisation is needed), four days is for hypomania and two years is for persistent depressive disorder; examiners swap these numbers between mood disorders.", "topic": "Major depressive disorder criteria"}, {"q": "Which disorder was formerly known as 'Dysthymia'?", "options": {"a": "Cyclothymic disorder", "b": "Bipolar I disorder", "c": "Bipolar II disorder", "d": "Persistent depressive disorder"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: persistent depressive disorder is the disorder formerly known as dysthymia. 'Dysthymia' comes from Greek words meaning 'bad state of mind'. DSM-III (1980) and DSM-IV (1994) listed dysthymic disorder, a chronic, milder depression lasting years. DSM-5 (2013) merged dysthymic disorder with chronic major depressive disorder into a single diagnosis, persistent depressive disorder (PDD), because research found the two hard to separate and similar in course and treatment response. The criteria are depressed mood for most of the day, on more days than not, for at least two years in adults (one year in children and adolescents, in whom mood can be irritable), plus at least two of six symptoms: poor appetite or overeating, insomnia or hypersomnia, low energy, low self-esteem, poor concentration or indecisiveness, and hopelessness. During those two years the person has never been free of symptoms for more than two months at a time. Specifiers record whether full major depressive episodes also occur; when a major depressive episode sits on top of chronic low mood, older texts call it 'double depression'. ICD-11 still uses the name dysthymic disorder. Example: Ramesh, 34, says he has felt 'grey' for as long as he can remember: tired, low in confidence and pessimistic, though he keeps working. It has lasted many years with no break longer than a few weeks. That is persistent depressive disorder. Option d is right because PDD is the renamed and widened home of dysthymia in DSM-5. Exam trap: cyclothymic disorder is the classic distractor because its name looks similar and it also lasts at least two years; but cyclothymia swings between hypomanic and depressive symptoms, while dysthymia is low mood only, with no highs.", "topic": "Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia)"}, {"q": "Saurabh mistakenly believes that his upper body is misshapen and ugly. He is likely suffering from –", "options": {"a": "Hoarding disorder", "b": "Body dysmorphic disorder", "c": "Trichotillomania", "d": "Generalized anxiety disorder"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: Saurabh's mistaken belief that his upper body is misshapen and ugly points to body dysmorphic disorder (BDD). BDD is a preoccupation with one or more perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance that are not observable, or look only slight, to other people. The Italian psychiatrist Enrico Morselli (1891) first called it dysmorphophobia. DSM-5 (2013) moved BDD from the somatoform disorders into the new obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter because, like OCD, it combines intrusive preoccupation with repetitive behaviour. The criteria are: the preoccupation; at some point, repetitive behaviours (mirror checking, excessive grooming, skin picking, reassurance seeking) or mental acts (comparing one's looks with others') in response to the concern; clinically significant distress or impairment; and a concern not better explained by worry about weight or body fat in an eating disorder. A muscle dysmorphia specifier covers the belief that one's build is too small or not muscular enough, common in men who lift weights. An insight specifier runs from good insight to absent insight (delusional beliefs), which is relevant because Saurabh 'mistakenly believes'. People with BDD often seek cosmetic surgery, which rarely helps; CBT with exposure and response prevention, and SSRIs, are the evidence-based treatments. Example: Saurabh spends two hours a day checking his chest and shoulders in mirrors, wears baggy clothes and avoids the gym changing room, although his friends see nothing wrong. Option b is right because a preoccupation with an imagined or slight flaw in appearance is the defining feature of BDD. Exam trap: hoarding disorder and trichotillomania sit in the same DSM-5 chapter as BDD, which makes them tempting, but they concern possessions and hair pulling, not beliefs about how one looks.", "topic": "Body dysmorphic disorder"}, {"q": "Lithium is mostly used for treating –", "options": {"a": "Schizophrenia", "b": "OCD", "c": "Bipolar disorder", "d": "Somatoform disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: lithium is mostly used for treating bipolar disorder. Lithium is a simple salt, usually given as lithium carbonate, and it is the classic mood stabiliser. The Australian psychiatrist John Cade (1949) noticed that lithium made guinea pigs calm and then reported that it settled manic patients. The Danish psychiatrist Mogens Schou (1954) confirmed the effect in a controlled trial and later showed that lithium prevents relapse. Today it is used to treat acute mania and, above all, as maintenance treatment to prevent future manic and depressive episodes in bipolar disorder. It is also the mood stabiliser with the strongest evidence that it lowers suicide risk. Its exact mechanism is still debated; proposed actions include effects on second-messenger systems (inositol depletion) and inhibition of the enzyme GSK-3. Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index: the useful blood level is about 0.6 to 1.2 mEq/L, and toxicity begins just above it, with coarse tremor, vomiting, diarrhoea, ataxia and confusion. Patients therefore need regular blood tests, plus checks of kidney and thyroid function, because long-term use can cause hypothyroidism and kidney problems. Dehydration and some drugs raise lithium levels. Other mood stabilisers include valproate, carbamazepine and lamotrigine. Example: Priya, 28, has had two manic episodes with sleepless nights and reckless spending. Her psychiatrist starts lithium, checks her blood level every few months and keeps her on it for years to prevent relapse. Option c is right because bipolar disorder is lithium's main and best-established use. Exam trap: lithium is sometimes added to an antidepressant in stubborn unipolar depression (augmentation), or used in schizoaffective disorder, which can tempt students towards other options; its primary use remains bipolar disorder.", "topic": "Lithium for bipolar disorder"}, {"q": "The most successful treatment technique that can be used for treating phobias is", "options": {"a": "Systematic desensitization", "b": "Electroconvulsive therapy", "c": "Psychoanalysis", "d": "Transactional analysis"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: systematic desensitisation is the most successful technique among these options for treating phobias. The South African psychiatrist Joseph Wolpe (1958), in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, built it on the principle of reciprocal inhibition: two incompatible responses cannot occur at the same time, so a relaxed body cannot also be anxious. It is a form of counterconditioning, replacing the conditioned fear response with relaxation. The method has three steps. First, the client learns deep muscle relaxation, based on the progressive relaxation of Edmund Jacobson (1938). Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, a list of feared situations ranked from least to most frightening, often rated in subjective units of distress (SUDS). Third, the relaxed client imagines each item, starting at the bottom, and moves up only when an item no longer causes anxiety. An early forerunner was Mary Cover Jones (1924), who cured a boy called Peter of his fear of rabbits by bringing a rabbit gradually closer while he ate. Example: Kavya fears dogs. Her hierarchy begins with the word 'dog', moves to photos, then a dog behind a fence, and ends with patting a neighbour's dog. She relaxes at each step until she can stay calm. Modern research shows that real-life (in vivo) exposure is usually even more powerful, and graded exposure keeps the hierarchy idea; among these four options, though, systematic desensitisation is clearly the best answer. Option a is right because systematic desensitisation was designed specifically for phobic anxiety and has strong evidence behind it. Exam trap: electroconvulsive therapy is offered as a 'powerful' treatment, but it is used for severe depression and catatonia, not phobias, and psychoanalysis treats a phobia as a symptom of hidden conflict, with weak evidence.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation for phobias"}, {"q": "Acute schizophrenic episode most likely can be treated using which of the following drug?", "options": {"a": "Diphenylhydantoin", "b": "Chlorpromazine", "c": "MAO inhibitors", "d": "L-dopa"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: chlorpromazine, an antipsychotic, is the drug most likely to be used for an acute schizophrenic episode. Chlorpromazine was synthesised in 1950 at the French firm Rhone-Poulenc as an antihistamine. The surgeon Henri Laborit (1952) noticed that it made patients calm and indifferent before surgery, and the psychiatrists Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker (1952) reported that it reduced agitation, hallucinations and delusions in psychotic patients in Paris. It was the first antipsychotic and it transformed psychiatry: mental hospital populations fell sharply in the following decades. Chlorpromazine is a phenothiazine and a typical (first-generation) antipsychotic. Its main action is blocking dopamine D2 receptors, especially in the mesolimbic pathway, which reduces positive symptoms. Arvid Carlsson (1963) linked antipsychotic action to dopamine, supporting the dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia. Blocking D2 receptors in other pathways causes side effects: extrapyramidal symptoms (parkinsonism, acute dystonia, akathisia), tardive dyskinesia after long use, raised prolactin, sedation and, rarely, neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Newer atypical drugs such as risperidone, olanzapine and clozapine cause fewer motor effects but more weight gain. Example: Rahul, 22, is brought to hospital hearing voices that threaten him and refusing food because he thinks it is poisoned. He is started on an antipsychotic such as chlorpromazine (today often a newer agent), and his positive symptoms settle over the following weeks. Option b is right because chlorpromazine is the prototype antipsychotic for acute psychosis. Exam trap: L-dopa is tempting because it also acts on dopamine, but it raises dopamine to treat Parkinson's disease and can actually trigger psychotic symptoms, the opposite of what an antipsychotic does.", "topic": "Chlorpromazine and antipsychotics"}, {"q": "The technique used to extinguish fear by forcing clients to remain in anxiety arousing situation is called –", "options": {"a": "Time-out", "b": "Flooding", "c": "Inhibition", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: flooding is the technique that extinguishes fear by keeping the client in the anxiety-arousing situation until the fear fades. Flooding is a form of exposure therapy. The client is exposed, usually in real life (in vivo), to the most feared stimulus at full intensity, all at once, and is prevented from escaping. It rests on classical extinction: if the conditioned stimulus (the feared object) appears again and again without the feared outcome, the conditioned fear response weakens. It also blocks avoidance. In the two-factor theory of Orval Hobart Mowrer (1947), fears are learnt by classical conditioning and kept alive by operant avoidance, because each escape is rewarded by relief, and that relief stops the person from ever learning that the danger is not real. When escape is prevented, anxiety rises to a peak and then falls through habituation, and the client learns that nothing terrible happens. Sessions are long, often hours, because ending while anxiety is still high could strengthen the fear. A related method, implosive therapy (Thomas Stampfl, 1967), floods the client in imagination with exaggerated scenes. Flooding is quicker than graded exposure but more distressing, so it needs full informed consent. Example: Rohan is terrified of lifts. With his therapist he rides a lift up and down a tall building for two hours. His panic peaks in the first 20 minutes, then slowly subsides until he feels bored. Option b is right because keeping the client in the feared situation with no escape is the definition of flooding. Exam trap: students confuse flooding with systematic desensitisation; both are exposure-based, but desensitisation is gradual and paired with relaxation, while flooding starts at the top of the hierarchy with no relaxation.", "topic": "Flooding in behaviour therapy"}, {"q": "A primary characteristic of catatonia in schizophrenic patient is –", "options": {"a": "Auditory hallucination", "b": "Paranoia", "c": "Delusional thinking", "d": "Motor abnormality"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: the primary characteristic of catatonia is motor abnormality. Catatonia was first described by Karl Ludwig Kahlbaum (1874) as a syndrome of stupor, posturing and excitement. Emil Kraepelin (1899) folded it into dementia praecox, and for decades catatonic schizophrenia was a subtype. DSM-5 (2013) dropped the schizophrenia subtypes and made catatonia a specifier that can be attached to schizophrenia, mood disorders or medical conditions. It requires three or more of twelve psychomotor signs: stupor (no activity and no response), catalepsy (passively holding a posture against gravity), waxy flexibility (slight, even resistance when the examiner moves the limbs, which then stay where placed), mutism, negativism (opposing or not responding to instructions), posturing, mannerism, stereotypy (repetitive, non-goal-directed movement), agitation not influenced by outside stimuli, grimacing, echolalia (repeating others' words) and echopraxia (copying others' movements). So catatonia can look like extreme stillness or like purposeless excitement; both are disturbances of movement. It can be life-threatening if the person stops eating or drinking, and it often responds dramatically to benzodiazepines such as lorazepam, and to electroconvulsive therapy. Example: Sunita, who has schizophrenia, has stood by the window holding one arm in the air for hours. When a nurse lowers the arm, it stays wherever it is put, and Sunita does not speak. These are catalepsy, waxy flexibility and mutism. Option d is right because every defining sign of catatonia is a motor (psychomotor) abnormality. Exam trap: hallucinations, paranoia and delusions are all real features of schizophrenia, so each sounds plausible, but they are positive symptoms of perception and thought content and define other presentations, such as the old paranoid subtype, not catatonia.", "topic": "Catatonia: motor symptoms"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a personality disorder according to DSM-5", "options": {"a": "Impulsive", "b": "Antisocial", "c": "Borderline", "d": "Narcissistic"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: 'impulsive' is not a personality disorder according to DSM-5. A personality disorder is an enduring pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from the expectations of the person's culture, is pervasive and inflexible, begins in adolescence or early adulthood, is stable over time and causes distress or impairment. DSM-5 (2013) lists ten specific personality disorders in three clusters. Cluster A, odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal. Cluster B, dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic. Cluster C, anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive. So options b, c and d (antisocial, borderline and narcissistic) are all genuine DSM-5 personality disorders, all from Cluster B, and 'impulsive' is the odd one out. Impulsivity is a trait, and it appears as a criterion within borderline and antisocial personality disorders, but it is not a diagnosis of its own. It confuses students for two reasons. ICD-10 (1992) has emotionally unstable personality disorder with two subtypes, impulsive type and borderline type. And DSM-5 has a separate group of impulse-control disorders (intermittent explosive disorder, kleptomania, pyromania), which are not personality disorders. A memory aid for the clusters is 'weird, wild and worried'. Example: Karan has had stormy relationships, self-harm and sudden mood swings since his teens; his clinician might diagnose borderline personality disorder and record impulsivity as one of its features, never as 'impulsive personality disorder'. Option a is right because no DSM-5 personality disorder is called 'impulsive'. Exam trap: the ICD-10 'impulsive type' of emotionally unstable personality disorder is the source of this trap; it is an ICD-10 subtype, not a DSM-5 disorder.", "topic": "DSM-5 personality disorder clusters"}, {"q": "Which of the following personality disorders is associated with a pervasive pattern of detachment from social relationships and a restricted range of emotional expression?", "options": {"a": "Narcissistic", "b": "Schizoid", "c": "Borderline", "d": "Antisocial"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: a pervasive pattern of detachment from social relationships and a restricted range of emotional expression is schizoid personality disorder. That phrase is almost word for word the DSM-5 (2013) definition. Schizoid personality disorder sits in Cluster A (odd or eccentric). It needs four or more of seven features, beginning by early adulthood: neither desires nor enjoys close relationships, including being part of a family; almost always chooses solitary activities; has little, if any, interest in sexual experiences with another person; takes pleasure in few, if any, activities; lacks close friends or confidants other than first-degree relatives; appears indifferent to praise or criticism; and shows emotional coldness, detachment or flattened affect. The person is a loner by preference, not out of fear. Ernst Kretschmer (1921) described a schizoid temperament in some relatives of people with schizophrenia, and the term has been used ever since. Two neighbours must be kept apart. In avoidant personality disorder (Cluster C), the person longs for closeness but stays away for fear of rejection. In schizotypal personality disorder (Cluster A), there is also social discomfort, but with eccentric behaviour, odd beliefs such as magical thinking, and perceptual distortions. Example: Vivek, 35, a night-shift data analyst, lives alone, eats by himself, has no friends and no wish for any, and shrugs at both praise and criticism from his boss. He is not anxious about people; he is simply not interested. Option b is right because the stem quotes the defining phrase of schizoid personality disorder. Exam trap: borderline personality disorder is also defined as 'a pervasive pattern of instability' of relationships, so the shared opening words tempt students; the key words here are 'detachment' and 'restricted' emotion, which mean schizoid.", "topic": "Schizoid personality disorder"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a symptom of dissociative fugue?", "options": {"a": "A sudden enexpected travel away from home", "b": "Identity confusion", "c": "Hallucination", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: hallucination is not a symptom of dissociative fugue. Dissociation is a disruption in the normal integration of consciousness, memory, identity, emotion, perception and behaviour. In dissociative fugue (from the Latin fuga, flight), a person suddenly and unexpectedly travels away from home or work, cannot recall some or all of their past, and is confused about who they are, sometimes taking on a partly or wholly new identity. The fugue usually follows severe stress or trauma and may last from hours to months; when it ends, the person often has no memory of the fugue itself. DSM-IV (1994) treated fugue as a separate disorder, while DSM-5 (2013) made it a specifier, dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue, defined as apparently purposeful travel or bewildered wandering linked with amnesia for identity or other important personal information. So option a (sudden, unexpected travel away from home) and option b (identity confusion) are both true core features. Hallucinations are not part of the picture: during a fugue the person often looks quite normal to strangers, and perception is intact. Prominent hallucinations would suggest a psychotic disorder, delirium or a substance effect instead. Because c is the odd one out, d ('none of the above') is wrong too. Example: after his business collapses, Mahesh vanishes from Pune and is found three weeks later working at a roadside dhaba in another state under a new name. He cannot say who he is or how he got there, but he hears no voices and sees nothing unusual. Option c is right because hallucination is a perceptual, psychotic symptom that is not a feature of dissociative fugue. Exam trap: a fugue is sometimes misread as a break with reality like psychosis; the break is in memory and identity, not in perception.", "topic": "Dissociative fugue symptoms"}, {"q": "Pooja witnessed brutal killings, and screams of children during war. After returning, she suffered from recurring haunting memories and nightmares, a numbed social withdrawal, jumpy anxiety, and insomnia. She may be experiencing –", "options": {"a": "Post- Traumatic Stress disorder", "b": "Generalised Anxiety Disorder", "c": "Dissociative Disorder", "d": "None of the Above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: Pooja's symptoms after witnessing wartime killings fit post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). PTSD entered the diagnostic manuals in DSM-III (1980), largely through work with Vietnam veterans, although similar reactions had earlier been called shell shock by Charles Myers (1915) and later combat fatigue. DSM-5 (2013) first requires exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence, by experiencing it directly, witnessing it, learning that it happened to someone close, or repeated exposure to grim details at work. Then come symptoms in four clusters. Intrusion: unwanted memories, nightmares, flashbacks and distress at reminders. Avoidance: of trauma-related thoughts, feelings, people or places. Negative changes in cognition and mood: persistent fear, guilt or shame, distorted blame, loss of interest, detachment from others and inability to feel positive emotions (emotional numbing). Changes in arousal and reactivity: irritability, reckless behaviour, hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, poor concentration and sleep problems. Symptoms must last more than one month and cause distress or impairment; if they last 3 days to 1 month, the diagnosis is acute stress disorder. Effective treatments include trauma-focused CBT, prolonged exposure (Edna Foa, 1998), cognitive processing therapy and EMDR (Francine Shapiro, 1989). Applying this to Pooja: she witnessed killings (exposure), has haunting memories and nightmares (intrusion), numbed social withdrawal (negative mood and detachment), jumpy anxiety (startle and hypervigilance) and insomnia (arousal). Every cluster is present. Option a is right because a traumatic event followed by intrusions, numbing and hyperarousal is the textbook picture of PTSD. Exam trap: generalised anxiety disorder also involves anxiety and poor sleep, but its worry is spread across everyday matters and needs no trauma; a trauma plus re-experiencing points to PTSD.", "topic": "Post-traumatic stress disorder"}, {"q": "Suraj is experiencing sudden episodes of intense dread along with shortness of breath, heart palpitations which come and go. He is most likely suffering from -?", "options": {"a": "Generalised anxiety disorder", "b": "Panic Disorder", "c": "Obsessive Compulsive Disorder", "d": "Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: sudden, recurring episodes of intense dread with breathlessness and palpitations suggest panic disorder. In DSM-5 (2013), a panic attack is an abrupt surge of intense fear or discomfort that peaks within minutes, with four or more of thirteen symptoms: palpitations or racing heart, sweating, trembling, shortness of breath, choking feelings, chest pain, nausea, dizziness, chills or heat sensations, numbness or tingling, derealisation or depersonalisation, fear of losing control or 'going crazy', and fear of dying. Panic disorder needs recurrent unexpected attacks, coming 'out of the blue', plus at least one month of persistent worry about further attacks or their consequences, or a maladaptive change in behaviour such as avoiding exercise or certain places. Donald Klein (1964) helped separate panic from general anxiety when he found that the antidepressant imipramine blocked panic attacks but not chronic worry. David M. Clark (1986) offered the main cognitive model: panic arises from catastrophic misinterpretation of normal bodily sensations, such as reading a racing heart as a heart attack, which raises fear and so the sensations in a vicious circle. CBT with interoceptive exposure (deliberately bringing on the sensations) and SSRIs are first-line treatments. Many people with panic disorder develop agoraphobia, which DSM-5 now diagnoses separately. Applying this to Suraj: his episodes are sudden, intense, short and recurrent ('come and go'), with classic bodily symptoms. Option b is right because discrete, recurrent surges of intense fear with physical symptoms define panic attacks and panic disorder. Exam trap: generalised anxiety disorder is the near miss, but GAD is steady, long-lasting worry over at least six months, not sudden attacks that peak and pass; 'sudden episodes that come and go' signals panic.", "topic": "Panic disorder"}]};
window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["gurugram-2025-ma"] = {"id": "gurugram-2025-ma", "title": "Gurugram 2025 M.A.", "fullTitle": "Gurugram University 2025, M.A. Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper, official key)", "sub": "70 real questions · official answer key · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "At what stage in Piaget's theory do children begin to understand conservation, reverse mental operations, and classify objects by multiple features?", "options": {"a": "Sensorimotor stage", "b": "Preoperational stage", "c": "Concrete operational stage", "d": "Formal operational stage"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: children begin to conserve, reverse mental operations and classify objects by several features at once in the concrete operational stage, roughly 7 to 11 years. Jean Piaget (1936) described four stages of cognitive development: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 onwards). An operation is a mental action that can be reversed, like adding and then subtracting. Conservation is the understanding that quantity, number, mass or volume stays the same when only the shape or arrangement changes. In Piaget's classic beaker task, water is poured from a short wide glass into a tall thin one. A preoperational child says the tall glass has more, because she centres on height alone. A concrete operational child says it is the same and gives three reasons: identity (nothing was added), reversibility (pour it back and it will look the same) and compensation (taller but thinner). Classification also matures: the child can sort by colour and shape at once (multiple classification), solve class inclusion (more flowers than roses) and seriate sticks by length. Conservation arrives in a fixed order (horizontal décalage): number and liquid first, then mass, weight and finally volume. Example: Riya, aged 8, watches her mother roll a ball of atta dough into a long snake and says, \"Still the same dough, you can roll it back.\" Option c is right because all three abilities in the stem, conservation, reversibility and multiple classification, are the defining achievements of this stage. Exam trap: the preoperational stage is defined by the absence of these skills (centration, irreversibility, egocentrism), so examiners use it as the near-miss; formal operations add abstract and hypothetical reasoning, not conservation.", "topic": "Piaget's concrete operational stage"}, {"q": "Who proposed that development unfolds naturally as a biological process, largely independent of experience or training?", "options": {"a": "Sigmund Freud", "b": "Arnold Gesell", "c": "John Watson", "d": "Jean Piaget"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: Arnold Gesell (1925) proposed that development unfolds naturally as a biological process, largely independent of experience or training. Gesell, an American paediatrician and psychologist at Yale, built the maturational theory. Maturation is growth that follows a genetically programmed timetable, the same sequence in every child of the species, whatever the teaching. Gesell filmed thousands of infants and published age norms for motor, adaptive, language and personal-social behaviour in The Mental Growth of the Pre-School Child (1925); these became the Gesell Developmental Schedules. His key evidence was the co-twin control study with Helen Thompson (1929). Identical twin girls, T and C, shared the same heredity. Twin T practised stair climbing daily for six weeks from 46 weeks of age. Twin C began training only at 53 weeks, yet after about two weeks she had caught up. Early practice had not hurried an unready body. Gesell's advice to parents followed: wait for readiness rather than push. Myrtle McGraw (1935) later refined the point with the twins Johnny and Jimmy: training did not speed species-wide skills such as walking, but it did advance individual skills such as roller skating once the child was ready. Example: Kabir's grandmother worries that he is not walking at 10 months; a Gesell-style answer is that he will walk when his nervous system is ready, and daily drills will not change that. Option b is right because Gesell is the textbook name for a nature-driven, maturational view in which experience plays only a minor role. Exam trap: Jean Piaget (1936) also used biological language (adaptation, equilibration), but he stressed the child's active interaction with the environment, so he is the near-miss; John B. Watson (1913) is the opposite, environmentalist extreme.", "topic": "Gesell's maturational theory"}, {"q": "Which of the following accurately reflects Erik Erikson's theory of development?", "options": {"a": "Erikson was a neo-Piagetian theorist", "b": "His theory emphasizes sexual instincts as central to development", "c": "He proposed a psychosocial model that spans the entire human lifespan", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: Erik Erikson (1950) proposed a psychosocial model of development that spans the entire human lifespan, from infancy to old age. Erikson, a German-born psychoanalyst who trained in Vienna, set out his theory in Childhood and Society (1950). He kept the idea of stages from Sigmund Freud (1905) but made two big changes. First, he shifted the focus from sexual drives to social relationships and the growing ego, so his stages are psychosocial, not psychosexual. Second, he carried development beyond adolescence into adulthood and old age. There are eight stages, each a crisis between two poles: trust versus mistrust (infancy), autonomy versus shame and doubt (toddlerhood), initiative versus guilt (preschool), industry versus inferiority (school age), identity versus role confusion (adolescence), intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood), generativity versus stagnation (middle age) and integrity versus despair (old age). Resolving each crisis well yields a virtue: hope, will, purpose, competence, fidelity, love, care and wisdom. Each stage builds on the ones before, but a later crisis can still be reworked. Example: Meera, aged 45, mentors young teachers at her school and feels her work will outlive her; that is generativity. Her father, aged 75, looks back on his life with acceptance rather than regret; that is integrity. Option c is right because \"psychosocial\" and \"whole lifespan\" are the two features that define Erikson's theory. Exam trap: Erikson was a neo-Freudian ego psychologist, not a neo-Piagetian; examiners also swap his \"psychosocial\" with Freud's \"psychosexual\", and \"None of the above\" tempts students who doubt option c because Erikson came from psychoanalysis.", "topic": "Erikson's psychosocial theory"}, {"q": "What is the correct sequence in Kohlberg's stages of gender development?", "options": {"a": "Gender consistency – Gender stability – Gender labeling", "b": "Gender stability – Gender labeling – Gender consistency", "c": "Gender labeling – Gender stability – Gender consistency", "d": "Gender labeling – Gender consistency – Gender stability"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: in the cognitive-developmental theory of Lawrence Kohlberg (1966), children move from gender labelling to gender stability to gender consistency (constancy). Kohlberg argued that understanding comes first: a child first works out \"I am a girl\" and then seeks out girl things, so she socialises herself. This reversed the order of social learning accounts, in which rewards and models shape gender behaviour first. He described three steps. Gender labelling, or basic gender identity (about 2 to 3 years): the child can correctly call herself and others a boy or a girl. Gender stability (about 3 to 5): she knows gender stays the same over time, so a boy grows up to be a man, not a woman. Gender consistency or constancy (about 5 to 7): she knows gender stays the same across situations and surface changes, so a boy who wears a frock or grows long hair is still a boy. Ronald Slaby and Karin Frey (1975) supported the theory: children high in constancy watched the same-sex adult longer in a split-screen film. A criticism is that children prefer gender-typed toys by about two, long before constancy. Example: Arjun, aged 3, says \"I am a boy\" (labelling); at 4 he says he will be a papa when he grows up (stability); at 6 he laughs when told that wearing his sister's dupatta would make him a girl (consistency). Option c is right because each step builds on the one before: a child cannot know gender lasts over time until she can label it, and cannot know it survives disguises until she knows it lasts. Exam trap: the options swap stability and consistency; the time-based step (stability) always comes before the situation-based step (consistency), and constancy is the final step, never the first.", "topic": "Kohlberg's gender constancy stages"}, {"q": "In the preconventional stage of moral development, how is morality defined?", "options": {"a": "right or wrong are defined by the hedonistic consequences of a given action", "b": "it is based on to social rules", "c": "moral rules are seen as conventions designed to ensure greater good", "d": "there is no set stages of moral development"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: at the preconventional level, right and wrong are defined by the hedonistic consequences of an action, that is, by the punishment or reward it brings to oneself. Lawrence Kohlberg (1958) studied moral reasoning by posing dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma (should a poor man steal a drug to save his dying wife?) and scoring the reasons given, not the choice. He found three levels, each with two stages. Level I, preconventional (typical of children under about 9): morality is under external control. Stage 1, the punishment and obedience orientation, judges an act by whether it is punished. Stage 2, instrumental exchange or naive hedonism, counts as right whatever serves one's own interest (\"you scratch my back, I scratch yours\"). Level II, conventional: rules are followed to win approval (Stage 3, good boy and nice girl) and to keep social order (Stage 4, law and order). Level III, postconventional: rules are seen as social contracts that can be changed for the greater good (Stage 5), and finally as universal ethical principles (Stage 6). Example: asked why Heinz should not steal, Kabir, aged 7, says \"because he will go to jail\"; asked why he shares his tiffin, he says \"so Rohan will share his tomorrow\". Both answers are preconventional because they rest on consequences to the self. Option a is right because \"hedonistic consequences\" (gaining pleasure or reward, avoiding pain or punishment) is exactly the preconventional basis of morality. Exam trap: option b describes the conventional level and option c the postconventional social contract stage; examiners reverse the levels, so remember: pre (self), conventional (society's rules), post (principles beyond the rules).", "topic": "Kohlberg's preconventional morality"}, {"q": "Who demonstrated that \"contact comfort\" is more critical than providing for physical needs in forming attachment bonds?", "options": {"a": "John Bowlby", "b": "Jerome Kagan", "c": "Mary Ainsworth", "d": "Harry Harlow"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: Harry Harlow (1958) showed that contact comfort matters more than feeding in forming attachment bonds. Until the 1950s, both psychoanalysis and behaviourism held a \"cupboard love\" view: infants love the mother because she feeds them, so she becomes a secondary reinforcer. Harlow tested this at the University of Wisconsin with infant rhesus monkeys separated from their mothers. Each monkey had two surrogate mothers: a bare wire mother and a mother covered in soft terry cloth. In the key condition only the wire mother held the milk bottle. The monkeys went to the wire mother just to feed and spent most of the day clinging to the cloth mother. When a frightening mechanical toy was put in the cage, they ran to the cloth mother, not the feeding one, and then used her as a secure base from which to explore. Harlow called this contact comfort and reported it in his paper \"The Nature of Love\" (1958). Later work showed that monkeys raised with surrogates alone grew into socially and sexually abnormal adults, so cloth alone is not enough for normal development. Example: baby Ananya, nine months old, takes her bottle from her aunt, but whenever a loud pressure cooker whistle frightens her she reaches for her grandmother, who usually holds and rocks her. Option d is right because Harlow's surrogate mother experiments are the classic evidence that comfort, not food, drives attachment. Exam trap: John Bowlby (1969) also rejected cupboard love and cited Harlow, so he is the near-miss; Bowlby built attachment theory, while Harlow ran the contact comfort experiments.", "topic": "Harlow's contact comfort"}, {"q": "Psychology students first refused to counsel drug addicts but agreed to conduct a seminar afterward. Which compliance strategy is being used here?", "options": {"a": "Door-in-the-Face technique", "b": "Conformity", "c": "That's-not-All technique", "d": "Foot-in-the-Door technique"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: this is the door-in-the-face technique, in which a large request that is refused is followed by a smaller, more reasonable request, which people are then more likely to accept. Robert Cialdini (1975) and his colleagues showed it on a university campus. They asked students to serve as unpaid counsellors to juvenile delinquents for two hours a week for at least two years. All refused. They then asked the same students to take a group of delinquents on a two-hour trip to the zoo. About 50 per cent agreed, compared with under 17 per cent of students who were asked only for the zoo trip. Two forces explain it. Reciprocal concessions: the requester seems to back down, and the norm of reciprocity pushes the target to concede in return. Perceptual contrast: after the huge request, the small one looks smaller still. It works best when the same person makes both requests and the second follows soon after the first. The stem follows the same pattern: counselling drug addicts (large, refused), then conducting a seminar (small, accepted). Example: Arjun asks his father for a new motorbike and is refused; a minute later he asks for a new phone, and his father, feeling he has already said no once, agrees. Option a is right because the sequence in the stem is large request, refusal, then a smaller request accepted. Exam trap: foot-in-the-door runs the other way, a small request first and then a larger one, so examiners rely on students mixing up the order; always ask which request came first and whether the first one was refused or accepted.", "topic": "Door-in-the-face compliance"}, {"q": "What concept explains why repeated exposure to a stimulus leads to increased liking?", "options": {"a": "Mere exposure effect", "b": "Attractiveness stereotype", "c": "Spatial proximity", "d": "Need complementarity"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: the mere exposure effect is the finding that repeated exposure to a stimulus increases liking for it, even when nothing else about the stimulus changes. Robert Zajonc (1968) showed people nonsense words, Chinese characters and faces from a yearbook different numbers of times, from zero to 25. The more often an item had been seen, the more positively it was rated. Later studies found the effect even when exposures were too brief to be consciously recognised, which led Zajonc (1980) to argue that \"preferences need no inferences\": feelings can arise before thinking. The usual explanation is perceptual fluency. A familiar stimulus is easier to process, and that ease feels pleasant and safe. Evolution may have favoured this bias, since what is familiar has already proved harmless. The effect has limits. It is strongest with neutral or mildly positive stimuli, it levels off or reverses after very many exposures as boredom sets in, and it can make an already disliked stimulus more disliked. It helps explain why advertisers repeat jingles and why neighbours and classmates often become friends. Example: Meera did not like a new Punjabi song when she first heard it, but after hearing it every day on the college bus for two weeks she now hums it and calls it her favourite. Option a is right because the stem names exactly the mechanism Zajonc described: repetition alone raises liking. Exam trap: spatial proximity also raises attraction, partly because nearness creates repeated exposure, so it is the near-miss; but proximity is about physical distance, while the stem asks about repeated exposure itself.", "topic": "Mere exposure effect"}, {"q": "What term describes reduced likelihood of helping when more bystanders are present?", "options": {"a": "Social influence", "b": "Diffusion of responsibility", "c": "Negative state relief", "d": "Altruism"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: diffusion of responsibility is the reduced likelihood of helping when more bystanders are present, because each person feels less personally responsible for acting. The idea grew out of the 1964 murder of Kitty Genovese in New York, when newspapers reported that 38 neighbours saw or heard the attack and nobody acted (later research showed the number was exaggerated). John Darley and Bibb Latane (1968) suspected that the number of witnesses, not big-city apathy, was the key. In their intercom study, students talking over an intercom heard another student apparently have an epileptic seizure. When a student believed they were the only listener, about 85 per cent went for help before the seizure ended. When they believed four others could also hear, only about 31 per cent did. Responsibility was shared out among the group, so each person's share shrank. Bibb Latane and John Darley (1970) later described five steps a bystander must pass: notice the event, interpret it as an emergency, take responsibility, decide how to help, and act. Diffusion of responsibility blocks the third step. Pluralistic ignorance blocks the second: everyone looks calm, so each person decides there is no emergency. Example: Kabir sees a man collapse on a crowded Delhi metro platform and thinks, \"With so many people here, someone must already have called for help,\" and walks on. Option b is right because it is the precise term for the drop in helping caused by more bystanders. Exam trap: the whole phenomenon is called the bystander effect, and diffusion of responsibility is the mechanism behind it; examiners also offer \"social influence\" (pluralistic ignorance) as the near-miss.", "topic": "Diffusion of responsibility"}, {"q": "What is the primary function of the limbic system?", "options": {"a": "Motor control", "b": "Processing sensory information", "c": "Regulating emotions and memory", "d": "Coordinating muscle movements"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the primary function of the limbic system is regulating emotion and memory, along with motivation and basic drives. The limbic system is a ring of structures on the inner border of each cerebral hemisphere, wrapped around the brainstem; \"limbus\" is Latin for border. Paul Broca (1878) first described this \"great limbic lobe\". James Papez (1937) proposed an emotion circuit linking the hippocampus, fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamus and cingulate gyrus. Paul MacLean (1952) added the amygdala and other structures and named the whole the limbic system. Its key parts have distinct jobs. The amygdala detects threat and attaches fear and emotional meaning to events. The hippocampus consolidates new explicit memories; the famous patient H.M. could no longer form them after surgery removed much of both hippocampi in 1953. The hypothalamus drives hunger, thirst, sex and the stress response. The cingulate gyrus links emotion with attention and pain. Classic evidence came from Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy (1937), who removed the temporal lobes, including the amygdala, in monkeys; the animals became tame, fearless and hypersexual (Kluver-Bucy syndrome). Example: Riya smells the same agarbatti her late grandmother used to light and is flooded with warm memories and tears; her amygdala and hippocampus, closely linked to smell pathways, are tying emotion to memory. Option c is right because emotion and memory are the two functions every textbook assigns to the limbic system. Exam trap: motor control belongs to the motor cortex and basal ganglia, sensory processing to the thalamus and sensory cortex, and muscle coordination to the cerebellum; examiners pick these because the basal ganglia and thalamus sit close to limbic structures.", "topic": "Limbic system functions"}, {"q": "Which brain lobe is primarily involved in processing auditory information?", "options": {"a": "Frontal lobe", "b": "Occipital lobe", "c": "Parietal lobe", "d": "Temporal lobe"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the temporal lobe is primarily involved in processing auditory information. Each cerebral hemisphere has four lobes, separated by landmarks: the central sulcus divides frontal from parietal, and the lateral (Sylvian) fissure separates the temporal lobe below from the frontal and parietal lobes above. The primary auditory cortex sits on the upper surface of the temporal lobe, on Heschl's gyrus, tucked inside the lateral fissure; on the map drawn by Korbinian Brodmann (1909) it is areas 41 and 42. Sound reaches it from the cochlea through the brainstem and the medial geniculate nucleus of the thalamus. It is tonotopically organised: different strips respond to different pitches, mirroring the layout of the cochlea. Around it lies the auditory association cortex. In the left hemisphere this includes Wernicke's area (area 22), identified by Carl Wernicke (1874), which is needed to understand speech; damage causes fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension. The temporal lobe also holds the hippocampus and amygdala in its depths, so it serves memory and emotion as well. Each ear sends signals to both temporal lobes, so damage on one side rarely causes complete deafness. Example: after a stroke in her left temporal lobe, Meera's grandmother hears her family speaking clearly but cannot make sense of their words, a sign of Wernicke's aphasia. Option d is right because both the primary auditory cortex and the main speech comprehension area lie in the temporal lobe. Exam trap: the occipital lobe is the primary area for vision and the parietal lobe for touch, so examiners use them as sensory near-misses; Broca's area, described by Paul Broca (1861) and needed for speech production, lies in the frontal lobe, not the temporal lobe.", "topic": "Temporal lobe and hearing"}, {"q": "How is information transmitted within a neuron?", "options": {"a": "Through electrical signals", "b": "Through chemical messengers", "c": "Both a and b", "d": "Magnetic transmission"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: information is transmitted within a neuron through electrical signals. A neuron does two different jobs, and they use two different codes. Inside the cell, from the dendrites and cell body down the axon, the message travels as an electrical change across the membrane. Graded postsynaptic potentials spread over the dendrites and cell body; if they push the axon hillock past threshold (about minus 55 millivolts in most textbooks), an all-or-none action potential fires and travels down the axon to the terminals. Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) worked out the mechanism in the giant axon of the squid: sodium ions rush in, then potassium ions flow out. In myelinated axons the impulse jumps between the nodes of Ranvier (saltatory conduction), reaching speeds of over 100 metres per second. Between neurons, at the synapse, the code changes. The electrical impulse triggers the release of chemical neurotransmitters, which cross the synaptic cleft and bind to receptors on the next cell. Otto Loewi (1921) proved this chemical step with two frog hearts and his \"vagus substance\", later identified as acetylcholine. So neural communication as a whole is called electrochemical: electrical within a neuron, chemical between neurons. Example: when Arjun touches a hot tawa, the signal races along the axon of his sensory neuron as an electrical impulse and then crosses to the next neuron in his spinal cord by chemical transmission. Option a is right because the stem says \"within a neuron\", which is the electrical part of the process. Exam trap: \"Both a and b\" tempts students who remember that neural transmission is electrochemical; that phrase describes the whole chain including synapses, not conduction inside a single neuron.", "topic": "Conduction within a neuron"}, {"q": "What is the resting membrane potential of a neuron?", "options": {"a": "–70 millivolts", "b": "+70 millivolts", "c": "–40 millivolts", "d": "+40 millivolts"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the resting membrane potential of a typical neuron is about minus 70 millivolts (written -70 mV), meaning the inside of the cell is 70 millivolts more negative than the outside. A neuron at rest is polarised. Measured with a fine microelectrode, the voltage across its membrane usually sits between about -60 and -80 mV, and textbooks round it to -70 mV. Three things create it. First, ions are unevenly spread: sodium (Na+) and chloride (Cl-) are concentrated outside, while potassium (K+) and large negative proteins are concentrated inside. Second, the resting membrane is far more permeable to potassium, which leaks out down its concentration gradient and leaves negative charge behind. Third, the sodium-potassium pump uses energy to push out three sodium ions for every two potassium ions it brings in. Two forces act on each ion: the concentration gradient (from crowded to scarce) and electrostatic pressure (opposite charges attract). Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) measured these events in the giant axon of the squid. When the cell is depolarised to threshold, about -55 mV, voltage-gated sodium channels open and the action potential shoots up to a peak of about +40 mV; the cell then repolarises, briefly dips below rest (hyperpolarisation) and returns to -70 mV. Example: Kabir's teacher compares a resting neuron to a charged phone battery: a stored voltage difference, ready to be used the moment a signal arrives. Option a is right because -70 mV is the standard textbook value for the resting potential. Exam trap: +40 mV is the peak of the action potential and -40 mV lies in the depolarised zone near threshold, so examiners offer these as near-misses; the sign matters too, since at rest the inside is negative, not positive.", "topic": "Resting membrane potential"}, {"q": "According to Yerkes-Dodson law, what is the relationship between arousal and performance?", "options": {"a": "U-shaped", "b": "Inverted U", "c": "Linear", "d": "Inverted V"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Yerkes-Dodson law describes an inverted U relationship between arousal and performance, so performance is best at a moderate level of arousal and falls off when arousal is too low or too high. Robert Yerkes and John Dodson (1908) trained mice to choose a white box over a black one, giving electric shocks of different strengths for mistakes. The mice learned fastest at an intermediate shock level, not at the weakest or the strongest. They also found that the harder the discrimination, the weaker the best shock. Later writers turned this into a general law of arousal (or anxiety) and performance. Too little arousal leaves a person drowsy and careless; too much makes them tense and disorganised, with narrowed attention. The second part of the law matters too: the optimum is lower for complex or new tasks and higher for simple or well-learned ones. Donald Hebb (1955) built on this in his optimal arousal theory, which says people seek a moderate level of stimulation. Example: Meera, preparing for her M.Phil interview, studies poorly when she is bored and relaxed months before, and also when she panics the night before, but she performs best on the day with a moderate, alert nervousness. A sprinter doing a simple, well-practised task can tolerate much higher arousal than Meera solving a difficult statistics problem. Option b is right because the curve rises, peaks at moderate arousal and then falls, which is the shape of an inverted U. Exam trap: students remember \"U\" and forget \"inverted\"; also watch for questions on the second part of the law, where complex tasks have the lower optimum, not the higher one.", "topic": "Yerkes-Dodson law"}, {"q": "Motivation driven by external rewards is explained by which theory?", "options": {"a": "Drive reduction theory", "b": "Instinct theory", "c": "Incentive theory", "d": "Cognitive theory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: incentive theory explains motivation driven by external rewards, holding that behaviour is pulled towards attractive goals in the environment rather than pushed by internal needs. An incentive is an external object or outcome that attracts or repels: money, grades, praise, food, a promotion. Drive-reduction theory said organisms act only to reduce internal tension, but it could not explain why people eat dessert when full or work hard for rewards they do not biologically need. Incentive theory fills that gap: the value and expected arrival of the reward itself energise behaviour. Classic evidence came from Otto Tinklepaugh (1928). Monkeys watched a banana being hidden under a cup; when they lifted the cup and found lettuce instead, a food they normally ate, they refused it and searched angrily. Their behaviour was driven by the expected incentive, not by hunger alone. Leo Crespi (1942) found a similar contrast effect in rats: when their reward was suddenly cut from large to small, they ran more slowly than rats that had always received the small reward. Incentive value depends on the person and the moment, so the same reward does not motivate everyone equally. Heavy external rewards can even undermine interest in a task enjoyed for its own sake, the overjustification effect shown by Edward Deci (1971). Example: Arjun stays late at the office through December because he has been promised a large year-end bonus; the bonus pulls his effort. Option c is right because incentive theory is the theory defined by external rewards and goals. Exam trap: remember \"drives push, incentives pull\"; examiners offer drive-reduction theory because it also involves reward in a loose sense, but drive theory is about internal biological tension.", "topic": "Incentive theory of motivation"}, {"q": "How many inkblot cards are used in Rorschach's test?", "options": {"a": "10", "b": "12", "c": "11", "d": "8"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Rorschach Inkblot Test uses 10 cards. Hermann Rorschach (1921), a Swiss psychiatrist, published the test in his book Psychodiagnostik after trying many inkblots with psychiatric patients and keeping the ten that best separated diagnostic groups. He died the next year, aged 37. Each card is a bilaterally symmetrical inkblot. Five are achromatic, in black and grey (cards I, IV, V, VI and VII); two are black with red (II and III); and three are fully coloured in pastel shades (VIII, IX and X). The test rests on the projective hypothesis: when a stimulus is ambiguous, the way a person organises it reveals their personality and thought processes. Administration has three phases. In the response phase the examiner asks \"What might this be?\" and records every answer word for word. In the inquiry phase the examiner returns to each response to find its location (where on the blot), its determinants (form, colour, shading, movement) and its content (human, animal and so on). An optional testing-the-limits phase may follow. Scoring uses systems such as the Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) or the newer R-PAS of Gregory Meyer and colleagues (2011). Example: Kabir sees two people dancing on card III; the examiner codes human movement as the determinant and human content, a response often linked to imagination and empathy. Option a is right because the standard Rorschach set has exactly ten cards, unchanged since 1921. Exam trap: do not confuse the Rorschach's 10 cards with the 31 cards of the TAT (30 pictures and one blank) or the 45 cards per form of the Holtzman Inkblot Technique (Wayne Holtzman, 1961); examiners also offer nearby numbers like 11 and 12.", "topic": "Rorschach inkblot cards"}, {"q": "Who introduced the IQ formula comparing mental and chronological age?", "options": {"a": "Alfred Binet", "b": "Lewis Terman", "c": "William Stern", "d": "Theodore Simon"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: William Stern (1912) introduced the formula that divides mental age by chronological age. In Paris, Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence scale to identify schoolchildren who needed special help. Their 1908 revision grouped items by age level, so a child's mental age (MA) was the age level of the items they could pass. But mental age alone is misleading: a child two years behind at age 4 is far more delayed than a child two years behind at age 14. Stern, a German psychologist, solved this by dividing mental age by chronological age (CA). He called the ratio the Intelligenzquotient, rendered in early English translations as the mental quotient. Lewis Terman (1916) then multiplied the ratio by 100 to remove decimals and used the label IQ in his Stanford-Binet, which made it famous. So the ratio IQ is MA divided by CA, times 100. Example: Riya is 10 years old with a mental age of 12, so her ratio IQ is 12 divided by 10, times 100, which is 120. The ratio IQ had a flaw: mental age stops rising in the mid-teens, so adults' ratio IQs fall as they age. David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ, a standard score with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. Option c is right because Stern was the first to express intelligence as the quotient of mental and chronological age. Exam trap: Binet gave mental age, not the quotient, and Terman added the 100. Key note: Lewis Terman multiplied the quotient by 100 and popularised the term \"IQ\" in the 1916 Stanford-Binet, so option b has support; but the idea of comparing mental with chronological age as a ratio was Stern's 1912 proposal, so the official key of c stands.", "topic": "Origin of the IQ formula"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a method to assess a test's reliability?", "options": {"a": "Test-retest", "b": "Split-half", "c": "Alternate forms", "d": "Construct"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: construct is not a method of assessing reliability; construct validity is a type of validity. Reliability is the consistency of a test: whether it gives similar scores when the measurement is repeated or when different parts of it are compared. In classical test theory an observed score is a true score plus error, and reliability is the proportion of score variance that is true variance. There are several standard ways to estimate it. Test-retest reliability gives the same test twice to the same people and correlates the scores (coefficient of stability). Alternate or parallel forms reliability gives two equivalent versions and correlates them (coefficient of equivalence). Split-half reliability divides one administration into two halves, often odd and even items, correlates them and corrects for length with the Spearman-Brown formula of Charles Spearman and William Brown (1910). Related internal consistency methods include the formulas of G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937) and coefficient alpha from Lee Cronbach (1951). Inter-scorer reliability checks agreement between raters. Construct validity, set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), asks a different question: does the test really measure the theoretical trait it claims to measure, such as anxiety or intelligence? Example: Kabir builds a test anxiety scale. Giving it twice, three weeks apart, and getting r = 0.85 shows test-retest reliability; showing that it correlates with other anxiety measures and rises before exams shows construct validity. Option d is right because it names a kind of validity, while options a, b and c are all genuine reliability methods. Exam trap: reliability asks \"is it consistent?\" and validity asks \"does it measure what it claims?\"; a test can be reliable without being valid, but it cannot be valid without being reasonably reliable.", "topic": "Methods of estimating reliability"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a type of construct validity?", "options": {"a": "Convergent validity", "b": "Criterion validity", "c": "Content validity", "d": "Concurrent validity"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: convergent validity is a type of construct validity. Construct validity, set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), asks whether a test truly measures the theoretical construct it claims to measure, such as self-esteem, anxiety or intelligence. Because a construct cannot be seen directly, evidence is gathered from many angles. Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) added two key forms in their multitrait-multimethod matrix. Convergent validity means the test correlates highly with other measures of the same construct, even when those use different methods. Discriminant (divergent) validity means it correlates weakly with measures of different constructs. Other construct evidence includes factor analysis, expected group differences and changes after an experimental manipulation. In the classic three-way scheme, validity has three types: content validity, criterion-related validity (with concurrent and predictive subtypes) and construct validity. Samuel Messick (1989) later argued that all validity evidence ultimately supports construct validity, but exam questions use the classic scheme. Example: Meera develops a self-esteem scale for Indian college students. It correlates 0.72 with the Rosenberg Self-Esteem Scale (Morris Rosenberg, 1965), showing convergent validity, and only 0.08 with a vocabulary test, showing discriminant validity. Option a is right because convergent validity is, by definition, evidence that a test converges with other measures of the same construct, which is construct validity. Exam trap: concurrent validity sounds almost the same as convergent validity, but concurrent validity is a subtype of criterion validity, where test scores are compared with a present criterion such as a psychiatrist's diagnosis.", "topic": "Convergent and construct validity"}, {"q": "What does norm-referenced testing involve?", "options": {"a": "Measuring individual performance against their own past performance", "b": "Comparing an individual's score with those of others", "c": "Testing domain-specific knowledge", "d": "Evaluating subject mastery"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: norm-referenced testing compares an individual's score with the scores of other people, the norm group. A raw score, such as 42 out of 60, means little on its own. Norm-referenced interpretation gives it meaning by placing it within the distribution of scores from a standardisation sample, a large, representative group tested under the same conditions. The result is reported as a derived score: a percentile rank, a z score, a T score (mean 50, standard deviation 10), a stanine or a deviation IQ (mean 100, standard deviation 15). Most intelligence, aptitude and personality tests are norm-referenced because they are used to rank or select people. The norms must fit the person: a test normed on American adults may mislead when used with rural Indian adolescents, which is why Indian adaptations are restandardised. Robert Glaser (1963) contrasted this with criterion-referenced testing, where a score is compared with a fixed standard of performance, whatever others do. A criterion-referenced test asks \"What can this person do?\", so a whole class can pass it or no one can, as in a driving test. A norm-referenced test asks \"How does this person rank?\", so by design about half the norm group scores below average. Example: Arjun scores 48 on a numerical aptitude test; the manual's norm table shows this is at the 84th percentile for Class 10 students, meaning he scored higher than 84 per cent of the norm group. Option b is right because comparison with others is the defining feature of norm-referenced testing. Exam trap: options about mastery or domain knowledge describe criterion-referenced testing, and comparison with one's own past scores is ipsative assessment; both sound like \"standards\", which is why they tempt.", "topic": "Norm-referenced testing"}, {"q": "Who developed the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT)?", "options": {"a": "Hathaway and McKinley", "b": "Hermann Rorschach", "c": "Morgan and Murray", "d": "F. Goodenough"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) was developed by Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935) at the Harvard Psychological Clinic. Apperception means interpreting a new perception through one's past experience, needs and feelings. The TAT shows ambiguous pictures of people in uncertain situations and asks the person to tell a story about each: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters think and feel, and how it ends. The idea is that the storyteller identifies with the main character, the hero, and projects their own needs and conflicts into the story. The 1943 version has 31 cards: 30 pictures and one blank card (card 16), on which the person imagines a picture of their own. Cards are marked for boys, girls, men and women, and usually about 20 are given over two sessions. Interpretation follows Murray's need-press theory, from his book Explorations in Personality (1938): needs are inner forces such as achievement, affiliation or aggression, and press is the push of the environment that helps or blocks them; a recurring combination of the two is a thema. David McClelland (1953) later used TAT-style pictures to score the need for achievement. Example: shown card 1, a boy looking at a violin, Kabir tells of a boy forced by his parents to practise music when he wants to play cricket; the examiner notes a need for autonomy and strong parental press. Option c is right because Morgan and Murray are the authors of the TAT. Exam trap: Christiana Morgan was first author of the 1935 paper but is often forgotten, so look for \"Morgan and Murray\"; and do not confuse the TAT with the Rorschach, which uses inkblots, not pictures of people.", "topic": "Thematic Apperception Test origin"}, {"q": "What best defines an operational definition?", "options": {"a": "A way to measure a variable", "b": "A testable statement about variable relationships", "c": "The process of identifying a research problem", "d": "A measurable factor that can vary"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: an operational definition is a way to measure a variable, that is, a definition in terms of the exact procedures or operations used to measure or manipulate it. Many concepts in psychology, such as anxiety, intelligence or aggression, cannot be observed directly. An operational definition turns them into something observable and countable. The idea comes from the physicist Percy Bridgman (1927), whose operationism held that a concept means the set of operations used to measure it; Stanley Smith Stevens (1935) brought operationism into psychology. For example, hunger can be operationally defined as hours without food, aggression as the number of times a child hits a Bobo doll in ten minutes, as in the studies of Albert Bandura (1961), and anxiety as a score on a standard anxiety inventory. Operational definitions do three jobs. They make research replicable, since another researcher can repeat the same procedure. They make communication precise, since readers know exactly what was measured. They make hypotheses testable. Their limit is that one operation captures only part of a construct, so good researchers use several measures (converging operations). Example: Riya wants to study exam stress among Delhi school students. She operationally defines exam stress as the score on the Perceived Stress Scale (Sheldon Cohen, 1983) taken one week before the board exams, plus resting heart rate measured that morning. Option a is right because an operational definition states how a variable will be measured or produced. Exam trap: examiners place the definition of a variable or of a hypothesis beside it; a variable is anything that can vary, a hypothesis is a testable prediction about variables, and the operational definition is the measuring recipe.", "topic": "Operational definition"}, {"q": "A repeated-measures design is also known as:", "options": {"a": "Between-subjects design", "b": "Matched-subjects design", "c": "Within-subjects design", "d": "Non-equivalent group design"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a repeated-measures design is also known as a within-subjects design, because the same participants are measured under every condition of the experiment. Instead of assigning different people to each condition, the researcher tests each person repeatedly, so comparisons are made within the same individuals. This has two big advantages. Each participant acts as their own control, so individual differences in ability, personality or motivation are held constant, which reduces error variance and increases statistical power. It also needs fewer participants. The main weakness is order effects (sequence effects): practice can improve later performance, fatigue or boredom can worsen it, and carryover occurs when one condition affects the next, as with a drug that stays in the body. The standard remedy is counterbalancing: half the participants do condition A then B and the other half B then A, or a Latin square is used when there are several conditions. When carryover cannot be undone, as when a learned skill cannot be unlearned, a between-subjects design is used instead. Repeated-measures data are analysed with tests such as the paired (dependent) t test or repeated-measures ANOVA. Example: Meera tests whether caffeine speeds reaction time. All 20 of her participants are tested once after coffee and once after decaffeinated coffee on different days, with half receiving the caffeine first and half second. Option c is right because \"repeated measures\" and \"within subjects\" are two names for the same design. Exam trap: matched-subjects designs also control individual differences, so they look similar, but they use different people paired on a variable, so they are not repeated measures.", "topic": "Within-subjects design"}, {"q": "What is the tendency for individuals to alter behavior when they know they are being observed?", "options": {"a": "Placebo effect", "b": "Halo effect", "c": "Horn effect", "d": "Hawthorne effect"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Hawthorne effect is the tendency of people to change their behaviour because they know they are being observed or studied, whatever the actual intervention. The name comes from the Hawthorne studies (1924 to 1932) at the Western Electric Hawthorne Works near Chicago. In the illumination experiments, researchers raised the lighting to see whether workers produced more. Output rose, but it also rose in the control group, and sometimes even when the lights were dimmed. In the later relay assembly test room, output rose with almost every change to rest breaks and hours. The researchers concluded that the attention of being studied, not the lighting or the breaks, was boosting performance. The studies are linked with Elton Mayo (1933) and were reported by Fritz Roethlisberger and William Dickson (1939) in Management and the Worker; Henry Landsberger (1958) coined the term Hawthorne effect. Later reanalyses suggest the original data were weaker than the legend, but the effect remains a standard threat to the validity of research. Researchers reduce it with control groups that receive equal attention, unobtrusive observation, letting people get used to the observer, and blinding. It is related to demand characteristics, described by Martin Orne (1962): participants guess what the researcher wants and act accordingly. Example: when a supervisor from head office visits Kabir's call centre to observe calls, every agent becomes unusually polite and quick, then slips back after she leaves. Option d is right because the stem describes exactly a change in behaviour caused by awareness of being observed. Exam trap: the placebo effect also involves expectation, but it comes from believing one is receiving a treatment, while the Hawthorne effect comes from knowing one is being watched.", "topic": "Hawthorne effect"}, {"q": "Which measure of central tendency is most affected by outliers?", "options": {"a": "Mean", "b": "Median", "c": "Mode", "d": "Frequency distribution"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the mean is the measure of central tendency most affected by outliers. A measure of central tendency is a single number that describes the centre, or typical value, of a set of scores. There are three: the mean (the arithmetic average, the sum of all scores divided by their number), the median (the middle score when the scores are put in order) and the mode (the most frequent score). An outlier is an extreme score that lies far from the rest of the data. Because the mean uses the actual value of every score, one extreme value pulls it strongly towards itself. Suppose Riya records the monthly pocket money of five friends: 500, 600, 700, 800 and 900 rupees. The mean is 700 and the median is 700. If Kabir, whose allowance is 20,000 rupees, replaces the friend with 900, the mean jumps to 4,520, while the median stays at 700. Option a is right because the mean is the only one of the three averages calculated from the size of every score, so its value shifts with any extreme one. This is why skewed data such as incomes, reaction times or hospital stays are usually summarised with the median: in a positively skewed distribution the mean is dragged out towards the long tail, beyond the median and the mode. The median depends only on rank position, so it is called a resistant or robust statistic, and the mode depends only on which value occurs most often. Exam trap: examiners offer the median or mode because students remember that skew 'affects' every average; only the mean is truly sensitive to outliers. Frequency distribution is a distractor of a different kind: it is a table or graph of how often each score occurs, not a measure of central tendency at all.", "topic": "Mean and outliers"}, {"q": "Saying the population mean equals the sample mean reflects which hypothesis?", "options": {"a": "Research hypothesis", "b": "Alternative hypothesis", "c": "Experimental hypothesis", "d": "Null hypothesis"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: a statement that the population mean equals the sample mean, so that there is no real difference, is the null hypothesis. A hypothesis is a testable statement about a population. In significance testing two competing statistical hypotheses are set up. The null hypothesis, written H0, states that there is no difference, no effect or no relationship: here, that the population mean (mu) equals the value being compared, so any gap between the sample mean and that value is just chance sampling error. The alternative hypothesis, H1, states that a real difference or relationship exists. Ronald Fisher (1935), in The Design of Experiments, named the null hypothesis as the one that evidence may 'nullify', or reject; Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) added the explicit alternative and the idea of two types of error. The test assumes H0 is true and asks how likely the observed data would be under it; if that probability (the p value) falls below the chosen alpha level, usually .05, H0 is rejected. For example, Meera wants to know whether students at her college sleep a different number of hours from the national average of 7. Her H0 is that the college population mean is 7, the same as the national figure; her H1 is that it is not 7. Option d is right because only the null hypothesis asserts equality, or 'no difference', between the parameter and the value it is compared with. Exam trap: research, alternative and experimental hypotheses are all names for the claim that a difference or effect exists, which is the opposite of the stem. Remember that the equals sign always belongs to H0, and that we 'reject' or 'fail to reject' the null, never 'prove' it.", "topic": "Null hypothesis"}, {"q": "When is a Type II error made in hypothesis testing?", "options": {"a": "Null hypothesis is true and rejected", "b": "Null hypothesis is true and accepted", "c": "Null hypothesis is false and accepted", "d": "Null hypothesis is false and rejected"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a Type II error is made when the null hypothesis is false but is accepted (more precisely, not rejected). In significance testing the researcher decides whether to reject the null hypothesis (H0, the claim of no effect). Reality has two states (H0 true or H0 false) and the decision has two outcomes (reject or retain), giving four possibilities. Two are correct: retaining a true null and rejecting a false null. Two are errors, named by Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933). A Type I error is rejecting a true null, a false positive or 'false alarm'; its probability is alpha, the significance level the researcher sets, usually .05. A Type II error is retaining a false null, a false negative or 'miss'; its probability is beta. Statistical power, 1 minus beta, is the chance of correctly detecting a real effect, and it rises with larger samples, larger effects and a more lenient alpha. The two errors trade off: making alpha stricter (say .01) lowers the Type I risk but raises the Type II risk. Take Arjun, who tests whether a new memory training programme improves recall. The programme truly works, but his sample is only ten students, the difference is not significant, and he concludes the programme has no effect. He has made a Type II error. Option c is right because 'null false and accepted' describes exactly this miss: a real effect exists and the test fails to detect it. Exam trap: the two errors are often reversed. A quick memory aid: Type I is 'seeing what is not there' (rejecting a true null), Type II is 'missing what is there' (keeping a false null). In medical terms, Type I tells a healthy person they are ill; Type II tells an ill person they are healthy.", "topic": "Type II error"}, {"q": "Which test is used for analyzing categorical data (equality of proportions)?", "options": {"a": "t-test", "b": "ANOVA", "c": "Chi-square test", "d": "Factorial design"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the chi-square test is used to analyse categorical data, including testing whether proportions are equal across groups. Categorical (nominal) data are counts of people or events falling into categories, such as yes or no, urban or rural, or type of diagnosis. Such data have no meaningful mean, so tests built on means cannot be used. Karl Pearson (1900) developed the chi-square test to compare the observed frequency in each category with the frequency expected if the null hypothesis were true. The statistic adds up, over all cells, the squared difference between observed and expected counts divided by the expected count. A large value means the observed counts depart from what chance predicts. There are two main forms. The goodness-of-fit test checks one variable against an expected distribution (for example, equal numbers choosing each of four colours). The test of independence checks whether two categorical variables are related, using a contingency table; when one variable is the group, this is the same as testing the equality of proportions. Chi-square is a non-parametric test: it assumes no normal distribution, though each expected frequency should usually be at least 5. Suppose Kabir surveys 100 urban and 100 rural students on whether they have ever seen a counsellor. Comparing the proportion of 'yes' answers in the two groups is a chi-square test on a 2 by 2 table. Option c is right because chi-square works directly on frequencies in categories, which is exactly what proportions are made of. Exam trap: the t-test and ANOVA sound like general group-comparison tools, but both compare means of interval or ratio data. If the dependent variable is a count in categories, think chi-square.", "topic": "Chi-square for categorical data"}, {"q": "Which of these represents the strongest correlation?", "options": {"a": "–0.93", "b": "–0.24", "c": "+0.91", "d": "+1.18"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: a correlation of -0.93 is the strongest of the valid coefficients offered. A correlation coefficient, such as Karl Pearson's (1896) product-moment r, describes how closely two variables move together. It carries two separate pieces of information. The sign gives the direction: positive means the variables rise together, negative means one rises as the other falls. The absolute size (the number ignoring its sign) gives the strength: 0 means no linear relationship, and 1 means a perfect one where every point lies on a straight line. So r always lies between -1.00 and +1.00. Comparing the options by absolute size: -0.93 has a size of 0.93, -0.24 has 0.24, and +0.91 has 0.91. Thus -0.93 is the strongest, just ahead of +0.91, while -0.24 is weak. The value +1.18 is impossible, because no correlation can exceed 1 in either direction; seeing it in a results table signals a calculation or typing error. As an example, Meera finds that the hours students spend on their phones before an exam correlate -0.93 with their marks: more phone time goes with lower marks, and the points hug a downward line very tightly. Option a is right because strength is judged by distance from zero, not by the sign, and -0.93 is the farthest from zero of the possible values. Squaring r gives the coefficient of determination: 0.93 squared is about 0.86, so about 86 percent of the variance in one variable is shared with the other. Exam trap: students treat a negative sign as 'weaker' and pick +0.91, or are tempted by +1.18 as the biggest number. Ignore the sign when judging strength, and reject any value beyond plus or minus 1.", "topic": "Strength of correlation"}, {"q": "Which of these is not a nonparametric test?", "options": {"a": "Sign test", "b": "Wilcoxon Signed Rank Sum test", "c": "Mann-Whitney U test", "d": "Pearson's Product-Moment Correlation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: Pearson's product-moment correlation is a parametric statistic, so it is the one that is not a non-parametric test. Parametric tests estimate population parameters (such as the mean or the population correlation) and assume interval or ratio data, a roughly normal distribution and, where groups are compared, similar variances. Non-parametric, or distribution-free, tests make fewer assumptions: they work on ranks, signs or frequencies and suit ordinal data, small samples and skewed scores. They are less powerful when parametric assumptions hold, but safer when they do not. Three of the options are classic non-parametric tests. The sign test, traced back to John Arbuthnot (1710), uses only the direction (plus or minus) of each paired difference. The Wilcoxon signed-rank test, from Frank Wilcoxon (1945), ranks the sizes of the paired differences and is the non-parametric alternative to the paired t-test; in the same paper Wilcoxon introduced the rank-sum test for two independent groups, which the option's mixed name echoes. The Mann-Whitney U test, from Henry Mann and Donald Whitney (1947), compares two independent groups by ranks and is the alternative to the independent t-test. Pearson's r, by contrast, was developed by Karl Pearson (1896); it uses the actual scores with their means and standard deviations, and its significance test assumes bivariate normality. Its non-parametric counterparts are Charles Spearman's (1904) rank-order correlation, rho, and Maurice Kendall's (1938) tau. Suppose Riya has two judges' rankings of 10 dance performances: she should use Spearman's rho, not Pearson's r. Option d is right because Pearson's r is the standard parametric correlation, whereas options a to c are all rank-based or sign-based. Exam trap: the word 'correlation' is not the clue; Spearman's rho is non-parametric while Pearson's r is parametric.", "topic": "Parametric versus non-parametric tests"}, {"q": "In which type of conditioning does the unconditioned stimulus (UCS) appear before the conditioned stimulus (CS)?", "options": {"a": "Simultaneous conditioning", "b": "Forward conditioning", "c": "Backward conditioning", "d": "Delay conditioning"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: in backward conditioning the unconditioned stimulus (UCS) is presented before the conditioned stimulus (CS). Classical conditioning was described by Ivan Pavlov (1927) from his work with dogs. A neutral stimulus such as a bell (the CS) is paired with a stimulus that naturally triggers a response, such as food (the UCS), which produces salivation (the unconditioned response). After repeated pairings the bell alone produces salivation (the conditioned response). Whether learning happens depends heavily on timing. In forward conditioning the CS comes first. In delay conditioning, a form of forward conditioning, the CS starts first and is still on when the UCS arrives; it is the most effective arrangement because the CS acts as a warning signal. In trace conditioning, the other forward form, the CS ends before the UCS begins, leaving a gap, and learning is weaker. In simultaneous conditioning the CS and UCS start together, giving no warning, so learning is weak. In backward conditioning the order is reversed: UCS first, then CS. It is the weakest arrangement, often producing little or no excitatory conditioning, because the CS predicts nothing; the CS may even become a signal that the UCS is over (inhibitory conditioning). For example, if Arjun's dog is given food and only then hears a bell, the bell never comes to announce food, so it rarely triggers salivation. Option c is right because 'backward' is defined precisely by the UCS appearing before the CS. Exam trap: delay conditioning sounds slow or weak, but it is the strongest form; trace conditioning is the one with the gap. Also keep the directions straight: forward means CS then UCS, the normal predictive order, and backward means UCS then CS.", "topic": "Timing in classical conditioning"}, {"q": "What is the term for applying an unpleasant stimulus to reinforce behaviors that help avoid or escape it?", "options": {"a": "Positive reinforcement", "b": "Negative reinforcement", "c": "Positive punishment", "d": "Negative punishment"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: using an unpleasant stimulus that the person can escape or avoid, so that the escaping or avoiding behaviour is strengthened, is negative reinforcement. In operant conditioning, developed by B. F. Skinner (1938) from Edward Thorndike's (1911) law of effect, consequences change how often a behaviour occurs. Reinforcement always increases a behaviour; punishment always decreases it. 'Positive' means a stimulus is added and 'negative' means a stimulus is taken away; the words describe arithmetic, not good or bad. That gives four combinations. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise for finishing homework). Negative reinforcement removes or prevents something unpleasant, and the behaviour that achieved the removal becomes more frequent. Positive punishment adds something unpleasant after a behaviour (a scolding for lying). Negative punishment removes something pleasant (taking away a phone). The stem describes the aversive stimulus as the setting for learning: it is applied, and behaviours that escape or avoid it are strengthened. This is escape learning (ending an aversive stimulus already present) and avoidance learning (acting early so it never arrives), both maintained by negative reinforcement. For instance, the car's seatbelt alarm beeps until Riya buckles up; the beeping stops, so buckling up quickly becomes a habit. Similarly, Kabir takes a painkiller and his headache goes away, so he reaches for pills sooner next time. Option b is right because the behaviours in question increase, and they increase because they end or prevent the unpleasant stimulus. Exam trap: because the stem says 'applying an unpleasant stimulus', many students pick positive punishment. Punishment, though, weakens the behaviour it follows, whereas here escaping is being strengthened. Ask two questions: is the behaviour going up or down, and is a stimulus being added or removed?", "topic": "Negative reinforcement"}, {"q": "An employer reviews employee performance at random times, prompting consistent work effort. Which reinforcement schedule does this represent?", "options": {"a": "Fixed ratio schedule", "b": "Variable ratio schedule", "c": "Fixed interval schedule", "d": "Variable interval schedule"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: reviews at random, unpredictable times that keep employees working steadily represent a variable interval schedule. Schedules of reinforcement, studied in detail by Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957), are rules for which responses earn reinforcement. Two questions define the four basic schedules. First, is reinforcement based on the number of responses (a ratio schedule) or on the time that has passed (an interval schedule)? Second, is that number or time fixed or variable? On a fixed ratio schedule a reward follows a set number of responses, such as being paid for every 10 garments stitched. On a variable ratio schedule the number of responses needed changes unpredictably around an average, as with a slot machine; it gives the highest response rate and the most resistance to extinction. On a fixed interval schedule the first response after a set time is rewarded, such as a review held every March; it produces a scalloped pattern, with little effort early in the interval and a rush near its end. On a variable interval schedule the first response after an unpredictable amount of time is rewarded. Because reinforcement could come at any moment, the person responds at a moderate, very steady rate with few pauses. Consider Meera's manager, who walks round the office at unannounced times and praises whoever is working well. Meera cannot predict the next visit, so she works consistently all day. Option d is right because the reviews are tied to time rather than to how many tasks are finished, and that time varies unpredictably, which yields the steady effort the stem describes. Exam trap: the word 'random' tempts students towards variable ratio, which they associate with gambling. Ask whether the reward depends on counting responses or on the passage of time; checks at unpredictable times are interval schedules.", "topic": "Variable interval schedule"}, {"q": "In the Atkinson-Shiffrin model, how does information move from sensory memory to short-term (working) memory?", "options": {"a": "Attention", "b": "Maintenance rehearsal", "c": "Elaborative rehearsal", "d": "Encoding"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in the Atkinson-Shiffrin model, information moves from sensory memory to short-term memory through attention. Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968) proposed the multi-store, or modal, model of memory: three stores linked by control processes that the person chooses. Sensory memory (the sensory register) holds a large amount of raw input for a very short time: the visual icon lasts about a quarter to half a second, and the auditory echo about 2 to 4 seconds. George Sperling (1960) showed with his partial-report method that people briefly register almost a whole display of letters, but the trace fades before they can report it all. Only the information we attend to passes on to short-term memory, which holds about 7 plus or minus 2 items (George Miller, 1956) for roughly 18 to 30 seconds unless rehearsed. Everything left unattended decays from the sensory register. Rehearsal then keeps information in short-term memory and, with enough repetition, transfers it to long-term memory, the store of practically unlimited capacity and duration. For example, as Kabir walks through a busy railway station, his eyes and ears register dozens of announcements and signs. When he hears his own train number, he attends to it, and only that announcement enters short-term memory; the rest vanishes within seconds. Option a is right because attention is the control process that selects what leaves sensory memory, acting at that first gateway. Exam trap: rehearsal is the gateway between short-term and long-term memory, not between sensory and short-term memory. Students who remember that rehearsal 'moves information along' apply it at the wrong stage. Encoding is a broader word for turning input into a memory code, and it happens at every stage.", "topic": "Atkinson-Shiffrin multi-store model"}, {"q": "What is retroactive interference?", "options": {"a": "When new information disrupts previously learned material", "b": "When old information hinders new learning", "c": "When retrieved information blocks related content", "d": "When memory fades over time"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: retroactive interference is when new information disrupts previously learned material. Interference theory explains forgetting as competition between memories rather than simple fading with time. Georg Muller and Alfons Pilzecker (1900) first described retroactive inhibition, finding that learning a second list soon after the first impaired recall of the first. The key words are 'retro', meaning backwards, and 'pro', meaning forwards. In retroactive interference, new learning works backwards in time and damages older memories. In proactive interference, old learning works forwards and gets in the way of new learning. The classic design has an experimental group learn list A, then list B, and then recall list A, while a control group learns list A, rests, and recalls A. Poorer recall in the experimental group shows retroactive interference. John Jenkins and Karl Dallenbach (1924) found that students who slept after learning nonsense syllables remembered more than those who stayed awake, suggesting that later activity, not just the passage of time, causes forgetting. Interference is strongest when the two sets of material are similar. For example, Riya changes her mobile number. After months of using the new number she cannot recall her old one: the new learning has overwritten the old, which is retroactive. If instead she keeps typing her old number when asked for the new one, the old learning is blocking the new, which is proactive. Option a is right because it states the direction precisely: later information interfering with earlier material. Exam trap: examiners simply swap the two definitions, as in option b. Remember 'RETRO: new disrupts old; PRO: old disrupts new'. Also keep interference separate from decay theory, which blames time and disuse alone.", "topic": "Retroactive interference"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not included in Sternberg's triarchic theory of intelligence?", "options": {"a": "Analytical intelligence", "b": "Emotional intelligence", "c": "Creative intelligence", "d": "Practical intelligence"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: emotional intelligence is not part of Robert Sternberg's triarchic theory of intelligence. Robert Sternberg (1985), in Beyond IQ, argued that traditional IQ tests capture only one side of intelligence. His triarchic, or three-part, theory names three related abilities. Analytical (componential) intelligence is the mental machinery used to analyse, compare and evaluate: metacomponents that plan and monitor, performance components that carry out the steps, and knowledge-acquisition components that learn. It is what school tests measure best. Creative (experiential) intelligence is the ability to deal with novel situations and to make familiar tasks automatic. Practical (contextual) intelligence is street smartness: adapting to the environment, shaping it or selecting a better one, using tacit knowledge to get things done. Sternberg later called the balanced use of all three 'successful intelligence' (1997). Emotional intelligence is a separate theory. Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990) defined it as the ability to perceive, understand, use and manage emotions in oneself and others, and Daniel Goleman (1995) popularised it in his best-selling book. As an example, Arjun scores well in exams (analytical), invents a clever new way to solve a puzzle no one taught him (creative), and knows exactly how to persuade a shopkeeper to give a refund (practical). His skill at calming a crying friend reflects emotional intelligence, a construct from outside Sternberg's model. Option b is right because analytical, creative and practical intelligence are the three parts of the triarchic theory, so they are included, whereas emotional intelligence belongs to Salovey, Mayer and Goleman. Exam trap: practical intelligence sounds like social or emotional know-how, so students wrongly merge the two. Also do not confuse the triarchic theory with Howard Gardner's (1983) multiple intelligences, whose interpersonal and intrapersonal intelligences overlap with emotional intelligence.", "topic": "Sternberg's triarchic theory"}, {"q": "Which defense mechanism was not originally proposed by Sigmund Freud?", "options": {"a": "Displacement", "b": "Denial", "c": "Rationalization", "d": "Regression"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: denial is the defence mechanism that, as a named and systematised defence, is credited to Anna Freud rather than to Sigmund Freud. A defence mechanism is an unconscious strategy the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety by distorting or blocking awareness of a threatening impulse or fact. Sigmund Freud (1894) first wrote of 'defence' in his early papers on the neuroses and described several mechanisms in his clinical work. Displacement, redirecting an impulse from a dangerous target to a safer one, appears in The Interpretation of Dreams (1900) as part of the dream work. Regression, retreating to the behaviour of an earlier psychosexual stage under stress, is also Freud's own concept. Rationalisation, a plausible excuse invented after the act, entered psychoanalysis in 1908 and became one of the classical defences. His daughter Anna Freud (1936), in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, collected, named and systematised the defences, and denial (refusing to acknowledge a painful outer reality) is the one most often credited to her treatment. Denial differs from repression: repression pushes an inner thought out of awareness, while denial blocks an outer fact. For example, when Meera's doctor tells her that her father's illness is terminal, she insists the reports are wrong and keeps planning his next birthday trip. Option b is right because displacement, regression and rationalisation were all in use within classical psychoanalysis before 1936, whereas denial as a distinct mechanism is attributed to Anna Freud's systematisation. Exam trap: these questions test the Sigmund Freud versus Anna Freud split, and students assume every defence came from the father. Key note: many texts credit rationalisation to Ernest Jones (1908), not to Sigmund Freud, so option c has strong support, and Freud himself wrote of disavowal in the 1920s. The key's view is that denial was systematised by Anna Freud (1936) in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, so the official key stands.", "topic": "Origins of defence mechanisms"}, {"q": "Choose the correct order of Freud's psychosexual stages of development:", "options": {"a": "Genital – Phallic – Latency – Anal – Oral", "b": "Anal – Latency – Phallic – Genital – Oral", "c": "Oral – Anal – Phallic – Latency – Genital", "d": "Anal – Oral – Phallic – Genital – Latency"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Freud's psychosexual stages run oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital. Sigmund Freud, in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality (1905, with the oral, anal and phallic stages added between 1913 and 1923), proposed that personality develops as the libido (sexual or life energy) becomes focused on different body areas, called erogenous zones, in a fixed order. In the oral stage (birth to about 1 year) pleasure centres on the mouth through sucking, biting and feeding, and the key conflict is weaning. In the anal stage (about 1 to 3 years) pleasure comes from holding and releasing faeces, and toilet training is the conflict. In the phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) the genitals become the focus, and the child passes through the Oedipus complex (for girls, the Electra complex, Carl Jung's 1913 term), resolved by identifying with the same-sex parent and forming the superego. In the latency period (about 6 years to puberty) sexual energy is dormant and channelled into school, friendships and skills. The genital stage (puberty onwards) brings mature sexual interest directed at others. If the conflict at any stage is badly handled, part of the libido stays fixated there, leaving adult traits. For instance, Kabir, harshly toilet trained, may grow into a very orderly, stubborn and stingy adult, which Freud called an anal character. Option c is right because it is the only option that follows the developmental sequence from infancy (the mouth) to adolescence (mature sexuality). Exam trap: latency is often misplaced after genital or before phallic. Remember that latency is a quiet pause between the Oedipal storm of the phallic stage and the arrival of puberty; Freud called it a period rather than a true stage, since no new erogenous zone appears. A handy mnemonic is 'Old Age Pensioners Love Gardening'.", "topic": "Freud's psychosexual stages"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not one of the Big Five personality traits?", "options": {"a": "Agreeableness", "b": "Conscientiousness", "c": "Extraversion", "d": "Introversion"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: introversion is not one of the Big Five traits; it is simply the low end of extraversion. The Big Five, or Five-Factor Model, is the most widely used description of personality traits. It grew out of the lexical hypothesis: important differences between people become encoded as words in everyday language. Researchers factor-analysing trait ratings kept finding five broad dimensions: Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961) in US Air Force ratings, Warren Norman (1963), and Lewis Goldberg, who named them the Big Five (1981). Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1985) reached the same five with questionnaires and built the NEO Personality Inventory. The five are remembered as OCEAN: Openness to experience, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness and Neuroticism. Each is a continuous dimension with two poles, and most people fall near the middle. Extraversion runs from high (sociable, talkative, energetic) to low, which is introversion (reserved, quiet, preferring solitude). Neuroticism likewise runs from anxious and moody to emotionally stable. So introversion is not a sixth factor or a separate trait; it is a low score on one of the five. For example, Riya, who loves parties and talks to everyone, scores high on extraversion; her brother Arjun, who prefers a book at home, scores low on the same dimension. Option d is right because agreeableness, conscientiousness and extraversion are three of the five named factors, whereas introversion is only the opposite pole of extraversion. Exam trap: the terms introvert and extravert come from Carl Jung (1921), and Hans Eysenck (1947) made introversion-extraversion one of his superfactors, so introversion feels like a standard trait. In the Big Five each factor is named by one pole only, so its opposite (introversion, emotional stability) is not a separate trait.", "topic": "Big Five traits"}, {"q": "Which disorder involves degeneration of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra?", "options": {"a": "Alzheimer's disease", "b": "Parkinson's disease", "c": "Multiple sclerosis", "d": "Epilepsy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: Parkinson's disease involves degeneration of the dopamine-producing neurons of the substantia nigra. The substantia nigra, 'black substance', named for its dark pigment, is a nucleus in the midbrain. Its pars compacta sends dopamine along the nigrostriatal pathway to the striatum (caudate nucleus and putamen), part of the basal ganglia that helps start and smooth voluntary movement. James Parkinson (1817) described the condition as the 'shaking palsy'. When roughly 60 to 80 percent of the nigral dopamine neurons have died, the classic signs appear: a resting tremor (often 'pill-rolling'), rigidity, bradykinesia (slowness of movement), difficulty starting movement, a shuffling gait, a mask-like face and poor balance. Oleh Hornykiewicz (1960) found the patients' striatum almost empty of dopamine, and the surviving nigral cells contain Lewy bodies, clumps of the protein alpha-synuclein. Treatment follows directly: L-dopa, a precursor that, unlike dopamine itself, crosses the blood-brain barrier, is converted to dopamine in the brain; dopamine agonists and deep brain stimulation also help. For example, Arjun's grandfather notices that his hand trembles while resting on his lap but steadies when he reaches for his cup, and he takes small, shuffling steps; this pattern points to Parkinson's disease. Option b is right because the loss of nigral dopamine neurons is the defining pathology of Parkinson's disease. Exam trap: dopamine excess is linked with schizophrenia, and antipsychotics that block dopamine can cause Parkinson-like side effects (drug-induced parkinsonism), so students sometimes reverse the direction. Also do not confuse Parkinson's with Huntington's disease, which damages the striatum itself and causes jerky, uncontrolled movements (chorea).", "topic": "Parkinson's disease and dopamine"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not categorized under Cluster B personality disorders?", "options": {"a": "Narcissistic personality disorder", "b": "Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder", "c": "Histrionic personality disorder", "d": "Borderline personality disorder"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: obsessive-compulsive personality disorder (OCPD) is not a Cluster B disorder; it belongs to Cluster C. A personality disorder is an enduring, inflexible pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from cultural expectations, is pervasive across situations, begins by adolescence or early adulthood, and causes distress or impairment. DSM-5 (2013) and DSM-5-TR (2022) group the ten specific personality disorders into three clusters by descriptive similarity. Cluster A is odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal. Cluster B is dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic. Cluster C is anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive. A handy memory aid is 'weird, wild, worried'. Narcissistic personality disorder involves grandiosity, a need for admiration and lack of empathy; histrionic involves excessive emotionality and attention-seeking; borderline involves unstable relationships, self-image and mood with marked impulsivity. All three are dramatic and erratic, which is why they sit in Cluster B with antisocial personality disorder. OCPD, by contrast, is a pervasive preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism and control, at the cost of flexibility and efficiency. Its tight, anxious over-control fits the fearful Cluster C. For example, Kabir rewrites every office report several times, cannot delegate because others 'will do it wrong' and misses deadlines through perfectionism; that is OCPD. Option b is right because OCPD is the only option outside the dramatic Cluster B, so it is the odd one out. Exam trap: OCPD is often confused with obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD), which has true obsessions and compulsions, is usually ego-dystonic, and sits in its own DSM-5 chapter of obsessive-compulsive and related disorders rather than among the personality disorders. Note that this same question is printed again as Q59 in this paper.", "topic": "Personality disorder clusters"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not recognized as a subtype of schizophrenia in DSM-5?", "options": {"a": "Catatonia", "b": "Paranoid", "c": "Undifferentiated", "d": "Dissociative"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: 'dissociative' has never been a subtype of schizophrenia, so it is the option that is not recognised. Eugen Bleuler (1911) coined the word schizophrenia, meaning 'split mind', for the splitting of thought, emotion and behaviour from one another, building on Emil Kraepelin's (1899) dementia praecox. Classification systems long divided it into subtypes by the most prominent symptoms. DSM-IV (1994) listed five: paranoid (delusions and hallucinations with fairly intact thinking), disorganised (disorganised speech and behaviour with flat or inappropriate affect), catatonic (motor signs such as stupor, rigidity, posturing or excitement), undifferentiated (criteria met but no type dominates) and residual (only mild or negative symptoms remain). DSM-5 (2013) dropped all five because they had poor reliability, were unstable over time and did not predict treatment response. Catatonia now appears as a specifier, 'with catatonia', that can be added to schizophrenia and to other disorders. A dissociative subtype has never existed in any edition. Dissociative disorders, such as dissociative identity disorder, form a separate chapter and involve disruptions of identity, memory or consciousness, not psychosis. For example, if Meera hears accusing voices and believes her neighbours are plotting against her, an older clinician might have written 'paranoid type', but a DSM-5 diagnosis is simply schizophrenia, perhaps with specifiers for course and severity. Option d is right because dissociative is the only label here that was never a schizophrenia subtype in any system, while catatonic, paranoid and undifferentiated all were. Exam trap: the popular image of schizophrenia as a 'split personality' blends it with dissociative identity disorder; Bleuler's split was within the mind's functions, not into separate identities. Key note: DSM-5 (2013) dropped all the subtypes, and catatonia is now a specifier, so strictly none of options a to c is a DSM-5 subtype; paranoid and undifferentiated were DSM-IV subtypes, while dissociative was never a schizophrenia subtype in any edition, which is why it is keyed. The official key stands.", "topic": "Schizophrenia subtypes and DSM-5"}, {"q": "Identify the non-positive symptom of schizophrenia:", "options": {"a": "Hallucinations", "b": "Disorganized and delusional speech", "c": "Inappropriate emotional expression", "d": "Toneless or flat voice"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: a toneless or flat voice is a negative symptom of schizophrenia, so it is the non-positive symptom. Schizophrenia symptoms are grouped by whether something is added to or taken away from normal functioning. Positive symptoms are the presence of something abnormal, an excess or distortion: hallucinations (false perceptions, usually voices), delusions (fixed false beliefs), disorganised speech (derailment, incoherence, word salad) and grossly disorganised behaviour, including inappropriate affect, such as laughing while describing a death. Negative symptoms are the absence or reduction of normal functions. The five recognised in DSM-5 are diminished emotional expression (affective flattening), alogia (poverty of speech), avolition (lack of self-started activity), anhedonia (reduced pleasure) and asociality. A monotonous, toneless voice, with an unchanging face and few gestures, is a core sign of flattened affect. Timothy Crow (1980) linked positive symptoms to his Type I schizophrenia, acute and responsive to dopamine-blocking drugs, and negative symptoms to Type II, chronic, poorly responsive and associated with enlarged ventricles. Nancy Andreasen (1982) built separate rating scales for each, the SANS and SAPS. Imagine Arjun, who tells his doctor in a flat, unchanging voice, with a blank face, that he has not left his room for weeks; those are negative symptoms. Option d is right because a toneless voice reflects a loss of normal emotional expression, while hallucinations, disorganised and delusional speech, and inappropriate emotional expression are all excesses or distortions, which makes them positive. Exam trap: inappropriate affect (option c) and flat affect (option d) both concern emotion, so students lump them together. Inappropriate affect is an abnormal emotion that is present, hence positive (disorganised); flat affect is normal emotion that is missing, hence negative. This question is printed again as Q61 in this paper.", "topic": "Positive and negative symptoms"}, {"q": "Whichof the following is a common symptom of Major Depressive Disorder (MDD)?", "options": {"a": "Increased energy and restlessness", "b": "Excessive happiness and euphoria", "c": "Persistent feelings of sadness and hopelessness", "d": "Extreme weight loss and insomnia"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: persistent feelings of sadness and hopelessness are a common, core symptom of major depressive disorder (MDD). MDD is diagnosed in DSM-5 (2013) when a person has a major depressive episode: five or more of nine symptoms, present most of the day, nearly every day, for at least two weeks, with distress or impairment. At least one of the five must be either depressed mood (sad, empty or hopeless, or irritable in children) or a marked loss of interest or pleasure (anhedonia). The other symptoms are significant weight or appetite change, insomnia or hypersomnia, psychomotor agitation or slowing, fatigue or loss of energy, feelings of worthlessness or excessive guilt, poor concentration or indecisiveness, and recurrent thoughts of death or suicide. The mnemonic SIG E CAPS (sleep, interest, guilt, energy, concentration, appetite, psychomotor, suicide) plus depressed mood covers them. Hopelessness also lies at the heart of Aaron Beck's (1967) cognitive triad, in which the depressed person views the self, the world and the future negatively. For example, Meera has felt low and hopeless for a month, cries most days and no longer enjoys painting; this persistent sad mood is what clinicians look for first. Option c is right because depressed mood with hopelessness is one of the two cardinal symptoms, and at least one cardinal symptom is required for the diagnosis. Exam trap: option d dresses real criteria in exaggerated form. Weight change and insomnia can occur in depression, but neither is required, they can just as well appear as weight gain and oversleeping (as in atypical depression), and 'extreme' weight loss points rather to an eating disorder or a medical illness. Options a and b describe mania, the opposite pole of bipolar disorder.", "topic": "Symptoms of major depression"}, {"q": "An individual who is impatient, driven, and overly competitive is likely to show characteristics of:", "options": {"a": "Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder", "b": "Type A personality", "c": "Type B personality", "d": "Machiavellian personality"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: an impatient, driven and overly competitive person shows the Type A behaviour pattern. In 1959 the cardiologists Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman noticed that their heart patients wore out the front edges of the waiting-room chairs, as if always ready to jump up. They described the Type A behaviour pattern: intense competitive striving for achievement, a sense of time urgency (impatience, doing several things at once, hurrying others) and easily aroused hostility. Type B people, by contrast, are relaxed, easy-going and unhurried, though they can still be ambitious. In the Western Collaborative Group Study, which followed over 3,000 men for about eight and a half years, Type A men developed coronary heart disease about twice as often as Type B men. Later research found that the global pattern predicts heart disease inconsistently, and that hostility (cynical anger) is the truly harmful component. The idea grew into later types: Type C, unassertive and suppressing negative emotion, was proposed as cancer-prone but has weak support, and Johan Denollet's (1996) Type D combines negative affectivity with social inhibition. Picture Riya, who drums her fingers in queues, finishes other people's sentences, eats lunch at her desk and treats every office task as a race against her colleagues; she is a typical Type A. Option b is right because impatience (time urgency), drive and competitiveness are the defining features of Type A. Exam trap: Type A is a behaviour pattern linked to stress and heart disease, not a personality disorder. Do not confuse its drive with the perfectionism of obsessive-compulsive personality disorder, or its competitiveness with the cold manipulation of Machiavellianism.", "topic": "Type A behaviour pattern"}, {"q": "Which of the following is considered the most widely accepted criterion for defining abnormality?", "options": {"a": "Personality traits", "b": "Pathology", "c": "Sociability", "d": "Statistical deviation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: statistical deviation is the classic, most widely cited single criterion for defining abnormality. Defining abnormality is hard because no single test separates normal from abnormal, so textbooks discuss several criteria. The statistical criterion calls behaviour abnormal if it is rare, that is, far from the population average. It rests on the normal (bell-shaped) curve, applied to human traits by Adolphe Quetelet (1835): most people cluster near the middle, and scores in the extreme tails, for example more than two standard deviations from the mean, count as deviant. Intellectual disability is partly defined this way, with an IQ of about 70 or below, two standard deviations under the mean of 100. The approach is objective and easy to measure, which is why it is so widely used. Its weakness is that rarity is not the same as illness: a genius IQ is just as rare, and common problems such as anxiety are not rare at all. Other criteria include deviation from social norms, personal distress, maladaptive functioning and danger. Marie Jahoda (1958) described ideal mental health in positive terms, and Jerome Wakefield (1992) defined disorder as harmful dysfunction. For example, Arjun's tested IQ of 65 lies well below the population average, so on the statistical criterion it counts as abnormal, while his classmate's IQ of 145 is equally rare but desirable. Option d is right because, of the four options, only statistical deviation is a recognised criterion of abnormality. Exam trap: 'pathology' sounds medical and authoritative, but it is the very thing being defined, so it cannot serve as the defining criterion. Key note: modern texts use several criteria together (distress, dysfunction, deviance, danger) and no single one is sufficient; among these four options, statistical deviation is the classic criterion, so the official key stands.", "topic": "Criteria of abnormality"}, {"q": "A patient has an intense fear of train travel without knowing the exact reason. What condition does this describe?", "options": {"a": "Psychosomatic disorder", "b": "Psychoneurosis", "c": "Psychosis", "d": "Fatigue"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: an intense fear of train travel without knowing the exact reason describes a psychoneurosis, specifically a phobia. Psychoneurosis, or simply neurosis, is the older term for a group of non-psychotic disorders in which anxiety is the central feature. William Cullen (1769) coined 'neurosis' for nervous disorders without fever or local disease, and Sigmund Freud (1895) narrowed it to disorders arising from unconscious conflict. The key features are that the person stays in touch with reality, keeps insight (knows the fear is excessive) and remains largely able to function, yet suffers real distress. Classic psychoneuroses included anxiety states, phobias, obsessive-compulsive neurosis and hysteria. In Freud's view a phobia arises when anxiety from an unconscious conflict is displaced onto an outside object, which is why the person cannot say why that object frightens them; his case of Little Hans (1909), a boy who feared horses, is the textbook example. DSM-III (1980) dropped the term neurosis, and today this case would be diagnosed as specific phobia, situational type, in the DSM-5 anxiety disorders chapter, while ICD-10 still groups it under neurotic, stress-related and somatoform disorders. For example, Kabir panics at the thought of boarding a train, avoids family trips to Delhi, and admits the fear makes no sense, but cannot explain where it came from. Option b is right because a specific, irrational fear with intact reality testing is the prototype of a neurotic (psychoneurotic) disorder. Exam trap: students sometimes choose psychosis because the fear seems irrational. Psychosis means loss of contact with reality, with delusions or hallucinations and poor insight; a person with a phobia knows the fear is unreasonable.", "topic": "Psychoneurosis and phobia"}, {"q": "Which therapeutic approach involves counterconditioning to treat anxiety responses?", "options": {"a": "Psychotherapy", "b": "Non-directive therapy", "c": "Systematic desensitization", "d": "Milieu therapy"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: systematic desensitisation is the therapy that treats anxiety by counterconditioning. Counterconditioning means replacing a learned response to a cue with a new response that cannot exist alongside it. Joseph Wolpe (1958) built the method on reciprocal inhibition: a person cannot be deeply relaxed and anxious at the same moment, so if relaxation is repeatedly paired with a feared cue, the fear bond weakens. The roots go back to Mary Cover Jones (1924), who cured little Peter's fear of rabbits by bringing a rabbit gradually closer while he ate food he liked. Wolpe's procedure has three steps. First, the client learns deep muscle relaxation, usually the progressive muscle relaxation of Edmund Jacobson (1938). Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, a ladder of situations ranked from mildly to severely frightening, often rated in subjective units of distress (SUDS). Third, the relaxed client imagines (or meets in real life) each step, starting at the bottom, and moves up only when a step no longer causes anxiety. Example: Riya fears dogs. She relaxes and pictures a dog in a photograph, then a dog across the road, then a puppy in her lap, climbing the ladder over several sessions until real dogs no longer trigger panic. Option c is right because systematic desensitisation is the classic case of counterconditioning: a calm response is conditioned to the very cue that once produced fear, and it replaces the old anxiety response rather than merely suppressing it. Exam trap: examiners swap in flooding or exposure and response prevention, which rely on extinction and habituation through prolonged exposure without any competing response, or they offer \"operant extinction\" or the \"law of effect\" as the principle. The principle is reciprocal inhibition and the technique is Wolpe's.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation"}, {"q": "Biofeedback is now commonly used in the treatment of:", "options": {"a": "Paranoia", "b": "Catatonia", "c": "Phobias", "d": "Psychosomatic disorders"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: biofeedback is now used mainly in the treatment of psychosomatic (psychophysiological) disorders, physical illnesses in which stress and arousal play a large part. In biofeedback a sensor measures a bodily process that we normally cannot feel, such as muscle tension, skin temperature, heart rate or blood pressure, and shows it to the person moment by moment as a tone, a graph or a light. With this feedback the person learns, by trial and error, to shift the reading in a healthy direction; each change is reinforced by the signal itself. The idea rests on Neal Miller (1969), who reported that autonomic responses, long thought to be involuntary, could be operantly conditioned in rats. The rat findings later proved hard to replicate, but clinical biofeedback grew anyway. Common uses are electromyographic (EMG) biofeedback for tension headache, thermal (hand-warming) biofeedback for migraine and Raynaud's disease, and heart-rate or blood-pressure feedback for essential hypertension; it is also used for chronic pain, irritable bowel syndrome and incontinence. Franz Alexander (1950) grouped such complaints as psychosomatic; the modern DSM-5 label is psychological factors affecting other medical conditions. Example: Arjun, an accountant with daily tension headaches, watches a screen showing the electrical activity of his forehead muscles and learns to lower it; after eight sessions his headaches are fewer and milder. Option d is right because biofeedback works directly on the physiological arousal that maintains psychosomatic symptoms, giving the person voluntary control over the body system that is going wrong. Exam trap: phobias tempt students because biofeedback teaches relaxation, but phobias are treated mainly by exposure and systematic desensitisation, and the psychotic options (paranoia, catatonia) need medication, not self-regulation training.", "topic": "Biofeedback and psychosomatic disorders"}, {"q": "In transactional analysis which ego state involves rational thinking and decision making in the present moment", "options": {"a": "Parent", "b": "Adult", "c": "Child", "d": "Subconscious"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: in transactional analysis the Adult ego state handles rational thinking and decision making in the present moment. Transactional analysis (TA) was founded by Eric Berne (1961) in Transactional Analysis in Psychotherapy and made famous by Games People Play (1964). Berne borrowed the term ego state from his analyst Paul Federn (1952) and described personality as three ego states, the PAC model. The Parent holds attitudes and behaviour copied from parents and other authority figures; it can be critical (\"You should never be late\") or nurturing (\"Let me help you\"). The Child holds feelings and reactions recorded in childhood; the Free Child is playful and spontaneous, the Adapted Child compliant or rebellious. The Adult works like a computer: it gathers facts about present reality, estimates probabilities and makes decisions without being driven by old rules or old feelings. A transaction is a stimulus from one ego state and a response from another. Complementary transactions (Adult to Adult) keep talk flowing; crossed transactions stop it. Example: Meera's manager asks, \"What time will the report be ready?\" (Adult to Adult). If Meera replies, \"By four, after I check the figures,\" she answers from her Adult. If she replies, \"Why are you always pressurising me?\" she answers from her Child, the transaction crosses, and conflict follows. Thomas Harris (1967) popularised strengthening the Adult as the goal of TA in I'm OK, You're OK. Option b is right because Berne defined the Adult as the ego state that processes here-and-now information objectively and makes reasoned choices. Exam trap: students map Berne's states onto the structural model of Sigmund Freud (1923) and assume the Parent, which resembles the superego, is the \"rational\" one; the Parent is about rules and values, while rational, present-focused judgement is the Adult. Berne's states are observable patterns of behaviour, not hidden structures.", "topic": "Transactional analysis ego states"}, {"q": "Which therapeutic method, developed by Perls, aims to restore a sense of wholeness for personal growth?", "options": {"a": "Gestalt therapy", "b": "Yoga therapy", "c": "Family therapy", "d": "Psychoanalytic therapy"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: Gestalt therapy, developed by Fritz Perls, aims to restore a sense of wholeness so that the person can grow. Frederick Salomon (Fritz) Perls (1951) set out the approach in the book Gestalt Therapy, written with Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman, and developed it with his wife Laura Perls from the 1940s. The German word Gestalt means a whole or complete form. Perls borrowed from Gestalt psychology the idea that the whole is more than the sum of its parts and applied it to the person: thoughts, feelings, body and actions should form an integrated whole. Problems arise when people disown parts of themselves (anger, grief, need), carry \"unfinished business\" from the past, or live in their heads instead of in present experience. The therapy is experiential and here-and-now. The therapist asks \"what\" and \"how\" rather than \"why\", points to body language, and asks clients to speak in the first person and take responsibility (\"I am angry\", not \"it makes me angry\"). Its best-known technique is the empty chair: the client talks to an imagined person or a disowned part of the self, then switches chairs and answers. Example: Kabir, who never told his late father how hurt he felt, speaks to his father in the empty chair, feels the grief fully and closes that unfinished business, so a split-off feeling is owned again. Option a is right because Perls created Gestalt therapy, and its stated aim is integration, the reowning of fragmented parts so that a whole, self-supporting person can mature. Exam trap: do not confuse Gestalt therapy with Gestalt psychology, the school of perception founded by Max Wertheimer (1912) with Wolfgang Kohler (1917) and Kurt Koffka (1935); Perls was a therapist, not a founder of that school. The empty chair and the hot seat belong to Perls, not to the client-centred therapy of Carl Rogers (1951).", "topic": "Gestalt therapy"}, {"q": "Which of the following is best defined as a state of persistent worry, apprehension, and fear?", "options": {"a": "Anxiety", "b": "Depression", "c": "Phobia", "d": "Schizophrenia"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: anxiety is best defined as a state of persistent worry, apprehension and fear. Anxiety is a future-oriented emotion: a sense that something bad might happen, felt as worry in the mind, tension and vigilance in the body, and a pull towards caution and avoidance. DSM-5 (2013) separates it from fear. Fear is the alarm response to a threat that is present now (a snake on the path), while anxiety is anticipation of a future threat (wondering whether there will be snakes on tomorrow's trek). David Barlow (2002) called this state anxious apprehension. Anxiety has three parts: cognitive (worry and \"what if\" thoughts), physiological (racing heart, muscle tension, sweating, poor sleep) and behavioural (avoidance, checking, seeking reassurance). It is normal, and useful in moderate amounts; the law of Robert Yerkes and John Dodson (1908) shows that moderate arousal improves performance. It becomes a disorder when it is out of proportion to the danger, lasts (often six months or more) and impairs life, as in generalised anxiety disorder. Sigmund Freud (1926) treated anxiety as a signal of danger to the ego. Example: Riya worries for weeks before her board exams, sleeps badly, feels a knot in her stomach and keeps thinking she will fail even though she is well prepared; that ongoing apprehension is anxiety. Option a is right because only anxiety is defined by the triad of persistent worry, apprehension and fear without a specific, present object. Exam trap: phobia also involves fear, so it lures students, but a phobia is fear tied to a specific object or situation and switched on by it; the stem describes a general, persistent state, which is anxiety. Depression is a state of low mood, not of fearful apprehension.", "topic": "Definition of anxiety"}, {"q": "How are specific phobias best described?", "options": {"a": "Periodic worry about specific situations", "b": "Heightened light sensitivity", "c": "Persistent, irrational, excessive fear triggered by a specific object or situation", "d": "Intense fear of social interactions"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: a specific phobia is a persistent, irrational, excessive fear triggered by a specific object or situation. In DSM-5 (2013) the person shows marked fear or anxiety about a particular object or situation; the cue almost always provokes immediate fear; it is actively avoided or endured with intense distress; the fear is out of proportion to the real danger; and it lasts typically six months or more and causes distress or impairment. DSM-5 names five types: animal (dogs, spiders), natural environment (heights, storms, water), blood-injection-injury, situational (lifts, flying, enclosed spaces) and other (choking, vomiting). Older definitions, such as DSM-IV (1994), also required the person to recognise that the fear is excessive or unreasonable; DSM-5 dropped that insight rule and lets the clinician judge proportion, but \"irrational\" survives in exam wording. The blood-injection-injury type is unusual: heart rate rises and then drops sharply, so the person may faint. Classic explanations include classical conditioning, as in the Little Albert study of John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920), and the three pathways of Stanley Rachman (1977): direct conditioning, watching others and being told. Example: Arjun has feared lifts since childhood. He climbs twelve floors to his office every day and his heart pounds if a colleague suggests the lift, even though he knows the lift is safe. That is a specific phobia, situational type. Option c is right because it states every defining feature: persistence, excessiveness, irrationality and a trigger that is one specific object or situation. Exam trap: option d also describes a phobia, but fear of social interactions and scrutiny is social anxiety disorder (social phobia), a separate DSM-5 diagnosis; and option a describes worry, the mark of generalised anxiety disorder, not phobic fear.", "topic": "Specific phobia definition"}, {"q": "What is a key evolutionary theory explaining phobia development?", "options": {"a": "Non-associative fear acquisition", "b": "Learned fear responses", "c": "Biological preparedness", "d": "Specific phobia acquisition"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: biological preparedness is the key evolutionary theory of how phobias develop. Martin Seligman (1971), in his paper \"Phobias and preparedness\", argued that evolution has prepared humans to learn some fears far more easily than others. Fears of snakes, spiders, heights, darkness, deep water and strangers protected our ancestors, so a brain that learned them fast, and kept them, had a survival advantage. Seligman built on the taste-aversion work of John Garcia (1966), which showed that rats link a taste with sickness in one trial, even with hours of delay, but cannot easily link a light or sound with sickness. This refuted equipotentiality, the old idea that any stimulus can be conditioned to any response equally well. Prepared fears have four features: they are acquired quickly (often in one trial), they resist extinction, they feel irrational, and they cluster around ancestral dangers. Arne Ohman and Susan Mineka (2001) reviewed the evidence: people condition fear to pictures of snakes and spiders faster than to pictures of flowers, and these fears extinguish more slowly. In work by Michael Cook and Susan Mineka (1989), rhesus monkeys learned fear of toy snakes, but not of flowers, by watching videos of other monkeys. Example: Meera has never been bitten, but after seeing one snake on a village path she avoids tall grass for years, while she has no fear of electric sockets, which are far more dangerous in her house. Option c is right because preparedness is explicitly an evolutionary account: it explains why phobias pick out the narrow set of dangers that mattered in human evolution. Exam trap: examiners offer plain conditioning (\"learned fear responses\") as the answer. Conditioning explains how a fear is learned, but not why phobias cluster around snakes and heights rather than guns and cars; that selectivity is what preparedness explains.", "topic": "Preparedness and phobias"}, {"q": "Which combination of techniques is often part of effective CBT for social phobia?", "options": {"a": "Exposure therapy and cognitive restructuring", "b": "Social skills training and mindfulness", "c": "Relaxation techniques and cognitive reframing", "d": "Psychoeducation and systematic desensitization"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: exposure therapy combined with cognitive restructuring is the core of effective CBT for social phobia (social anxiety disorder). Social anxiety disorder is an intense fear of situations in which the person may be watched and judged, such as speaking in class, eating in public or meeting strangers. In the cognitive model of David M. Clark and Adrian Wells (1995), the socially anxious person turns attention inward, builds a distorted picture of how they look (\"everyone can see me shaking\"), and uses safety behaviours (avoiding eye contact, rehearsing sentences, gripping a glass) that keep the fear going and prevent disconfirmation. Treatment therefore has two linked parts. Cognitive restructuring teaches the client to catch automatic thoughts (\"they will think I am stupid\"), examine the evidence and replace them with more realistic beliefs. Exposure puts the client, step by step, into the feared situations without safety behaviours, so the predictions can be tested and anxiety can fall. The cognitive-behavioural group therapy of Richard Heimberg (1990) combines both in group role-plays, and Clark and Wells's individual cognitive therapy adds video feedback and behavioural experiments; both have strong trial evidence. Example: Kabir avoids speaking in meetings because he believes his voice shakes badly. He writes down that prediction, gives a two-minute talk to colleagues without his script, then watches the video: his voice barely shakes and nobody frowns. Option a is right because these two components attack both halves of the problem: distorted social predictions (restructuring) and the avoidance that keeps them untested (exposure). Exam trap: each wrong pair contains a respectable technique, so students choose on recognition. Social skills training, relaxation, mindfulness and psychoeducation are useful add-ons, but exposure plus cognitive restructuring is the evidence-based core.", "topic": "CBT for social anxiety"}, {"q": "Which of the following are common physiological symptoms of panic attacks?", "options": {"a": "Heart palpitations, dizziness and excitement", "b": "Shortness of breath and trembling", "c": "Nausea and chest pain", "d": "Heart palpitation, excessive sweating and rapid breathing"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: heart palpitations, excessive sweating and rapid breathing are common physiological symptoms of panic attacks. A panic attack, in DSM-5 (2013), is an abrupt surge of intense fear or discomfort that peaks within minutes, with four or more of thirteen symptoms: palpitations or a racing heart; sweating; trembling or shaking; shortness of breath or smothering; a feeling of choking; chest pain; nausea or abdominal distress; dizziness or light-headedness; chills or heat sensations; numbness or tingling; derealisation or depersonalisation; fear of losing control or \"going crazy\"; and fear of dying. The physical symptoms are the fight-or-flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915), firing without a real danger. Rapid breathing (hyperventilation) lowers carbon dioxide in the blood, which causes dizziness and tingling and adds to the alarm. David M. Clark (1986) explained why attacks escalate: the person catastrophically misinterprets these sensations (a pounding heart means a heart attack), which raises anxiety and intensifies the sensations in a vicious circle. Recurrent unexpected attacks plus a month of worry or avoidance make panic disorder. Example: Meera is standing in a metro coach when her heart suddenly pounds, she starts sweating and breathing fast, and she is sure she will collapse; ten minutes later it has passed, and her ECG at the hospital is normal. Option d is right because it lists three of the most frequent and characteristic bodily signs of sympathetic arousal in panic, the classic cardiac, sweating and breathing triad. Exam trap: option a starts with two genuine symptoms, palpitations and dizziness, then slips in \"excitement\", a pleasant emotional state that is not a panic symptom; one wrong item makes a combination option wrong. Key note: shortness of breath, trembling, nausea and chest pain (options b and c) are also real DSM-5 panic symptoms. Option d is keyed as the most complete classic triad, and a fails only on \"excitement\"; the official key stands.", "topic": "Panic attack symptoms"}, {"q": "Which class of medications is used in the treatment of social anxiety disorder?", "options": {"a": "Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs)", "b": "Antibiotics", "c": "Antipsychotics", "d": "Anti-inflammatory drugs"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) are the class of medications used, as first-line drugs, in the treatment of social anxiety disorder. An SSRI blocks the serotonin transporter on the presynaptic neuron, so serotonin (5-HT) stays longer in the synapse. The first widely used SSRI, fluoxetine, was approved in the United States in 1987; paroxetine became the first drug approved there for social anxiety disorder in 1999, and sertraline, escitalopram and fluvoxamine are also used. The serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor (SNRI) venlafaxine is an alternative. Benefits usually appear only after 2 to 6 weeks, and treatment typically continues for 6 to 12 months or more to prevent relapse. Common side effects include nausea, sleep change and sexual dysfunction. Other drugs have narrower roles. Beta-blockers such as propranolol damp the racing heart and tremor of performance-only anxiety (a musician before a concert), and benzodiazepines such as clonazepam act fast but carry a risk of dependence. Guidelines such as those of the National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (2013) recommend individual CBT first and an SSRI when the person prefers medication or CBT is not enough; the two can be combined. Example: Arjun, a software engineer, is so afraid of meetings that he takes sick leave on review days. His psychiatrist starts escitalopram while he begins CBT, and after two months he can present his work with only mild nerves. Option a is right because SSRIs are the best-tested and most widely recommended drug class for social anxiety disorder, with consistent support from randomised trials. Exam trap: students sometimes pick antipsychotics because social anxiety is a \"mental disorder\"; antipsychotics treat psychosis, not anxiety. Do not be put off by the fact that SSRIs are called antidepressants: they are standard anxiety treatment too.", "topic": "Drug treatment of social anxiety"}, {"q": "Generalized anxiety disorder is mainly characterized by:", "options": {"a": "Fear of fear", "b": "Chronic worry and tension about future events", "c": "Persistent flashbacks to past trauma", "d": "Constant checking for environmental safety"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: generalised anxiety disorder (GAD) is mainly characterised by chronic worry and tension about future events. GAD entered the diagnostic manuals in DSM-III (1980). In DSM-5 (2013) its core is excessive anxiety and worry (apprehensive expectation) about a number of events or activities, such as work, health, money or family, occurring more days than not for at least six months. The person finds the worry hard to control, and it comes with three or more of six symptoms (only one in children): restlessness or feeling on edge, being easily tired, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension and disturbed sleep. The worry is \"free-floating\": it moves from topic to topic rather than staying on one feared object. Thomas Borkovec (1994) proposed the avoidance theory of worry: worry is mostly verbal thinking, which damps the vivid images and bodily arousal of fear, so worrying is negatively reinforced and becomes a habit. Adrian Wells (1995) added that people with GAD hold positive beliefs about worry (\"worrying keeps me prepared\") as well as negative ones (\"my worry is uncontrollable\"). Example: Riya, a bank officer, worries daily that she will make an error, that her parents will fall ill, that the rent will rise and that her son will fail his exams; she has felt tense and slept badly for a year. No single fear explains it: the worry is general and chronic. Option b is right because chronic, hard-to-control worry and tension about what might happen is the defining feature of GAD. Exam trap: each wrong option is the signature of a neighbouring disorder. \"Fear of fear\" is panic disorder, flashbacks belong to post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), and repeated safety checking is a compulsion of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD).", "topic": "Generalised anxiety disorder"}, {"q": "Which among the following does not fall under Cluster B personality disorders?", "options": {"a": "Narcissistic personality disorder", "b": "Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder", "c": "Histrionic personality disorder.", "d": "Borderline personality disorder"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: obsessive-compulsive personality disorder (OCPD) does not fall under Cluster B; it belongs to Cluster C. This question repeats Question 41 almost word for word, so the reasoning is the same. DSM-5 (2013), like DSM-III (1980) and DSM-IV before it, groups the ten personality disorders into three clusters by descriptive similarity. Cluster A, odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal. Cluster B, dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic. Cluster C, anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive. A handy memory aid is weird, wild and worried. OCPD is a pervasive preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism and control at the expense of flexibility and efficiency: rigid rules, lists, reluctance to delegate, excessive devotion to work and moral scrupulousness. It is anxious and over-controlled, the opposite of the impulsive, attention-seeking style of Cluster B. Example: Arjun, a project manager, redoes his team's slides because the fonts are not uniform, misses deadlines through checking, and cannot take a holiday; he is rigid and anxious, not dramatic. By contrast his colleague Meera, who must be the centre of attention and speaks and dresses theatrically, fits histrionic personality disorder in Cluster B. Option b is right because OCPD is a Cluster C disorder, while narcissistic, histrionic and borderline personality disorders are three of the four Cluster B disorders. Exam trap: the word \"obsessive-compulsive\" makes students think of OCD, but OCPD is not OCD. OCD has true obsessions and compulsions that the person usually finds unwanted (ego-dystonic); OCPD is a personality style the person usually sees as correct (ego-syntonic). Neither belongs to Cluster B.", "topic": "Personality disorder clusters"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the concept of homeostasis?", "options": {"a": "The body's ability to adapt to environmental changes", "b": "The process of storing and retrieving memories", "c": "The body's ability to maintain a stable internal environment", "d": "The process of making decisions based on emotion"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: homeostasis is the body's ability to maintain a stable internal environment. The idea began with the French physiologist Claude Bernard (1878), who said that the constancy of the milieu interieur (internal environment) is the condition of a free and independent life. Walter Bradford Cannon (1926) named it homeostasis, from the Greek for \"similar\" and \"standing still\", and explained it in The Wisdom of the Body (1932). Homeostasis works like a thermostat, by negative feedback. Each variable (body temperature near 37 degrees Celsius, blood glucose, water balance, blood pH, oxygen) has a set point; sensors detect a departure, and responses push the value back. The hypothalamus is the main control centre, acting through the autonomic nervous system, hormones and behaviour. If you get too hot you sweat and the vessels in your skin widen; if you get too cold you shiver and reach for a blanket. In psychology, homeostasis is the basis of the drive-reduction theory of Clark Hull (1943): a bodily need creates a drive (hunger, thirst), and behaviour that reduces it is reinforced. Example: Kabir plays cricket on a 42 degree afternoon in Delhi. He sweats heavily, his thirst rises and his kidneys conserve water; when he drinks, the thirst fades. All these responses keep his internal temperature and fluid level steady. Option c is right because homeostasis is, by definition, the maintenance of stable internal conditions through self-correcting feedback. Exam trap: option a sounds close, but adapting to environmental change is adaptation, or allostasis. Allostasis, a term of Peter Sterling and Joseph Eyer (1988), means stability through change, achieved by shifting set points to meet demands. Homeostasis is about keeping the inside constant, not about changing to fit the outside.", "topic": "Homeostasis"}, {"q": "Identify the option that is not classified as a positive symptom of schizophrenia:", "options": {"a": "Hallucinations", "b": "Disorganized and delusional speech", "c": "Inappropriate emotional reactions like laughter", "d": "Flat or toneless voice"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: a flat or toneless voice is not a positive symptom of schizophrenia; it is a negative symptom. This question repeats Question 43 in slightly different words. Symptoms of schizophrenia are sorted into positive and negative groups. Positive symptoms are additions to normal experience: hallucinations, delusions, disorganised speech and grossly disorganised behaviour, which includes inappropriate affect such as laughing at sad news. Negative symptoms are losses or reductions of normal function. DSM-5 (2013) names two as most prominent: diminished emotional expression (an unmoving face, little eye contact, a monotonous voice) and avolition (loss of goal-directed activity); others are alogia (poverty of speech), anhedonia and asociality. Timothy Crow (1980) linked positive symptoms (Type I) to dopamine overactivity and good drug response, and negative symptoms (Type II) to structural brain changes and poor drug response. Nancy Andreasen (1982) built separate rating scales for each group, the SANS and the SAPS. Peter Liddle (1987) later split the positive symptoms into reality distortion and disorganisation, placing inappropriate affect with disorganisation. Example: Arjun has heard voices commenting on him (a positive symptom) for a year. His sister notices that he now speaks in a dull monotone, shows almost no expression even when his nephew visits, and rarely starts a conversation; this flat, toneless speech is a negative symptom. Option d is right because a flat or toneless voice is a sign of diminished emotional expression (affective flattening), a reduction of normal function, which places it in the negative group. Exam trap: inappropriate affect (option c) and flat affect sound alike but fall in opposite groups. Inappropriate affect is emotion that is present but wrong for the situation, so it counts as positive or disorganised; flat affect is emotion that is absent or reduced, so it is negative.", "topic": "Positive versus negative symptoms"}, {"q": "The animal instincts that humans inherited in their evolution from lower forms of life is", "options": {"a": "The persona", "b": "The Anima", "c": "The animus", "d": "The shadow"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: the shadow is the archetype made up of the animal instincts that humans inherited in their evolution from lower forms of life. Carl Gustav Jung (1875 to 1961) divided the unconscious into a personal unconscious of forgotten and repressed experience and, beneath it, a collective unconscious, an inherited layer shared by all humanity. The collective unconscious contains archetypes, universal predispositions to experience the world in certain ways, which appear again and again in myths, dreams and religions. Jung described four major archetypes of personality. The persona is the social mask we show to others. The anima is the feminine side of a man, and the animus the masculine side of a woman. The shadow is the darkest and deepest part: the primitive, animal instincts carried over from our pre-human past, the source of what we see as evil, immoral or shameful, and so usually disowned. Jung did not see the shadow as purely bad. It is also the source of vitality, spontaneity and creativity, and a person who denies it completely becomes flat and lifeless. Unacknowledged, it is projected onto others, as when we hate in someone else what we refuse to see in ourselves. Calvin Hall and Gardner Lindzey (1957) summarised Jung in almost exactly the stem's words. Example: Meera prides herself on being gentle, yet in a dream she savagely attacks a rival; Jung would read the dream figure as her shadow, rage she does not admit when awake. Option d is right because Jung defined the shadow as the archetype that holds humanity's inherited animal instincts. Exam trap: the anima and animus also sound primitive and inherited, and both are archetypes, but they concern the other sex within the psyche, not animal instincts. The persona is a public social role, the opposite of the hidden shadow.", "topic": "Jung's archetypes: the shadow"}, {"q": "Which of the following works on the 'rationality principle '", "options": {"a": "Id", "b": "Ego", "c": "Superego", "d": "Libido"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: the ego is the part of the personality that works on the rational, reality-based principle the stem calls the \"rationality principle\". Sigmund Freud (1923), in The Ego and the Id, divided the mind into three structures. The id, present at birth, is the reservoir of instinctual energy. It obeys the pleasure principle, demanding immediate gratification, and thinks in primary process (wishes and images, without logic or time). The ego develops out of the id as the child meets the real world. It obeys the reality principle, a term Freud introduced in 1911: it delays gratification until a safe, realistic way to satisfy the need is found. It uses secondary process thinking, which is logical, realistic and problem-solving, and it controls perception, memory and movement. Freud compared it to a rider steering a stronger horse, the id. The superego, the last to form, takes in parents' and society's moral standards through identification at the end of the phallic stage; it works on moral, perfectionist lines through the conscience (guilt) and the ego ideal (pride). The ego mediates between the id, the superego and reality, and uses defence mechanisms when the pressure turns into anxiety. Example: Arjun is hungry during a lecture (id: \"eat now\"). His superego says eating in class is rude. His ego plans: he will wait twenty minutes and buy a samosa at the break. Option b is right because only the ego reasons about reality and chooses realistic, rational ways to meet needs. Exam trap: examiners pair each structure with a principle and then swap them. The id goes with the pleasure principle, the ego with the reality principle, and the superego with morality and perfection. Key note: \"rationality principle\" is the paper's wording; Freud's term is the reality principle, which is the ego's working rule, so the official key stands.", "topic": "Freud's ego and reality principle"}, {"q": "The prisoner's dilemma is used to study", "options": {"a": "The autokinetic effect", "b": "Affiliation and attraction", "c": "Cooperation and competition", "d": "Need complementarity"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: the prisoner's dilemma is used to study cooperation and competition. It is a mixed-motive game, first devised by Merrill Flood and Melvin Dresher (1950) at the RAND Corporation and given its prison story by Albert Tucker (1950). Two suspects are questioned in separate rooms. If both stay silent (cooperate with each other), each gets a light sentence. If both confess (compete), each gets a fairly heavy sentence. If one confesses and the other stays silent, the confessor goes free and the silent one gets the heaviest sentence. Whatever the other does, each person gains by confessing, yet if both reason this way, both end up worse off than if both had trusted each other. The game therefore captures a social dilemma, in which individual self-interest clashes with the shared good. Social psychologists play many rounds for points or money to see when people cooperate. Morton Deutsch (1958) used such games to show that communication and trust raise cooperation, while threats lower it. Robert Axelrod (1984) ran computer tournaments and found that tit-for-tat (cooperate first, then copy the partner's last move) earned the most over repeated games. The tragedy of the commons of Garrett Hardin (1968) is the many-person version. Example: Kabir and Meera run tea stalls side by side near a college. Both can keep prices fair, or one can start a price war. Each is tempted to undercut, but if both do, both lose money. Option c is right because the game is built to pit cooperation against competition and to measure which one people choose, and under what conditions. Exam trap: examiners offer other classic social psychology topics. The autokinetic effect belongs to the norm studies of Muzafer Sherif (1935), and need complementarity to the mate selection theory of Robert Winch (1958); neither uses a payoff matrix.", "topic": "Prisoner's dilemma"}, {"q": "\"one should not take more love than one can give\" best reflects –", "options": {"a": "The halo effect", "b": "Mere exposure theory", "c": "Romeo and Juliet effect", "d": "Equity theory"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: \"one should not take more love than one can give\" reflects equity theory. Equity theory began with John Stacy Adams (1963) as a theory of fairness at work: people compare the ratio of their outcomes (pay, praise) to their inputs (effort, skill) with the same ratio for others, and feel distress when the ratios are unequal. Elaine Walster (later Hatfield), G. William Walster and Ellen Berscheid (1978) applied it to close relationships. A relationship feels fair when each partner's benefits are proportional to their contributions. Inputs include love, care, time, money and good looks; outcomes include the love, attention and support received. Inequity has two forms. The under-benefited partner, who gives more than they get, feels angry or resentful. The over-benefited partner, who gets more than they give, feels guilty. Both states are uncomfortable, so people try to restore equity, either by changing what they give or get, or by changing how they see the balance; if that fails, they may leave. Couples who feel equitably treated report more contentment and have more stable relationships. Example: Riya cooks, plans every outing and remembers every birthday, while Arjun gives little back. Riya feels used, and Arjun, if he notices, feels guilty; both feelings push them to rebalance. The stem's maxim tells Arjun not to take more love than he gives, which is exactly the equity rule. Option d is right because equity theory makes fairness, giving in proportion to what one receives, the rule that keeps a relationship satisfying. Exam trap: social exchange theory, from John Thibaut and Harold Kelley (1959), is the close cousin. Exchange theory says people seek the most rewards for the least cost; equity theory adds that the balance must also feel fair, which is what the stem's moral \"should not\" expresses.", "topic": "Equity theory in relationships"}, {"q": "Which of the given situations involving two people P and O and an object X, will not be in a balanced state according to the balance theory?", "options": {"a": "P likes O and they both like X", "b": "P dislikes O and they both like X", "c": "P likes O and they both dislike X", "d": "P dislikes O, P likes X and O dislike X"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: \"P dislikes O and they both like X\" is not a balanced state in balance theory. Fritz Heider (1946, 1958) proposed that people want their likes and dislikes to fit together consistently. He described a triad of a person (P), another person (O) and an object or issue (X). Each link is positive (likes, agrees) or negative (dislikes, disagrees). The rule is simple: multiply the three signs. If the product is positive, the triad is balanced and stable; if it is negative, it is imbalanced, feels uncomfortable and pushes the person to change one of the links. A quick check: a balanced triad has zero or two negative links, an imbalanced one has one or three. Now apply it. Option a: P likes O (+), P likes X (+), O likes X (+); no negatives, so balanced, like friends who share a taste. Option c: P likes O (+), P dislikes X (minus), O dislikes X (minus); two negatives, so balanced, like friends who share a dislike. Option d: P dislikes O (minus), P likes X (+), O dislikes X (minus); two negatives, so balanced, because disagreeing with someone you dislike feels consistent. Option b: P dislikes O (minus), P likes X (+), O likes X (+); one negative, so the product is negative and the triad is imbalanced. Example: Kabir dislikes his neighbour Arjun, yet they both love the same IPL team. This sits uneasily, so Kabir may warm to Arjun a little, cool on the team, or tell himself Arjun is not a real fan. Option b is right because it is the only triad with a single negative link, so its sign product is negative. Exam trap: students assume any dislike means imbalance, but options c and d contain dislikes and are still balanced. Count the negatives: even means balanced, odd means imbalanced.", "topic": "Heider's balance theory"}, {"q": "Which of theory assumes that individuals have a need to evaluate their own attitudes and abilities?", "options": {"a": "Social comparison theory", "b": "Social facilitation", "c": "Social influence", "d": "Social exchange"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: social comparison theory assumes that individuals have a need to evaluate their own attitudes and abilities. Leon Festinger (1954) proposed that people have a basic drive to know whether their opinions are correct and how good their abilities are. When an objective, physical standard exists (a stopwatch, a fixed pass mark), they use it. When it does not, they compare themselves with other people, and they prefer similar others, because comparison with someone very different tells them little. A mark of 85 percent means little until you know how your classmates scored. Later work added two directions. Upward comparison, with someone better off, can inspire but often brings envy and lower self-esteem. Downward comparison, with someone worse off, described by Thomas Ashby Wills (1981), protects self-esteem, especially under threat. Stanley Schachter (1959) linked the theory to affiliation: frightened participants preferred to wait with others facing the same situation, partly to compare their feelings. Festinger built on the same drive for accurate self-knowledge in his cognitive dissonance theory (1957). Example: Meera, a first-year student, is unsure whether her sketches are good. She does not compare herself with a famous painter or with a child; she looks at the work of others in her art class. Seeing that hers are among the better ones, she feels confident. Option a is right because the need to evaluate one's own opinions and abilities is the opening assumption, the first hypothesis, of Festinger's theory. Exam trap: all four options start with \"social\", so students guess on familiarity. Social facilitation is about performance in the presence of others, social influence is the broad field of conformity and obedience, and social exchange is about costs and rewards in relationships; only social comparison is about self-evaluation.", "topic": "Social comparison theory"}, {"q": "Transferability in qualitative research is", "options": {"a": "Degree to which the results of the qualitative research cab be generalized to other context", "b": "Different methods of the qualitative research van be used interchangeably", "c": "Transferring the qualitative finding in numbers to be used in quantitative method", "d": "Ability of research findings to resonate with individuals of another group"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: transferability is the degree to which the results of qualitative research can be generalised, or transferred, to other contexts. Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba (1985) argued in Naturalistic Inquiry that qualitative research should not be judged by the experimental criteria of internal validity, external validity, reliability and objectivity. They proposed trustworthiness, made of four parallel criteria: credibility (parallel to internal validity), transferability (parallel to external validity), dependability (parallel to reliability) and confirmability (parallel to objectivity). Transferability is the qualitative answer to the question \"does this apply elsewhere?\" Because qualitative studies use small, purposive samples, the researcher cannot claim statistical generalisation. Instead, the researcher provides thick description, a term from the anthropologist Clifford Geertz (1973): a detailed account of the setting, participants, time and processes. The reader, who knows their own setting, then judges how similar the two contexts are and whether the findings fit. The responsibility is shared, but the final judgement lies with the person applying the findings. Example: Riya interviews 12 nurses in a Pune hospital about burnout and describes the ward routines, staffing and shift patterns in depth. A researcher in a Chennai hospital reads the description, sees that her wards are similar, and judges that Riya's themes are likely to transfer. Option a is right because transferability is precisely the extent to which qualitative findings apply to other contexts, the qualitative counterpart of generalisability. Exam trap: the other trustworthiness criteria are often swapped in. Credibility asks whether the findings are believable to participants (checked by member checking), dependability whether the process is consistent (shown by an audit trail), and confirmability whether the findings come from the data rather than the researcher's bias.", "topic": "Trustworthiness: transferability"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the process of making inference about behaviour", "options": {"a": "Cohesiveness", "b": "Proxemics", "c": "Attribution", "d": "Attachment"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: attribution is the process of making inferences about the causes of behaviour, our own and other people's. Fritz Heider (1958), in The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations, described ordinary people as naive scientists who try to explain why things happen. His key distinction was between internal (dispositional) attributions, which locate the cause in the person (ability, effort, personality), and external (situational) attributions, which locate it in the environment (luck, task difficulty, pressure from others). Edward Jones and Keith Davis (1965) explained when we infer a trait from an act (correspondent inference): when the act is freely chosen, has distinctive effects and is not socially expected. Harold Kelley (1967) proposed the covariation model, in which we weigh consensus, distinctiveness and consistency. Bernard Weiner (1972) added the dimensions of locus, stability and controllability, especially for success and failure. Attribution is also prone to biases, above all the fundamental attribution error named by Lee Ross (1977): overestimating personal causes and underestimating the situation when explaining others' behaviour. Example: Arjun's classmate arrives late to the exam. Arjun thinks, \"He is careless\", an internal attribution. Later he learns that the metro broke down, an external cause, and Arjun revises his attribution. Option c is right because inferring the causes behind observed behaviour is the very definition of attribution. Exam trap: proxemics also involves watching behaviour, but it is the study of how people use space, not of how they explain behaviour. Cohesiveness and attachment are about bonds, within a group or between child and caregiver, not about causal inference.", "topic": "Attribution"}, {"q": "Which theory of emotion is based on the interaction between changes in physiological arousal and cognitive interpretation of that arousal", "options": {"a": "James-Lange theory", "b": "Cannon-Bard theory", "c": "Schachter- Singer theory", "d": "Cognitive Appraisal Theory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Schachter-Singer theory explains emotion as the interaction between physiological arousal and the cognitive interpretation of that arousal. Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer (1962) proposed the two-factor theory: arousal gives an emotion its intensity, and a cognitive label, drawn from the situation, gives it its identity. Arousal alone is general and similar across emotions, so without a label we do not know whether we feel fear, anger or joy. In their classic experiment, students were injected with adrenaline (epinephrine), described as a vitamin called Suproxin. Some were told the real side effects (racing heart, trembling); others were misinformed or told nothing. Each then waited with a confederate who acted either euphoric (playing with paper planes) or angry (complaining about an insulting questionnaire). Participants who had no explanation for their arousal tended to catch the confederate's mood; informed participants, who could blame the injection, did not. Donald Dutton and Arthur Aron (1974) found a related misattribution effect: men who crossed a high, swaying bridge were more attracted to a woman interviewer than men on a low, stable bridge. Example: Meera's heart is pounding after she runs up the stairs to a job interview. If she reads it as nerves, she feels anxious; if she reads it as excitement about the opportunity, she feels eager. Option c is right because only Schachter and Singer made emotion the product of arousal plus a cognitive label for that arousal. Exam trap: cognitive appraisal theory (option d) also stresses cognition, but in the version of Richard Lazarus (1966) the appraisal of the situation comes first and produces the emotion; it does not depend on interpreting arousal that is already present.", "topic": "Two-factor theory of emotion"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"gurugram-2025-mphil": [{"q": "The probability that the desired response will be performed is increased by taking away something undesirable whenever the desired response is made is called", "concept": "The answer is b: taking away something unpleasant whenever the desired response occurs, so that the response becomes more likely, is negative reinforcement. In operant conditioning, learning through consequences, Edward Lee Thorndike (1898) first stated the law of effect: responses followed by satisfying outcomes are stamped in. Burrhus Frederic (B. F.) Skinner (1938) then sorted consequences on two questions. Does the behaviour increase (reinforcement) or decrease (punishment)? Is a stimulus added (positive) or removed (negative)? Positive and negative mean plus and minus, not good and bad. So reinforcement of either kind always strengthens behaviour, and punishment of either kind always weakens it. Negative reinforcement comes in two forms: escape, where the response ends an aversive stimulus already present, and avoidance, where the response stops it from arriving. In a Skinner box a rat presses a lever that switches off a mild shock, and lever pressing rises. Example: whenever Arjun starts his car it beeps, and the irritating beep stops only when he fastens his seatbelt. Removing the beep strengthens the seatbelt habit, so Arjun buckles up faster each time. The same logic explains why a headache pill gets taken again, and why avoidance keeps phobias alive: escaping the feared situation brings relief, which reinforces the escape. Option b is right because the stem has both defining features: the probability of the response goes up (reinforcement), and this happens by taking something undesirable away (negative). Exam trap: students hear 'negative' and think 'punishment'. Negative reinforcement is not punishment; it increases behaviour. Examiners also swap the halves: removing something pleasant to reduce behaviour (a fine, losing phone time) is negative punishment, not negative reinforcement.", "traps": {"a": "Positive reinforcement also increases behaviour, but by adding something pleasant (praise, money, Skinner's food pellet). The stem takes something away, so it is negative, not positive.", "c": "Positive punishment adds an unpleasant stimulus (a scolding, extra homework) to decrease a behaviour. The stem describes a response becoming more likely, which is reinforcement, not punishment.", "d": "Negative punishment removes something pleasant (time-out, a fine, lost pocket money) to decrease a behaviour. The stem removes something unpleasant and the behaviour increases, the exact reverse."}}, {"q": "When the conditioned stimulus is presented before the unconditioned stimulus it is called", "concept": "The answer is a: when the conditioned stimulus (CS) is presented before the unconditioned stimulus (UCS), the arrangement is called forward conditioning. In classical conditioning, studied by Ivan Pavlov (1927) with salivating dogs, a neutral stimulus such as a bell is paired with a UCS such as food, which naturally produces an unconditioned response (salivation). After repeated pairings the bell alone produces salivation, now a conditioned response. Pavlov found that the timing of the pairing matters a great deal. In forward conditioning the CS comes first, so it works as a signal that the UCS is on its way. There are two forward forms. In delay conditioning the CS starts first and is still on when the UCS arrives; this is the most effective arrangement of all. In trace conditioning the CS starts and stops, and a gap follows before the UCS; it works but is weaker, because the learner must hold a memory trace across the gap. In simultaneous conditioning the CS and UCS start together, and in backward conditioning the UCS comes first; both are weak, backward the weakest, because the CS then predicts nothing. Robert Rescorla (1968) later showed that what matters is how well the CS predicts the UCS, which is why forward order works best. Example: Meera's phone buzzes (CS) a second before a message from her strict manager appears (UCS) and makes her anxious. Soon the buzz alone makes her tense. Option a is right because the stem defines exactly this order: CS first, UCS second. Exam trap: 'delay' conditioning sounds slow or weak, but it is the strongest forward form; trace is the one with the gap. Do not confuse backward conditioning (UCS before CS) with spontaneous recovery, which is not about timing at all.", "traps": {"b": "Backward conditioning is the reverse order: the UCS is presented first and the CS after it. Because the CS then predicts nothing, it is the weakest arrangement.", "c": "Spontaneous recovery is Pavlov's finding that an extinguished conditioned response reappears after a rest period. It describes what happens after extinction, not the order of CS and UCS.", "d": "Simultaneous conditioning presents the CS and UCS at the same moment, so the CS gives no warning. The stem says the CS comes before the UCS, which is forward."}}, {"q": "The internal or external locus of control theory was given by", "concept": "The answer is d: the theory of internal versus external locus of control was given by Julian Rotter (1966). Locus of control is a generalised expectancy about where the control of reinforcement lies. A person with an internal locus believes outcomes depend mainly on their own effort and ability. A person with an external locus believes outcomes depend on luck, fate, chance or powerful others. The idea grew out of Rotter's social learning theory (1954), in which behaviour potential depends on expectancy (how likely I think a reward is) and reinforcement value (how much I want it). Rotter measured the construct with his Internal-External (I-E) Scale, published in his 1966 monograph. It has 29 forced-choice items (23 scored plus 6 fillers), and a higher score means a more external orientation. Research links an internal locus with better academic achievement, health behaviour and coping, though extreme internality can bring self-blame. Hanna Levenson (1973) later split externality into two kinds: powerful others and chance. Example: after failing a test, Kabir says 'I did not revise enough', an internal attribution, while his friend Rohan says 'the paper was unfair and the examiner dislikes me', an external one. Kabir is more likely to change his study habits next time. Option d is right because Rotter coined the term, built the theory and wrote the standard scale for it. Exam trap: locus of control is often confused with Albert Bandura's self-efficacy (1977), which is belief in one's ability to perform a specific task, and with Bernard Weiner's attribution theory (1979), which uses locus as one dimension of causal attributions. Neither Bandura nor Weiner gave the internal-external locus of control theory.", "traps": {"a": "Hans Eysenck (1947) gave the type theory of personality built on extraversion-introversion and neuroticism, later adding psychoticism (the PEN model). He did not propose locus of control.", "b": "David McClelland (1961) is known for the need for achievement (nAch), along with the needs for power and affiliation, measured with TAT-style stories. That is a motive theory, not a theory of control expectancy.", "c": "Herman Witkin (1954) gave the cognitive style of field dependence versus field independence, tested with the Rod and Frame Test and the Embedded Figures Test. It concerns perceptual style, not beliefs about who controls rewards."}}, {"q": "Which of the following does not form a part of Gordon Allport's trait theory", "concept": "The answer is a: 'primary trait' is not part of Gordon Allport's trait theory. Allport (1937) saw traits as real neuropsychic structures inside the person that make many situations functionally equivalent and so guide consistent behaviour. He later renamed individual traits 'personal dispositions' (1961) and arranged them on three levels according to how much of a life they govern. Cardinal traits are a single ruling passion that dominates almost everything a person does; few people have one, and those who do often lend it a name, such as Machiavellian or narcissistic. Central traits are the five to ten outstanding characteristics you would mention in a reference letter: honest, warm, ambitious. Secondary traits are narrower, less consistent preferences and attitudes that appear only in certain situations, such as a liking for spicy food or irritability when hungry. Example: Mahatma Gandhi's commitment to non-violence is often given as a cardinal trait; Priya's central traits might be kindness, punctuality and humour, and a secondary trait is that she becomes impatient in long queues. So options b, c and d are all genuine Allport levels, and 'primary' is the odd one out. It sounds right because it pairs so neatly with 'secondary', but Allport never used it. The word belongs elsewhere: Raymond Cattell (1946) called his 16 source traits the primary factors of the 16PF, and Louis Leon Thurstone (1938) proposed seven primary mental abilities. Option a is right because the stem asks for the item that does not belong, and primary trait is the only one not among Allport's cardinal, central and secondary traits. Exam trap: the pairing 'primary and secondary' is a natural guess, and examiners exploit it. Allport's three levels are cardinal, central and secondary; Cattell's distinction is surface versus source traits.", "traps": {"b": "Secondary traits are a genuine Allport level: narrow, situation-bound preferences that show up only occasionally. Because they belong to his theory, b cannot be the answer.", "c": "Cardinal traits are Allport's most pervasive level, a single master trait that dominates a whole life. They are part of his theory, so c is not the exception.", "d": "Central traits are Allport's five to ten outstanding traits that describe a person well. They are part of his theory, so d is not the answer."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a part of Gardner's theory of multiple intelligence", "concept": "The answer is d: emotional intelligence is not one of Howard Gardner's multiple intelligences. Gardner (1983), in Frames of Mind, argued that intelligence is not one general ability (Charles Spearman's g, 1904) but several relatively independent abilities. He chose them by criteria rather than by factor analysis: each should be isolable by brain damage, show up in prodigies or savants, have its own developmental history and a distinctive core operation. His original seven were linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinaesthetic, interpersonal and intrapersonal. Naturalist intelligence was added in 1999, and existential intelligence was considered but not fully accepted. So linguistic (a), spatial (b) and interpersonal (c) are all on Gardner's list. Emotional intelligence came from a different line of work. Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990) defined it as the ability to perceive, use, understand and manage emotions, and Daniel Goleman (1995) popularised it in his bestseller. It overlaps with Gardner's interpersonal (understanding others) and intrapersonal (understanding oneself) intelligences, which is why examiners use it as a decoy, but it is a separate theory with its own tests, such as the MSCEIT. Example: Ananya, a poet, is strong in linguistic intelligence; her brother Vikram, a cricketer, in bodily-kinaesthetic intelligence; their friend Sara, who reads the mood of a classroom at once, shows interpersonal intelligence. Option d is right because emotional intelligence is the only option that was never among Gardner's intelligences; it belongs to Salovey, Mayer and Goleman. Exam trap: interpersonal and intrapersonal intelligence look like 'emotional intelligence' and tempt students to pick c, but they are genuine Gardner intelligences. Also do not credit Gardner with the triarchic theory; that is Robert Sternberg (1985).", "traps": {"a": "Linguistic intelligence, sensitivity to the sounds, meanings and uses of words, is one of Gardner's original seven (1983). Because it is part of the theory, a is not the answer.", "b": "Spatial intelligence, the ability to form and manipulate mental images of space, is on Gardner's original list, as seen in a sailor or architect. So b is a genuine part of the theory.", "c": "Interpersonal intelligence, understanding other people's moods and intentions, is one of Gardner's seven. It resembles emotional intelligence, but it is Gardner's own, so c is not the exception."}}, {"q": "Syntax is", "concept": "The answer is a: syntax is the set of rules for the grammatical arrangement of words into phrases and sentences. Language is built in layers. Phonemes are the smallest units of sound that change meaning, such as the /b/ and /p/ that separate 'bat' from 'pat'. Morphemes are the smallest units that carry meaning: 'unhappiness' has three (un, happy, ness). Semantics is the meaning of words and sentences. Syntax governs order and structure: who did what to whom. Pragmatics covers how language is used in social context. Noam Chomsky (1957), in Syntactic Structures, made syntax central to the study of language. He showed that a sentence can be perfectly grammatical yet meaningless ('Colourless green ideas sleep furiously'), which shows that syntax and semantics are separate systems. He also distinguished surface structure (the actual word order) from deep structure (the underlying meaning), linked by transformational rules. Syntax explains why word order changes meaning: 'The dog bit Rahul' and 'Rahul bit the dog' use identical words but say different things. Languages differ in syntax: English is mostly subject-verb-object, while Hindi is subject-object-verb. Example: when three-year-old Ishaan says 'Me want biscuit', his words and meaning are clear, but his syntax is still developing; this telegraphic speech keeps the content words and drops the grammatical ones. Option a is right because the grammatical arrangement of words and sentences is the definition of syntax. Exam trap: the four options list four layers of language, and each wrong answer is the real definition of a neighbouring term. Meaning is semantics, the smallest units of meaning are morphemes, and the smallest sound units are phonemes. Do not confuse syntax (structure) with semantics (meaning).", "traps": {"b": "The meaning of words and sentences is semantics, not syntax. Chomsky's 'Colourless green ideas' sentence shows that a sentence can have perfect syntax and no sensible meaning.", "c": "The smallest units of meaning are morphemes, such as 'un' or 'ness'. They are the building blocks that syntax then arranges, not the arrangement itself.", "d": "The smallest sound units are phonemes, such as /b/ and /p/. They form the sound layer of language, below morphemes and syntax."}}, {"q": "Which type of memory is also called echoic memory", "concept": "The answer is d: echoic memory is the auditory form of sensory memory. In the multi-store (modal) model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), information first enters a sensory register that holds a large amount of raw, unprocessed input for a very short time, with one store for each sense. Ulric Neisser (1967) named the visual store iconic memory and the auditory store echoic memory, because it holds an 'echo' of what was heard. Iconic memory lasts only about a quarter to half a second, as George Sperling (1960) showed with his partial-report method. Echoic memory lasts longer, about 2 to 4 seconds. Christopher Darwin, Michael Turvey and Robert Crowder (1972) showed this with their 'three-eared man' study: letters were played to the left ear, the right ear and both ears at once, and a cue said which channel to report. Partial report beat whole report for a few seconds, then the advantage faded. The longer echo is useful because speech unfolds over time; you need to hold the start of a word until it ends. Example: Neha is reading when her mother asks, 'Have you eaten?' Neha says 'What?' and then, before her mother can repeat it, answers 'Yes, I have'. The question was still in her echoic store, so she could replay it. Option d is right because echoic memory is, by definition, sensory memory for sounds, that is, auditory memory. Exam trap: iconic and echoic are easy to swap. Remember icon equals image (visual) and echo equals sound (auditory). Also note the durations: echoic lasts longer than iconic, so do not assume all sensory memory fades in under a second.", "traps": {"a": "Visual sensory memory is iconic memory, named by Neisser (1967) and studied by Sperling (1960). It is the visual store, not the echoic one.", "b": "Kinesis is not a memory store at all; in biology it means movement in response to a stimulus. It is a lookalike of kinaesthesis, the sense of body position and movement.", "c": "Haptic memory is sensory memory for touch, a third sensory store that is little studied. Echoic memory concerns hearing, not touch."}}, {"q": "Upon being asked to list various uses of a bedsheet Payal responds, \" a parachute\", \"A swing\", or \" for posing as a ghost\" . Here Payal is using", "concept": "The answer is c: listing many unusual uses for a bedsheet is divergent thinking. Joy Paul Guilford (1950), in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, called for research on creativity, and in his Structure of Intellect model (1967) he contrasted two kinds of production. Convergent thinking narrows down to the one correct answer, as in most intelligence and exam items. Divergent thinking spreads outwards to produce many possible answers to an open problem. Guilford's Alternate (Unusual) Uses Task asks people to list as many uses as they can for a common object such as a brick. Answers are scored for fluency (how many ideas), flexibility (how many different categories), originality (how rare the ideas are) and elaboration (how much detail is added). Ellis Paul Torrance (1966) built these scores into the Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking, and in India Baqer Mehdi (1973) built similar verbal and non-verbal tests of creative thinking. Payal's answers show all the signs. 'Parachute', 'swing' and 'ghost costume' are three ideas (fluency) from three different categories, safety, play and drama (flexibility), and none is a typical use like covering a bed (originality). Option c is right because the task is open-ended and Payal is generating many varied, original possibilities rather than searching for one correct answer. Exam trap: divergent thinking is swapped with its opposite, convergent thinking, or with metacognition, which sounds like a 'higher' skill. A good rule: one right answer means convergent; many possible answers means divergent. Note also that divergent thinking is a component of creativity, not the whole of it; real-world creativity also needs knowledge, motivation and the judgement to evaluate ideas.", "traps": {"a": "Metacognition, described by John Flavell (1976), is thinking about one's own thinking: monitoring and controlling how you learn or solve problems. Payal is generating ideas, not reflecting on her own thought processes.", "b": "Crystallised intelligence (Raymond Cattell, 1963) is accumulated knowledge and vocabulary, usually tested by questions with one right answer. Inventing new uses for a bedsheet calls for fresh idea generation, not stored facts.", "d": "Transformational rules are Noam Chomsky's (1957) grammatical rules that convert deep structure into surface structure in language. They have nothing to do with generating uses for an object."}}, {"q": "The tendency to keep repeating solutions that that worked in other situations is called", "concept": "The answer is a: the tendency to keep repeating a solution that worked before, even when a better one is available, is mental set. The classic evidence is Abraham Luchins's (1942) water-jar problems. Participants used three jars of different sizes (A, B and C) to measure out an exact amount of water. The first several problems could only be solved by the long formula B minus A minus 2C. Later problems could be solved by a much simpler route, such as A minus C, yet most participants who had been drilled on the long formula kept using it, and some failed problems the long formula could not solve. A control group that started on the later problems found the short route at once. Luchins called this Einstellung, German for 'attitude' or 'set'. A mental set is not always bad: it saves effort on familiar problems. It becomes an obstacle when the situation changes. Gestalt psychologists, who studied problem solving through insight, saw such sets as a barrier to restructuring a problem. Example: Kabir has always fixed his laptop by restarting it. When the screen freezes because a cable is loose, he restarts it again and again, never checking the cable, because the old solution has become his set. Option a is right because the stem describes reusing past solutions out of habit, which is the definition of mental set. Exam trap: mental set is easily confused with functional fixedness. Both are forms of fixation, but functional fixedness (Karl Duncker, 1945) is about seeing an object only in its usual function, as in the candle problem, whereas mental set is about repeating a strategy or method. Object, think fixedness; method, think set.", "traps": {"b": "Functional fixedness, shown by Karl Duncker (1945) in the candle problem, is the inability to see a new use for an object beyond its usual function. It concerns objects, not repeated strategies.", "c": "A schema, described by Frederic Bartlett (1932) and Jean Piaget, is an organised mental framework of knowledge about something. It guides perception and memory but is not the specific habit of reusing old solutions.", "d": "Heuristics, studied by Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1974), are mental shortcuts or rules of thumb that are usually but not always right. They are general strategies, not the rigid repetition of a previous solution."}}, {"q": "A researcher wishes to know whether there is any significant association between political choice and music choices. Which statistic should be employed to decide this question?", "concept": "The answer is a: to test whether two categorical variables, such as political choice and music choice, are associated, use the chi-square test. Karl Pearson (1900) introduced chi-square for frequency data. Nominal variables like 'party supported' or 'preferred music genre' have no means or standard deviations; you can only count how many people fall in each category. The chi-square test of independence arranges these counts in a contingency table (for example 3 parties by 4 music genres) and compares the observed frequency in each cell with the expected frequency, the count you would expect if the two variables were unrelated. The expected frequency for a cell equals the row total times the column total, divided by the grand total. Chi-square is the sum, across all cells, of (observed minus expected) squared divided by expected. A large value means the observed pattern departs from independence. It is tested against degrees of freedom equal to (rows minus 1) times (columns minus 1). Chi-square is non-parametric and needs independent observations, with expected counts of at least 5 in most cells; Cramer's V then shows how strong the association is. Example: Meera surveys 200 students in Delhi on which party they support and whether they prefer film, classical, rock or hip-hop music. If far more classical lovers support one party than chance predicts, chi-square will be significant. Option a is right because both variables are nominal and the question is about association between categories, which is exactly what chi-square tests. Exam trap: the words 'significant association' can pull students towards correlation or t-tests. Check the level of measurement first. Counts in categories mean chi-square; means of a continuous score mean a t-test or ANOVA.", "traps": {"b": "The independent-samples t-test (William Sealy Gosset, 1908) compares the means of a continuous variable between two separate groups. Political and music choices are categories with no means, so a t-test cannot be used.", "c": "The within-subjects (paired) t-test compares two means from the same people measured twice, such as before and after training. The stem has no repeated measurement and no continuous scores.", "d": "Analysis of variance (Ronald Fisher, 1925) compares the means of three or more groups on a continuous dependent variable. Here both variables are categorical, so there is no mean to analyse."}}, {"q": "Which among the following measures of central tendency is most sensitive to outlying observation?", "concept": "The answer is a: the mean is the measure of central tendency most sensitive to outlying observations. A measure of central tendency gives one typical value for a set of scores. The three main ones are the mean, the median and the mode. The mean is the arithmetic average: add every score and divide by the number of scores. Because every score enters the sum with its full value, one extreme score can pull the mean far towards it. The median is the middle score when the data are put in order; it depends only on rank position, so an extreme score at one end barely moves it. The mode is the most frequent score; an outlier, being rare, almost never changes it. This is why the median is preferred for skewed data such as incomes or house prices, and why, in a positively skewed distribution, the mean sits above the median, which sits above the mode. Example: five friends in Pune earn 20, 22, 25, 28 and 30 thousand rupees a month, so the mean and the median are both 25 thousand. Now Arjun's cousin, who earns 500 thousand, joins the group. The median moves only to 26.5 thousand, but the mean jumps to about 104 thousand, a figure that describes nobody in the group. Option a is right because the mean uses the actual size of every score, so a single outlier distorts it more than it distorts the median or the mode. Exam trap: standard deviation is also strongly affected by outliers, since it is built on squared deviations from the mean, so it looks tempting. But it is a measure of variability, not of central tendency, so it cannot answer a question about which average is most sensitive.", "traps": {"b": "The median is the middle score of ordered data and depends only on position, so it resists outliers. That is why it is the preferred average for skewed data.", "c": "The mode is the most frequent score and is the least affected by a single extreme value, since one outlier rarely changes which score is most common.", "d": "Standard deviation is sensitive to outliers, but it is a measure of variability (spread), not of central tendency. It does not fit a question about averages."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is an assumption of Pearson's Product moment correlation", "concept": "The answer is c: Pearson's product-moment correlation assumes both homoscedasticity and a linear relationship. Karl Pearson (1896) developed r, which measures the strength and direction of the straight-line relationship between two variables, running from minus 1 to plus 1. Its main assumptions are these. First, linearity: r captures only straight-line relationships. If the true relation is curved, such as the inverted U of the Yerkes-Dodson law (Robert Yerkes and John Dodson, 1908) linking arousal and performance, r can be near zero even though the variables are strongly related; the correlation ratio eta is used instead. Second, homoscedasticity: the spread of Y scores should be about the same at every level of X, so the scatterplot looks like an even, cigar-shaped band rather than a fan. When the spread widens at one end (heteroscedasticity), r misrepresents the relationship. Third, both variables should be measured on an interval or ratio scale, and for significance testing they should be roughly bivariate normal. Restriction of range lowers r, and outliers can inflate or deflate it, so the usual check is to draw a scatterplot before computing r. Example: Riya correlates hours of study with marks for 100 students. If the dots form a straight, evenly spread band, r is appropriate. If marks rise and then flatten, linearity fails; if marks vary widely only among heavy studiers, homoscedasticity fails. Option c is right because linearity and homoscedasticity are both standard assumptions of Pearson's r, so neither a nor b alone is complete. Exam trap: nominal variables are the opposite of what Pearson's r needs. For categories use the phi coefficient or the contingency coefficient; for ranks use Charles Spearman's (1904) rho.", "traps": {"a": "Homoscedasticity is a true assumption of Pearson's r, but choosing it alone leaves out linearity. Since both are required, a is incomplete.", "b": "Linearity is a true assumption, because r measures only straight-line association. Choosing it alone leaves out homoscedasticity, so b is incomplete.", "d": "Pearson's r needs interval or ratio data; nominal variables call for chi-square, the phi coefficient or the contingency coefficient. This option reverses the actual requirement."}}, {"q": "A normal distribution of 200 score has a mean of 30 and a standard deviation of 4. Rahul received a score of 38. which of the following is not true of his score", "concept": "The answer is d: the statement that approximately 68% of students scored lower than Rahul is not true. Work it through with a z-score, which tells you how many standard deviations a score lies from the mean: z equals (score minus mean) divided by the standard deviation. Rahul's z equals (38 minus 30) divided by 4, which is plus 2. So statements a and b are true: his score equals a z of plus 2, and it lies 2 standard deviations above the mean. Now use the normal curve, described by Abraham de Moivre (1733) and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809). It is symmetrical, so 50% of scores lie below the mean. The empirical rule says about 68% of scores fall within 1 SD of the mean (34% on each side), about 95% within 2 SD (47.7% on each side) and about 99.7% within 3 SD. The area between the mean and z equals plus 2 is 47.72%, so statement c (about 48% between his score and the mean) is true. The percentage below Rahul is 50% plus 47.72%, about 97.7%. He outscored roughly 98% of the group, which puts him near the 98th percentile, so 68% is far too low. In concrete terms, of the 200 students about 195 scored below Rahul, not 136. Option d is right because it is the only false statement; the true figure for scores below z equals plus 2 is about 98%, not 68%. Exam trap: 68% is the famous figure for the area within plus or minus 1 SD, not the area below plus 2 SD. Examiners plant it because students remember the number without its meaning. Always add the 50% below the mean when you turn a positive z into a percentile.", "traps": {"a": "Option a is true: z equals (38 minus 30) divided by 4, which is plus 2. Since it is correct, it cannot be the 'not true' answer.", "b": "Option b is true: a z of plus 2 means the score lies exactly 2 standard deviations above the mean. It restates a, so it is not the exception.", "c": "Option c is true: the normal curve table gives 47.72% of the area between the mean and z equals plus 2, which is about 48%. So c is a correct statement."}}, {"q": "A graphical representation of correlational data is called a", "concept": "The answer is b: a graphical representation of correlational data is a scatterplot, also called a scatter diagram. Correlation describes how two variables vary together. To draw a scatterplot, you put one variable on the X axis and the other on the Y axis, and each person becomes a single dot placed at their pair of scores. The pattern of dots then shows three things at a glance. Direction: dots rising from lower left to upper right show a positive correlation; dots falling from upper left to lower right show a negative one. Strength: the tighter the dots cluster around a straight line, the stronger the correlation; a shapeless cloud means r is near zero. Form: the dots reveal whether the relation is linear or curved, and whether there are outliers or uneven spread (heteroscedasticity). Francis Galton (1886) used such plots of parents' and children's heights to discover regression towards the mean, and Karl Pearson (1896) then formalised the correlation coefficient r. A line of best fit, the regression line, can be drawn through the dots to predict one variable from the other. Example: Aarav plots hours of sleep against exam marks for his 40 classmates. If the dots rise steadily, more sleep goes with higher marks, a positive correlation, though this does not prove that sleep causes better marks. Option b is right because a scatterplot is the graph designed to show the relationship between two variables measured on the same people. Exam trap: the other three options are all shapes of a frequency distribution of a single variable. A bell curve, a bimodal curve and a skewed curve each show how scores on one variable are spread, not how two variables relate to each other.", "traps": {"a": "A bell curve is the normal distribution (Abraham de Moivre, 1733; Carl Friedrich Gauss, 1809), the symmetrical shape of one variable's frequency distribution. It does not plot two variables against each other.", "c": "A bimodal distribution is a frequency distribution of one variable with two peaks, such as heights in a mixed group of men and women. It describes one variable, not a correlation.", "d": "A skewed distribution is an asymmetrical frequency distribution with a long tail to one side, such as income. It shows the spread of one variable, not the relation between two."}}, {"q": "The variable whose effect is being studied and that the experimenter manipulates is –", "concept": "The answer is a: the variable the experimenter manipulates, and whose effect is being studied, is the independent variable (IV). An experiment is a study in which the researcher deliberately changes one thing and watches what happens to another while holding everything else constant. The thing deliberately changed is the IV; its different values are called levels or conditions. The thing measured to see the effect is the dependent variable (DV), so called because its value depends on the IV. Robert Sessions Woodworth (1938), in his textbook Experimental Psychology, popularised this IV and DV language, and the logic goes back to John Stuart Mill's (1843) method of difference. If the IV is the only thing that differs between conditions, any difference in the DV can be put down to it, which is why only experiments can show cause and effect. Random assignment spreads other influences evenly across groups. A useful memory aid: the IV is the cause, what I change; the DV is the effect, the data I collect. Example: Dr Kavya Rao wants to know whether caffeine speeds up reaction time. She gives one group of students 200 mg of caffeine and another a placebo, then measures their reaction times. Caffeine (present or absent) is the IV; reaction time is the DV. Option a is right because manipulation by the experimenter is the defining feature of the IV. Exam trap: the stem says 'whose effect is being studied', and students sometimes pick the DV because it sounds like the 'effect'. But the IV is the one whose effect is studied; the DV is the outcome on which that effect shows. A confounding variable also changes alongside the IV, but by accident, not by the experimenter's design.", "traps": {"b": "The dependent variable is the outcome the researcher measures, such as reaction time or a test score. It changes in response to the IV; the experimenter does not manipulate it.", "c": "A confounding variable is an uncontrolled factor that changes along with the IV and so offers a rival explanation for the result. It varies by accident, not by the experimenter's design.", "d": "A mediating variable, defined by Reuben Baron and David Kenny (1986), explains how or why the IV affects the DV, sitting in the causal chain between them. It is not the variable the experimenter manipulates."}}, {"q": "A study where the researcher does not intervene, is a type of –", "concept": "The answer is c: a study in which the researcher does not intervene at all is naturalistic observation. In naturalistic observation the researcher watches and records behaviour as it happens in its natural setting, such as a home, a classroom or a forest, without manipulating any variable, setting up any situation or interacting with the people observed. Its strength is ecological validity: behaviour is seen as it really occurs. Its weaknesses are reactivity (people may act differently when they know they are being watched), observer bias and lack of control, so it describes behaviour but cannot show cause. Researchers reduce reactivity by staying hidden or by letting those observed get used to them. The classic example is Jane Goodall (1960), who watched chimpanzees at Gombe in Tanzania for decades and discovered that they make and use tools. Example: Sana sits quietly at the edge of an Ahmedabad playground and records how often boys and girls share toys, never talking to the children or changing anything. Option c is right because naturalistic observation is defined by the absence of intervention: the researcher only observes. Exam trap: experimental and quasi-experimental studies both involve applying or manipulating an independent variable, so 'does not intervene' rules them out. The quasi-experiment, described by Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963), still applies a treatment; it only lacks random assignment. Key note: a correlational study also involves no manipulation, since variables are measured as they exist, so option d has support. The key takes naturalistic observation because there the researcher watches behaviour in its natural setting without intervening at all, whereas a correlational study usually still gives tests or questionnaires. The official key stands.", "traps": {"a": "A quasi-experimental study, described by Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963), still applies or compares a treatment, only without random assignment. The researcher intervenes, so it does not fit.", "b": "An experimental study is defined by intervention: the researcher manipulates the independent variable and randomly assigns participants. It is the opposite of a non-intervening study.", "d": "A correlational study does not manipulate variables, which gives it some support here, but the researcher usually still intervenes by giving tests or questionnaires. Naturalistic observation is the purest non-intervening method."}}, {"q": "When a researcher wants to compare the means of two groups, which type of significant test is best suited?", "concept": "The answer is b: to compare the means of two groups, the best-suited test of significance is the t-test. The t-test was developed by William Sealy Gosset (1908), a chemist at the Guinness brewery in Dublin who published under the pen name 'Student', which is why it is called Student's t. He needed a test that worked with small samples, where the normal (z) distribution is not accurate. The t statistic is a ratio: the difference between the two means divided by the standard error of that difference. A large t means the difference is big relative to the variability within the groups, so it is unlikely to be due to chance. The value is checked against the t distribution at the right degrees of freedom (for independent groups, n1 plus n2 minus 2). There are two main forms: the independent-samples t-test for two separate groups, and the paired (dependent or within-subjects) t-test for the same people measured twice or for matched pairs. It assumes interval or ratio data, roughly normal distributions and, for independent groups, similar variances (homogeneity of variance). Example: Ritika compares the mean anxiety scores of 30 students who did a yoga programme with those of 30 who did not. A significant t shows that the two means differ by more than chance would produce. Option b is right because the t-test is designed for exactly this comparison: two means. Exam trap: ANOVA can also compare two means, and then F equals t squared, so it tempts students. But ANOVA (Ronald Fisher, 1925) is built for three or more groups, where running many t-tests would inflate the Type I error rate. For two groups, t is the standard answer.", "traps": {"a": "ANOVA, developed by Ronald Fisher (1925), compares the means of three or more groups in a single test. With only two groups it gives the same result as t (F equals t squared), so the t-test is the better-suited choice.", "c": "A factorial design is a research design in which two or more independent variables are crossed, such as a 2 by 3 design. It is a plan for an experiment, not a significance test.", "d": "Regression predicts one variable from one or more others and describes their relationship. It is not the standard test for comparing two group means."}}, {"q": "Which of the following can reduce experimenter bias?", "concept": "The answer is d: double blinding reduces experimenter bias. Experimenter bias, or the experimenter expectancy effect, is the way a researcher's expectations can unintentionally shape results: through subtle cues to participants, through how ambiguous responses are scored, or through how data are recorded. Robert Rosenthal (1966) made the effect famous. In Robert Rosenthal and Kermit Fode's (1963) study, students were told their rats were 'maze-bright' or 'maze-dull', though the rats had been assigned at random; the 'bright' rats learned faster, apparently because the students handled them differently. In a single-blind study the participants do not know which condition they are in, which controls their own expectations, such as placebo effects. In a double-blind study neither the participants nor the researchers who deal with them and record the data know who is in which condition. Because the experimenter does not know, their expectations cannot leak into the participants' behaviour or the scoring. Double-blind designs are standard in drug trials, where an independent person holds the code that links each participant to drug or placebo. Example: Dr Anil tests a new antidepressant. A pharmacist labels identical capsules with codes, and neither Dr Anil, who rates the patients' mood, nor the patients know which capsules hold the drug. The code is broken only after rating ends. Option d is right because double blinding removes the experimenter's knowledge of the conditions, which is the very source of experimenter bias. Exam trap: control groups are vital, but they control extraneous variables and placebo effects, not the experimenter's own expectations; an experimenter who knows which group is which can still bias the results. Demand characteristics and the Barnum effect are sources of bias, not cures for it.", "traps": {"a": "A control group gives a baseline for comparison and controls extraneous factors such as maturation or placebo response. It does not stop an experimenter who knows the groups from biasing results, so it is not the best answer.", "b": "Demand characteristics, described by Martin Orne (1962), are cues in a study that tell participants what the researcher expects, so they change their behaviour. They are a source of bias, not a way to reduce it.", "c": "The Barnum effect, shown by Bertram Forer (1949) and named by Paul Meehl (1956), is the tendency to accept vague, general personality descriptions as accurate for oneself. It is an error in judging feedback, not a method of control."}}, {"q": "The Minnesota Multiphasic personality inventory yields score on how many clinical scales?", "concept": "The answer is c: the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) yields scores on 10 clinical scales. Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) built the MMPI at the University of Minnesota by empirical criterion keying: an item went onto a scale if patients with a given diagnosis answered it differently from normal people, whatever its content. The original test had 550 true-false items; the MMPI-2 (James Butcher and colleagues, 1989) has 567. The 10 clinical scales are known by number: 1 Hypochondriasis, 2 Depression, 3 Hysteria, 4 Psychopathic Deviate, 5 Masculinity-Femininity, 6 Paranoia, 7 Psychasthenia, 8 Schizophrenia, 9 Hypomania and 0 Social Introversion. Eight were keyed against psychiatric groups; scales 5 and 0 were built later from other criterion groups. Scales 1, 2 and 3 form the 'neurotic triad', and 6, 7, 8 and 9 the 'psychotic tetrad'. Scores are T scores with a mean of 50 and an SD of 10; on the MMPI-2 a T of 65 or more is clinically significant, and clinicians read the profile as a pattern, often by two-point code types. Separate validity scales, ? (cannot say), L (lie), F (infrequency) and K (correction), check for faking or careless answering. Example: Rakesh's profile peaks on scales 2 and 7, a 2-7 code type typical of anxious depression. Option c is right because the standard MMPI and MMPI-2 profile has exactly ten clinical scales. Exam trap: 13 is the number of 'basic scales', the 10 clinical scales plus the 3 validity scales L, F and K, and the MMPI-2-RF (Yossef Ben-Porath and Auke Tellegen, 2008) has 9 Restructured Clinical scales. None of 12, 15 or 11 is the clinical-scale count.", "traps": {"a": "Twelve is not a recognised count of MMPI clinical scales. The standard profile shows 10 clinical scales, with the validity scales (?, L, F, K) plotted separately.", "b": "Fifteen is the number of MMPI-2 content scales (James Butcher and colleagues, 1990), which group items by obvious content. They are a different family from the 10 clinical scales.", "d": "Eleven is a near-miss: the clinical scales are numbered 1 to 9 and then 0, which makes ten, not eleven. Validity scales such as L, F and K are counted separately."}}, {"q": "Which of the following indicates how well a person performed on an IQ test relative to her/his same-age peers?", "concept": "The answer is b: the deviation IQ shows how well a person performed relative to same-age peers. David Wechsler (1939) introduced it in the Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scale to replace the older ratio IQ. A deviation IQ is a standard score. The test is given to a large norm sample at each age, and a person's raw score is compared with the distribution of scores of people of the same age. The scores are converted so that each age group has a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15 (the older Stanford-Binet used 16). An IQ of 115 therefore means one SD above the mean for your age, about the 84th percentile; 130 means two SD above, about the 98th percentile; 70 means two SD below. Because the meaning is the same at every age, a deviation IQ works for adults, whose mental age stops growing. The earlier ratio IQ was proposed by William Stern (1912) as mental age divided by chronological age, and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied it by 100 in the Stanford-Binet. It compares a person's mental age with their own chronological age. It breaks down after about age 16, because mental age levels off while chronological age keeps rising, and its spread differs from one age to another, so the same ratio IQ does not mean the same rank at different ages. Example: Tanvi, aged 25, scores 115 on the WAIS-IV, which means she did better than about 84% of adults her age in the norm group. Option b is right because the deviation IQ is defined by comparison with the score distribution of same-age peers. Exam trap: the ratio IQ also involves age, so 'both a and b' looks safe. But the ratio IQ compares mental age with chronological age, not a person with the spread of peers' scores.", "traps": {"a": "The ratio IQ (William Stern, 1912; Lewis Terman, 1916) is mental age divided by chronological age, times 100. It compares a child's mental age with their own age rather than placing them in the distribution of same-age peers, and it fails for adults.", "c": "Only the deviation IQ is defined by direct comparison with the score distribution of same-age peers; the ratio IQ is a quotient of two ages. So 'both' overstates what the ratio IQ does.", "d": "A raw score is simply the number of items correct. On its own it has no reference group, so it says nothing about standing among peers until it is converted into a standard score."}}, {"q": "A test's coverage of the particular skill or knowledge area that it is supposed to measure is", "concept": "The answer is a: a test's coverage of the skill or knowledge area it is supposed to measure is its content validity. Validity is the extent to which a test measures what it claims to measure. Content validity asks whether the items are a fair, representative sample of the whole domain. It is built in during test construction rather than computed afterwards. The developer first defines the domain and draws up a test blueprint, or table of specifications, listing the topics and skills to be covered and how much weight each should get. Items are then written to match the blueprint, and subject experts judge whether the content is relevant and well balanced. Charles H. Lawshe (1975) proposed the content validity ratio to quantify how many experts rate each item as essential. Content validity matters most for achievement tests, licensing examinations and job knowledge tests. Example: Mr Iyer sets a Class 10 mathematics test, but 18 of its 20 questions are on algebra and only 2 on geometry, though the syllabus gives them equal weight. The test lacks content validity because it under-samples geometry, even if each question is well written. Option a is right because the stem's key word, 'coverage', is the defining idea of content validity: does the test cover the domain adequately? Exam trap: content validity is often confused with face validity. Face validity is only whether the test looks, to the people taking it, as if it measures the right thing; it is a matter of appearance and is not technical evidence. Content validity is a systematic judgement by experts against a defined domain.", "traps": {"b": "Face validity is whether a test appears, to the people taking it, to measure what it claims. It is about surface appearance, not systematic coverage of the domain, and is not technical evidence of validity.", "c": "Convergent validity, from Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske's (1959) multitrait-multimethod matrix, is shown when a test correlates highly with other measures of the same construct. It is construct evidence, not domain coverage.", "d": "Criterion validity is how well test scores correlate with an external criterion, either now (concurrent) or later (predictive), such as job performance. It concerns prediction, not how fully the items cover the content."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a type of reliability", "concept": "The answer is d: item difficulty is not a type of reliability; it is an index from item analysis. Reliability is the consistency of a test's scores. In classical test theory, set out by Charles Spearman (1904), every observed score equals a true score plus error, and reliability is the proportion of score variance that is true variance. The main types each check consistency in a different way, and options a, b and c are all genuine types. Test-retest reliability (a) gives the same test to the same people twice and correlates the two sets of scores; it measures stability over time. Alternate-form, or parallel-form, reliability (c) gives two equivalent versions of the test and correlates them; it measures equivalence. Split-half reliability (b) divides one test into two halves, usually odd versus even items, correlates them, and corrects the result with the Spearman-Brown formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910), because each half is only half as long as the full test. G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937) and Lee Cronbach (1951, coefficient alpha) extended this internal consistency idea. Item difficulty, by contrast, is the proportion of test takers who answer an item correctly, written p. An item that 80% pass has p equal to 0.80, an easy item; items near 0.50 usually discriminate best. It describes single items, not the consistency of total scores. Example: Dr Sharma builds a reasoning test. She uses item difficulty to drop items that nobody or everybody gets right, then checks split-half and test-retest reliability for the final version. Option d is right because item difficulty is an item-analysis statistic, while the other three are recognised methods of estimating reliability. Exam trap: item difficulty sounds technical, like a reliability term, but it belongs with item discrimination in item analysis.", "traps": {"a": "Test-retest reliability is a genuine type: the same test is given twice and the scores are correlated to show stability over time. Since it is a type of reliability, a cannot be the answer.", "b": "Split-half reliability is a genuine type of internal consistency, corrected with the Spearman-Brown formula (1910). It belongs on the list, so b is not the exception.", "c": "Alternate-form (parallel-form) reliability is a genuine type: two equivalent versions of a test are correlated to show equivalence. It is valid, so c is not the answer."}}, {"q": "A test is constructed by including items that differentiate between addicted group and normals. The test therefore relies on the concept of –", "concept": "The answer is c: a test built from items that separate an addicted group from normals relies on concurrent validity. Criterion-related validity asks how well test scores relate to an external criterion, an independent measure of what the test is meant to show. It has two forms that differ only in timing. In concurrent validity the test and the criterion are measured at about the same time, here the person's present membership of an addicted or a normal group. In predictive validity the criterion is measured later, such as relapse a year after treatment. The method in the stem is the contrasted-groups, or empirical criterion-keying, approach: items are tried on two groups already known to differ, and only items that separate them are kept, whatever their content. Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) built the MMPI this way, and Craig MacAndrew (1965) built his alcoholism scale by comparing alcoholic and non-alcoholic psychiatric patients. Anne Anastasi (1954), in Psychological Testing, lists contrasted groups as criterion-related, concurrent evidence, because the criterion (current diagnosis) already exists at the time of testing. Example: Dr Fatima gives 300 items to 100 patients in a Bengaluru de-addiction centre and 100 people from the community, and keeps the 40 items the two groups answer most differently. Option c is right because the test is validated against a present, known criterion: current group membership. Exam trap: predictive validity sounds right because the test may later be used to predict, but nothing in the stem is measured in the future. Key note: other texts file the contrasted-groups (known-groups) method under construct validity, so option b has support. Following Anastasi, the key treats it as concurrent criterion evidence. The official key stands.", "traps": {"a": "Predictive validity correlates test scores with a criterion measured in the future, such as later relapse or job success. Here the groups are already known at the time of testing, so the criterion is concurrent, not future.", "b": "Construct validity (Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl, 1955) is whether a test measures a theoretical trait, shown by many lines of evidence, and some texts place the known-groups method under it. Here, though, items are selected directly against a present criterion group, which is concurrent validity.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because concurrent validity accounts for the method, as Anne Anastasi's treatment of contrasted groups shows."}}, {"q": "Which of the following test results is based at least partly on Holland's model of occupational themes?", "concept": "The answer is d: the Strong-Campbell Interest Inventory is based at least partly on John Holland's model of occupational themes. Edward Kellogg Strong Jr. (1927) published the Strong Vocational Interest Blank, built by empirical keying: a person's likes and dislikes were compared with those of people already working happily in each occupation, such as engineers or teachers. In 1974 David Campbell revised it as the Strong-Campbell Interest Inventory and organised it around John L. Holland's (1959, set out fully 1973) theory of vocational choice. Holland proposed six personality types and matching work environments, known as RIASEC: Realistic (practical, hands-on), Investigative (scientific), Artistic (creative), Social (helping), Enterprising (persuading, leading) and Conventional (orderly, clerical). He arranged them on a hexagon: neighbouring types are similar, and opposite types such as Realistic and Social are least alike. People are expected to be most satisfied in environments that match their type. The Strong-Campbell reports six General Occupational Themes taken from RIASEC, along with Basic Interest Scales and Occupational Scales; today it is the Strong Interest Inventory. Holland's own Self-Directed Search (1971) also uses RIASEC. Example: Siddharth's Strong-Campbell profile is highest on Investigative and Artistic, so his counsellor suggests fields such as architecture, design research or psychology. Option d is right because it is the only interest inventory among the options, and it explicitly scores Holland's six occupational themes. Exam trap: the other three options are all projective personality tests, which aim at unconscious needs and conflicts, not vocational interests. Also do not confuse Strong's empirical occupational keying with the forced-choice Kuder Preference Record of G. Frederic Kuder (1939).", "traps": {"a": "The Rorschach inkblot test (Hermann Rorschach, 1921) is a projective test in which people describe what they see in ten inkblots. It assesses personality and thought processes, not occupational interests.", "b": "The Blacky Pictures (Gerald Blum, 1950) are projective cartoons of a dog family built around Freud's psychosexual stages. They are psychoanalytic, not based on Holland's themes.", "c": "The Thematic Apperception Test (Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, 1935) asks people to tell stories about ambiguous pictures to reveal needs and press. It is projective and has no link to Holland's model."}}, {"q": "Which type of neurons id called efferent neurons", "concept": "The answer is b: efferent neurons are motor neurons. Neurons are classified by function into three types. Sensory neurons are afferent: they carry information from receptors in the skin, eyes, ears and organs towards the central nervous system (CNS). Motor neurons are efferent: they carry commands away from the CNS out to effectors, the muscles and glands, telling a muscle to contract or a gland to secrete. Interneurons lie wholly inside the brain and spinal cord and connect sensory to motor neurons; they are by far the most numerous. The words come from Latin: afferent means 'carrying towards' and efferent means 'carrying away'. A handy memory aid is SAME: Sensory Afferent, Motor Efferent. Charles Bell (1811) and Francois Magendie (1822) showed that the dorsal roots of spinal nerves carry sensory (afferent) fibres in and the ventral roots carry motor (efferent) fibres out, the Bell-Magendie law. The reflex arc shows all three types in order: receptor, sensory neuron, interneuron in the spinal cord, motor neuron, muscle. Example: Divya touches a hot tawa. Sensory neurons send the pain signal to her spinal cord, an interneuron relays it, and motor (efferent) neurons fire to her arm muscles, so her hand jerks away before she even feels the pain. Option b is right because a motor neuron carries signals away from the CNS to muscles and glands, which is exactly what efferent means. Exam trap: afferent and efferent differ by one letter and are easily swapped. Remember that afferent arrives at the CNS and efferent exits it. Interneurons are neither afferent nor efferent in the usual sense, because they stay inside the CNS.", "traps": {"a": "Sensory neurons are afferent: they carry information from receptors towards the CNS, the opposite direction to efferent neurons.", "c": "Interneurons lie entirely within the brain and spinal cord and link sensory to motor neurons. They neither bring signals in nor send them out, so they are not efferent.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because motor neurons are exactly the efferent neurons, as the Bell-Magendie law shows for the ventral roots."}}, {"q": "Parasympathetic and sympathetic nervous system are a part of –", "concept": "The answer is c: the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems are the two main divisions of the autonomic nervous system (ANS). The nervous system has two major parts. The central nervous system (CNS) is the brain and spinal cord. The peripheral nervous system (PNS) is all the nerves outside them, and it has two branches. The somatic nervous system carries information from the senses and controls voluntary skeletal muscles. The autonomic nervous system controls involuntary functions: heart rate, breathing, digestion, sweating, pupil size and the glands. John Newport Langley (1898) coined the term 'autonomic', meaning self-governing, and later named its parasympathetic division. The sympathetic division prepares the body for action: it speeds the heart, widens the pupils, releases glucose and slows digestion, the 'fight-or-flight' response described by Walter Cannon (1915). Its nerves leave the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord. The parasympathetic division calms the body and conserves energy, 'rest and digest': it slows the heart, narrows the pupils and stimulates digestion. Its nerves leave the brainstem (through cranial nerves such as the vagus) and the sacral spinal cord. Most organs receive both divisions, which usually work in opposition. Example: when a dog suddenly runs at Rohit, his heart pounds and his mouth goes dry (sympathetic); once he is safely home, his heart slows and he feels hungry again (parasympathetic). Option c is right because both systems are divisions of the ANS, which is itself part of the PNS. Exam trap: the somatic system is the other half of the PNS, so students sometimes pick it. Somatic means voluntary skeletal muscle; autonomic means involuntary organs and glands.", "traps": {"a": "The central nervous system is the brain and spinal cord. The sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves and ganglia lie outside it, in the peripheral nervous system, even though centres such as the hypothalamus regulate them.", "b": "The somatic nervous system is the voluntary branch of the PNS, controlling skeletal muscles and carrying sensory input. Sympathetic and parasympathetic belong to the other branch, the autonomic.", "d": "'Brain' is too narrow and anatomically wrong: autonomic nerves run throughout the body, and the brain is part of the CNS. Brain areas such as the hypothalamus control the ANS, but its two divisions are not parts of the brain."}}, {"q": "Which neurotransmitter is responsible for parasympathetic response in the bosy?", "concept": "The answer is a: acetylcholine (ACh) is the neurotransmitter responsible for the parasympathetic response. Autonomic pathways have two neurons in series: a preganglionic neuron leaves the CNS and synapses in a ganglion, and a postganglionic neuron runs on to the target organ. All preganglionic neurons, sympathetic and parasympathetic, release ACh. The divisions differ at the target. Parasympathetic postganglionic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic receptors, so the parasympathetic division is called cholinergic. Most sympathetic postganglionic neurons release norepinephrine (noradrenaline), so the sympathetic division is called adrenergic. The classic evidence is Otto Loewi's (1921) frog heart experiment. He stimulated the vagus nerve, a parasympathetic nerve, of one frog heart, which slowed it, then moved the fluid bathing it to a second heart, which also slowed. A chemical he called Vagusstoff ('vagus substance') had carried the message, and it was soon identified as acetylcholine, whose actions Henry Dale (1914) had already described; the two shared the 1936 Nobel Prize. This was the first proof of chemical transmission between nerve and organ. Example: after a heavy lunch, Aditya feels drowsy and his heart rate falls while his stomach gets busy digesting. Parasympathetic fibres in his vagus nerve are releasing ACh onto his heart and gut. Drugs that block ACh at muscarinic receptors, such as atropine, produce the opposite: a fast heart, dry mouth and dilated pupils. Option a is right because ACh is the transmitter that parasympathetic neurons release at their target organs, as Loewi's 'vagus substance' showed. Exam trap: epinephrine and norepinephrine go with the sympathetic 'fight-or-flight' response, not the parasympathetic one; epinephrine is mainly a hormone from the adrenal medulla. Dopamine and serotonin are mainly brain transmitters for reward, movement and mood.", "traps": {"b": "Dopamine is a brain neurotransmitter for reward, movement and motivation, linked to Parkinson's disease and schizophrenia. It is not the parasympathetic transmitter.", "c": "Serotonin is a transmitter involved in mood, sleep and appetite and is the target of SSRIs. Much of the body's serotonin is in the gut, but it is not the parasympathetic transmitter.", "d": "Epinephrine (adrenaline) is released from the adrenal medulla during sympathetic arousal, and its relative norepinephrine is the main sympathetic postganglionic transmitter. It drives fight-or-flight, the opposite of the parasympathetic response."}}, {"q": "Reticular formation extends from –", "concept": "The answer is c: the reticular formation extends from the hindbrain to the midbrain. The reticular formation is a net-like web of about 100 small nuclei and their connecting fibres (Latin reticulum, 'little net') that runs through the central core of the brainstem: the medulla and pons of the hindbrain and the tegmentum of the midbrain. It has descending and ascending parts. The descending part helps control muscle tone, posture and the cardiac and respiratory reflexes. The ascending part, the reticular activating system (RAS), sends fibres up through the thalamus to the cortex and regulates arousal, wakefulness, attention and consciousness. It filters incoming stimulation, which is why a parent can sleep through traffic noise yet wake at a baby's cry. The classic study is by Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun (1949). When they electrically stimulated the reticular formation of drowsy or lightly anaesthetised cats, their EEG changed from slow, synchronised waves to the fast, desynchronised pattern of alertness. Damage to the RAS causes coma, and general anaesthetics work partly by suppressing it. Example: Sneha is dozing in a late lecture when her name is suddenly called, and her reticular activating system snaps her cortex into alertness. Option c is right because the reticular formation's nuclei lie in the brainstem core, from the medulla through the pons to the midbrain, that is, from hindbrain to midbrain. Exam trap: the RAS projects upwards into the forebrain, so students confuse where its fibres go with where its nuclei sit. Key note: many texts say the reticular formation extends up into the thalamus of the forebrain, so option b has support. The key takes the brainstem core, hindbrain to midbrain, as its extent. The official key stands.", "traps": {"a": "Forebrain to midbrain misses the main bulk of the reticular formation, which lies in the medulla and pons of the hindbrain. The forebrain only receives its ascending projections.", "b": "Hindbrain to forebrain has some support, because the reticular activating system projects up through the thalamus and some texts include thalamic nuclei. But the formation itself is the brainstem core, hindbrain to midbrain.", "d": "Left hemisphere to right hemisphere describes the corpus callosum, the fibre bundle that links the two cerebral hemispheres. The reticular formation runs vertically through the brainstem, not across the cortex."}}, {"q": "Which of the following endocrine gland affects metabolism rate?", "concept": "The answer is d: the thyroid gland controls the body's metabolic rate. The endocrine system is a set of ductless glands that release hormones directly into the bloodstream. The thyroid is a butterfly-shaped gland at the front of the neck, below the larynx. It makes thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), both of which need iodine, and these set the basal metabolic rate: how fast cells burn fuel and produce heat. Edward Calvin Kendall (1914) first isolated thyroxine. The thyroid is run by a feedback chain, the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis: the hypothalamus releases TRH, which tells the pituitary to release TSH, which tells the thyroid to release T3 and T4. Too little thyroid hormone (hypothyroidism) brings sluggishness, weight gain, feeling cold, slowed thinking and low mood, so it can be mistaken for depression; in infants, untreated deficiency causes congenital hypothyroidism (cretinism) with intellectual disability. Too much (hyperthyroidism, as in Graves' disease) brings weight loss, a racing heart, heat intolerance, restlessness and anxiety. Iodine deficiency causes goitre, a swollen thyroid, which is why India iodises salt. Example: Lakshmi has felt tired, cold and low for months and has gained weight. Her psychiatrist orders a thyroid function test before starting antidepressants, and it shows hypothyroidism. Option d is right because thyroid hormones are the main regulators of metabolic rate. Exam trap: the adrenal glands also affect metabolism, since cortisol raises blood sugar, and the hypothalamus starts the thyroid chain, so both are tempting. But the gland whose hormones directly set the metabolic rate is the thyroid. For clinical work, remember that thyroid problems can mimic depression and anxiety.", "traps": {"a": "The adrenal glands release cortisol (from the cortex) and adrenaline (from the medulla) in stress responses. Cortisol mobilises glucose, but the regulator of basal metabolic rate is the thyroid.", "b": "The hypothalamus is a brain structure that controls the pituitary and starts the thyroid chain by releasing TRH. It influences metabolism indirectly, through the thyroid, rather than setting the rate itself.", "c": "The ovaries release oestrogen and progesterone, which regulate the menstrual cycle and female sex characteristics. They are not the main regulator of metabolic rate."}}, {"q": "Who gave the opponent process theory of colour vision?", "concept": "The answer is b: the opponent-process theory of colour vision was given by Ewald Hering (1878). Hering proposed that colour is coded by three opposed pairs of processes: red versus green, blue versus yellow, and black versus white. Each pair works like a seesaw: activity for one member of a pair inhibits the other. This explains facts the earlier trichromatic theory could not. Nobody sees a 'reddish green' or a 'bluish yellow', because both members of a pair cannot be signalled at once. It also explains negative afterimages, in which a fatigued process lets its opponent rebound, and the fact that colour blindness often removes a whole pair, such as red-green. Leo Hurvich and Dorothea Jameson (1957) gave the theory quantitative support, and Russell De Valois (1966) found opponent cells in the lateral geniculate nucleus of monkeys. The modern dual-process view combines both theories: the trichromatic theory of Thomas Young (1802) and Hermann von Helmholtz (1852) holds at the level of the three cone types in the retina, while opponent processing holds in the ganglion cells and the thalamus beyond them. Example: Zoya stares at a bright red circle for 30 seconds, then looks at a white wall and sees a green circle floating there. Her red process has tired, so its opponent, green, briefly dominates. Option b is right because Hering is the author of the opponent-process theory of colour vision. Exam trap: Young and Helmholtz appear in the options because their trichromatic theory is the rival account of colour vision. Also do not confuse Hering's theory with the opponent-process theory of motivation and emotion by Richard Solomon and John Corbit (1974), which explains drug tolerance and withdrawal.", "traps": {"a": "Thomas Young (1802) and Hermann von Helmholtz (1852) proposed the trichromatic theory: three receptor types, now known to be short-, medium- and long-wavelength cones. It is the rival theory, not opponent process.", "c": "George Berkeley (1709), in An Essay Towards a New Theory of Vision, argued that depth perception is learned by associating visual cues with touch and movement. He wrote on space and distance, not colour coding.", "d": "James J. Gibson (1950, 1979) gave the ecological theory of direct perception, with texture gradients and affordances. He did not propose a theory of colour vision."}}, {"q": "Stanley Milgram is a Pioneer in the field of –", "concept": "The answer is a: Stanley Milgram is the pioneer of research on obedience to authority. Obedience is a form of social influence in which a person follows a direct order from someone seen as a legitimate authority. It differs from conformity (going along with a group when nobody gives an order) and from compliance (agreeing to a request from an equal). Stanley Milgram (1963), working at Yale University, told volunteers they were helping with a study of punishment and learning. Each volunteer, the 'teacher', had to give electric shocks of rising strength, from 15 to 450 volts, to a 'learner' (in fact an actor who received no shocks) every time he made a mistake. When the teacher hesitated, the experimenter in a grey lab coat used fixed prods such as 'The experiment requires that you continue'. About 65 per cent of participants went all the way to 450 volts, far more than psychiatrists had predicted. In later variations obedience fell when the experimenter gave orders by telephone, when the learner sat in the same room, and when two other 'teachers' refused. Milgram explained the results through the agentic state: people stop seeing themselves as responsible and act as agents of the authority. The study also started the modern debate on research ethics, because participants suffered real stress. Example: Arjun, a junior nurse, gives a patient a dose he thinks is too high simply because a senior doctor orders it; that is obedience, not conformity. Option a is right because obedience is the topic Milgram's experiments created and defined. Exam trap: examiners place Milgram next to Solomon Asch (1951), who is the pioneer of conformity with his line-judgement study, while Milgram is obedience. Remember 'Asch is the group, Milgram is the order'.", "traps": {"b": "Conformity, changing behaviour to match a group norm without any direct order, was pioneered by Muzafer Sherif (1935) with the autokinetic effect and Solomon Asch (1951) with line judgements, not by Stanley Milgram.", "c": "Compliance, saying yes to a direct request from someone with no authority, is linked with Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser (1966, foot-in-the-door) and Robert Cialdini (1984); Milgram's orders came from an authority figure, which makes them obedience.", "d": "Social learning, learning by watching and imitating models, belongs to Albert Bandura (1961, the Bobo doll studies) and earlier to Neal Miller and John Dollard (1941); it is not Stanley Milgram's field."}}, {"q": "The tendency that overtime the persuasive impact of a high credibility source decreases while the persuasive impact of a low credibility source increases is called", "concept": "The answer is c: the sleeper effect is the delayed rise in the persuasive impact of a message from a low-credibility source, while a high-credibility message loses some of its impact over time. Source credibility, meaning how expert and trustworthy a speaker seems, matters a lot at first: we believe a doctor more than a stranger. But memory for the source and memory for the message fade at different rates. Carl Hovland and Walter Weiss (1951) gave American students identical articles, credited either to a trusted source such as the physicist Robert Oppenheimer or to a distrusted source such as the Soviet newspaper Pravda. Straight after reading, the high-credibility version changed opinions much more. Four weeks later the difference had almost vanished: agreement with the trusted source had dropped and agreement with the distrusted source had risen. The usual explanation is the discounting cue hypothesis. The low-credibility source works as a cue that tells us to discount the message; when that cue is forgotten, or dissociated from the content, the arguments act on their own. Anthony Pratkanis and colleagues (1988) later found the effect is most reliable when the discounting cue comes after a strong message. Example: Kabir sees a forwarded WhatsApp claim about a herbal cure and dismisses it because it came from an unknown group. A month later he remembers the claim but not where it came from, and now he half believes it. Option c is right because the stem describes exactly this crossover over time: high-credibility impact falls while low-credibility impact rises. Exam trap: students pick 'placebo effect' because both involve belief without a real cause, but placebo is improvement after an inactive treatment, not persuasion changing with source credibility over time.", "traps": {"a": "Reciprocity refers to liking people who like us (reciprocal liking) or to the norm of returning favours described by Alvin Gouldner (1960); it says nothing about credibility effects changing over time.", "b": "Social exchange theory, from George Homans (1958) and John Thibaut and Harold Kelley (1959), explains relationships as a weighing of rewards against costs; it is about relationships, not delayed persuasion.", "d": "The placebo effect is improvement after an inert treatment because the person expects to get better; it concerns expectancy in treatment, not high and low credibility sources in persuasion."}}, {"q": "If Sohail like Rajat, Rajat likes movies and Sohail hated movies, the relationship between them is in a state of", "concept": "The answer is b: the triad is in a state of imbalance, because the product of its three relations is negative. Fritz Heider (1946, 1958) proposed balance theory, one of the first consistency theories of attitudes. It describes a triangle of three elements: a person (P), another person (O) and an object or issue (X). Each link is either positive (like, agree) or negative (dislike, disagree). To test a triad, multiply the three signs. If the product is positive, the system is balanced and feels comfortable. If it is negative, the system is imbalanced, it feels tense, and the person is motivated to change something. Here P is Sohail, O is Rajat and X is movies. Sohail likes Rajat (plus), Rajat likes movies (plus) and Sohail hates movies (minus). Plus times plus times minus gives minus, so the triad is imbalanced. Sohail can restore balance in three ways: start to enjoy movies, like Rajat less, or persuade Rajat that movies are a waste of time. Heider held that people usually choose the change that needs the least effort. A quick rule: a triad is balanced when it has zero or two negative links, and imbalanced when it has one or three. For example, if Meera dislikes Tara and Tara dislikes cricket, Meera liking cricket gives two negatives and is balanced, because disagreeing with someone you dislike feels fine. Balance theory led to the congruity theory of Charles Osgood and Percy Tannenbaum (1955) and to the cognitive dissonance theory of Leon Festinger (1957). Option b is right because one negative relation among three makes the sign product negative, which is the definition of imbalance. Exam trap: some students count 'two likes and one dislike' as mostly positive and call it balanced, or invent a middle state; Heider's model has only two states, balanced or imbalanced, decided by the sign product.", "traps": {"a": "Balance would need a positive sign product, for example Sohail also liking movies (three plus signs) or Sohail disliking Rajat (two minus signs); with exactly one negative link the triad cannot be balanced.", "c": "'Semi-balance' is not a state in Fritz Heider's (1958) balance theory; the model is all or none, and the sign product makes every triad either balanced or imbalanced.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because imbalance describes this triad exactly."}}, {"q": "Electra conflict occurs during which stage of development ?", "concept": "The answer is b: the Electra conflict occurs during the phallic stage, roughly ages 3 to 6. Sigmund Freud (1905) proposed that personality develops through psychosexual stages, each centred on a body zone where the libido (sexual energy) seeks pleasure: oral (birth to about 1), anal (about 1 to 3), phallic (about 3 to 6), latency (6 to puberty) and genital (puberty onwards). In the phallic stage the genitals become the focus, and the child forms an unconscious attraction to the opposite-sex parent along with rivalry towards the same-sex parent. For boys Freud called this the Oedipus complex, driven by castration anxiety. For girls he described penis envy: the girl blames her mother, turns towards her father and finally resolves the conflict by identifying with her mother, though in Freud's view less completely than boys do. Carl Gustav Jung (1913) named the girls' version the Electra complex, after the Greek heroine who helps to kill her mother; Freud himself rejected the term and spoke of the female Oedipus complex. Resolving the conflict through identification with the same-sex parent forms the superego and gender identity. Example: four-year-old Riya announces that she will marry her father and gets cross when her mother sits next to him; a Freudian reads this as the Electra phase, expected to fade as Riya starts copying her mother. Option b is right because the Electra and Oedipus conflicts are the defining events of the phallic stage. Exam trap: examiners offer 'genital' because the words sound alike; the genital stage begins at puberty and concerns mature adult sexuality, while the family triangle belongs to the phallic stage. Also note that 'Electra' is Jung's label, not Freud's, a favourite attribution question.", "traps": {"a": "The oral stage (birth to about 1) centres on sucking, feeding and dependence; fixation there gives oral traits, but it involves no rivalry with a parent.", "c": "The genital stage begins at puberty and concerns mature adult sexual relationships; Sigmund Freud placed the Oedipus and Electra conflicts earlier, in the phallic stage.", "d": "The anal stage (about 1 to 3) centres on toilet training and control, linked with anal-retentive and anal-expulsive traits, not with attraction to a parent."}}, {"q": "The zone of proximal development was given by –", "concept": "The answer is c: the zone of proximal development (ZPD) was given by the Russian psychologist Lev Vygotsky. Lev Vygotsky (1896 to 1934) developed sociocultural theory, which holds that higher mental functions begin in social interaction and are then internalised by the child. His ideas reached English readers mainly through Mind in Society (1978). The ZPD is the gap between what a child can do alone (the actual developmental level) and what the child can do with guidance from an adult or a more capable peer (the potential developmental level). Teaching works best inside this zone, on tasks the child cannot yet do alone but can manage with help. If the task is too easy nothing new is learnt; if it is too hard, help cannot bridge the gap. David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976) later coined 'scaffolding' for the support a tutor gives and gradually withdraws as competence grows. Vygotsky also stressed private speech (children talking aloud to guide their own actions) and the role of language and culture in shaping thought. Example: Arjun, aged six, cannot finish a 30-piece jigsaw alone, but when his older sister suggests starting with the edges and sorting pieces by colour, he completes it. A week later he does it by himself. The jigsaw was in his ZPD, and his sister's hints were scaffolding. Option c is right because the ZPD is Vygotsky's central educational concept. Exam trap: Jean Piaget (1936) is the common wrong answer because both theorists studied cognitive development; Piaget saw the child as a lone scientist moving through fixed stages, while Vygotsky stressed social guidance. Also, the word 'scaffolding' is not Vygotsky's own; it came from Wood, Bruner and Ross.", "traps": {"a": "Erik Erikson (1950) proposed eight psychosocial stages, each a crisis such as trust versus mistrust; his theory is about identity and social relationships, not guided learning within a zone.", "b": "Jean Piaget (1936) proposed four stages of cognitive development driven by the child's own exploration through assimilation and accommodation; the ZPD is the social rival to his view, not part of it.", "d": "Sigmund Freud (1905) proposed the psychosexual stages and the id, ego and superego; his theory concerns unconscious drives, not learning with adult help."}}, {"q": "An important feature of cognition during the preoperational stage is –", "concept": "The answer is a: centration is an important feature of cognition in the preoperational stage. Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980) described four stages of cognitive development: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 onwards). In the preoperational stage the child can use symbols (words, images, pretend play) but cannot yet carry out 'operations', which are mental actions that can be reversed. Centration is the tendency to focus on one striking feature of a situation and ignore the others. It explains failure on conservation tasks. In the classic liquid task, water is poured from a short, wide glass into a tall, thin one, and a five-year-old says the tall glass now holds more, because the child centres on height and ignores width. The child also fails because of irreversibility (being unable to pour the water back mentally) and because of a focus on end states rather than on the transformation. Other preoperational features include egocentrism (shown in the three mountains task), animism (believing objects are alive) and transductive reasoning. Example: Meera, aged four, cries that her brother got 'more' biscuit because his was broken into four pieces and hers into two; she centres on the number of pieces, not the total amount. Decentration, attending to several features at once, arrives in the concrete operational stage. Option a is right because centration is a defining limit of preoperational thought. Exam trap: object permanence is the distractor because it is also a famous Piagetian term, but it is the main achievement of the earlier sensorimotor stage, usually secure by about 18 to 24 months, not a feature of preoperational thinking.", "traps": {"b": "Object permanence, knowing that objects still exist when out of sight, is the key achievement of Jean Piaget's sensorimotor stage (birth to 2), not a feature of preoperational thought.", "c": "Primary circular reactions, repeating pleasant actions centred on the infant's own body such as thumb sucking, belong to substage 2 (about 1 to 4 months) of the sensorimotor stage.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because centration is a correct and classic answer."}}, {"q": "Which among the following of Erickson's stage of conflict encompasses physiological revolution", "concept": "The answer is b: identity versus role confusion is the stage that Erik Erikson linked with the 'physiological revolution' of puberty. Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, proposed eight psychosocial stages across the lifespan. Each stage poses a crisis between two poles, and a good resolution adds a virtue to the ego. Stage five, identity versus role confusion, covers adolescence (about 12 to 18 years). Erikson wrote that young people, 'faced with this physiological revolution within them', meaning the rapid growth spurt and sexual maturation of puberty, must work out who they are while also facing the adult tasks ahead. Because body and feelings change so fast, the earlier sense of sameness and continuity is questioned again. The task is to integrate past identifications, new sexual feelings, abilities and social roles into a coherent identity. Success brings the virtue of fidelity; failure brings role confusion, an unclear sense of self and direction. Erikson also described the psychosocial moratorium, a period society allows for trying out roles. James Marcia (1966) later turned these ideas into four identity statuses: diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium and achievement. Example: Kabir, aged 15, has grown 10 centimetres in a year and feels awkward in his new body. He switches from wanting to be a cricketer to an engineer to a musician and keeps asking 'who am I?'. That is the identity crisis. Option b is right because the bodily upheaval of puberty is the trigger Erikson named for the adolescent identity crisis. Exam trap: students pick intimacy versus isolation because it involves sexuality, but that is the young-adult stage of forming close relationships after identity is settled; puberty itself belongs to identity versus role confusion.", "traps": {"a": "Initiative versus guilt is Erik Erikson's third stage (about 3 to 6, the play age), about planning and starting activities; its virtue is purpose, and it comes long before puberty.", "c": "Intimacy versus isolation is the sixth stage (young adulthood), about forming committed close relationships once identity is in place; its virtue is love, not coping with bodily change.", "d": "Industry versus inferiority is the fourth stage (about 6 to 12, school age), about competence in schoolwork and skills; its virtue is competence, and it ends as puberty begins."}}, {"q": "The neonatal reflex wherein the infant's toes automatically spreads apart when the soles of their feet are stimulated is called –", "concept": "The answer is b: the Babinski reflex is the neonatal reflex in which the toes fan out when the sole of the foot is stroked. Newborns arrive with a set of reflexes, automatic responses to specific stimuli controlled mainly by the brainstem and spinal cord. Some are survival reflexes (rooting, sucking, breathing); others are primitive reflexes with no clear present use, which fade as the cerebral cortex matures. The Babinski reflex is named after the French neurologist Joseph Babinski (1896). When the sole is stroked firmly from the heel towards the toes, the big toe bends upwards and the other toes spread like a fan. It is normal until about 12 months, sometimes a little longer, and then gives way to the adult response, in which the toes curl downwards. Its disappearance shows that the corticospinal (pyramidal) tract has matured. So its return in an adult, called a positive Babinski sign, points to damage to the upper motor neurones, for example after a stroke, which makes it one of the most useful bedside tests in neurology. Paediatricians check a whole battery of reflexes, because both absence in infancy and persistence beyond the usual age can signal a nervous system problem. Example: at Meera's 3-month check-up, the paediatrician strokes the sole of her foot and her toes fan out, which is normal. If her 40-year-old uncle showed the same response, the doctor would order a brain scan. Option b is right because spreading of the toes when the sole is stimulated is the textbook description of the Babinski reflex. Exam trap: the grasping reflex is the near miss; in the plantar grasp the toes curl around a finger pressed at the base of the toes, which is the opposite of fanning out.", "traps": {"a": "The rooting reflex is turning the head and opening the mouth when the cheek or corner of the mouth is stroked, which helps the baby find the nipple; it fades by about 3 to 4 months.", "c": "The Moro reflex, described by Ernst Moro (1918), is a startle: after a loud noise or sudden drop the arms fling outwards and then come together as if embracing; it fades by about 4 to 6 months.", "d": "The grasping (palmar) reflex is the fingers closing tightly around an object placed in the palm; its foot version, the plantar grasp, curls the toes, the reverse of the Babinski fan."}}, {"q": "The term 'holophrasis' means", "concept": "The answer is a: holophrasis means a toddler's use of a single word to express a complete thought. 'Holophrase' comes from Greek roots meaning 'whole phrase'. Language develops in a predictable order: cooing at about 2 months (vowel-like sounds), babbling at about 6 months (consonant-vowel strings such as 'ba-ba'), then the first words at about 12 months. In this one-word stage, roughly 12 to 18 months, a single word does the work of a whole sentence. Its meaning depends on context, tone of voice and gesture. 'Milk' can mean 'I want milk', 'The milk has spilled' or 'That is milk', and parents read the intended meaning from the situation. Around 18 to 24 months comes what Roger Brown (1973) called telegraphic speech: two-word or short utterances that keep the content words and drop the function words, like a telegram ('more juice', 'daddy go'). By 2 to 3 years children start overregularising grammar rules ('two foots', 'I goed'). The holophrase stage also shows that comprehension runs ahead of production: toddlers understand many more words than they can say. Example: 14-month-old Kabir points at the door and says 'Out!', meaning 'I want to go outside now'. The single word carries a full, complete thought. Option a is right because the defining feature of a holophrase is one word standing for a complete idea or sentence. Exam trap: the four options differ on two details, a single word versus a sentence, and a complete versus an incomplete thought. A sentence expressing a complete thought is simply mature speech, and 'incomplete thought' reverses the point, since the holophrase packs a whole idea into one word.", "traps": {"b": "Using a full sentence to express a complete thought is ordinary mature speech, reached well after the one-word and telegraphic stages; it is not holophrasis.", "c": "This keeps the single word but reverses the meaning: the whole point of a holophrase is that one word carries a complete thought, not a fragment of one.", "d": "A sentence expressing an incomplete thought describes no recognised stage of language development; it changes both defining features of the holophrase."}}, {"q": "Who gave the learned helpnessness theory of depression?", "concept": "The answer is d: Martin Seligman gave the learned helplessness theory of depression. Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967) placed dogs in a harness and gave them electric shocks. One group could stop the shock by pressing a panel; a yoked group received identical shocks but had no control; a third group received none. Later, in a shuttle box, the dogs could escape shock by jumping a low barrier. Most of the yoked dogs did not even try; they lay down and whimpered. The key was not the pain but the lack of control: the animals had learned that their actions did not matter. Seligman (1975) proposed that human depression works the same way, since helpless dogs showed passivity, weight loss and low motivation, much like depressed people. The original model could not explain why only some people become depressed, or why depressed people blame themselves. So Lyn Abramson, Martin Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) produced the attributional reformulation: people who explain bad events by internal ('it is me'), stable ('it will last') and global ('it ruins everything') causes are most at risk. This later grew into the hopelessness theory of Lyn Abramson, Gerald Metalsky and Lauren Alloy (1989). Seligman went on to study learned optimism and, in 1998, launched positive psychology. Example: Riya fails three maths tests despite studying hard, decides 'I am stupid, I will always fail, I fail at everything', and stops studying altogether; that pattern is learned helplessness. Option d is right because Seligman's dog experiments and his model of depression created the concept. Exam trap: Albert Ellis (1955) is a tempting wrong answer because he also wrote about the thinking of depressed people, but Ellis gave rational emotive behaviour therapy and irrational beliefs, not learned helplessness.", "traps": {"a": "John Dollard, with Leonard Doob, Neal Miller, Orval Hobart Mowrer and Robert Sears (1939), gave the frustration-aggression hypothesis; it explains aggression, not depression.", "b": "Neal Miller is known for approach-avoidance conflict and, with John Dollard (1950), for translating psychoanalysis into learning theory, and later for biofeedback; he did not propose learned helplessness.", "c": "Albert Ellis (1955) founded rational emotive behaviour therapy and the ABC model of irrational beliefs; his theory concerns faulty thinking, not learned lack of control."}}, {"q": "A key feature of which is that the therapist might challenge an irrational belief that the client has, helping him/her recognize these beliefs and change them into more rational ones.", "concept": "The answer is b: actively challenging a client's irrational beliefs and turning them into rational ones is the key feature of rational-emotive therapy. Albert Ellis (1955) founded it as rational therapy, renamed it rational-emotive therapy in 1961 and rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT) in 1993. Its core is the ABC model. A is the activating event, B is the belief about the event, and C is the emotional and behavioural consequence. Ellis argued that it is B, not A, that causes C. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands, such as 'I must be loved by everyone', which he called 'musturbation'; they lead to awfulising ('this is the worst thing that could happen') and low frustration tolerance ('I can't stand it'). Therapy adds D, disputing, and E, the effective new belief. The therapist is direct and even confrontational, asking logical, empirical and practical questions such as 'Where is the evidence that you must be liked by everyone?' Homework and shame-attacking exercises help the client practise the new beliefs. Example: Arjun panics before presentations. A: his manager asks him to present. B: 'I must perform perfectly or I am worthless.' C: anxiety and avoidance. His therapist disputes the 'must' until Arjun reaches E: 'I would like to do well, but a mistake does not make me worthless.' Option b is right because 'irrational beliefs' and their direct disputation are the trademark of Ellis's approach. Exam trap: cognitive-behaviour therapy is the near miss, because Aaron Beck's (1967) cognitive therapy also tests thoughts; Beck targets automatic thoughts and cognitive distortions through gentle, collaborative Socratic questioning, while 'irrational beliefs' challenged head-on is Ellis's language. Key note: Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy and CBT in general also dispute unhelpful thoughts, so option c has some support. But 'irrational beliefs' is Albert Ellis's own term and the hallmark of REBT, so the official key b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Behaviour therapy, built on the conditioning work of Joseph Wolpe (1958) and others, changes behaviour directly through exposure, reinforcement and similar techniques; it does not centre on disputing beliefs.", "c": "Cognitive-behaviour therapy, especially Aaron Beck's (1967) cognitive therapy, also examines thoughts, but it targets automatic thoughts and distortions collaboratively; 'irrational beliefs' is Albert Ellis's hallmark term, which points to REBT.", "d": "Psychoanalysis, founded by Sigmund Freud (1896), seeks insight into unconscious conflicts through free association and interpretation; the analyst does not argue with the client's conscious beliefs."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a range of neurodevelopmental disorders characterized by impairment in social skills and communication skills as well as by repetitive behaviours ?", "concept": "The answer is c: autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is the neurodevelopmental condition defined by impaired social and communication skills together with repetitive behaviours. Neurodevelopmental disorders begin in the developmental period, often before school age, and include intellectual disability, communication disorders, ASD, ADHD, specific learning disorder and motor disorders. Leo Kanner (1943) first described 'early infantile autism' in children who were aloof and insisted on sameness, and Hans Asperger (1944) described more verbal children with similar social difficulties. DSM-5 (2013) merged autistic disorder, Asperger's disorder, childhood disintegrative disorder and pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified into one spectrum. Diagnosis needs two domains. First, persistent deficits in social communication and interaction: poor back-and-forth conversation, reduced eye contact and gestures, and difficulty making and keeping friends. Second, restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests or activities: hand-flapping, lining up toys, insistence on routines, intense narrow interests, and over- or under-reaction to sensory input. Severity is rated at three levels of support needed. 'Spectrum' means the picture ranges from a non-verbal child with intellectual disability to a fluent, highly able adult. Example: four-year-old Kabir rarely looks at his parents, does not point to share interest, repeats lines from TV adverts and screams if his toy cars are moved out of their row. Features in both domains suggest ASD. Option c is right because the stem lists the two DSM-5 domains of autism, social-communication deficits plus repetitive behaviours, within the neurodevelopmental group. Exam trap: ADHD is also neurodevelopmental and children with it can struggle socially, but its core is inattention and hyperactivity-impulsivity, not repetitive behaviour. OCD has repetitive acts, but they are driven by obsessions and OCD is not a neurodevelopmental disorder.", "traps": {"a": "ADHD is a neurodevelopmental disorder, but its defining features are inattention and hyperactivity-impulsivity; social problems are a by-product, and repetitive behaviours are not among its criteria.", "b": "Manic depressive psychosis is Emil Kraepelin's (1899) old name for what is now bipolar disorder, a mood disorder of manic and depressive episodes, not a neurodevelopmental condition.", "d": "OCD involves repetitive compulsions, but they are driven by anxiety-provoking obsessions; it sits in the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter and has no core social-communication deficit."}}, {"q": "The term schizophrenia was coined by", "concept": "The answer is a: the term schizophrenia was coined by Eugen Bleuler. The Swiss psychiatrist Eugen Bleuler (1857 to 1939) introduced the word in a 1908 lecture and set it out fully in his 1911 book Dementia Praecox or the Group of Schizophrenias. Before him, Emil Kraepelin (1893, 1899) had grouped several pictures under 'dementia praecox' (premature dementia), stressing early onset and a deteriorating course. Bleuler disagreed on two points: not every patient deteriorates, and onset is not always early. He built 'schizophrenia' from the Greek schizein (to split) and phren (mind). The split he meant is a splitting of psychic functions, so that thought, emotion and behaviour lose their normal connection; it does not mean a split or multiple personality. He also wrote of a 'group' of schizophrenias, an early form of today's spectrum idea. Bleuler's fundamental symptoms are remembered as the four As: loosening of associations, disturbed affect, ambivalence and autism (withdrawal into a private inner world). He regarded hallucinations and delusions as accessory symptoms. Example: Rohan's relatives say he has a 'split personality' because of his schizophrenia. The clinician corrects them: Bleuler's split is between Rohan's thoughts and feelings, as when he smiles while describing his father's death. Option a is right because both the word and the idea of a group of schizophrenias come from Bleuler. Exam trap: Emil Kraepelin is the classic distractor because he described the illness first, as dementia praecox; Kraepelin named and classified it, and Bleuler renamed it. Also remember that 'split mind' never means dissociative identity disorder.", "traps": {"b": "David Rosenhan (1973) wrote 'On being sane in insane places', in which healthy pseudopatients were admitted with a diagnosis of schizophrenia; he criticised psychiatric diagnosis, he did not name the disorder.", "c": "Thomas Szasz (1961) wrote The Myth of Mental Illness, arguing that mental illness is a label for problems in living; he was a critic of psychiatry, not the coiner of the term.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because Eugen Bleuler is the correct and well-known answer."}}, {"q": "False beliefs, discordant with reality that are maintained in spite of string evidence to the contrary are called –", "concept": "The answer is b: false beliefs that clash with reality and are kept despite strong evidence to the contrary are called delusions. A delusion is a disorder of thought content, not of perception. DSM-5 (2013) defines delusions as fixed beliefs that are not amenable to change in the light of conflicting evidence. Karl Jaspers (1913), in General Psychopathology, gave three classic criteria: the belief is held with extraordinary conviction (certainty), it cannot be changed by experience or argument (incorrigibility), and its content is false or impossible. A further rule is cultural: a belief shared by the person's culture or religion, such as belief in karma or the evil eye, is not a delusion. Common types are persecutory (being plotted against), grandiose (special powers or identity), referential (ordinary events carry a special personal message), erotomanic (someone famous is in love with the person), nihilistic (organs are rotting or the world has ended) and somatic. Delusions are called bizarre when they are clearly impossible, such as aliens removing organs without leaving a scar. They are a positive symptom of schizophrenia and also occur in delusional disorder, mania and psychotic depression. Example: Vikram insists his neighbours have hidden cameras in his ceiling fan to read his thoughts. His family takes the fan apart to show him there is nothing inside, but he says the cameras were moved during the night. The belief is false, fixed and resistant to evidence. Option b is right because the stem gives the textbook definition of a delusion. Exam trap: hallucinations are the near miss because both are psychotic positive symptoms; a hallucination is a false perception (hearing a voice when no one is there), while a delusion is a false belief.", "traps": {"a": "Hallucinations are perceptions without an external stimulus, such as hearing voices, that feel like real sensory experiences; they are disorders of perception, not of belief.", "c": "Hypomania is a milder form of mania lasting at least 4 consecutive days, with raised mood, energy and less need for sleep but no psychotic features; it is a mood state, not a false belief.", "d": "Agoraphobia is fear and avoidance of situations where escape might be hard, such as crowds, public transport or being outside home alone; it is an anxiety disorder, not a false belief."}}, {"q": "Tourette's disorder is characterized by", "concept": "The answer is c: Tourette's disorder is characterised by both multiple motor tics and one or more vocal tics. A tic is a sudden, rapid, recurrent, non-rhythmic movement or vocalisation. Motor tics range from simple (eye blinking, shoulder shrugging, grimacing) to complex (touching, jumping, copying other people's gestures). Vocal or phonic tics range from simple (throat clearing, sniffing, grunting) to complex (repeating words, echolalia and, in a minority, coprolalia, the involuntary utterance of obscenities). The French physician Georges Gilles de la Tourette (1885) described the syndrome. DSM-5 (2013) criteria for Tourette's disorder are: both multiple motor tics and one or more vocal tics have been present at some time during the illness, though not necessarily at the same time; the tics may wax and wane but have persisted for more than 1 year since the first tic; onset is before age 18; and the tics are not due to a substance or another medical condition. If a child has only motor or only vocal tics for over a year, the diagnosis is persistent (chronic) motor or vocal tic disorder; tics present for less than a year give provisional tic disorder. Tics are often preceded by a premonitory urge, can be held back for a short time, and commonly occur with ADHD and OCD. Habit reversal training is a first-line behavioural treatment. Example: eight-year-old Aarav blinks hard and jerks his neck, and several times an hour makes sniffing and barking sounds; this has gone on for 18 months. With both kinds of tic for over a year, he meets the criteria for Tourette's. Option c is right because Tourette's requires the combination of multiple motor tics and at least one vocal tic. Exam trap: coprolalia is the famous media image of Tourette's, but it occurs in only a minority and is not required for the diagnosis.", "traps": {"a": "Multiple motor tics alone, lasting over a year, give persistent (chronic) motor tic disorder; Tourette's also needs at least one vocal tic.", "b": "One or more vocal tics alone give persistent (chronic) vocal tic disorder; Tourette's also requires multiple motor tics.", "d": "Repetitive compulsions belong to OCD: purposeful acts done to reduce the anxiety of an obsession, whereas tics are sudden and largely involuntary."}}, {"q": "'Flat affect', which is a characteristic of schizophrenia is –", "concept": "The answer is d: flat affect means virtually no signs of affective expression. Affect is the outward expression of emotion that an observer sees in the face, voice and gestures; mood is the inner feeling the person reports. Clinicians grade reduced affect by degree. Restricted (constricted) affect shows a narrower range of emotion than usual. Blunted affect is a severe reduction in the intensity of expression. Flat affect is the extreme end: almost no expression at all, with an immobile face, little eye contact, few gestures and a monotonous voice. Flat affect is a negative symptom of schizophrenia, part of what DSM-5 (2013) calls diminished emotional expression, alongside alogia (poverty of speech), avolition, anhedonia and asociality. Eugen Bleuler (1911) counted disturbed affect among his fundamental four As. Negative symptoms predict poorer functioning and respond less well to medication than positive symptoms, and Nancy Andreasen (1982) introduced the Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms (SANS) to rate them. Flat affect does not always mean the person feels nothing inside; many report emotions they cannot show. Example: when Sunil is told his brother has been hurt in an accident, his face stays still and his voice keeps the same even tone he uses for the weather. He shows no visible emotion at all, which is flat affect. Option d is right because 'virtually no signs of affect expression' is the standard definition of flat affect. Exam trap: option a describes blunted affect, the step just before flat on the same scale; blunted is a severe reduction, flat is a near absence. Option c, emotion that does not match what the person is saying, is inappropriate affect, such as laughing while describing a death.", "traps": {"a": "A severe reduction in the intensity of affect expression is blunted affect; emotion is still visible, only weakened, whereas flat affect is a near total absence.", "b": "A phase of poor adjustment and declining functioning describes the prodromal (or premorbid) phase of schizophrenia, a stage of the illness, not a kind of affect.", "c": "Affect that clashes with the content of speech or ideation, such as giggling about a tragedy, is inappropriate (incongruous) affect, a disorganised symptom, not flat affect."}}, {"q": "Sometimes, a person with schizophrenia may invent new words. These new words are called –", "concept": "The answer is b: new words invented by a person with schizophrenia are called neologisms. The term comes from the Greek neos (new) and logos (word). Neologisms are a disorder of the form of thought and speech: the person creates a word that has no meaning in the shared language, or gives an ordinary word a private meaning, and uses it as though everyone should understand. They often arise by fusing two words or through sound associations. They are a sign of formal thought disorder, along with derailment (loosening of associations), tangentiality, incoherence or word salad, clang associations and thought blocking. In DSM-5 (2013) these come under disorganised speech, one of the positive symptoms. Eugen Bleuler (1911) saw loosening of associations as fundamental to schizophrenia, and neologisms are one visible form of that loosening. Neologisms also occur in Wernicke's (fluent) aphasia after damage to the left temporal lobe, where speech is fluent but empty and full of made-up words; there the cause is a brain lesion, not psychosis. Young children invent words in play too, but that is normal and not a symptom. Example: Ankit tells his psychologist that his neighbour is sending 'glorbitrons' into his room through the wall. Asked what a glorbitron is, he says it is obvious and everyone knows. The word is his own invention, a neologism. Option b is right because neologism is the exact technical term for a newly coined word in psychotic speech. Exam trap: catatonia and blunting are real schizophrenia terms, which makes them tempting, but catatonia is a motor syndrome and blunting is reduced emotional expression; neither concerns invented words.", "traps": {"a": "Blunting (blunted affect) is a marked reduction in the intensity of emotional expression, a negative symptom; it concerns emotion, not language.", "c": "'Prodromal' refers to the prodromal phase, the period of gradual decline before the active psychotic phase; it is a stage of the illness, not a kind of word.", "d": "Catatonia, first described by Karl Ludwig Kahlbaum (1874), is a motor syndrome of stupor, posturing, waxy flexibility, mutism or excitement, not word invention."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not true about the diagnosis of major depressive disorder", "concept": "The answer is a: 'at least one week' of depressed mood is not true, because DSM-5 requires at least two weeks for a major depressive episode. DSM-5 (2013) diagnoses major depressive disorder (MDD) when a major depressive episode is present. That needs five or more of nine symptoms, present nearly every day during the same two-week period and marking a change from previous functioning, and at least one of the five must be either (1) depressed mood or (2) markedly diminished interest or pleasure in all, or almost all, activities (anhedonia). The other symptoms are (3) significant weight or appetite change, (4) insomnia or hypersomnia, (5) psychomotor agitation or retardation, (6) fatigue or loss of energy, (7) feelings of worthlessness or excessive or inappropriate guilt, (8) poor concentration or indecisiveness, and (9) recurrent thoughts of death or suicide. The symptoms must cause clinically significant distress or impairment, must not be due to a substance or medical condition, and there must never have been a manic or hypomanic episode. So options b, c and d are all true: b is criterion 2, c combines criteria 3 and 4, and d is criterion 7. Only a is false, because it halves the duration. Example: after losing her job, Neha feels low, sleeps badly and stops enjoying music for nine days, then gradually recovers. However painful, nine days does not meet the two-week rule, so this is not a major depressive episode. Option a is right because 'at least one week' is the wrong duration; the correct figure is at least two weeks. Exam trap: one week is the duration for a manic episode (or any length if hospitalisation is needed), four days is for hypomania and two years is for persistent depressive disorder; examiners swap these numbers between mood disorders.", "traps": {"b": "True: markedly diminished interest or pleasure in all, or almost all, activities (anhedonia) is DSM-5 criterion 2 and one of the two core symptoms, so it cannot be the false statement.", "c": "True: significant appetite or weight change (criterion 3) and insomnia or hypersomnia (criterion 4) are both listed DSM-5 symptoms of a major depressive episode.", "d": "True: feelings of worthlessness or excessive or inappropriate guilt is DSM-5 criterion 7, a classic cognitive symptom of depression."}}, {"q": "Which disorder was formerly known as 'Dysthymia'?", "concept": "The answer is d: persistent depressive disorder is the disorder formerly known as dysthymia. 'Dysthymia' comes from Greek words meaning 'bad state of mind'. DSM-III (1980) and DSM-IV (1994) listed dysthymic disorder, a chronic, milder depression lasting years. DSM-5 (2013) merged dysthymic disorder with chronic major depressive disorder into a single diagnosis, persistent depressive disorder (PDD), because research found the two hard to separate and similar in course and treatment response. The criteria are depressed mood for most of the day, on more days than not, for at least two years in adults (one year in children and adolescents, in whom mood can be irritable), plus at least two of six symptoms: poor appetite or overeating, insomnia or hypersomnia, low energy, low self-esteem, poor concentration or indecisiveness, and hopelessness. During those two years the person has never been free of symptoms for more than two months at a time. Specifiers record whether full major depressive episodes also occur; when a major depressive episode sits on top of chronic low mood, older texts call it 'double depression'. ICD-11 still uses the name dysthymic disorder. Example: Ramesh, 34, says he has felt 'grey' for as long as he can remember: tired, low in confidence and pessimistic, though he keeps working. It has lasted many years with no break longer than a few weeks. That is persistent depressive disorder. Option d is right because PDD is the renamed and widened home of dysthymia in DSM-5. Exam trap: cyclothymic disorder is the classic distractor because its name looks similar and it also lasts at least two years; but cyclothymia swings between hypomanic and depressive symptoms, while dysthymia is low mood only, with no highs.", "traps": {"a": "Cyclothymic disorder is at least two years (one year in youth) of fluctuating hypomanic and depressive symptoms that never meet full episode criteria; it is a bipolar-related disorder, not the old dysthymia.", "b": "Bipolar I disorder requires at least one full manic episode, lasting a week or needing hospitalisation; it has never been called dysthymia.", "c": "Bipolar II disorder requires at least one hypomanic episode and at least one major depressive episode, with no full mania; its highs rule out dysthymia, which is chronic low mood only."}}, {"q": "Saurabh mistakenly believes that his upper body is misshapen and ugly. He is likely suffering from –", "concept": "The answer is b: Saurabh's mistaken belief that his upper body is misshapen and ugly points to body dysmorphic disorder (BDD). BDD is a preoccupation with one or more perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance that are not observable, or look only slight, to other people. The Italian psychiatrist Enrico Morselli (1891) first called it dysmorphophobia. DSM-5 (2013) moved BDD from the somatoform disorders into the new obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter because, like OCD, it combines intrusive preoccupation with repetitive behaviour. The criteria are: the preoccupation; at some point, repetitive behaviours (mirror checking, excessive grooming, skin picking, reassurance seeking) or mental acts (comparing one's looks with others') in response to the concern; clinically significant distress or impairment; and a concern not better explained by worry about weight or body fat in an eating disorder. A muscle dysmorphia specifier covers the belief that one's build is too small or not muscular enough, common in men who lift weights. An insight specifier runs from good insight to absent insight (delusional beliefs), which is relevant because Saurabh 'mistakenly believes'. People with BDD often seek cosmetic surgery, which rarely helps; CBT with exposure and response prevention, and SSRIs, are the evidence-based treatments. Example: Saurabh spends two hours a day checking his chest and shoulders in mirrors, wears baggy clothes and avoids the gym changing room, although his friends see nothing wrong. Option b is right because a preoccupation with an imagined or slight flaw in appearance is the defining feature of BDD. Exam trap: hoarding disorder and trichotillomania sit in the same DSM-5 chapter as BDD, which makes them tempting, but they concern possessions and hair pulling, not beliefs about how one looks.", "traps": {"a": "Hoarding disorder is persistent difficulty discarding possessions, whatever their value, leading to cluttered living spaces; it is about objects, not appearance.", "c": "Trichotillomania (hair-pulling disorder) is recurrent pulling out of one's own hair despite repeated attempts to stop; it is a body-focused repetitive behaviour, not a belief about a flaw.", "d": "Generalised anxiety disorder is excessive, hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters for at least six months; it is not focused on one imagined defect in appearance."}}, {"q": "Lithium is mostly used for treating –", "concept": "The answer is c: lithium is mostly used for treating bipolar disorder. Lithium is a simple salt, usually given as lithium carbonate, and it is the classic mood stabiliser. The Australian psychiatrist John Cade (1949) noticed that lithium made guinea pigs calm and then reported that it settled manic patients. The Danish psychiatrist Mogens Schou (1954) confirmed the effect in a controlled trial and later showed that lithium prevents relapse. Today it is used to treat acute mania and, above all, as maintenance treatment to prevent future manic and depressive episodes in bipolar disorder. It is also the mood stabiliser with the strongest evidence that it lowers suicide risk. Its exact mechanism is still debated; proposed actions include effects on second-messenger systems (inositol depletion) and inhibition of the enzyme GSK-3. Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index: the useful blood level is about 0.6 to 1.2 mEq/L, and toxicity begins just above it, with coarse tremor, vomiting, diarrhoea, ataxia and confusion. Patients therefore need regular blood tests, plus checks of kidney and thyroid function, because long-term use can cause hypothyroidism and kidney problems. Dehydration and some drugs raise lithium levels. Other mood stabilisers include valproate, carbamazepine and lamotrigine. Example: Priya, 28, has had two manic episodes with sleepless nights and reckless spending. Her psychiatrist starts lithium, checks her blood level every few months and keeps her on it for years to prevent relapse. Option c is right because bipolar disorder is lithium's main and best-established use. Exam trap: lithium is sometimes added to an antidepressant in stubborn unipolar depression (augmentation), or used in schizoaffective disorder, which can tempt students towards other options; its primary use remains bipolar disorder.", "traps": {"a": "Schizophrenia is treated mainly with antipsychotics, from chlorpromazine to newer drugs such as risperidone and clozapine; lithium is not a first-line treatment for it.", "b": "OCD is treated with SSRIs such as fluoxetine, the tricyclic clomipramine, and exposure and response prevention, not with lithium.", "d": "Somatoform (now somatic symptom) disorders are treated mainly with CBT and sometimes antidepressants; lithium has no main role in them."}}, {"q": "The most successful treatment technique that can be used for treating phobias is", "concept": "The answer is a: systematic desensitisation is the most successful technique among these options for treating phobias. The South African psychiatrist Joseph Wolpe (1958), in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, built it on the principle of reciprocal inhibition: two incompatible responses cannot occur at the same time, so a relaxed body cannot also be anxious. It is a form of counterconditioning, replacing the conditioned fear response with relaxation. The method has three steps. First, the client learns deep muscle relaxation, based on the progressive relaxation of Edmund Jacobson (1938). Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, a list of feared situations ranked from least to most frightening, often rated in subjective units of distress (SUDS). Third, the relaxed client imagines each item, starting at the bottom, and moves up only when an item no longer causes anxiety. An early forerunner was Mary Cover Jones (1924), who cured a boy called Peter of his fear of rabbits by bringing a rabbit gradually closer while he ate. Example: Kavya fears dogs. Her hierarchy begins with the word 'dog', moves to photos, then a dog behind a fence, and ends with patting a neighbour's dog. She relaxes at each step until she can stay calm. Modern research shows that real-life (in vivo) exposure is usually even more powerful, and graded exposure keeps the hierarchy idea; among these four options, though, systematic desensitisation is clearly the best answer. Option a is right because systematic desensitisation was designed specifically for phobic anxiety and has strong evidence behind it. Exam trap: electroconvulsive therapy is offered as a 'powerful' treatment, but it is used for severe depression and catatonia, not phobias, and psychoanalysis treats a phobia as a symptom of hidden conflict, with weak evidence.", "traps": {"b": "Electroconvulsive therapy, introduced by Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini (1938), induces a brief seizure under anaesthesia and is used for severe or treatment-resistant depression, mania and catatonia, not phobias.", "c": "Psychoanalysis, as in Sigmund Freud's (1909) Little Hans case, treats a phobia as anxiety displaced from an unconscious conflict and seeks insight; it is slow and has weak evidence for phobias.", "d": "Transactional analysis, founded by Eric Berne (1961), analyses Parent, Adult and Child ego states and the 'games' people play; it is not a fear-reduction technique."}}, {"q": "Acute schizophrenic episode most likely can be treated using which of the following drug?", "concept": "The answer is b: chlorpromazine, an antipsychotic, is the drug most likely to be used for an acute schizophrenic episode. Chlorpromazine was synthesised in 1950 at the French firm Rhone-Poulenc as an antihistamine. The surgeon Henri Laborit (1952) noticed that it made patients calm and indifferent before surgery, and the psychiatrists Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker (1952) reported that it reduced agitation, hallucinations and delusions in psychotic patients in Paris. It was the first antipsychotic and it transformed psychiatry: mental hospital populations fell sharply in the following decades. Chlorpromazine is a phenothiazine and a typical (first-generation) antipsychotic. Its main action is blocking dopamine D2 receptors, especially in the mesolimbic pathway, which reduces positive symptoms. Arvid Carlsson (1963) linked antipsychotic action to dopamine, supporting the dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia. Blocking D2 receptors in other pathways causes side effects: extrapyramidal symptoms (parkinsonism, acute dystonia, akathisia), tardive dyskinesia after long use, raised prolactin, sedation and, rarely, neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Newer atypical drugs such as risperidone, olanzapine and clozapine cause fewer motor effects but more weight gain. Example: Rahul, 22, is brought to hospital hearing voices that threaten him and refusing food because he thinks it is poisoned. He is started on an antipsychotic such as chlorpromazine (today often a newer agent), and his positive symptoms settle over the following weeks. Option b is right because chlorpromazine is the prototype antipsychotic for acute psychosis. Exam trap: L-dopa is tempting because it also acts on dopamine, but it raises dopamine to treat Parkinson's disease and can actually trigger psychotic symptoms, the opposite of what an antipsychotic does.", "traps": {"a": "Diphenylhydantoin (phenytoin), introduced by Houston Merritt and Tracy Putnam (1938), is an anticonvulsant used to control epileptic seizures; it does not treat psychosis.", "c": "MAO inhibitors, such as phenelzine, are antidepressants that block the enzyme monoamine oxidase and so raise monoamine levels; they can worsen psychosis rather than treat it.", "d": "L-dopa (levodopa) is a dopamine precursor used in Parkinson's disease; raising dopamine can provoke hallucinations and paranoia, the reverse of an antipsychotic effect."}}, {"q": "The technique used to extinguish fear by forcing clients to remain in anxiety arousing situation is called –", "concept": "The answer is b: flooding is the technique that extinguishes fear by keeping the client in the anxiety-arousing situation until the fear fades. Flooding is a form of exposure therapy. The client is exposed, usually in real life (in vivo), to the most feared stimulus at full intensity, all at once, and is prevented from escaping. It rests on classical extinction: if the conditioned stimulus (the feared object) appears again and again without the feared outcome, the conditioned fear response weakens. It also blocks avoidance. In the two-factor theory of Orval Hobart Mowrer (1947), fears are learnt by classical conditioning and kept alive by operant avoidance, because each escape is rewarded by relief, and that relief stops the person from ever learning that the danger is not real. When escape is prevented, anxiety rises to a peak and then falls through habituation, and the client learns that nothing terrible happens. Sessions are long, often hours, because ending while anxiety is still high could strengthen the fear. A related method, implosive therapy (Thomas Stampfl, 1967), floods the client in imagination with exaggerated scenes. Flooding is quicker than graded exposure but more distressing, so it needs full informed consent. Example: Rohan is terrified of lifts. With his therapist he rides a lift up and down a tall building for two hours. His panic peaks in the first 20 minutes, then slowly subsides until he feels bored. Option b is right because keeping the client in the feared situation with no escape is the definition of flooding. Exam trap: students confuse flooding with systematic desensitisation; both are exposure-based, but desensitisation is gradual and paired with relaxation, while flooding starts at the top of the hierarchy with no relaxation.", "traps": {"a": "Time-out (time out from positive reinforcement) is an operant technique, a form of negative punishment, that removes a child from a rewarding setting to reduce misbehaviour; it does not involve fear.", "c": "Inhibition here points to Joseph Wolpe's (1958) reciprocal inhibition, the principle behind systematic desensitisation, in which relaxation blocks anxiety; it does not keep the client in a highly anxious situation.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because flooding is the exact name of the technique described."}}, {"q": "A primary characteristic of catatonia in schizophrenic patient is –", "concept": "The answer is d: the primary characteristic of catatonia is motor abnormality. Catatonia was first described by Karl Ludwig Kahlbaum (1874) as a syndrome of stupor, posturing and excitement. Emil Kraepelin (1899) folded it into dementia praecox, and for decades catatonic schizophrenia was a subtype. DSM-5 (2013) dropped the schizophrenia subtypes and made catatonia a specifier that can be attached to schizophrenia, mood disorders or medical conditions. It requires three or more of twelve psychomotor signs: stupor (no activity and no response), catalepsy (passively holding a posture against gravity), waxy flexibility (slight, even resistance when the examiner moves the limbs, which then stay where placed), mutism, negativism (opposing or not responding to instructions), posturing, mannerism, stereotypy (repetitive, non-goal-directed movement), agitation not influenced by outside stimuli, grimacing, echolalia (repeating others' words) and echopraxia (copying others' movements). So catatonia can look like extreme stillness or like purposeless excitement; both are disturbances of movement. It can be life-threatening if the person stops eating or drinking, and it often responds dramatically to benzodiazepines such as lorazepam, and to electroconvulsive therapy. Example: Sunita, who has schizophrenia, has stood by the window holding one arm in the air for hours. When a nurse lowers the arm, it stays wherever it is put, and Sunita does not speak. These are catalepsy, waxy flexibility and mutism. Option d is right because every defining sign of catatonia is a motor (psychomotor) abnormality. Exam trap: hallucinations, paranoia and delusions are all real features of schizophrenia, so each sounds plausible, but they are positive symptoms of perception and thought content and define other presentations, such as the old paranoid subtype, not catatonia.", "traps": {"a": "Auditory hallucinations, hearing voices with no source, are a positive symptom of schizophrenia, a disturbance of perception rather than of movement.", "b": "Paranoia, suspicious beliefs of being persecuted, characterised the old paranoid subtype of schizophrenia, a picture quite different from catatonia's motor disturbance.", "c": "Delusional thinking is a disorder of thought content and a positive symptom of schizophrenia; it can occur alongside catatonia but is not its defining feature."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a personality disorder according to DSM-5", "concept": "The answer is a: 'impulsive' is not a personality disorder according to DSM-5. A personality disorder is an enduring pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from the expectations of the person's culture, is pervasive and inflexible, begins in adolescence or early adulthood, is stable over time and causes distress or impairment. DSM-5 (2013) lists ten specific personality disorders in three clusters. Cluster A, odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal. Cluster B, dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic. Cluster C, anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive. So options b, c and d (antisocial, borderline and narcissistic) are all genuine DSM-5 personality disorders, all from Cluster B, and 'impulsive' is the odd one out. Impulsivity is a trait, and it appears as a criterion within borderline and antisocial personality disorders, but it is not a diagnosis of its own. It confuses students for two reasons. ICD-10 (1992) has emotionally unstable personality disorder with two subtypes, impulsive type and borderline type. And DSM-5 has a separate group of impulse-control disorders (intermittent explosive disorder, kleptomania, pyromania), which are not personality disorders. A memory aid for the clusters is 'weird, wild and worried'. Example: Karan has had stormy relationships, self-harm and sudden mood swings since his teens; his clinician might diagnose borderline personality disorder and record impulsivity as one of its features, never as 'impulsive personality disorder'. Option a is right because no DSM-5 personality disorder is called 'impulsive'. Exam trap: the ICD-10 'impulsive type' of emotionally unstable personality disorder is the source of this trap; it is an ICD-10 subtype, not a DSM-5 disorder.", "traps": {"b": "Antisocial personality disorder is a genuine DSM-5 Cluster B disorder, a pervasive disregard for and violation of others' rights since age 15, diagnosed only at 18 or over; so it is a real personality disorder and cannot be the answer.", "c": "Borderline personality disorder is a genuine DSM-5 Cluster B disorder, marked by instability of relationships, self-image and emotion with marked impulsivity; so it is a real personality disorder.", "d": "Narcissistic personality disorder is a genuine DSM-5 Cluster B disorder, marked by grandiosity, need for admiration and lack of empathy; so it is a real personality disorder."}}, {"q": "Which of the following personality disorders is associated with a pervasive pattern of detachment from social relationships and a restricted range of emotional expression?", "concept": "The answer is b: a pervasive pattern of detachment from social relationships and a restricted range of emotional expression is schizoid personality disorder. That phrase is almost word for word the DSM-5 (2013) definition. Schizoid personality disorder sits in Cluster A (odd or eccentric). It needs four or more of seven features, beginning by early adulthood: neither desires nor enjoys close relationships, including being part of a family; almost always chooses solitary activities; has little, if any, interest in sexual experiences with another person; takes pleasure in few, if any, activities; lacks close friends or confidants other than first-degree relatives; appears indifferent to praise or criticism; and shows emotional coldness, detachment or flattened affect. The person is a loner by preference, not out of fear. Ernst Kretschmer (1921) described a schizoid temperament in some relatives of people with schizophrenia, and the term has been used ever since. Two neighbours must be kept apart. In avoidant personality disorder (Cluster C), the person longs for closeness but stays away for fear of rejection. In schizotypal personality disorder (Cluster A), there is also social discomfort, but with eccentric behaviour, odd beliefs such as magical thinking, and perceptual distortions. Example: Vivek, 35, a night-shift data analyst, lives alone, eats by himself, has no friends and no wish for any, and shrugs at both praise and criticism from his boss. He is not anxious about people; he is simply not interested. Option b is right because the stem quotes the defining phrase of schizoid personality disorder. Exam trap: borderline personality disorder is also defined as 'a pervasive pattern of instability' of relationships, so the shared opening words tempt students; the key words here are 'detachment' and 'restricted' emotion, which mean schizoid.", "traps": {"a": "Narcissistic personality disorder (Cluster B) is a pervasive pattern of grandiosity, need for admiration and lack of empathy; such people seek attention rather than detachment.", "c": "Borderline personality disorder (Cluster B) is a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotion, with marked impulsivity; its emotions are intense, not restricted.", "d": "Antisocial personality disorder (Cluster B) is a pervasive pattern of disregard for and violation of the rights of others, with deceit and lack of remorse; it is about rule breaking, not emotional coldness and withdrawal."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a symptom of dissociative fugue?", "concept": "The answer is c: hallucination is not a symptom of dissociative fugue. Dissociation is a disruption in the normal integration of consciousness, memory, identity, emotion, perception and behaviour. In dissociative fugue (from the Latin fuga, flight), a person suddenly and unexpectedly travels away from home or work, cannot recall some or all of their past, and is confused about who they are, sometimes taking on a partly or wholly new identity. The fugue usually follows severe stress or trauma and may last from hours to months; when it ends, the person often has no memory of the fugue itself. DSM-IV (1994) treated fugue as a separate disorder, while DSM-5 (2013) made it a specifier, dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue, defined as apparently purposeful travel or bewildered wandering linked with amnesia for identity or other important personal information. So option a (sudden, unexpected travel away from home) and option b (identity confusion) are both true core features. Hallucinations are not part of the picture: during a fugue the person often looks quite normal to strangers, and perception is intact. Prominent hallucinations would suggest a psychotic disorder, delirium or a substance effect instead. Because c is the odd one out, d ('none of the above') is wrong too. Example: after his business collapses, Mahesh vanishes from Pune and is found three weeks later working at a roadside dhaba in another state under a new name. He cannot say who he is or how he got there, but he hears no voices and sees nothing unusual. Option c is right because hallucination is a perceptual, psychotic symptom that is not a feature of dissociative fugue. Exam trap: a fugue is sometimes misread as a break with reality like psychosis; the break is in memory and identity, not in perception.", "traps": {"a": "True: sudden, unexpected travel away from home or the usual place of work is the defining feature of fugue in DSM-IV, and DSM-5 keeps it as purposeful travel or bewildered wandering, so it is a real symptom.", "b": "True: confusion about personal identity, or taking on a new identity, is a core feature of fugue, so it is a genuine symptom and not the answer.", "d": "'None of the above' would mean all three options are symptoms of fugue; since hallucination is not one, d cannot be correct."}}, {"q": "Pooja witnessed brutal killings, and screams of children during war. After returning, she suffered from recurring haunting memories and nightmares, a numbed social withdrawal, jumpy anxiety, and insomnia. She may be experiencing –", "concept": "The answer is a: Pooja's symptoms after witnessing wartime killings fit post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). PTSD entered the diagnostic manuals in DSM-III (1980), largely through work with Vietnam veterans, although similar reactions had earlier been called shell shock by Charles Myers (1915) and later combat fatigue. DSM-5 (2013) first requires exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence, by experiencing it directly, witnessing it, learning that it happened to someone close, or repeated exposure to grim details at work. Then come symptoms in four clusters. Intrusion: unwanted memories, nightmares, flashbacks and distress at reminders. Avoidance: of trauma-related thoughts, feelings, people or places. Negative changes in cognition and mood: persistent fear, guilt or shame, distorted blame, loss of interest, detachment from others and inability to feel positive emotions (emotional numbing). Changes in arousal and reactivity: irritability, reckless behaviour, hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, poor concentration and sleep problems. Symptoms must last more than one month and cause distress or impairment; if they last 3 days to 1 month, the diagnosis is acute stress disorder. Effective treatments include trauma-focused CBT, prolonged exposure (Edna Foa, 1998), cognitive processing therapy and EMDR (Francine Shapiro, 1989). Applying this to Pooja: she witnessed killings (exposure), has haunting memories and nightmares (intrusion), numbed social withdrawal (negative mood and detachment), jumpy anxiety (startle and hypervigilance) and insomnia (arousal). Every cluster is present. Option a is right because a traumatic event followed by intrusions, numbing and hyperarousal is the textbook picture of PTSD. Exam trap: generalised anxiety disorder also involves anxiety and poor sleep, but its worry is spread across everyday matters and needs no trauma; a trauma plus re-experiencing points to PTSD.", "traps": {"b": "Generalised anxiety disorder is excessive, uncontrollable worry about many everyday matters for at least six months; it needs no trauma and has no intrusive memories or nightmares of an event.", "c": "Dissociative disorders (amnesia, depersonalisation, identity disorder) disrupt memory and identity; Pooja remembers the trauma all too vividly, and DSM-5 handles dissociation after trauma as a dissociative subtype of PTSD.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because PTSD fits every symptom cluster in the vignette."}}, {"q": "Suraj is experiencing sudden episodes of intense dread along with shortness of breath, heart palpitations which come and go. He is most likely suffering from -?", "concept": "The answer is b: sudden, recurring episodes of intense dread with breathlessness and palpitations suggest panic disorder. In DSM-5 (2013), a panic attack is an abrupt surge of intense fear or discomfort that peaks within minutes, with four or more of thirteen symptoms: palpitations or racing heart, sweating, trembling, shortness of breath, choking feelings, chest pain, nausea, dizziness, chills or heat sensations, numbness or tingling, derealisation or depersonalisation, fear of losing control or 'going crazy', and fear of dying. Panic disorder needs recurrent unexpected attacks, coming 'out of the blue', plus at least one month of persistent worry about further attacks or their consequences, or a maladaptive change in behaviour such as avoiding exercise or certain places. Donald Klein (1964) helped separate panic from general anxiety when he found that the antidepressant imipramine blocked panic attacks but not chronic worry. David M. Clark (1986) offered the main cognitive model: panic arises from catastrophic misinterpretation of normal bodily sensations, such as reading a racing heart as a heart attack, which raises fear and so the sensations in a vicious circle. CBT with interoceptive exposure (deliberately bringing on the sensations) and SSRIs are first-line treatments. Many people with panic disorder develop agoraphobia, which DSM-5 now diagnoses separately. Applying this to Suraj: his episodes are sudden, intense, short and recurrent ('come and go'), with classic bodily symptoms. Option b is right because discrete, recurrent surges of intense fear with physical symptoms define panic attacks and panic disorder. Exam trap: generalised anxiety disorder is the near miss, but GAD is steady, long-lasting worry over at least six months, not sudden attacks that peak and pass; 'sudden episodes that come and go' signals panic.", "traps": {"a": "Generalised anxiety disorder is chronic, excessive worry about many matters for at least six months, with restlessness and muscle tension; it is a constant background state, not sudden discrete attacks.", "c": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves intrusive obsessions and repetitive compulsions done to reduce the anxiety they cause; Suraj reports no obsessions or rituals.", "d": "PTSD needs exposure to a traumatic event followed by intrusive memories, avoidance and hyperarousal; panic attacks can occur in PTSD, but nothing in the stem mentions a trauma."}}], "gurugram-2025-ma": [{"q": "At what stage in Piaget's theory do children begin to understand conservation, reverse mental operations, and classify objects by multiple features?", "concept": "The answer is c: children begin to conserve, reverse mental operations and classify objects by several features at once in the concrete operational stage, roughly 7 to 11 years. Jean Piaget (1936) described four stages of cognitive development: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 onwards). An operation is a mental action that can be reversed, like adding and then subtracting. Conservation is the understanding that quantity, number, mass or volume stays the same when only the shape or arrangement changes. In Piaget's classic beaker task, water is poured from a short wide glass into a tall thin one. A preoperational child says the tall glass has more, because she centres on height alone. A concrete operational child says it is the same and gives three reasons: identity (nothing was added), reversibility (pour it back and it will look the same) and compensation (taller but thinner). Classification also matures: the child can sort by colour and shape at once (multiple classification), solve class inclusion (more flowers than roses) and seriate sticks by length. Conservation arrives in a fixed order (horizontal décalage): number and liquid first, then mass, weight and finally volume. Example: Riya, aged 8, watches her mother roll a ball of atta dough into a long snake and says, \"Still the same dough, you can roll it back.\" Option c is right because all three abilities in the stem, conservation, reversibility and multiple classification, are the defining achievements of this stage. Exam trap: the preoperational stage is defined by the absence of these skills (centration, irreversibility, egocentrism), so examiners use it as the near-miss; formal operations add abstract and hypothetical reasoning, not conservation.", "traps": {"a": "The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2) is Jean Piaget's first stage, when infants think through senses and actions and achieve object permanence; there are no mental operations yet, so conservation is impossible.", "b": "The preoperational stage (about 2 to 7) brings symbolic thought but is marked by centration, irreversibility and egocentrism; children here typically fail conservation tasks, the opposite of what the stem describes.", "d": "The formal operational stage (about 11 onwards) adds abstract, hypothetical and systematic reasoning, as in the pendulum task of Barbel Inhelder and Jean Piaget (1958); conservation and classification are already in place before it begins."}}, {"q": "Who proposed that development unfolds naturally as a biological process, largely independent of experience or training?", "concept": "The answer is b: Arnold Gesell (1925) proposed that development unfolds naturally as a biological process, largely independent of experience or training. Gesell, an American paediatrician and psychologist at Yale, built the maturational theory. Maturation is growth that follows a genetically programmed timetable, the same sequence in every child of the species, whatever the teaching. Gesell filmed thousands of infants and published age norms for motor, adaptive, language and personal-social behaviour in The Mental Growth of the Pre-School Child (1925); these became the Gesell Developmental Schedules. His key evidence was the co-twin control study with Helen Thompson (1929). Identical twin girls, T and C, shared the same heredity. Twin T practised stair climbing daily for six weeks from 46 weeks of age. Twin C began training only at 53 weeks, yet after about two weeks she had caught up. Early practice had not hurried an unready body. Gesell's advice to parents followed: wait for readiness rather than push. Myrtle McGraw (1935) later refined the point with the twins Johnny and Jimmy: training did not speed species-wide skills such as walking, but it did advance individual skills such as roller skating once the child was ready. Example: Kabir's grandmother worries that he is not walking at 10 months; a Gesell-style answer is that he will walk when his nervous system is ready, and daily drills will not change that. Option b is right because Gesell is the textbook name for a nature-driven, maturational view in which experience plays only a minor role. Exam trap: Jean Piaget (1936) also used biological language (adaptation, equilibration), but he stressed the child's active interaction with the environment, so he is the near-miss; John B. Watson (1913) is the opposite, environmentalist extreme.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud (1905) proposed psychosexual stages driven by biological instincts, but he gave early experience, especially parenting, a decisive role in shaping personality, so for him development is not independent of experience.", "c": "John B. Watson (1913, 1925) founded behaviourism and claimed he could train any healthy infant into any kind of specialist; he is the extreme environmentalist, the opposite of the stem.", "d": "Jean Piaget (1936) saw development as an interaction in which the child actively builds knowledge from experience; maturation is only one of his factors, alongside experience, social transmission and equilibration."}}, {"q": "Which of the following accurately reflects Erik Erikson's theory of development?", "concept": "The answer is c: Erik Erikson (1950) proposed a psychosocial model of development that spans the entire human lifespan, from infancy to old age. Erikson, a German-born psychoanalyst who trained in Vienna, set out his theory in Childhood and Society (1950). He kept the idea of stages from Sigmund Freud (1905) but made two big changes. First, he shifted the focus from sexual drives to social relationships and the growing ego, so his stages are psychosocial, not psychosexual. Second, he carried development beyond adolescence into adulthood and old age. There are eight stages, each a crisis between two poles: trust versus mistrust (infancy), autonomy versus shame and doubt (toddlerhood), initiative versus guilt (preschool), industry versus inferiority (school age), identity versus role confusion (adolescence), intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood), generativity versus stagnation (middle age) and integrity versus despair (old age). Resolving each crisis well yields a virtue: hope, will, purpose, competence, fidelity, love, care and wisdom. Each stage builds on the ones before, but a later crisis can still be reworked. Example: Meera, aged 45, mentors young teachers at her school and feels her work will outlive her; that is generativity. Her father, aged 75, looks back on his life with acceptance rather than regret; that is integrity. Option c is right because \"psychosocial\" and \"whole lifespan\" are the two features that define Erikson's theory. Exam trap: Erikson was a neo-Freudian ego psychologist, not a neo-Piagetian; examiners also swap his \"psychosocial\" with Freud's \"psychosexual\", and \"None of the above\" tempts students who doubt option c because Erikson came from psychoanalysis.", "traps": {"a": "Neo-Piagetian theorists such as Juan Pascual-Leone (1970) and Robbie Case (1985) revised Jean Piaget's cognitive stages using information-processing ideas; Erik Erikson was a neo-Freudian ego psychologist, not a neo-Piagetian.", "b": "Sexual instincts as the engine of development define the psychosexual theory of Sigmund Freud (1905); Erik Erikson moved the emphasis to social relationships and ego identity.", "d": "\"None of the above\" would be right only if every statement were false, but option c accurately describes Erik Erikson's theory, so d cannot be correct."}}, {"q": "What is the correct sequence in Kohlberg's stages of gender development?", "concept": "The answer is c: in the cognitive-developmental theory of Lawrence Kohlberg (1966), children move from gender labelling to gender stability to gender consistency (constancy). Kohlberg argued that understanding comes first: a child first works out \"I am a girl\" and then seeks out girl things, so she socialises herself. This reversed the order of social learning accounts, in which rewards and models shape gender behaviour first. He described three steps. Gender labelling, or basic gender identity (about 2 to 3 years): the child can correctly call herself and others a boy or a girl. Gender stability (about 3 to 5): she knows gender stays the same over time, so a boy grows up to be a man, not a woman. Gender consistency or constancy (about 5 to 7): she knows gender stays the same across situations and surface changes, so a boy who wears a frock or grows long hair is still a boy. Ronald Slaby and Karin Frey (1975) supported the theory: children high in constancy watched the same-sex adult longer in a split-screen film. A criticism is that children prefer gender-typed toys by about two, long before constancy. Example: Arjun, aged 3, says \"I am a boy\" (labelling); at 4 he says he will be a papa when he grows up (stability); at 6 he laughs when told that wearing his sister's dupatta would make him a girl (consistency). Option c is right because each step builds on the one before: a child cannot know gender lasts over time until she can label it, and cannot know it survives disguises until she knows it lasts. Exam trap: the options swap stability and consistency; the time-based step (stability) always comes before the situation-based step (consistency), and constancy is the final step, never the first.", "traps": {"a": "This lists the three steps of Lawrence Kohlberg (1966) in exactly reverse order; consistency is the final achievement at about 5 to 7, not the first.", "b": "This puts stability before labelling, but a child must first be able to label gender (about 2 to 3) before understanding that it stays the same over time.", "d": "This swaps the last two steps; in the sequence of Lawrence Kohlberg (1966), stability across time (about 3 to 5) comes before consistency across situations and appearances (about 5 to 7)."}}, {"q": "In the preconventional stage of moral development, how is morality defined?", "concept": "The answer is a: at the preconventional level, right and wrong are defined by the hedonistic consequences of an action, that is, by the punishment or reward it brings to oneself. Lawrence Kohlberg (1958) studied moral reasoning by posing dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma (should a poor man steal a drug to save his dying wife?) and scoring the reasons given, not the choice. He found three levels, each with two stages. Level I, preconventional (typical of children under about 9): morality is under external control. Stage 1, the punishment and obedience orientation, judges an act by whether it is punished. Stage 2, instrumental exchange or naive hedonism, counts as right whatever serves one's own interest (\"you scratch my back, I scratch yours\"). Level II, conventional: rules are followed to win approval (Stage 3, good boy and nice girl) and to keep social order (Stage 4, law and order). Level III, postconventional: rules are seen as social contracts that can be changed for the greater good (Stage 5), and finally as universal ethical principles (Stage 6). Example: asked why Heinz should not steal, Kabir, aged 7, says \"because he will go to jail\"; asked why he shares his tiffin, he says \"so Rohan will share his tomorrow\". Both answers are preconventional because they rest on consequences to the self. Option a is right because \"hedonistic consequences\" (gaining pleasure or reward, avoiding pain or punishment) is exactly the preconventional basis of morality. Exam trap: option b describes the conventional level and option c the postconventional social contract stage; examiners reverse the levels, so remember: pre (self), conventional (society's rules), post (principles beyond the rules).", "traps": {"b": "Morality based on social rules, approval and law and order describes the conventional level of Lawrence Kohlberg (Stages 3 and 4), reached by most adolescents and adults.", "c": "Seeing rules as conventions or social contracts that serve the greater good, and that can be changed, is Stage 5 of Lawrence Kohlberg's postconventional level, the most advanced reasoning, not the earliest.", "d": "The theory of Lawrence Kohlberg (1958) is a stage theory by definition, with three levels and six stages; critics such as Carol Gilligan (1982) challenged its content, but the stem asks about one of its stages."}}, {"q": "Who demonstrated that \"contact comfort\" is more critical than providing for physical needs in forming attachment bonds?", "concept": "The answer is d: Harry Harlow (1958) showed that contact comfort matters more than feeding in forming attachment bonds. Until the 1950s, both psychoanalysis and behaviourism held a \"cupboard love\" view: infants love the mother because she feeds them, so she becomes a secondary reinforcer. Harlow tested this at the University of Wisconsin with infant rhesus monkeys separated from their mothers. Each monkey had two surrogate mothers: a bare wire mother and a mother covered in soft terry cloth. In the key condition only the wire mother held the milk bottle. The monkeys went to the wire mother just to feed and spent most of the day clinging to the cloth mother. When a frightening mechanical toy was put in the cage, they ran to the cloth mother, not the feeding one, and then used her as a secure base from which to explore. Harlow called this contact comfort and reported it in his paper \"The Nature of Love\" (1958). Later work showed that monkeys raised with surrogates alone grew into socially and sexually abnormal adults, so cloth alone is not enough for normal development. Example: baby Ananya, nine months old, takes her bottle from her aunt, but whenever a loud pressure cooker whistle frightens her she reaches for her grandmother, who usually holds and rocks her. Option d is right because Harlow's surrogate mother experiments are the classic evidence that comfort, not food, drives attachment. Exam trap: John Bowlby (1969) also rejected cupboard love and cited Harlow, so he is the near-miss; Bowlby built attachment theory, while Harlow ran the contact comfort experiments.", "traps": {"a": "John Bowlby (1958, 1969) built attachment theory, describing attachment as an evolved system that keeps infants close to caregivers; he drew on Harry Harlow's findings but did not run the contact comfort experiments.", "b": "Jerome Kagan (1984) is known for research on temperament and behavioural inhibition (shy versus bold children), not for contact comfort or surrogate mothers.", "c": "Mary Ainsworth (1978) devised the Strange Situation and described secure, avoidant and resistant attachment; she measured the quality of human attachment rather than testing feeding against comfort."}}, {"q": "Psychology students first refused to counsel drug addicts but agreed to conduct a seminar afterward. Which compliance strategy is being used here?", "concept": "The answer is a: this is the door-in-the-face technique, in which a large request that is refused is followed by a smaller, more reasonable request, which people are then more likely to accept. Robert Cialdini (1975) and his colleagues showed it on a university campus. They asked students to serve as unpaid counsellors to juvenile delinquents for two hours a week for at least two years. All refused. They then asked the same students to take a group of delinquents on a two-hour trip to the zoo. About 50 per cent agreed, compared with under 17 per cent of students who were asked only for the zoo trip. Two forces explain it. Reciprocal concessions: the requester seems to back down, and the norm of reciprocity pushes the target to concede in return. Perceptual contrast: after the huge request, the small one looks smaller still. It works best when the same person makes both requests and the second follows soon after the first. The stem follows the same pattern: counselling drug addicts (large, refused), then conducting a seminar (small, accepted). Example: Arjun asks his father for a new motorbike and is refused; a minute later he asks for a new phone, and his father, feeling he has already said no once, agrees. Option a is right because the sequence in the stem is large request, refusal, then a smaller request accepted. Exam trap: foot-in-the-door runs the other way, a small request first and then a larger one, so examiners rely on students mixing up the order; always ask which request came first and whether the first one was refused or accepted.", "traps": {"b": "Conformity, as in the line-judgement studies of Solomon Asch (1951), is changing behaviour to match a group norm without any direct request; the stem involves two direct requests from one source, which is compliance.", "c": "The that's-not-all technique, studied by Jerry Burger (1986), sweetens an offer before the person can answer (adding extras or cutting the price); there is no first request that gets refused.", "d": "The foot-in-the-door technique, shown by Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser (1966), starts with a small request that is accepted and then makes a larger one; the stem runs from large and refused to small."}}, {"q": "What concept explains why repeated exposure to a stimulus leads to increased liking?", "concept": "The answer is a: the mere exposure effect is the finding that repeated exposure to a stimulus increases liking for it, even when nothing else about the stimulus changes. Robert Zajonc (1968) showed people nonsense words, Chinese characters and faces from a yearbook different numbers of times, from zero to 25. The more often an item had been seen, the more positively it was rated. Later studies found the effect even when exposures were too brief to be consciously recognised, which led Zajonc (1980) to argue that \"preferences need no inferences\": feelings can arise before thinking. The usual explanation is perceptual fluency. A familiar stimulus is easier to process, and that ease feels pleasant and safe. Evolution may have favoured this bias, since what is familiar has already proved harmless. The effect has limits. It is strongest with neutral or mildly positive stimuli, it levels off or reverses after very many exposures as boredom sets in, and it can make an already disliked stimulus more disliked. It helps explain why advertisers repeat jingles and why neighbours and classmates often become friends. Example: Meera did not like a new Punjabi song when she first heard it, but after hearing it every day on the college bus for two weeks she now hums it and calls it her favourite. Option a is right because the stem names exactly the mechanism Zajonc described: repetition alone raises liking. Exam trap: spatial proximity also raises attraction, partly because nearness creates repeated exposure, so it is the near-miss; but proximity is about physical distance, while the stem asks about repeated exposure itself.", "traps": {"b": "The attractiveness stereotype (\"what is beautiful is good\", Karen Dion, Ellen Berscheid and Elaine Walster, 1972) is the belief that good-looking people also have other good traits; it concerns looks, not repetition.", "c": "Spatial proximity, shown by Leon Festinger, Stanley Schachter and Kurt Back (1950) in the Westgate housing study, is physical nearness raising attraction; it works partly through repeated exposure, but the stem names the exposure itself.", "d": "Need complementarity, proposed by Robert Winch (1958), says people are drawn to partners whose needs complement their own (a dominant person with a submissive one); it has nothing to do with repeated exposure."}}, {"q": "What term describes reduced likelihood of helping when more bystanders are present?", "concept": "The answer is b: diffusion of responsibility is the reduced likelihood of helping when more bystanders are present, because each person feels less personally responsible for acting. The idea grew out of the 1964 murder of Kitty Genovese in New York, when newspapers reported that 38 neighbours saw or heard the attack and nobody acted (later research showed the number was exaggerated). John Darley and Bibb Latane (1968) suspected that the number of witnesses, not big-city apathy, was the key. In their intercom study, students talking over an intercom heard another student apparently have an epileptic seizure. When a student believed they were the only listener, about 85 per cent went for help before the seizure ended. When they believed four others could also hear, only about 31 per cent did. Responsibility was shared out among the group, so each person's share shrank. Bibb Latane and John Darley (1970) later described five steps a bystander must pass: notice the event, interpret it as an emergency, take responsibility, decide how to help, and act. Diffusion of responsibility blocks the third step. Pluralistic ignorance blocks the second: everyone looks calm, so each person decides there is no emergency. Example: Kabir sees a man collapse on a crowded Delhi metro platform and thinks, \"With so many people here, someone must already have called for help,\" and walks on. Option b is right because it is the precise term for the drop in helping caused by more bystanders. Exam trap: the whole phenomenon is called the bystander effect, and diffusion of responsibility is the mechanism behind it; examiners also offer \"social influence\" (pluralistic ignorance) as the near-miss.", "traps": {"a": "Social influence, in the model of Bibb Latane and John Darley (1970), means bystanders using each other's calm reactions to decide there is no emergency (pluralistic ignorance); it blocks interpreting the event, not taking responsibility.", "c": "The negative state relief model of Robert Cialdini (1973) says people help to relieve their own sadness; it explains why sad people may help more, not why more bystanders reduce helping.", "d": "Altruism is helping purely for another person's benefit, as in the empathy-altruism hypothesis of Daniel Batson (1981); it is a motive for helping, not the decline in helping caused by a crowd."}}, {"q": "What is the primary function of the limbic system?", "concept": "The answer is c: the primary function of the limbic system is regulating emotion and memory, along with motivation and basic drives. The limbic system is a ring of structures on the inner border of each cerebral hemisphere, wrapped around the brainstem; \"limbus\" is Latin for border. Paul Broca (1878) first described this \"great limbic lobe\". James Papez (1937) proposed an emotion circuit linking the hippocampus, fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamus and cingulate gyrus. Paul MacLean (1952) added the amygdala and other structures and named the whole the limbic system. Its key parts have distinct jobs. The amygdala detects threat and attaches fear and emotional meaning to events. The hippocampus consolidates new explicit memories; the famous patient H.M. could no longer form them after surgery removed much of both hippocampi in 1953. The hypothalamus drives hunger, thirst, sex and the stress response. The cingulate gyrus links emotion with attention and pain. Classic evidence came from Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy (1937), who removed the temporal lobes, including the amygdala, in monkeys; the animals became tame, fearless and hypersexual (Kluver-Bucy syndrome). Example: Riya smells the same agarbatti her late grandmother used to light and is flooded with warm memories and tears; her amygdala and hippocampus, closely linked to smell pathways, are tying emotion to memory. Option c is right because emotion and memory are the two functions every textbook assigns to the limbic system. Exam trap: motor control belongs to the motor cortex and basal ganglia, sensory processing to the thalamus and sensory cortex, and muscle coordination to the cerebellum; examiners pick these because the basal ganglia and thalamus sit close to limbic structures.", "traps": {"a": "Motor control is handled mainly by the primary motor cortex in the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe and by the basal ganglia, not by the limbic system.", "b": "Processing sensory information is the job of the thalamus, which relays most senses, and of the sensory cortices (visual, auditory, somatosensory); the limbic system adds emotional colour but is not the main sensory processor.", "d": "Coordinating muscle movements, balance and timing is the main function of the cerebellum in the hindbrain; cerebellar damage causes ataxia, not problems of emotion or memory."}}, {"q": "Which brain lobe is primarily involved in processing auditory information?", "concept": "The answer is d: the temporal lobe is primarily involved in processing auditory information. Each cerebral hemisphere has four lobes, separated by landmarks: the central sulcus divides frontal from parietal, and the lateral (Sylvian) fissure separates the temporal lobe below from the frontal and parietal lobes above. The primary auditory cortex sits on the upper surface of the temporal lobe, on Heschl's gyrus, tucked inside the lateral fissure; on the map drawn by Korbinian Brodmann (1909) it is areas 41 and 42. Sound reaches it from the cochlea through the brainstem and the medial geniculate nucleus of the thalamus. It is tonotopically organised: different strips respond to different pitches, mirroring the layout of the cochlea. Around it lies the auditory association cortex. In the left hemisphere this includes Wernicke's area (area 22), identified by Carl Wernicke (1874), which is needed to understand speech; damage causes fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension. The temporal lobe also holds the hippocampus and amygdala in its depths, so it serves memory and emotion as well. Each ear sends signals to both temporal lobes, so damage on one side rarely causes complete deafness. Example: after a stroke in her left temporal lobe, Meera's grandmother hears her family speaking clearly but cannot make sense of their words, a sign of Wernicke's aphasia. Option d is right because both the primary auditory cortex and the main speech comprehension area lie in the temporal lobe. Exam trap: the occipital lobe is the primary area for vision and the parietal lobe for touch, so examiners use them as sensory near-misses; Broca's area, described by Paul Broca (1861) and needed for speech production, lies in the frontal lobe, not the temporal lobe.", "traps": {"a": "The frontal lobe handles planning, decision-making, voluntary movement (primary motor cortex) and speech production (Broca's area, Paul Broca, 1861); it is not the primary auditory area.", "b": "The occipital lobe at the back of the brain contains the primary visual cortex (Brodmann area 17), so it processes vision, not hearing.", "c": "The parietal lobe contains the primary somatosensory cortex in the postcentral gyrus and handles touch, body position and spatial attention; its damage causes problems such as hemispatial neglect, not loss of hearing."}}, {"q": "How is information transmitted within a neuron?", "concept": "The answer is a: information is transmitted within a neuron through electrical signals. A neuron does two different jobs, and they use two different codes. Inside the cell, from the dendrites and cell body down the axon, the message travels as an electrical change across the membrane. Graded postsynaptic potentials spread over the dendrites and cell body; if they push the axon hillock past threshold (about minus 55 millivolts in most textbooks), an all-or-none action potential fires and travels down the axon to the terminals. Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) worked out the mechanism in the giant axon of the squid: sodium ions rush in, then potassium ions flow out. In myelinated axons the impulse jumps between the nodes of Ranvier (saltatory conduction), reaching speeds of over 100 metres per second. Between neurons, at the synapse, the code changes. The electrical impulse triggers the release of chemical neurotransmitters, which cross the synaptic cleft and bind to receptors on the next cell. Otto Loewi (1921) proved this chemical step with two frog hearts and his \"vagus substance\", later identified as acetylcholine. So neural communication as a whole is called electrochemical: electrical within a neuron, chemical between neurons. Example: when Arjun touches a hot tawa, the signal races along the axon of his sensory neuron as an electrical impulse and then crosses to the next neuron in his spinal cord by chemical transmission. Option a is right because the stem says \"within a neuron\", which is the electrical part of the process. Exam trap: \"Both a and b\" tempts students who remember that neural transmission is electrochemical; that phrase describes the whole chain including synapses, not conduction inside a single neuron.", "traps": {"b": "Chemical messengers (neurotransmitters such as acetylcholine, shown by Otto Loewi, 1921) carry the signal between neurons across the synapse, not within a single neuron.", "c": "\"Both\" describes neural communication as a whole, which is electrochemical; but the stem asks only about transmission within one neuron, which is electrical.", "d": "Neurons do not transmit information by magnetism; magnetic fields are used only by tools such as transcranial magnetic stimulation and MRI to stimulate or image the brain."}}, {"q": "What is the resting membrane potential of a neuron?", "concept": "The answer is a: the resting membrane potential of a typical neuron is about minus 70 millivolts (written -70 mV), meaning the inside of the cell is 70 millivolts more negative than the outside. A neuron at rest is polarised. Measured with a fine microelectrode, the voltage across its membrane usually sits between about -60 and -80 mV, and textbooks round it to -70 mV. Three things create it. First, ions are unevenly spread: sodium (Na+) and chloride (Cl-) are concentrated outside, while potassium (K+) and large negative proteins are concentrated inside. Second, the resting membrane is far more permeable to potassium, which leaks out down its concentration gradient and leaves negative charge behind. Third, the sodium-potassium pump uses energy to push out three sodium ions for every two potassium ions it brings in. Two forces act on each ion: the concentration gradient (from crowded to scarce) and electrostatic pressure (opposite charges attract). Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) measured these events in the giant axon of the squid. When the cell is depolarised to threshold, about -55 mV, voltage-gated sodium channels open and the action potential shoots up to a peak of about +40 mV; the cell then repolarises, briefly dips below rest (hyperpolarisation) and returns to -70 mV. Example: Kabir's teacher compares a resting neuron to a charged phone battery: a stored voltage difference, ready to be used the moment a signal arrives. Option a is right because -70 mV is the standard textbook value for the resting potential. Exam trap: +40 mV is the peak of the action potential and -40 mV lies in the depolarised zone near threshold, so examiners offer these as near-misses; the sign matters too, since at rest the inside is negative, not positive.", "traps": {"b": "+70 mV has the wrong sign: at rest the inside of the neuron is negative relative to the outside, not positive, and no standard neural value is +70 mV.", "c": "-40 mV is a depolarised value near or past the threshold of excitation (usually given as about -55 mV), where the action potential is triggered, not the resting level.", "d": "+40 mV is roughly the peak of the action potential, when sodium has rushed in and the inside briefly becomes positive; it is the firing state, not the resting state."}}, {"q": "According to Yerkes-Dodson law, what is the relationship between arousal and performance?", "concept": "The answer is b: the Yerkes-Dodson law describes an inverted U relationship between arousal and performance, so performance is best at a moderate level of arousal and falls off when arousal is too low or too high. Robert Yerkes and John Dodson (1908) trained mice to choose a white box over a black one, giving electric shocks of different strengths for mistakes. The mice learned fastest at an intermediate shock level, not at the weakest or the strongest. They also found that the harder the discrimination, the weaker the best shock. Later writers turned this into a general law of arousal (or anxiety) and performance. Too little arousal leaves a person drowsy and careless; too much makes them tense and disorganised, with narrowed attention. The second part of the law matters too: the optimum is lower for complex or new tasks and higher for simple or well-learned ones. Donald Hebb (1955) built on this in his optimal arousal theory, which says people seek a moderate level of stimulation. Example: Meera, preparing for her M.Phil interview, studies poorly when she is bored and relaxed months before, and also when she panics the night before, but she performs best on the day with a moderate, alert nervousness. A sprinter doing a simple, well-practised task can tolerate much higher arousal than Meera solving a difficult statistics problem. Option b is right because the curve rises, peaks at moderate arousal and then falls, which is the shape of an inverted U. Exam trap: students remember \"U\" and forget \"inverted\"; also watch for questions on the second part of the law, where complex tasks have the lower optimum, not the higher one.", "traps": {"a": "A U-shape would mean performance is best at very low and very high arousal and worst at moderate arousal, the exact reverse of what Robert Yerkes and John Dodson (1908) found.", "c": "A linear relation, with more arousal always giving better performance, is closer to the drive theory of Clark Hull (1943); the Yerkes-Dodson law says that beyond the optimum, extra arousal harms performance.", "d": "\"Inverted V\" is not a standard term in this area; it suggests a sharp peak with straight sides, whereas the Yerkes-Dodson curve is a smooth, rounded inverted U."}}, {"q": "Motivation driven by external rewards is explained by which theory?", "concept": "The answer is c: incentive theory explains motivation driven by external rewards, holding that behaviour is pulled towards attractive goals in the environment rather than pushed by internal needs. An incentive is an external object or outcome that attracts or repels: money, grades, praise, food, a promotion. Drive-reduction theory said organisms act only to reduce internal tension, but it could not explain why people eat dessert when full or work hard for rewards they do not biologically need. Incentive theory fills that gap: the value and expected arrival of the reward itself energise behaviour. Classic evidence came from Otto Tinklepaugh (1928). Monkeys watched a banana being hidden under a cup; when they lifted the cup and found lettuce instead, a food they normally ate, they refused it and searched angrily. Their behaviour was driven by the expected incentive, not by hunger alone. Leo Crespi (1942) found a similar contrast effect in rats: when their reward was suddenly cut from large to small, they ran more slowly than rats that had always received the small reward. Incentive value depends on the person and the moment, so the same reward does not motivate everyone equally. Heavy external rewards can even undermine interest in a task enjoyed for its own sake, the overjustification effect shown by Edward Deci (1971). Example: Arjun stays late at the office through December because he has been promised a large year-end bonus; the bonus pulls his effort. Option c is right because incentive theory is the theory defined by external rewards and goals. Exam trap: remember \"drives push, incentives pull\"; examiners offer drive-reduction theory because it also involves reward in a loose sense, but drive theory is about internal biological tension.", "traps": {"a": "Drive-reduction theory, proposed by Clark Hull (1943), says internal biological needs create unpleasant drives that push behaviour until the need is reduced; it is about internal tension, not external rewards.", "b": "Instinct theory, associated with William McDougall (1908), explains behaviour by inborn, unlearned, species-wide tendencies; it does not centre on external rewards.", "d": "Cognitive theories, such as the expectancy theory of Victor Vroom (1964), stress expectations, beliefs and interpretations; rewards enter only through what a person thinks about them, so the stem's plain \"external rewards\" points to incentive theory."}}, {"q": "How many inkblot cards are used in Rorschach's test?", "concept": "The answer is a: the Rorschach Inkblot Test uses 10 cards. Hermann Rorschach (1921), a Swiss psychiatrist, published the test in his book Psychodiagnostik after trying many inkblots with psychiatric patients and keeping the ten that best separated diagnostic groups. He died the next year, aged 37. Each card is a bilaterally symmetrical inkblot. Five are achromatic, in black and grey (cards I, IV, V, VI and VII); two are black with red (II and III); and three are fully coloured in pastel shades (VIII, IX and X). The test rests on the projective hypothesis: when a stimulus is ambiguous, the way a person organises it reveals their personality and thought processes. Administration has three phases. In the response phase the examiner asks \"What might this be?\" and records every answer word for word. In the inquiry phase the examiner returns to each response to find its location (where on the blot), its determinants (form, colour, shading, movement) and its content (human, animal and so on). An optional testing-the-limits phase may follow. Scoring uses systems such as the Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) or the newer R-PAS of Gregory Meyer and colleagues (2011). Example: Kabir sees two people dancing on card III; the examiner codes human movement as the determinant and human content, a response often linked to imagination and empathy. Option a is right because the standard Rorschach set has exactly ten cards, unchanged since 1921. Exam trap: do not confuse the Rorschach's 10 cards with the 31 cards of the TAT (30 pictures and one blank) or the 45 cards per form of the Holtzman Inkblot Technique (Wayne Holtzman, 1961); examiners also offer nearby numbers like 11 and 12.", "traps": {"b": "Twelve is not the number in any standard Rorschach set; it is a distractor close to ten. The related Holtzman Inkblot Technique (Wayne Holtzman, 1961) has 45 cards in each of its two forms.", "c": "Eleven is not a Rorschach number either; students sometimes add a \"blank card\" to the ten, but the blank card belongs to the TAT of Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935), as card 16, not the Rorschach.", "d": "Eight does not match the Rorschach; it may echo the Szondi Test (Leopold Szondi, 1947), whose 48 portraits are shown in six sets of eight."}}, {"q": "Who introduced the IQ formula comparing mental and chronological age?", "concept": "The answer is c: William Stern (1912) introduced the formula that divides mental age by chronological age. In Paris, Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence scale to identify schoolchildren who needed special help. Their 1908 revision grouped items by age level, so a child's mental age (MA) was the age level of the items they could pass. But mental age alone is misleading: a child two years behind at age 4 is far more delayed than a child two years behind at age 14. Stern, a German psychologist, solved this by dividing mental age by chronological age (CA). He called the ratio the Intelligenzquotient, rendered in early English translations as the mental quotient. Lewis Terman (1916) then multiplied the ratio by 100 to remove decimals and used the label IQ in his Stanford-Binet, which made it famous. So the ratio IQ is MA divided by CA, times 100. Example: Riya is 10 years old with a mental age of 12, so her ratio IQ is 12 divided by 10, times 100, which is 120. The ratio IQ had a flaw: mental age stops rising in the mid-teens, so adults' ratio IQs fall as they age. David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ, a standard score with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. Option c is right because Stern was the first to express intelligence as the quotient of mental and chronological age. Exam trap: Binet gave mental age, not the quotient, and Terman added the 100. Key note: Lewis Terman multiplied the quotient by 100 and popularised the term \"IQ\" in the 1916 Stanford-Binet, so option b has support; but the idea of comparing mental with chronological age as a ratio was Stern's 1912 proposal, so the official key of c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Alfred Binet, with Theodore Simon, built the first practical intelligence scale (1905) and introduced the idea of mental level or mental age (1908), but he did not divide it by chronological age.", "b": "Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied the quotient of William Stern (1912) by 100 and made \"IQ\" famous through the Stanford-Binet; he refined and popularised the formula rather than first introducing it.", "d": "Theodore Simon was Alfred Binet's collaborator on the 1905, 1908 and 1911 Binet-Simon scales; he helped create mental age testing but did not propose the quotient."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a method to assess a test's reliability?", "concept": "The answer is d: construct is not a method of assessing reliability; construct validity is a type of validity. Reliability is the consistency of a test: whether it gives similar scores when the measurement is repeated or when different parts of it are compared. In classical test theory an observed score is a true score plus error, and reliability is the proportion of score variance that is true variance. There are several standard ways to estimate it. Test-retest reliability gives the same test twice to the same people and correlates the scores (coefficient of stability). Alternate or parallel forms reliability gives two equivalent versions and correlates them (coefficient of equivalence). Split-half reliability divides one administration into two halves, often odd and even items, correlates them and corrects for length with the Spearman-Brown formula of Charles Spearman and William Brown (1910). Related internal consistency methods include the formulas of G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937) and coefficient alpha from Lee Cronbach (1951). Inter-scorer reliability checks agreement between raters. Construct validity, set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), asks a different question: does the test really measure the theoretical trait it claims to measure, such as anxiety or intelligence? Example: Kabir builds a test anxiety scale. Giving it twice, three weeks apart, and getting r = 0.85 shows test-retest reliability; showing that it correlates with other anxiety measures and rises before exams shows construct validity. Option d is right because it names a kind of validity, while options a, b and c are all genuine reliability methods. Exam trap: reliability asks \"is it consistent?\" and validity asks \"does it measure what it claims?\"; a test can be reliable without being valid, but it cannot be valid without being reasonably reliable.", "traps": {"a": "Test-retest is a true reliability method: the same test is given twice to the same people and the two sets of scores are correlated, giving a coefficient of stability.", "b": "Split-half is a true reliability method: one administration is split into two halves (often odd and even items), the halves are correlated, and the Spearman-Brown formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910) corrects for the shorter length.", "c": "Alternate (parallel) forms is a true reliability method: two equivalent versions of the test are given to the same people and correlated, giving a coefficient of equivalence."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a type of construct validity?", "concept": "The answer is a: convergent validity is a type of construct validity. Construct validity, set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), asks whether a test truly measures the theoretical construct it claims to measure, such as self-esteem, anxiety or intelligence. Because a construct cannot be seen directly, evidence is gathered from many angles. Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) added two key forms in their multitrait-multimethod matrix. Convergent validity means the test correlates highly with other measures of the same construct, even when those use different methods. Discriminant (divergent) validity means it correlates weakly with measures of different constructs. Other construct evidence includes factor analysis, expected group differences and changes after an experimental manipulation. In the classic three-way scheme, validity has three types: content validity, criterion-related validity (with concurrent and predictive subtypes) and construct validity. Samuel Messick (1989) later argued that all validity evidence ultimately supports construct validity, but exam questions use the classic scheme. Example: Meera develops a self-esteem scale for Indian college students. It correlates 0.72 with the Rosenberg Self-Esteem Scale (Morris Rosenberg, 1965), showing convergent validity, and only 0.08 with a vocabulary test, showing discriminant validity. Option a is right because convergent validity is, by definition, evidence that a test converges with other measures of the same construct, which is construct validity. Exam trap: concurrent validity sounds almost the same as convergent validity, but concurrent validity is a subtype of criterion validity, where test scores are compared with a present criterion such as a psychiatrist's diagnosis.", "traps": {"b": "Criterion (criterion-related) validity is a separate type in the classic trio: test scores are checked against an outside criterion such as job ratings or diagnosis, not against other measures of the same theoretical construct.", "c": "Content validity is a separate type, judged by experts asking whether the items fairly sample the domain to be measured, as when an exam covers the whole syllabus.", "d": "Concurrent validity is a subtype of criterion validity, in which test and criterion are measured at the same time; its name sounds like \"convergent\", which is the trap."}}, {"q": "What does norm-referenced testing involve?", "concept": "The answer is b: norm-referenced testing compares an individual's score with the scores of other people, the norm group. A raw score, such as 42 out of 60, means little on its own. Norm-referenced interpretation gives it meaning by placing it within the distribution of scores from a standardisation sample, a large, representative group tested under the same conditions. The result is reported as a derived score: a percentile rank, a z score, a T score (mean 50, standard deviation 10), a stanine or a deviation IQ (mean 100, standard deviation 15). Most intelligence, aptitude and personality tests are norm-referenced because they are used to rank or select people. The norms must fit the person: a test normed on American adults may mislead when used with rural Indian adolescents, which is why Indian adaptations are restandardised. Robert Glaser (1963) contrasted this with criterion-referenced testing, where a score is compared with a fixed standard of performance, whatever others do. A criterion-referenced test asks \"What can this person do?\", so a whole class can pass it or no one can, as in a driving test. A norm-referenced test asks \"How does this person rank?\", so by design about half the norm group scores below average. Example: Arjun scores 48 on a numerical aptitude test; the manual's norm table shows this is at the 84th percentile for Class 10 students, meaning he scored higher than 84 per cent of the norm group. Option b is right because comparison with others is the defining feature of norm-referenced testing. Exam trap: options about mastery or domain knowledge describe criterion-referenced testing, and comparison with one's own past scores is ipsative assessment; both sound like \"standards\", which is why they tempt.", "traps": {"a": "Comparing a person with their own past performance is ipsative (self-referenced) assessment, used for tracking individual progress, not norm-referenced testing.", "c": "Testing knowledge of a defined domain is domain-referenced or criterion-referenced testing (Robert Glaser, 1963), which reports what a person can do, not how they rank among others.", "d": "Evaluating mastery against a fixed cut-off (for example, 80 per cent correct to pass) is mastery or criterion-referenced testing, in which everyone or no one can pass."}}, {"q": "Who developed the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT)?", "concept": "The answer is c: the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) was developed by Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935) at the Harvard Psychological Clinic. Apperception means interpreting a new perception through one's past experience, needs and feelings. The TAT shows ambiguous pictures of people in uncertain situations and asks the person to tell a story about each: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters think and feel, and how it ends. The idea is that the storyteller identifies with the main character, the hero, and projects their own needs and conflicts into the story. The 1943 version has 31 cards: 30 pictures and one blank card (card 16), on which the person imagines a picture of their own. Cards are marked for boys, girls, men and women, and usually about 20 are given over two sessions. Interpretation follows Murray's need-press theory, from his book Explorations in Personality (1938): needs are inner forces such as achievement, affiliation or aggression, and press is the push of the environment that helps or blocks them; a recurring combination of the two is a thema. David McClelland (1953) later used TAT-style pictures to score the need for achievement. Example: shown card 1, a boy looking at a violin, Kabir tells of a boy forced by his parents to practise music when he wants to play cricket; the examiner notes a need for autonomy and strong parental press. Option c is right because Morgan and Murray are the authors of the TAT. Exam trap: Christiana Morgan was first author of the 1935 paper but is often forgotten, so look for \"Morgan and Murray\"; and do not confuse the TAT with the Rorschach, which uses inkblots, not pictures of people.", "traps": {"a": "Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley developed the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI, 1943), an objective true-false inventory built by empirical criterion keying, not a picture-story test.", "b": "Hermann Rorschach (1921) developed the Rorschach Inkblot Test of ten symmetrical inkblots; it is projective too, but it asks what the person sees, not for stories about pictures.", "d": "Florence Goodenough (1926) devised the Draw-a-Man test, which scores children's drawings of a person as a measure of intelligence; it is a drawing technique, not the TAT."}}, {"q": "What best defines an operational definition?", "concept": "The answer is a: an operational definition is a way to measure a variable, that is, a definition in terms of the exact procedures or operations used to measure or manipulate it. Many concepts in psychology, such as anxiety, intelligence or aggression, cannot be observed directly. An operational definition turns them into something observable and countable. The idea comes from the physicist Percy Bridgman (1927), whose operationism held that a concept means the set of operations used to measure it; Stanley Smith Stevens (1935) brought operationism into psychology. For example, hunger can be operationally defined as hours without food, aggression as the number of times a child hits a Bobo doll in ten minutes, as in the studies of Albert Bandura (1961), and anxiety as a score on a standard anxiety inventory. Operational definitions do three jobs. They make research replicable, since another researcher can repeat the same procedure. They make communication precise, since readers know exactly what was measured. They make hypotheses testable. Their limit is that one operation captures only part of a construct, so good researchers use several measures (converging operations). Example: Riya wants to study exam stress among Delhi school students. She operationally defines exam stress as the score on the Perceived Stress Scale (Sheldon Cohen, 1983) taken one week before the board exams, plus resting heart rate measured that morning. Option a is right because an operational definition states how a variable will be measured or produced. Exam trap: examiners place the definition of a variable or of a hypothesis beside it; a variable is anything that can vary, a hypothesis is a testable prediction about variables, and the operational definition is the measuring recipe.", "traps": {"b": "A testable statement about the relationship between variables is a hypothesis; it is tested using operationally defined variables but is not itself an operational definition.", "c": "Identifying a research problem is the first step of the research process (problem formulation), which comes before variables are defined and measured.", "d": "A measurable factor that can vary is the definition of a variable itself; the operational definition is the procedure that turns such a variable into something observable."}}, {"q": "A repeated-measures design is also known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: a repeated-measures design is also known as a within-subjects design, because the same participants are measured under every condition of the experiment. Instead of assigning different people to each condition, the researcher tests each person repeatedly, so comparisons are made within the same individuals. This has two big advantages. Each participant acts as their own control, so individual differences in ability, personality or motivation are held constant, which reduces error variance and increases statistical power. It also needs fewer participants. The main weakness is order effects (sequence effects): practice can improve later performance, fatigue or boredom can worsen it, and carryover occurs when one condition affects the next, as with a drug that stays in the body. The standard remedy is counterbalancing: half the participants do condition A then B and the other half B then A, or a Latin square is used when there are several conditions. When carryover cannot be undone, as when a learned skill cannot be unlearned, a between-subjects design is used instead. Repeated-measures data are analysed with tests such as the paired (dependent) t test or repeated-measures ANOVA. Example: Meera tests whether caffeine speeds reaction time. All 20 of her participants are tested once after coffee and once after decaffeinated coffee on different days, with half receiving the caffeine first and half second. Option c is right because \"repeated measures\" and \"within subjects\" are two names for the same design. Exam trap: matched-subjects designs also control individual differences, so they look similar, but they use different people paired on a variable, so they are not repeated measures.", "traps": {"a": "A between-subjects (independent groups) design is the opposite: each participant serves in only one condition, and conditions are compared across different groups of people.", "b": "A matched-subjects (matched pairs) design uses different participants in each condition, paired on a relevant variable such as IQ; it borrows some of the control of a within design but is still a between-groups design.", "d": "A non-equivalent group design is a quasi-experimental design, described by Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963), that compares intact groups (such as two classrooms) without random assignment."}}, {"q": "What is the tendency for individuals to alter behavior when they know they are being observed?", "concept": "The answer is d: the Hawthorne effect is the tendency of people to change their behaviour because they know they are being observed or studied, whatever the actual intervention. The name comes from the Hawthorne studies (1924 to 1932) at the Western Electric Hawthorne Works near Chicago. In the illumination experiments, researchers raised the lighting to see whether workers produced more. Output rose, but it also rose in the control group, and sometimes even when the lights were dimmed. In the later relay assembly test room, output rose with almost every change to rest breaks and hours. The researchers concluded that the attention of being studied, not the lighting or the breaks, was boosting performance. The studies are linked with Elton Mayo (1933) and were reported by Fritz Roethlisberger and William Dickson (1939) in Management and the Worker; Henry Landsberger (1958) coined the term Hawthorne effect. Later reanalyses suggest the original data were weaker than the legend, but the effect remains a standard threat to the validity of research. Researchers reduce it with control groups that receive equal attention, unobtrusive observation, letting people get used to the observer, and blinding. It is related to demand characteristics, described by Martin Orne (1962): participants guess what the researcher wants and act accordingly. Example: when a supervisor from head office visits Kabir's call centre to observe calls, every agent becomes unusually polite and quick, then slips back after she leaves. Option d is right because the stem describes exactly a change in behaviour caused by awareness of being observed. Exam trap: the placebo effect also involves expectation, but it comes from believing one is receiving a treatment, while the Hawthorne effect comes from knowing one is being watched.", "traps": {"a": "The placebo effect is improvement caused by a person's belief that they are receiving an effective treatment, even an inert one; it comes from expectations about a treatment, not from knowing one is observed.", "b": "The halo effect, named by Edward Thorndike (1920), is letting one good trait colour judgements of a person's other traits; it is a rater's bias, not a change in the behaviour of the person observed.", "c": "The horn effect is the reverse of the halo effect: one bad trait leads a rater to judge the person's other traits negatively; it is also a judgement bias, not a reaction to being observed."}}, {"q": "Which measure of central tendency is most affected by outliers?", "concept": "The answer is a: the mean is the measure of central tendency most affected by outliers. A measure of central tendency is a single number that describes the centre, or typical value, of a set of scores. There are three: the mean (the arithmetic average, the sum of all scores divided by their number), the median (the middle score when the scores are put in order) and the mode (the most frequent score). An outlier is an extreme score that lies far from the rest of the data. Because the mean uses the actual value of every score, one extreme value pulls it strongly towards itself. Suppose Riya records the monthly pocket money of five friends: 500, 600, 700, 800 and 900 rupees. The mean is 700 and the median is 700. If Kabir, whose allowance is 20,000 rupees, replaces the friend with 900, the mean jumps to 4,520, while the median stays at 700. Option a is right because the mean is the only one of the three averages calculated from the size of every score, so its value shifts with any extreme one. This is why skewed data such as incomes, reaction times or hospital stays are usually summarised with the median: in a positively skewed distribution the mean is dragged out towards the long tail, beyond the median and the mode. The median depends only on rank position, so it is called a resistant or robust statistic, and the mode depends only on which value occurs most often. Exam trap: examiners offer the median or mode because students remember that skew 'affects' every average; only the mean is truly sensitive to outliers. Frequency distribution is a distractor of a different kind: it is a table or graph of how often each score occurs, not a measure of central tendency at all.", "traps": {"b": "The median is the middle score of the ordered data and depends only on rank position, so an extreme score barely moves it; it is the resistant average, the opposite of what the stem asks.", "c": "The mode is simply the most frequently occurring score; an outlier usually occurs only once, so it does not change the mode at all.", "d": "A frequency distribution is a table or graph showing how often each score occurs; it organises data but is not a measure of central tendency."}}, {"q": "Saying the population mean equals the sample mean reflects which hypothesis?", "concept": "The answer is d: a statement that the population mean equals the sample mean, so that there is no real difference, is the null hypothesis. A hypothesis is a testable statement about a population. In significance testing two competing statistical hypotheses are set up. The null hypothesis, written H0, states that there is no difference, no effect or no relationship: here, that the population mean (mu) equals the value being compared, so any gap between the sample mean and that value is just chance sampling error. The alternative hypothesis, H1, states that a real difference or relationship exists. Ronald Fisher (1935), in The Design of Experiments, named the null hypothesis as the one that evidence may 'nullify', or reject; Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) added the explicit alternative and the idea of two types of error. The test assumes H0 is true and asks how likely the observed data would be under it; if that probability (the p value) falls below the chosen alpha level, usually .05, H0 is rejected. For example, Meera wants to know whether students at her college sleep a different number of hours from the national average of 7. Her H0 is that the college population mean is 7, the same as the national figure; her H1 is that it is not 7. Option d is right because only the null hypothesis asserts equality, or 'no difference', between the parameter and the value it is compared with. Exam trap: research, alternative and experimental hypotheses are all names for the claim that a difference or effect exists, which is the opposite of the stem. Remember that the equals sign always belongs to H0, and that we 'reject' or 'fail to reject' the null, never 'prove' it.", "traps": {"a": "The research hypothesis is the investigator's prediction derived from theory, usually that a difference or relationship exists; it is the substantive claim the study hopes to support, not a statement of equality.", "b": "The alternative hypothesis (H1), formalised by Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson, states that the population mean differs from the compared value; it is the logical opposite of the equality in the stem.", "c": "The experimental hypothesis is the alternative hypothesis in an experiment, predicting that the independent variable changes the dependent variable; it claims an effect rather than no difference."}}, {"q": "When is a Type II error made in hypothesis testing?", "concept": "The answer is c: a Type II error is made when the null hypothesis is false but is accepted (more precisely, not rejected). In significance testing the researcher decides whether to reject the null hypothesis (H0, the claim of no effect). Reality has two states (H0 true or H0 false) and the decision has two outcomes (reject or retain), giving four possibilities. Two are correct: retaining a true null and rejecting a false null. Two are errors, named by Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933). A Type I error is rejecting a true null, a false positive or 'false alarm'; its probability is alpha, the significance level the researcher sets, usually .05. A Type II error is retaining a false null, a false negative or 'miss'; its probability is beta. Statistical power, 1 minus beta, is the chance of correctly detecting a real effect, and it rises with larger samples, larger effects and a more lenient alpha. The two errors trade off: making alpha stricter (say .01) lowers the Type I risk but raises the Type II risk. Take Arjun, who tests whether a new memory training programme improves recall. The programme truly works, but his sample is only ten students, the difference is not significant, and he concludes the programme has no effect. He has made a Type II error. Option c is right because 'null false and accepted' describes exactly this miss: a real effect exists and the test fails to detect it. Exam trap: the two errors are often reversed. A quick memory aid: Type I is 'seeing what is not there' (rejecting a true null), Type II is 'missing what is there' (keeping a false null). In medical terms, Type I tells a healthy person they are ill; Type II tells an ill person they are healthy.", "traps": {"a": "Rejecting a true null hypothesis is a Type I error (a false positive), whose probability is alpha; it is the opposite error to the one asked.", "b": "Accepting (retaining) a true null hypothesis is a correct decision, with probability 1 minus alpha; no error is made.", "d": "Rejecting a false null hypothesis is also a correct decision; its probability is the power of the test, 1 minus beta."}}, {"q": "Which test is used for analyzing categorical data (equality of proportions)?", "concept": "The answer is c: the chi-square test is used to analyse categorical data, including testing whether proportions are equal across groups. Categorical (nominal) data are counts of people or events falling into categories, such as yes or no, urban or rural, or type of diagnosis. Such data have no meaningful mean, so tests built on means cannot be used. Karl Pearson (1900) developed the chi-square test to compare the observed frequency in each category with the frequency expected if the null hypothesis were true. The statistic adds up, over all cells, the squared difference between observed and expected counts divided by the expected count. A large value means the observed counts depart from what chance predicts. There are two main forms. The goodness-of-fit test checks one variable against an expected distribution (for example, equal numbers choosing each of four colours). The test of independence checks whether two categorical variables are related, using a contingency table; when one variable is the group, this is the same as testing the equality of proportions. Chi-square is a non-parametric test: it assumes no normal distribution, though each expected frequency should usually be at least 5. Suppose Kabir surveys 100 urban and 100 rural students on whether they have ever seen a counsellor. Comparing the proportion of 'yes' answers in the two groups is a chi-square test on a 2 by 2 table. Option c is right because chi-square works directly on frequencies in categories, which is exactly what proportions are made of. Exam trap: the t-test and ANOVA sound like general group-comparison tools, but both compare means of interval or ratio data. If the dependent variable is a count in categories, think chi-square.", "traps": {"a": "The t-test, devised by William Sealy Gosset writing as 'Student' (1908), compares the means of two groups on interval or ratio data; it cannot handle categorical counts.", "b": "Analysis of variance (ANOVA), developed by Ronald Fisher in the 1920s, compares the means of three or more groups using the F ratio; it also needs a continuous dependent variable.", "d": "A factorial design is a research design with two or more independent variables studied together (analysed, for example, by two-way ANOVA); it is a plan for an experiment, not a statistical test for proportions."}}, {"q": "Which of these represents the strongest correlation?", "concept": "The answer is a: a correlation of -0.93 is the strongest of the valid coefficients offered. A correlation coefficient, such as Karl Pearson's (1896) product-moment r, describes how closely two variables move together. It carries two separate pieces of information. The sign gives the direction: positive means the variables rise together, negative means one rises as the other falls. The absolute size (the number ignoring its sign) gives the strength: 0 means no linear relationship, and 1 means a perfect one where every point lies on a straight line. So r always lies between -1.00 and +1.00. Comparing the options by absolute size: -0.93 has a size of 0.93, -0.24 has 0.24, and +0.91 has 0.91. Thus -0.93 is the strongest, just ahead of +0.91, while -0.24 is weak. The value +1.18 is impossible, because no correlation can exceed 1 in either direction; seeing it in a results table signals a calculation or typing error. As an example, Meera finds that the hours students spend on their phones before an exam correlate -0.93 with their marks: more phone time goes with lower marks, and the points hug a downward line very tightly. Option a is right because strength is judged by distance from zero, not by the sign, and -0.93 is the farthest from zero of the possible values. Squaring r gives the coefficient of determination: 0.93 squared is about 0.86, so about 86 percent of the variance in one variable is shared with the other. Exam trap: students treat a negative sign as 'weaker' and pick +0.91, or are tempted by +1.18 as the biggest number. Ignore the sign when judging strength, and reject any value beyond plus or minus 1.", "traps": {"b": "-0.24 is a weak negative correlation; its absolute size of 0.24 is far from 1, so it is the weakest of the valid options.", "c": "+0.91 is a strong positive correlation, but its absolute size (0.91) is slightly smaller than that of -0.93, so it comes second.", "d": "+1.18 is impossible: Pearson's r and every other correlation coefficient is bounded by -1 and +1, so this value signals an error, not a very strong relationship."}}, {"q": "Which of these is not a nonparametric test?", "concept": "The answer is d: Pearson's product-moment correlation is a parametric statistic, so it is the one that is not a non-parametric test. Parametric tests estimate population parameters (such as the mean or the population correlation) and assume interval or ratio data, a roughly normal distribution and, where groups are compared, similar variances. Non-parametric, or distribution-free, tests make fewer assumptions: they work on ranks, signs or frequencies and suit ordinal data, small samples and skewed scores. They are less powerful when parametric assumptions hold, but safer when they do not. Three of the options are classic non-parametric tests. The sign test, traced back to John Arbuthnot (1710), uses only the direction (plus or minus) of each paired difference. The Wilcoxon signed-rank test, from Frank Wilcoxon (1945), ranks the sizes of the paired differences and is the non-parametric alternative to the paired t-test; in the same paper Wilcoxon introduced the rank-sum test for two independent groups, which the option's mixed name echoes. The Mann-Whitney U test, from Henry Mann and Donald Whitney (1947), compares two independent groups by ranks and is the alternative to the independent t-test. Pearson's r, by contrast, was developed by Karl Pearson (1896); it uses the actual scores with their means and standard deviations, and its significance test assumes bivariate normality. Its non-parametric counterparts are Charles Spearman's (1904) rank-order correlation, rho, and Maurice Kendall's (1938) tau. Suppose Riya has two judges' rankings of 10 dance performances: she should use Spearman's rho, not Pearson's r. Option d is right because Pearson's r is the standard parametric correlation, whereas options a to c are all rank-based or sign-based. Exam trap: the word 'correlation' is not the clue; Spearman's rho is non-parametric while Pearson's r is parametric.", "traps": {"a": "The sign test is non-parametric: it uses only whether each paired difference is positive or negative, ignoring its size, so it needs no normality assumption.", "b": "Frank Wilcoxon's (1945) signed-rank test ranks paired differences and is the non-parametric alternative to the paired t-test (his rank-sum test is the independent-groups version); either way it is non-parametric.", "c": "The Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947) compares two independent groups using ranks; it is the non-parametric alternative to the independent t-test."}}, {"q": "In which type of conditioning does the unconditioned stimulus (UCS) appear before the conditioned stimulus (CS)?", "concept": "The answer is c: in backward conditioning the unconditioned stimulus (UCS) is presented before the conditioned stimulus (CS). Classical conditioning was described by Ivan Pavlov (1927) from his work with dogs. A neutral stimulus such as a bell (the CS) is paired with a stimulus that naturally triggers a response, such as food (the UCS), which produces salivation (the unconditioned response). After repeated pairings the bell alone produces salivation (the conditioned response). Whether learning happens depends heavily on timing. In forward conditioning the CS comes first. In delay conditioning, a form of forward conditioning, the CS starts first and is still on when the UCS arrives; it is the most effective arrangement because the CS acts as a warning signal. In trace conditioning, the other forward form, the CS ends before the UCS begins, leaving a gap, and learning is weaker. In simultaneous conditioning the CS and UCS start together, giving no warning, so learning is weak. In backward conditioning the order is reversed: UCS first, then CS. It is the weakest arrangement, often producing little or no excitatory conditioning, because the CS predicts nothing; the CS may even become a signal that the UCS is over (inhibitory conditioning). For example, if Arjun's dog is given food and only then hears a bell, the bell never comes to announce food, so it rarely triggers salivation. Option c is right because 'backward' is defined precisely by the UCS appearing before the CS. Exam trap: delay conditioning sounds slow or weak, but it is the strongest form; trace conditioning is the one with the gap. Also keep the directions straight: forward means CS then UCS, the normal predictive order, and backward means UCS then CS.", "traps": {"a": "Simultaneous conditioning presents the CS and UCS at the same moment; neither comes first, and learning is weak because the CS gives no advance warning.", "b": "Forward conditioning presents the CS before the UCS (the normal, predictive order) and includes both delay and trace conditioning; it is the reverse of the stem.", "d": "Delay conditioning is a forward procedure in which the CS begins first and overlaps the UCS; it is the most effective arrangement, not one in which the UCS comes first."}}, {"q": "What is the term for applying an unpleasant stimulus to reinforce behaviors that help avoid or escape it?", "concept": "The answer is b: using an unpleasant stimulus that the person can escape or avoid, so that the escaping or avoiding behaviour is strengthened, is negative reinforcement. In operant conditioning, developed by B. F. Skinner (1938) from Edward Thorndike's (1911) law of effect, consequences change how often a behaviour occurs. Reinforcement always increases a behaviour; punishment always decreases it. 'Positive' means a stimulus is added and 'negative' means a stimulus is taken away; the words describe arithmetic, not good or bad. That gives four combinations. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise for finishing homework). Negative reinforcement removes or prevents something unpleasant, and the behaviour that achieved the removal becomes more frequent. Positive punishment adds something unpleasant after a behaviour (a scolding for lying). Negative punishment removes something pleasant (taking away a phone). The stem describes the aversive stimulus as the setting for learning: it is applied, and behaviours that escape or avoid it are strengthened. This is escape learning (ending an aversive stimulus already present) and avoidance learning (acting early so it never arrives), both maintained by negative reinforcement. For instance, the car's seatbelt alarm beeps until Riya buckles up; the beeping stops, so buckling up quickly becomes a habit. Similarly, Kabir takes a painkiller and his headache goes away, so he reaches for pills sooner next time. Option b is right because the behaviours in question increase, and they increase because they end or prevent the unpleasant stimulus. Exam trap: because the stem says 'applying an unpleasant stimulus', many students pick positive punishment. Punishment, though, weakens the behaviour it follows, whereas here escaping is being strengthened. Ask two questions: is the behaviour going up or down, and is a stimulus being added or removed?", "traps": {"a": "Positive reinforcement strengthens a behaviour by adding a pleasant consequence, such as a reward; there is no unpleasant stimulus to escape.", "c": "Positive punishment adds an unpleasant stimulus after a behaviour in order to decrease it; the stem's escape and avoidance behaviours are being strengthened, not suppressed.", "d": "Negative punishment (response cost or time-out) removes a pleasant stimulus to decrease a behaviour; it involves neither an aversive stimulus to escape nor strengthening."}}, {"q": "An employer reviews employee performance at random times, prompting consistent work effort. Which reinforcement schedule does this represent?", "concept": "The answer is d: reviews at random, unpredictable times that keep employees working steadily represent a variable interval schedule. Schedules of reinforcement, studied in detail by Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957), are rules for which responses earn reinforcement. Two questions define the four basic schedules. First, is reinforcement based on the number of responses (a ratio schedule) or on the time that has passed (an interval schedule)? Second, is that number or time fixed or variable? On a fixed ratio schedule a reward follows a set number of responses, such as being paid for every 10 garments stitched. On a variable ratio schedule the number of responses needed changes unpredictably around an average, as with a slot machine; it gives the highest response rate and the most resistance to extinction. On a fixed interval schedule the first response after a set time is rewarded, such as a review held every March; it produces a scalloped pattern, with little effort early in the interval and a rush near its end. On a variable interval schedule the first response after an unpredictable amount of time is rewarded. Because reinforcement could come at any moment, the person responds at a moderate, very steady rate with few pauses. Consider Meera's manager, who walks round the office at unannounced times and praises whoever is working well. Meera cannot predict the next visit, so she works consistently all day. Option d is right because the reviews are tied to time rather than to how many tasks are finished, and that time varies unpredictably, which yields the steady effort the stem describes. Exam trap: the word 'random' tempts students towards variable ratio, which they associate with gambling. Ask whether the reward depends on counting responses or on the passage of time; checks at unpredictable times are interval schedules.", "traps": {"a": "A fixed ratio schedule reinforces after a set number of responses, such as piece-rate pay; it depends on output count, not on random timing, and produces bursts with short pauses after each reward.", "b": "A variable ratio schedule reinforces after an unpredictable number of responses, as in gambling; the unpredictability lies in the count of responses, not in the timing of checks.", "c": "A fixed interval schedule reinforces the first response after a set period, such as an annual review; it produces a scalloped pattern of slacking and then rushing, not consistent effort."}}, {"q": "In the Atkinson-Shiffrin model, how does information move from sensory memory to short-term (working) memory?", "concept": "The answer is a: in the Atkinson-Shiffrin model, information moves from sensory memory to short-term memory through attention. Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968) proposed the multi-store, or modal, model of memory: three stores linked by control processes that the person chooses. Sensory memory (the sensory register) holds a large amount of raw input for a very short time: the visual icon lasts about a quarter to half a second, and the auditory echo about 2 to 4 seconds. George Sperling (1960) showed with his partial-report method that people briefly register almost a whole display of letters, but the trace fades before they can report it all. Only the information we attend to passes on to short-term memory, which holds about 7 plus or minus 2 items (George Miller, 1956) for roughly 18 to 30 seconds unless rehearsed. Everything left unattended decays from the sensory register. Rehearsal then keeps information in short-term memory and, with enough repetition, transfers it to long-term memory, the store of practically unlimited capacity and duration. For example, as Kabir walks through a busy railway station, his eyes and ears register dozens of announcements and signs. When he hears his own train number, he attends to it, and only that announcement enters short-term memory; the rest vanishes within seconds. Option a is right because attention is the control process that selects what leaves sensory memory, acting at that first gateway. Exam trap: rehearsal is the gateway between short-term and long-term memory, not between sensory and short-term memory. Students who remember that rehearsal 'moves information along' apply it at the wrong stage. Encoding is a broader word for turning input into a memory code, and it happens at every stage.", "traps": {"b": "Maintenance rehearsal, repeating information over and over, keeps it alive in short-term memory (and in the model helps transfer it to long-term memory); it acts only after information is already in short-term memory.", "c": "Elaborative rehearsal, linking new material to its meaning, comes from Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart's (1972) levels-of-processing approach and moves information into long-term memory; it is not the sensory-to-short-term step.", "d": "Encoding is the general process of converting input into a form memory can store, and it occurs at every stage; it is too broad to name the specific control process at the first transfer."}}, {"q": "What is retroactive interference?", "concept": "The answer is a: retroactive interference is when new information disrupts previously learned material. Interference theory explains forgetting as competition between memories rather than simple fading with time. Georg Muller and Alfons Pilzecker (1900) first described retroactive inhibition, finding that learning a second list soon after the first impaired recall of the first. The key words are 'retro', meaning backwards, and 'pro', meaning forwards. In retroactive interference, new learning works backwards in time and damages older memories. In proactive interference, old learning works forwards and gets in the way of new learning. The classic design has an experimental group learn list A, then list B, and then recall list A, while a control group learns list A, rests, and recalls A. Poorer recall in the experimental group shows retroactive interference. John Jenkins and Karl Dallenbach (1924) found that students who slept after learning nonsense syllables remembered more than those who stayed awake, suggesting that later activity, not just the passage of time, causes forgetting. Interference is strongest when the two sets of material are similar. For example, Riya changes her mobile number. After months of using the new number she cannot recall her old one: the new learning has overwritten the old, which is retroactive. If instead she keeps typing her old number when asked for the new one, the old learning is blocking the new, which is proactive. Option a is right because it states the direction precisely: later information interfering with earlier material. Exam trap: examiners simply swap the two definitions, as in option b. Remember 'RETRO: new disrupts old; PRO: old disrupts new'. Also keep interference separate from decay theory, which blames time and disuse alone.", "traps": {"b": "Old information hindering new learning is proactive interference, the reverse direction; it explains why an old phone number keeps intruding when you try to recall the new one.", "c": "Retrieved information blocking related content describes retrieval-induced forgetting (Michael Anderson, Robert Bjork and Elizabeth Bjork, 1994) or tip-of-the-tongue blocking, not the new-over-old interference this term names.", "d": "Memory fading over time through disuse is decay theory, reflected in Hermann Ebbinghaus's (1885) forgetting curve; interference theory blames competing memories instead."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not included in Sternberg's triarchic theory of intelligence?", "concept": "The answer is b: emotional intelligence is not part of Robert Sternberg's triarchic theory of intelligence. Robert Sternberg (1985), in Beyond IQ, argued that traditional IQ tests capture only one side of intelligence. His triarchic, or three-part, theory names three related abilities. Analytical (componential) intelligence is the mental machinery used to analyse, compare and evaluate: metacomponents that plan and monitor, performance components that carry out the steps, and knowledge-acquisition components that learn. It is what school tests measure best. Creative (experiential) intelligence is the ability to deal with novel situations and to make familiar tasks automatic. Practical (contextual) intelligence is street smartness: adapting to the environment, shaping it or selecting a better one, using tacit knowledge to get things done. Sternberg later called the balanced use of all three 'successful intelligence' (1997). Emotional intelligence is a separate theory. Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990) defined it as the ability to perceive, understand, use and manage emotions in oneself and others, and Daniel Goleman (1995) popularised it in his best-selling book. As an example, Arjun scores well in exams (analytical), invents a clever new way to solve a puzzle no one taught him (creative), and knows exactly how to persuade a shopkeeper to give a refund (practical). His skill at calming a crying friend reflects emotional intelligence, a construct from outside Sternberg's model. Option b is right because analytical, creative and practical intelligence are the three parts of the triarchic theory, so they are included, whereas emotional intelligence belongs to Salovey, Mayer and Goleman. Exam trap: practical intelligence sounds like social or emotional know-how, so students wrongly merge the two. Also do not confuse the triarchic theory with Howard Gardner's (1983) multiple intelligences, whose interpersonal and intrapersonal intelligences overlap with emotional intelligence.", "traps": {"a": "Analytical (componential) intelligence is one of Sternberg's three parts: the ability to analyse, judge and solve familiar academic problems, the side IQ tests measure.", "c": "Creative (experiential) intelligence is Sternberg's second part: handling novelty and automatising familiar tasks, so it is included in the theory.", "d": "Practical (contextual) intelligence is Sternberg's third part: adapting to, shaping or selecting environments in everyday life, so it is included in the theory."}}, {"q": "Which defense mechanism was not originally proposed by Sigmund Freud?", "concept": "The answer is b: denial is the defence mechanism that, as a named and systematised defence, is credited to Anna Freud rather than to Sigmund Freud. A defence mechanism is an unconscious strategy the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety by distorting or blocking awareness of a threatening impulse or fact. Sigmund Freud (1894) first wrote of 'defence' in his early papers on the neuroses and described several mechanisms in his clinical work. Displacement, redirecting an impulse from a dangerous target to a safer one, appears in The Interpretation of Dreams (1900) as part of the dream work. Regression, retreating to the behaviour of an earlier psychosexual stage under stress, is also Freud's own concept. Rationalisation, a plausible excuse invented after the act, entered psychoanalysis in 1908 and became one of the classical defences. His daughter Anna Freud (1936), in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, collected, named and systematised the defences, and denial (refusing to acknowledge a painful outer reality) is the one most often credited to her treatment. Denial differs from repression: repression pushes an inner thought out of awareness, while denial blocks an outer fact. For example, when Meera's doctor tells her that her father's illness is terminal, she insists the reports are wrong and keeps planning his next birthday trip. Option b is right because displacement, regression and rationalisation were all in use within classical psychoanalysis before 1936, whereas denial as a distinct mechanism is attributed to Anna Freud's systematisation. Exam trap: these questions test the Sigmund Freud versus Anna Freud split, and students assume every defence came from the father. Key note: many texts credit rationalisation to Ernest Jones (1908), not to Sigmund Freud, so option c has strong support, and Freud himself wrote of disavowal in the 1920s. The key's view is that denial was systematised by Anna Freud (1936) in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, so the official key stands.", "traps": {"a": "Displacement, shifting an impulse onto a safer target, was described by Sigmund Freud in The Interpretation of Dreams (1900) as part of the dream work, so it is one of his originals.", "c": "Rationalisation, a plausible excuse invented after the act, is usually listed among the classical Freudian defences; the word itself was coined by Freud's follower Ernest Jones (1908), which is why this option is debated (see the Key note).", "d": "Regression, a retreat to an earlier stage's behaviour under stress, is Sigmund Freud's own concept tied to his psychosexual stages, so it was originally proposed by him."}}, {"q": "Choose the correct order of Freud's psychosexual stages of development:", "concept": "The answer is c: Freud's psychosexual stages run oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital. Sigmund Freud, in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality (1905, with the oral, anal and phallic stages added between 1913 and 1923), proposed that personality develops as the libido (sexual or life energy) becomes focused on different body areas, called erogenous zones, in a fixed order. In the oral stage (birth to about 1 year) pleasure centres on the mouth through sucking, biting and feeding, and the key conflict is weaning. In the anal stage (about 1 to 3 years) pleasure comes from holding and releasing faeces, and toilet training is the conflict. In the phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) the genitals become the focus, and the child passes through the Oedipus complex (for girls, the Electra complex, Carl Jung's 1913 term), resolved by identifying with the same-sex parent and forming the superego. In the latency period (about 6 years to puberty) sexual energy is dormant and channelled into school, friendships and skills. The genital stage (puberty onwards) brings mature sexual interest directed at others. If the conflict at any stage is badly handled, part of the libido stays fixated there, leaving adult traits. For instance, Kabir, harshly toilet trained, may grow into a very orderly, stubborn and stingy adult, which Freud called an anal character. Option c is right because it is the only option that follows the developmental sequence from infancy (the mouth) to adolescence (mature sexuality). Exam trap: latency is often misplaced after genital or before phallic. Remember that latency is a quiet pause between the Oedipal storm of the phallic stage and the arrival of puberty; Freud called it a period rather than a true stage, since no new erogenous zone appears. A handy mnemonic is 'Old Age Pensioners Love Gardening'.", "traps": {"a": "This option lists the five stages in exact reverse order, from genital back to oral; it is a reversal trap, since genital is the last stage, not the first.", "b": "This order puts anal first, oral last and latency before phallic; in fact the oral stage comes first, in infancy, and latency follows the phallic stage.", "d": "This order swaps oral and anal and places genital before latency; latency must come before the genital stage, which begins at puberty."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not one of the Big Five personality traits?", "concept": "The answer is d: introversion is not one of the Big Five traits; it is simply the low end of extraversion. The Big Five, or Five-Factor Model, is the most widely used description of personality traits. It grew out of the lexical hypothesis: important differences between people become encoded as words in everyday language. Researchers factor-analysing trait ratings kept finding five broad dimensions: Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961) in US Air Force ratings, Warren Norman (1963), and Lewis Goldberg, who named them the Big Five (1981). Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1985) reached the same five with questionnaires and built the NEO Personality Inventory. The five are remembered as OCEAN: Openness to experience, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness and Neuroticism. Each is a continuous dimension with two poles, and most people fall near the middle. Extraversion runs from high (sociable, talkative, energetic) to low, which is introversion (reserved, quiet, preferring solitude). Neuroticism likewise runs from anxious and moody to emotionally stable. So introversion is not a sixth factor or a separate trait; it is a low score on one of the five. For example, Riya, who loves parties and talks to everyone, scores high on extraversion; her brother Arjun, who prefers a book at home, scores low on the same dimension. Option d is right because agreeableness, conscientiousness and extraversion are three of the five named factors, whereas introversion is only the opposite pole of extraversion. Exam trap: the terms introvert and extravert come from Carl Jung (1921), and Hans Eysenck (1947) made introversion-extraversion one of his superfactors, so introversion feels like a standard trait. In the Big Five each factor is named by one pole only, so its opposite (introversion, emotional stability) is not a separate trait.", "traps": {"a": "Agreeableness (trusting, kind and cooperative versus antagonistic) is the A in OCEAN, so it is one of the Big Five.", "b": "Conscientiousness (organised, dependable and self-disciplined versus careless) is the C in OCEAN, so it is one of the Big Five.", "c": "Extraversion (sociable and energetic versus reserved) is the E in OCEAN, so it is one of the Big Five; introversion is merely its low pole."}}, {"q": "Which disorder involves degeneration of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra?", "concept": "The answer is b: Parkinson's disease involves degeneration of the dopamine-producing neurons of the substantia nigra. The substantia nigra, 'black substance', named for its dark pigment, is a nucleus in the midbrain. Its pars compacta sends dopamine along the nigrostriatal pathway to the striatum (caudate nucleus and putamen), part of the basal ganglia that helps start and smooth voluntary movement. James Parkinson (1817) described the condition as the 'shaking palsy'. When roughly 60 to 80 percent of the nigral dopamine neurons have died, the classic signs appear: a resting tremor (often 'pill-rolling'), rigidity, bradykinesia (slowness of movement), difficulty starting movement, a shuffling gait, a mask-like face and poor balance. Oleh Hornykiewicz (1960) found the patients' striatum almost empty of dopamine, and the surviving nigral cells contain Lewy bodies, clumps of the protein alpha-synuclein. Treatment follows directly: L-dopa, a precursor that, unlike dopamine itself, crosses the blood-brain barrier, is converted to dopamine in the brain; dopamine agonists and deep brain stimulation also help. For example, Arjun's grandfather notices that his hand trembles while resting on his lap but steadies when he reaches for his cup, and he takes small, shuffling steps; this pattern points to Parkinson's disease. Option b is right because the loss of nigral dopamine neurons is the defining pathology of Parkinson's disease. Exam trap: dopamine excess is linked with schizophrenia, and antipsychotics that block dopamine can cause Parkinson-like side effects (drug-induced parkinsonism), so students sometimes reverse the direction. Also do not confuse Parkinson's with Huntington's disease, which damages the striatum itself and causes jerky, uncontrolled movements (chorea).", "traps": {"a": "Alzheimer's disease (Alois Alzheimer, 1906) is a dementia marked by amyloid plaques, neurofibrillary tangles and loss of acetylcholine neurons, especially affecting the hippocampus and the nucleus basalis of Meynert, not the substantia nigra.", "c": "Multiple sclerosis, described by Jean-Martin Charcot (1868), is an autoimmune disease that destroys the myelin sheaths of central nervous system axons; it is a demyelinating disease, not a loss of dopamine neurons.", "d": "Epilepsy is a tendency to recurrent seizures caused by abnormal, synchronised bursts of neural firing; it is an electrical disorder, not a degeneration of nigral dopamine cells."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not categorized under Cluster B personality disorders?", "concept": "The answer is b: obsessive-compulsive personality disorder (OCPD) is not a Cluster B disorder; it belongs to Cluster C. A personality disorder is an enduring, inflexible pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from cultural expectations, is pervasive across situations, begins by adolescence or early adulthood, and causes distress or impairment. DSM-5 (2013) and DSM-5-TR (2022) group the ten specific personality disorders into three clusters by descriptive similarity. Cluster A is odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal. Cluster B is dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic. Cluster C is anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive. A handy memory aid is 'weird, wild, worried'. Narcissistic personality disorder involves grandiosity, a need for admiration and lack of empathy; histrionic involves excessive emotionality and attention-seeking; borderline involves unstable relationships, self-image and mood with marked impulsivity. All three are dramatic and erratic, which is why they sit in Cluster B with antisocial personality disorder. OCPD, by contrast, is a pervasive preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism and control, at the cost of flexibility and efficiency. Its tight, anxious over-control fits the fearful Cluster C. For example, Kabir rewrites every office report several times, cannot delegate because others 'will do it wrong' and misses deadlines through perfectionism; that is OCPD. Option b is right because OCPD is the only option outside the dramatic Cluster B, so it is the odd one out. Exam trap: OCPD is often confused with obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD), which has true obsessions and compulsions, is usually ego-dystonic, and sits in its own DSM-5 chapter of obsessive-compulsive and related disorders rather than among the personality disorders. Note that this same question is printed again as Q59 in this paper.", "traps": {"a": "Narcissistic personality disorder (grandiosity, need for admiration, lack of empathy) is a dramatic Cluster B disorder, so it is correctly categorised there.", "c": "Histrionic personality disorder (excessive emotionality and attention-seeking) is a classic Cluster B disorder, so it cannot be the odd one out.", "d": "Borderline personality disorder (instability of relationships, self-image and mood, with marked impulsivity) is the best-known Cluster B disorder."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not recognized as a subtype of schizophrenia in DSM-5?", "concept": "The answer is d: 'dissociative' has never been a subtype of schizophrenia, so it is the option that is not recognised. Eugen Bleuler (1911) coined the word schizophrenia, meaning 'split mind', for the splitting of thought, emotion and behaviour from one another, building on Emil Kraepelin's (1899) dementia praecox. Classification systems long divided it into subtypes by the most prominent symptoms. DSM-IV (1994) listed five: paranoid (delusions and hallucinations with fairly intact thinking), disorganised (disorganised speech and behaviour with flat or inappropriate affect), catatonic (motor signs such as stupor, rigidity, posturing or excitement), undifferentiated (criteria met but no type dominates) and residual (only mild or negative symptoms remain). DSM-5 (2013) dropped all five because they had poor reliability, were unstable over time and did not predict treatment response. Catatonia now appears as a specifier, 'with catatonia', that can be added to schizophrenia and to other disorders. A dissociative subtype has never existed in any edition. Dissociative disorders, such as dissociative identity disorder, form a separate chapter and involve disruptions of identity, memory or consciousness, not psychosis. For example, if Meera hears accusing voices and believes her neighbours are plotting against her, an older clinician might have written 'paranoid type', but a DSM-5 diagnosis is simply schizophrenia, perhaps with specifiers for course and severity. Option d is right because dissociative is the only label here that was never a schizophrenia subtype in any system, while catatonic, paranoid and undifferentiated all were. Exam trap: the popular image of schizophrenia as a 'split personality' blends it with dissociative identity disorder; Bleuler's split was within the mind's functions, not into separate identities. Key note: DSM-5 (2013) dropped all the subtypes, and catatonia is now a specifier, so strictly none of options a to c is a DSM-5 subtype; paranoid and undifferentiated were DSM-IV subtypes, while dissociative was never a schizophrenia subtype in any edition, which is why it is keyed. The official key stands.", "traps": {"a": "Catatonia (the catatonic subtype in DSM-IV and ICD-10) was a genuine schizophrenia subtype, and DSM-5 keeps it as the specifier 'with catatonia', so it has real standing in the classification.", "b": "Paranoid schizophrenia was the commonest DSM-IV subtype (delusions and hallucinations with fairly intact thinking) and remains in ICD-10, so it is a recognised, if retired, subtype.", "c": "Undifferentiated schizophrenia was the DSM-IV subtype for cases meeting the criteria without fitting the paranoid, disorganised or catatonic pictures, so it too was a real subtype."}}, {"q": "Identify the non-positive symptom of schizophrenia:", "concept": "The answer is d: a toneless or flat voice is a negative symptom of schizophrenia, so it is the non-positive symptom. Schizophrenia symptoms are grouped by whether something is added to or taken away from normal functioning. Positive symptoms are the presence of something abnormal, an excess or distortion: hallucinations (false perceptions, usually voices), delusions (fixed false beliefs), disorganised speech (derailment, incoherence, word salad) and grossly disorganised behaviour, including inappropriate affect, such as laughing while describing a death. Negative symptoms are the absence or reduction of normal functions. The five recognised in DSM-5 are diminished emotional expression (affective flattening), alogia (poverty of speech), avolition (lack of self-started activity), anhedonia (reduced pleasure) and asociality. A monotonous, toneless voice, with an unchanging face and few gestures, is a core sign of flattened affect. Timothy Crow (1980) linked positive symptoms to his Type I schizophrenia, acute and responsive to dopamine-blocking drugs, and negative symptoms to Type II, chronic, poorly responsive and associated with enlarged ventricles. Nancy Andreasen (1982) built separate rating scales for each, the SANS and SAPS. Imagine Arjun, who tells his doctor in a flat, unchanging voice, with a blank face, that he has not left his room for weeks; those are negative symptoms. Option d is right because a toneless voice reflects a loss of normal emotional expression, while hallucinations, disorganised and delusional speech, and inappropriate emotional expression are all excesses or distortions, which makes them positive. Exam trap: inappropriate affect (option c) and flat affect (option d) both concern emotion, so students lump them together. Inappropriate affect is an abnormal emotion that is present, hence positive (disorganised); flat affect is normal emotion that is missing, hence negative. This question is printed again as Q61 in this paper.", "traps": {"a": "Hallucinations, perceptions without an external stimulus such as hearing voices, are the classic positive symptom: an abnormal experience that is added.", "b": "Disorganised speech and delusional content are both positive symptoms, distortions of normal thinking and language.", "c": "Inappropriate emotional expression, such as giggling at sad news, is counted with the positive (disorganised) symptoms because an abnormal emotion is present rather than absent; Peter Liddle (1987) placed it in his disorganisation syndrome."}}, {"q": "Whichof the following is a common symptom of Major Depressive Disorder (MDD)?", "concept": "The answer is c: persistent feelings of sadness and hopelessness are a common, core symptom of major depressive disorder (MDD). MDD is diagnosed in DSM-5 (2013) when a person has a major depressive episode: five or more of nine symptoms, present most of the day, nearly every day, for at least two weeks, with distress or impairment. At least one of the five must be either depressed mood (sad, empty or hopeless, or irritable in children) or a marked loss of interest or pleasure (anhedonia). The other symptoms are significant weight or appetite change, insomnia or hypersomnia, psychomotor agitation or slowing, fatigue or loss of energy, feelings of worthlessness or excessive guilt, poor concentration or indecisiveness, and recurrent thoughts of death or suicide. The mnemonic SIG E CAPS (sleep, interest, guilt, energy, concentration, appetite, psychomotor, suicide) plus depressed mood covers them. Hopelessness also lies at the heart of Aaron Beck's (1967) cognitive triad, in which the depressed person views the self, the world and the future negatively. For example, Meera has felt low and hopeless for a month, cries most days and no longer enjoys painting; this persistent sad mood is what clinicians look for first. Option c is right because depressed mood with hopelessness is one of the two cardinal symptoms, and at least one cardinal symptom is required for the diagnosis. Exam trap: option d dresses real criteria in exaggerated form. Weight change and insomnia can occur in depression, but neither is required, they can just as well appear as weight gain and oversleeping (as in atypical depression), and 'extreme' weight loss points rather to an eating disorder or a medical illness. Options a and b describe mania, the opposite pole of bipolar disorder.", "traps": {"a": "Increased energy and restlessness are features of a manic or hypomanic episode in bipolar disorder; MDD brings fatigue and loss of energy instead.", "b": "Excessive happiness and euphoria describe the elevated mood of mania, the opposite of the low mood of depression.", "d": "Weight change and sleep problems are possible MDD criteria, but neither is required and both can go the other way (weight gain, hypersomnia); 'extreme' weight loss suggests anorexia nervosa or physical illness, so this is not the defining common symptom."}}, {"q": "An individual who is impatient, driven, and overly competitive is likely to show characteristics of:", "concept": "The answer is b: an impatient, driven and overly competitive person shows the Type A behaviour pattern. In 1959 the cardiologists Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman noticed that their heart patients wore out the front edges of the waiting-room chairs, as if always ready to jump up. They described the Type A behaviour pattern: intense competitive striving for achievement, a sense of time urgency (impatience, doing several things at once, hurrying others) and easily aroused hostility. Type B people, by contrast, are relaxed, easy-going and unhurried, though they can still be ambitious. In the Western Collaborative Group Study, which followed over 3,000 men for about eight and a half years, Type A men developed coronary heart disease about twice as often as Type B men. Later research found that the global pattern predicts heart disease inconsistently, and that hostility (cynical anger) is the truly harmful component. The idea grew into later types: Type C, unassertive and suppressing negative emotion, was proposed as cancer-prone but has weak support, and Johan Denollet's (1996) Type D combines negative affectivity with social inhibition. Picture Riya, who drums her fingers in queues, finishes other people's sentences, eats lunch at her desk and treats every office task as a race against her colleagues; she is a typical Type A. Option b is right because impatience (time urgency), drive and competitiveness are the defining features of Type A. Exam trap: Type A is a behaviour pattern linked to stress and heart disease, not a personality disorder. Do not confuse its drive with the perfectionism of obsessive-compulsive personality disorder, or its competitiveness with the cold manipulation of Machiavellianism.", "traps": {"a": "Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder is a Cluster C disorder of perfectionism, orderliness and control; it is marked by rigidity and indecision, not by impatient, hurried competitiveness.", "c": "Type B personality, in Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman's scheme, is the relaxed, patient and unhurried opposite of Type A.", "d": "Machiavellianism (Richard Christie and Florence Geis, 1970), one of the Dark Triad, is a cynical, manipulative and exploitative style; its core is deceit for personal gain, not impatience or time urgency."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is considered the most widely accepted criterion for defining abnormality?", "concept": "The answer is d: statistical deviation is the classic, most widely cited single criterion for defining abnormality. Defining abnormality is hard because no single test separates normal from abnormal, so textbooks discuss several criteria. The statistical criterion calls behaviour abnormal if it is rare, that is, far from the population average. It rests on the normal (bell-shaped) curve, applied to human traits by Adolphe Quetelet (1835): most people cluster near the middle, and scores in the extreme tails, for example more than two standard deviations from the mean, count as deviant. Intellectual disability is partly defined this way, with an IQ of about 70 or below, two standard deviations under the mean of 100. The approach is objective and easy to measure, which is why it is so widely used. Its weakness is that rarity is not the same as illness: a genius IQ is just as rare, and common problems such as anxiety are not rare at all. Other criteria include deviation from social norms, personal distress, maladaptive functioning and danger. Marie Jahoda (1958) described ideal mental health in positive terms, and Jerome Wakefield (1992) defined disorder as harmful dysfunction. For example, Arjun's tested IQ of 65 lies well below the population average, so on the statistical criterion it counts as abnormal, while his classmate's IQ of 145 is equally rare but desirable. Option d is right because, of the four options, only statistical deviation is a recognised criterion of abnormality. Exam trap: 'pathology' sounds medical and authoritative, but it is the very thing being defined, so it cannot serve as the defining criterion. Key note: modern texts use several criteria together (distress, dysfunction, deviance, danger) and no single one is sufficient; among these four options, statistical deviation is the classic criterion, so the official key stands.", "traps": {"a": "Personality traits are normal, continuous dimensions on which everyone varies; having traits is not a criterion for calling behaviour abnormal.", "b": "Pathology means disease; the medical model infers abnormality from it, but saying a person has 'pathology' assumes abnormality has already been defined, so it is circular as a defining criterion.", "c": "Sociability is a personality trait (part of extraversion), not a criterion of abnormality; low sociability alone does not make a person disordered."}}, {"q": "A patient has an intense fear of train travel without knowing the exact reason. What condition does this describe?", "concept": "The answer is b: an intense fear of train travel without knowing the exact reason describes a psychoneurosis, specifically a phobia. Psychoneurosis, or simply neurosis, is the older term for a group of non-psychotic disorders in which anxiety is the central feature. William Cullen (1769) coined 'neurosis' for nervous disorders without fever or local disease, and Sigmund Freud (1895) narrowed it to disorders arising from unconscious conflict. The key features are that the person stays in touch with reality, keeps insight (knows the fear is excessive) and remains largely able to function, yet suffers real distress. Classic psychoneuroses included anxiety states, phobias, obsessive-compulsive neurosis and hysteria. In Freud's view a phobia arises when anxiety from an unconscious conflict is displaced onto an outside object, which is why the person cannot say why that object frightens them; his case of Little Hans (1909), a boy who feared horses, is the textbook example. DSM-III (1980) dropped the term neurosis, and today this case would be diagnosed as specific phobia, situational type, in the DSM-5 anxiety disorders chapter, while ICD-10 still groups it under neurotic, stress-related and somatoform disorders. For example, Kabir panics at the thought of boarding a train, avoids family trips to Delhi, and admits the fear makes no sense, but cannot explain where it came from. Option b is right because a specific, irrational fear with intact reality testing is the prototype of a neurotic (psychoneurotic) disorder. Exam trap: students sometimes choose psychosis because the fear seems irrational. Psychosis means loss of contact with reality, with delusions or hallucinations and poor insight; a person with a phobia knows the fear is unreasonable.", "traps": {"a": "A psychosomatic disorder is a real physical illness, such as a peptic ulcer or hypertension, caused or worsened by psychological factors; the stem describes a fear, not a bodily disease.", "c": "Psychosis involves loss of contact with reality (delusions, hallucinations, impaired insight); a phobic person recognises the fear as excessive, so reality testing is intact.", "d": "Fatigue is tiredness or low energy found in many conditions (and the core of the old diagnosis neurasthenia); it is a symptom, not an intense fear of a situation."}}, {"q": "Which therapeutic approach involves counterconditioning to treat anxiety responses?", "concept": "The answer is c: systematic desensitisation is the therapy that treats anxiety by counterconditioning. Counterconditioning means replacing a learned response to a cue with a new response that cannot exist alongside it. Joseph Wolpe (1958) built the method on reciprocal inhibition: a person cannot be deeply relaxed and anxious at the same moment, so if relaxation is repeatedly paired with a feared cue, the fear bond weakens. The roots go back to Mary Cover Jones (1924), who cured little Peter's fear of rabbits by bringing a rabbit gradually closer while he ate food he liked. Wolpe's procedure has three steps. First, the client learns deep muscle relaxation, usually the progressive muscle relaxation of Edmund Jacobson (1938). Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, a ladder of situations ranked from mildly to severely frightening, often rated in subjective units of distress (SUDS). Third, the relaxed client imagines (or meets in real life) each step, starting at the bottom, and moves up only when a step no longer causes anxiety. Example: Riya fears dogs. She relaxes and pictures a dog in a photograph, then a dog across the road, then a puppy in her lap, climbing the ladder over several sessions until real dogs no longer trigger panic. Option c is right because systematic desensitisation is the classic case of counterconditioning: a calm response is conditioned to the very cue that once produced fear, and it replaces the old anxiety response rather than merely suppressing it. Exam trap: examiners swap in flooding or exposure and response prevention, which rely on extinction and habituation through prolonged exposure without any competing response, or they offer \"operant extinction\" or the \"law of effect\" as the principle. The principle is reciprocal inhibition and the technique is Wolpe's.", "traps": {"a": "Psychotherapy is the umbrella term for any psychological treatment, from Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis to cognitive therapy; it names no specific counterconditioning procedure.", "b": "Non-directive therapy is Carl Rogers's (1942) early name for client-centred therapy, which relies on empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness, not on pairing feared cues with relaxation.", "d": "Milieu therapy uses the whole ward environment (staff, routine and fellow patients) as the treatment, as in Maxwell Jones's therapeutic community from the late 1940s; it is a social treatment for inpatients, not a conditioning technique."}}, {"q": "Biofeedback is now commonly used in the treatment of:", "concept": "The answer is d: biofeedback is now used mainly in the treatment of psychosomatic (psychophysiological) disorders, physical illnesses in which stress and arousal play a large part. In biofeedback a sensor measures a bodily process that we normally cannot feel, such as muscle tension, skin temperature, heart rate or blood pressure, and shows it to the person moment by moment as a tone, a graph or a light. With this feedback the person learns, by trial and error, to shift the reading in a healthy direction; each change is reinforced by the signal itself. The idea rests on Neal Miller (1969), who reported that autonomic responses, long thought to be involuntary, could be operantly conditioned in rats. The rat findings later proved hard to replicate, but clinical biofeedback grew anyway. Common uses are electromyographic (EMG) biofeedback for tension headache, thermal (hand-warming) biofeedback for migraine and Raynaud's disease, and heart-rate or blood-pressure feedback for essential hypertension; it is also used for chronic pain, irritable bowel syndrome and incontinence. Franz Alexander (1950) grouped such complaints as psychosomatic; the modern DSM-5 label is psychological factors affecting other medical conditions. Example: Arjun, an accountant with daily tension headaches, watches a screen showing the electrical activity of his forehead muscles and learns to lower it; after eight sessions his headaches are fewer and milder. Option d is right because biofeedback works directly on the physiological arousal that maintains psychosomatic symptoms, giving the person voluntary control over the body system that is going wrong. Exam trap: phobias tempt students because biofeedback teaches relaxation, but phobias are treated mainly by exposure and systematic desensitisation, and the psychotic options (paranoia, catatonia) need medication, not self-regulation training.", "traps": {"a": "Paranoia is a psychotic state of fixed persecutory or grandiose delusions (delusional disorder in DSM-5); it is treated with antipsychotic medication and psychotherapy, and a false belief cannot be changed by watching a physiological signal.", "b": "Catatonia is a psychomotor syndrome of stupor, posturing, mutism or waxy flexibility, first described by Karl Kahlbaum (1874); it responds to benzodiazepines such as lorazepam and to electroconvulsive therapy, not to biofeedback.", "c": "Phobias are intense, specific fears treated chiefly by exposure, such as Joseph Wolpe's (1958) systematic desensitisation; biofeedback may assist relaxation, but it is not the standard treatment for phobias."}}, {"q": "In transactional analysis which ego state involves rational thinking and decision making in the present moment", "concept": "The answer is b: in transactional analysis the Adult ego state handles rational thinking and decision making in the present moment. Transactional analysis (TA) was founded by Eric Berne (1961) in Transactional Analysis in Psychotherapy and made famous by Games People Play (1964). Berne borrowed the term ego state from his analyst Paul Federn (1952) and described personality as three ego states, the PAC model. The Parent holds attitudes and behaviour copied from parents and other authority figures; it can be critical (\"You should never be late\") or nurturing (\"Let me help you\"). The Child holds feelings and reactions recorded in childhood; the Free Child is playful and spontaneous, the Adapted Child compliant or rebellious. The Adult works like a computer: it gathers facts about present reality, estimates probabilities and makes decisions without being driven by old rules or old feelings. A transaction is a stimulus from one ego state and a response from another. Complementary transactions (Adult to Adult) keep talk flowing; crossed transactions stop it. Example: Meera's manager asks, \"What time will the report be ready?\" (Adult to Adult). If Meera replies, \"By four, after I check the figures,\" she answers from her Adult. If she replies, \"Why are you always pressurising me?\" she answers from her Child, the transaction crosses, and conflict follows. Thomas Harris (1967) popularised strengthening the Adult as the goal of TA in I'm OK, You're OK. Option b is right because Berne defined the Adult as the ego state that processes here-and-now information objectively and makes reasoned choices. Exam trap: students map Berne's states onto the structural model of Sigmund Freud (1923) and assume the Parent, which resembles the superego, is the \"rational\" one; the Parent is about rules and values, while rational, present-focused judgement is the Adult. Berne's states are observable patterns of behaviour, not hidden structures.", "traps": {"a": "The Parent ego state holds the rules, values and attitudes copied from parents and authority figures (critical or nurturing); it judges by learned \"shoulds\", not by fresh reasoning about the present.", "c": "The Child ego state replays the feelings and impulses of childhood, free or adapted; it is the seat of spontaneity, creativity and emotional reaction, not of rational decision making.", "d": "Subconscious is not an ego state in Eric Berne's model at all; it is a popular term for mental activity outside awareness (Sigmund Freud preferred \"unconscious\"), so it does not belong to the Parent, Adult, Child scheme."}}, {"q": "Which therapeutic method, developed by Perls, aims to restore a sense of wholeness for personal growth?", "concept": "The answer is a: Gestalt therapy, developed by Fritz Perls, aims to restore a sense of wholeness so that the person can grow. Frederick Salomon (Fritz) Perls (1951) set out the approach in the book Gestalt Therapy, written with Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman, and developed it with his wife Laura Perls from the 1940s. The German word Gestalt means a whole or complete form. Perls borrowed from Gestalt psychology the idea that the whole is more than the sum of its parts and applied it to the person: thoughts, feelings, body and actions should form an integrated whole. Problems arise when people disown parts of themselves (anger, grief, need), carry \"unfinished business\" from the past, or live in their heads instead of in present experience. The therapy is experiential and here-and-now. The therapist asks \"what\" and \"how\" rather than \"why\", points to body language, and asks clients to speak in the first person and take responsibility (\"I am angry\", not \"it makes me angry\"). Its best-known technique is the empty chair: the client talks to an imagined person or a disowned part of the self, then switches chairs and answers. Example: Kabir, who never told his late father how hurt he felt, speaks to his father in the empty chair, feels the grief fully and closes that unfinished business, so a split-off feeling is owned again. Option a is right because Perls created Gestalt therapy, and its stated aim is integration, the reowning of fragmented parts so that a whole, self-supporting person can mature. Exam trap: do not confuse Gestalt therapy with Gestalt psychology, the school of perception founded by Max Wertheimer (1912) with Wolfgang Kohler (1917) and Kurt Koffka (1935); Perls was a therapist, not a founder of that school. The empty chair and the hot seat belong to Perls, not to the client-centred therapy of Carl Rogers (1951).", "traps": {"b": "Yoga therapy applies the postures, breathing (pranayama) and meditation of the yoga tradition, systematised in Patanjali's Yoga Sutras, to health problems; it is an Indian mind-body practice, not a method developed by Perls.", "c": "Family therapy treats the family as a system, as in Salvador Minuchin's structural therapy (1974) or Murray Bowen's family systems therapy; its focus is relationships within the family, not the wholeness of one individual.", "d": "Psychoanalytic therapy is Sigmund Freud's method (from the 1890s) of free association, dream analysis and interpretation of transference, aimed at making the unconscious conscious; Perls trained as an analyst but broke away from it."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is best defined as a state of persistent worry, apprehension, and fear?", "concept": "The answer is a: anxiety is best defined as a state of persistent worry, apprehension and fear. Anxiety is a future-oriented emotion: a sense that something bad might happen, felt as worry in the mind, tension and vigilance in the body, and a pull towards caution and avoidance. DSM-5 (2013) separates it from fear. Fear is the alarm response to a threat that is present now (a snake on the path), while anxiety is anticipation of a future threat (wondering whether there will be snakes on tomorrow's trek). David Barlow (2002) called this state anxious apprehension. Anxiety has three parts: cognitive (worry and \"what if\" thoughts), physiological (racing heart, muscle tension, sweating, poor sleep) and behavioural (avoidance, checking, seeking reassurance). It is normal, and useful in moderate amounts; the law of Robert Yerkes and John Dodson (1908) shows that moderate arousal improves performance. It becomes a disorder when it is out of proportion to the danger, lasts (often six months or more) and impairs life, as in generalised anxiety disorder. Sigmund Freud (1926) treated anxiety as a signal of danger to the ego. Example: Riya worries for weeks before her board exams, sleeps badly, feels a knot in her stomach and keeps thinking she will fail even though she is well prepared; that ongoing apprehension is anxiety. Option a is right because only anxiety is defined by the triad of persistent worry, apprehension and fear without a specific, present object. Exam trap: phobia also involves fear, so it lures students, but a phobia is fear tied to a specific object or situation and switched on by it; the stem describes a general, persistent state, which is anxiety. Depression is a state of low mood, not of fearful apprehension.", "traps": {"b": "Depression is a mood state of persistent sadness, emptiness and loss of interest or pleasure (anhedonia), as in DSM-5 major depressive disorder; its core is low mood and hopelessness, not apprehensive worry.", "c": "A phobia is an intense, irrational fear of a specific object or situation (spiders, heights, injections), triggered by that cue and followed by avoidance; the stem describes a general, persistent state, not object-bound fear.", "d": "Schizophrenia, named by Eugen Bleuler (1911), is a psychotic disorder of delusions, hallucinations, disorganised thinking and negative symptoms; worry and apprehension are not its defining features."}}, {"q": "How are specific phobias best described?", "concept": "The answer is c: a specific phobia is a persistent, irrational, excessive fear triggered by a specific object or situation. In DSM-5 (2013) the person shows marked fear or anxiety about a particular object or situation; the cue almost always provokes immediate fear; it is actively avoided or endured with intense distress; the fear is out of proportion to the real danger; and it lasts typically six months or more and causes distress or impairment. DSM-5 names five types: animal (dogs, spiders), natural environment (heights, storms, water), blood-injection-injury, situational (lifts, flying, enclosed spaces) and other (choking, vomiting). Older definitions, such as DSM-IV (1994), also required the person to recognise that the fear is excessive or unreasonable; DSM-5 dropped that insight rule and lets the clinician judge proportion, but \"irrational\" survives in exam wording. The blood-injection-injury type is unusual: heart rate rises and then drops sharply, so the person may faint. Classic explanations include classical conditioning, as in the Little Albert study of John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920), and the three pathways of Stanley Rachman (1977): direct conditioning, watching others and being told. Example: Arjun has feared lifts since childhood. He climbs twelve floors to his office every day and his heart pounds if a colleague suggests the lift, even though he knows the lift is safe. That is a specific phobia, situational type. Option c is right because it states every defining feature: persistence, excessiveness, irrationality and a trigger that is one specific object or situation. Exam trap: option d also describes a phobia, but fear of social interactions and scrutiny is social anxiety disorder (social phobia), a separate DSM-5 diagnosis; and option a describes worry, the mark of generalised anxiety disorder, not phobic fear.", "traps": {"a": "Periodic worry about specific situations is closer to ordinary situational anxiety or the worry of generalised anxiety disorder (which is chronic and spread across many topics); a phobia is intense, immediate fear with avoidance, not occasional worry.", "b": "Heightened light sensitivity is photophobia, a medical symptom of migraine, meningitis or eye disease; despite the \"-phobia\" ending it is a physical intolerance of light, not a psychological fear.", "d": "Intense fear of social interactions, where the person dreads being judged or embarrassed, is social anxiety disorder (social phobia), a separate DSM-5 diagnosis rather than a specific phobia."}}, {"q": "What is a key evolutionary theory explaining phobia development?", "concept": "The answer is c: biological preparedness is the key evolutionary theory of how phobias develop. Martin Seligman (1971), in his paper \"Phobias and preparedness\", argued that evolution has prepared humans to learn some fears far more easily than others. Fears of snakes, spiders, heights, darkness, deep water and strangers protected our ancestors, so a brain that learned them fast, and kept them, had a survival advantage. Seligman built on the taste-aversion work of John Garcia (1966), which showed that rats link a taste with sickness in one trial, even with hours of delay, but cannot easily link a light or sound with sickness. This refuted equipotentiality, the old idea that any stimulus can be conditioned to any response equally well. Prepared fears have four features: they are acquired quickly (often in one trial), they resist extinction, they feel irrational, and they cluster around ancestral dangers. Arne Ohman and Susan Mineka (2001) reviewed the evidence: people condition fear to pictures of snakes and spiders faster than to pictures of flowers, and these fears extinguish more slowly. In work by Michael Cook and Susan Mineka (1989), rhesus monkeys learned fear of toy snakes, but not of flowers, by watching videos of other monkeys. Example: Meera has never been bitten, but after seeing one snake on a village path she avoids tall grass for years, while she has no fear of electric sockets, which are far more dangerous in her house. Option c is right because preparedness is explicitly an evolutionary account: it explains why phobias pick out the narrow set of dangers that mattered in human evolution. Exam trap: examiners offer plain conditioning (\"learned fear responses\") as the answer. Conditioning explains how a fear is learned, but not why phobias cluster around snakes and heights rather than guns and cars; that selectivity is what preparedness explains.", "traps": {"a": "Non-associative fear acquisition, proposed by Ross Menzies and John Clarke (1995) and developed by Richie Poulton and Ross Menzies (2002), says some fears (heights, water) are innate and appear with no learning at all; it has a Darwinian flavour, but it is a newer, narrower account, not the classic evolutionary theory of phobias.", "b": "Learned fear responses describe the conditioning accounts, such as the Little Albert study of John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) and the two-factor theory of O. Hobart Mowrer (1947); these are learning theories that do not explain why some fears are acquired far more readily than others.", "d": "Specific phobia acquisition is not a theory at all; it simply restates what is to be explained (how specific phobias are acquired), so it cannot be the evolutionary explanation."}}, {"q": "Which combination of techniques is often part of effective CBT for social phobia?", "concept": "The answer is a: exposure therapy combined with cognitive restructuring is the core of effective CBT for social phobia (social anxiety disorder). Social anxiety disorder is an intense fear of situations in which the person may be watched and judged, such as speaking in class, eating in public or meeting strangers. In the cognitive model of David M. Clark and Adrian Wells (1995), the socially anxious person turns attention inward, builds a distorted picture of how they look (\"everyone can see me shaking\"), and uses safety behaviours (avoiding eye contact, rehearsing sentences, gripping a glass) that keep the fear going and prevent disconfirmation. Treatment therefore has two linked parts. Cognitive restructuring teaches the client to catch automatic thoughts (\"they will think I am stupid\"), examine the evidence and replace them with more realistic beliefs. Exposure puts the client, step by step, into the feared situations without safety behaviours, so the predictions can be tested and anxiety can fall. The cognitive-behavioural group therapy of Richard Heimberg (1990) combines both in group role-plays, and Clark and Wells's individual cognitive therapy adds video feedback and behavioural experiments; both have strong trial evidence. Example: Kabir avoids speaking in meetings because he believes his voice shakes badly. He writes down that prediction, gives a two-minute talk to colleagues without his script, then watches the video: his voice barely shakes and nobody frowns. Option a is right because these two components attack both halves of the problem: distorted social predictions (restructuring) and the avoidance that keeps them untested (exposure). Exam trap: each wrong pair contains a respectable technique, so students choose on recognition. Social skills training, relaxation, mindfulness and psychoeducation are useful add-ons, but exposure plus cognitive restructuring is the evidence-based core.", "traps": {"b": "Social skills training (rooted in the social skills model of Michael Argyle, 1967) helps clients who genuinely lack skills, and mindfulness belongs to the third-wave therapies that grew from Jon Kabat-Zinn's programme (1979); neither pair is the standard core of CBT for social phobia.", "c": "Relaxation techniques, such as the applied relaxation of Lars-Goran Ost (1987), and cognitive reframing are useful, but relaxation without exposure leaves avoidance untouched and has done less well in trials than exposure plus cognitive restructuring.", "d": "Psychoeducation is the opening phase of almost any CBT, and systematic desensitisation is Joseph Wolpe's (1958) imagined-hierarchy method for specific fears; together they lack the cognitive work on fear of negative evaluation that social phobia needs."}}, {"q": "Which of the following are common physiological symptoms of panic attacks?", "concept": "The answer is d: heart palpitations, excessive sweating and rapid breathing are common physiological symptoms of panic attacks. A panic attack, in DSM-5 (2013), is an abrupt surge of intense fear or discomfort that peaks within minutes, with four or more of thirteen symptoms: palpitations or a racing heart; sweating; trembling or shaking; shortness of breath or smothering; a feeling of choking; chest pain; nausea or abdominal distress; dizziness or light-headedness; chills or heat sensations; numbness or tingling; derealisation or depersonalisation; fear of losing control or \"going crazy\"; and fear of dying. The physical symptoms are the fight-or-flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915), firing without a real danger. Rapid breathing (hyperventilation) lowers carbon dioxide in the blood, which causes dizziness and tingling and adds to the alarm. David M. Clark (1986) explained why attacks escalate: the person catastrophically misinterprets these sensations (a pounding heart means a heart attack), which raises anxiety and intensifies the sensations in a vicious circle. Recurrent unexpected attacks plus a month of worry or avoidance make panic disorder. Example: Meera is standing in a metro coach when her heart suddenly pounds, she starts sweating and breathing fast, and she is sure she will collapse; ten minutes later it has passed, and her ECG at the hospital is normal. Option d is right because it lists three of the most frequent and characteristic bodily signs of sympathetic arousal in panic, the classic cardiac, sweating and breathing triad. Exam trap: option a starts with two genuine symptoms, palpitations and dizziness, then slips in \"excitement\", a pleasant emotional state that is not a panic symptom; one wrong item makes a combination option wrong. Key note: shortness of breath, trembling, nausea and chest pain (options b and c) are also real DSM-5 panic symptoms. Option d is keyed as the most complete classic triad, and a fails only on \"excitement\"; the official key stands.", "traps": {"a": "Palpitations and dizziness are genuine DSM-5 panic symptoms, but excitement is a pleasant, approach emotion and not part of a panic attack; one wrong element makes the whole option wrong.", "b": "Shortness of breath and trembling are real DSM-5 panic symptoms, so this option is partly true; it is not keyed because it offers only two signs and misses the racing heart that is the classic hallmark of panic.", "c": "Nausea and chest pain are also listed DSM-5 panic symptoms, so the option is true as far as it goes; it is not keyed because these are less characteristic than the palpitations, sweating and fast breathing of option d."}}, {"q": "Which class of medications is used in the treatment of social anxiety disorder?", "concept": "The answer is a: selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) are the class of medications used, as first-line drugs, in the treatment of social anxiety disorder. An SSRI blocks the serotonin transporter on the presynaptic neuron, so serotonin (5-HT) stays longer in the synapse. The first widely used SSRI, fluoxetine, was approved in the United States in 1987; paroxetine became the first drug approved there for social anxiety disorder in 1999, and sertraline, escitalopram and fluvoxamine are also used. The serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor (SNRI) venlafaxine is an alternative. Benefits usually appear only after 2 to 6 weeks, and treatment typically continues for 6 to 12 months or more to prevent relapse. Common side effects include nausea, sleep change and sexual dysfunction. Other drugs have narrower roles. Beta-blockers such as propranolol damp the racing heart and tremor of performance-only anxiety (a musician before a concert), and benzodiazepines such as clonazepam act fast but carry a risk of dependence. Guidelines such as those of the National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (2013) recommend individual CBT first and an SSRI when the person prefers medication or CBT is not enough; the two can be combined. Example: Arjun, a software engineer, is so afraid of meetings that he takes sick leave on review days. His psychiatrist starts escitalopram while he begins CBT, and after two months he can present his work with only mild nerves. Option a is right because SSRIs are the best-tested and most widely recommended drug class for social anxiety disorder, with consistent support from randomised trials. Exam trap: students sometimes pick antipsychotics because social anxiety is a \"mental disorder\"; antipsychotics treat psychosis, not anxiety. Do not be put off by the fact that SSRIs are called antidepressants: they are standard anxiety treatment too.", "traps": {"b": "Antibiotics kill or stop bacteria and treat infections such as pneumonia or tuberculosis; they have no role in treating anxiety.", "c": "Antipsychotics, from chlorpromazine (introduced into psychiatry by Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker, 1952) onwards, block dopamine D2 receptors to treat psychosis such as schizophrenia; they are not a recommended treatment for social anxiety disorder.", "d": "Anti-inflammatory drugs such as ibuprofen and aspirin reduce pain, fever and inflammation in conditions like arthritis and injury; they are not used to treat social anxiety."}}, {"q": "Generalized anxiety disorder is mainly characterized by:", "concept": "The answer is b: generalised anxiety disorder (GAD) is mainly characterised by chronic worry and tension about future events. GAD entered the diagnostic manuals in DSM-III (1980). In DSM-5 (2013) its core is excessive anxiety and worry (apprehensive expectation) about a number of events or activities, such as work, health, money or family, occurring more days than not for at least six months. The person finds the worry hard to control, and it comes with three or more of six symptoms (only one in children): restlessness or feeling on edge, being easily tired, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension and disturbed sleep. The worry is \"free-floating\": it moves from topic to topic rather than staying on one feared object. Thomas Borkovec (1994) proposed the avoidance theory of worry: worry is mostly verbal thinking, which damps the vivid images and bodily arousal of fear, so worrying is negatively reinforced and becomes a habit. Adrian Wells (1995) added that people with GAD hold positive beliefs about worry (\"worrying keeps me prepared\") as well as negative ones (\"my worry is uncontrollable\"). Example: Riya, a bank officer, worries daily that she will make an error, that her parents will fall ill, that the rent will rise and that her son will fail his exams; she has felt tense and slept badly for a year. No single fear explains it: the worry is general and chronic. Option b is right because chronic, hard-to-control worry and tension about what might happen is the defining feature of GAD. Exam trap: each wrong option is the signature of a neighbouring disorder. \"Fear of fear\" is panic disorder, flashbacks belong to post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), and repeated safety checking is a compulsion of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD).", "traps": {"a": "Fear of fear, the dread of one's own bodily arousal described by Alan Goldstein and Dianne Chambless (1978), is the hallmark of panic disorder and agoraphobia, not GAD.", "c": "Persistent flashbacks, the intrusive reliving of a traumatic event, are an intrusion symptom of post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), which requires a trauma; GAD worry is about the future, not replayed past trauma.", "d": "Constant checking (locks, gas, switches) to prevent harm is a compulsion of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD), driven by an intrusive obsession; people with GAD worry, but ritual checking is not the main feature."}}, {"q": "Which among the following does not fall under Cluster B personality disorders?", "concept": "The answer is b: obsessive-compulsive personality disorder (OCPD) does not fall under Cluster B; it belongs to Cluster C. This question repeats Question 41 almost word for word, so the reasoning is the same. DSM-5 (2013), like DSM-III (1980) and DSM-IV before it, groups the ten personality disorders into three clusters by descriptive similarity. Cluster A, odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal. Cluster B, dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic. Cluster C, anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive. A handy memory aid is weird, wild and worried. OCPD is a pervasive preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism and control at the expense of flexibility and efficiency: rigid rules, lists, reluctance to delegate, excessive devotion to work and moral scrupulousness. It is anxious and over-controlled, the opposite of the impulsive, attention-seeking style of Cluster B. Example: Arjun, a project manager, redoes his team's slides because the fonts are not uniform, misses deadlines through checking, and cannot take a holiday; he is rigid and anxious, not dramatic. By contrast his colleague Meera, who must be the centre of attention and speaks and dresses theatrically, fits histrionic personality disorder in Cluster B. Option b is right because OCPD is a Cluster C disorder, while narcissistic, histrionic and borderline personality disorders are three of the four Cluster B disorders. Exam trap: the word \"obsessive-compulsive\" makes students think of OCD, but OCPD is not OCD. OCD has true obsessions and compulsions that the person usually finds unwanted (ego-dystonic); OCPD is a personality style the person usually sees as correct (ego-syntonic). Neither belongs to Cluster B.", "traps": {"a": "Narcissistic personality disorder, a pervasive pattern of grandiosity, need for admiration and lack of empathy (drawing on the work of Heinz Kohut, 1971), IS a Cluster B disorder, so it cannot be the odd one out.", "c": "Histrionic personality disorder, a pattern of excessive emotionality and attention seeking, IS a Cluster B disorder, so it is not the answer.", "d": "Borderline personality disorder, instability of relationships, self-image and mood with marked impulsivity (a term first used by Adolph Stern, 1938), IS a Cluster B disorder, so it is not the answer."}}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the concept of homeostasis?", "concept": "The answer is c: homeostasis is the body's ability to maintain a stable internal environment. The idea began with the French physiologist Claude Bernard (1878), who said that the constancy of the milieu interieur (internal environment) is the condition of a free and independent life. Walter Bradford Cannon (1926) named it homeostasis, from the Greek for \"similar\" and \"standing still\", and explained it in The Wisdom of the Body (1932). Homeostasis works like a thermostat, by negative feedback. Each variable (body temperature near 37 degrees Celsius, blood glucose, water balance, blood pH, oxygen) has a set point; sensors detect a departure, and responses push the value back. The hypothalamus is the main control centre, acting through the autonomic nervous system, hormones and behaviour. If you get too hot you sweat and the vessels in your skin widen; if you get too cold you shiver and reach for a blanket. In psychology, homeostasis is the basis of the drive-reduction theory of Clark Hull (1943): a bodily need creates a drive (hunger, thirst), and behaviour that reduces it is reinforced. Example: Kabir plays cricket on a 42 degree afternoon in Delhi. He sweats heavily, his thirst rises and his kidneys conserve water; when he drinks, the thirst fades. All these responses keep his internal temperature and fluid level steady. Option c is right because homeostasis is, by definition, the maintenance of stable internal conditions through self-correcting feedback. Exam trap: option a sounds close, but adapting to environmental change is adaptation, or allostasis. Allostasis, a term of Peter Sterling and Joseph Eyer (1988), means stability through change, achieved by shifting set points to meet demands. Homeostasis is about keeping the inside constant, not about changing to fit the outside.", "traps": {"a": "Adapting to environmental change describes adaptation, or allostasis (Peter Sterling and Joseph Eyer, 1988), stability achieved by changing set points; homeostasis is the narrower idea of holding internal conditions constant.", "b": "Storing and retrieving memories is the work of memory, as in the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968); it has nothing to do with internal physiological balance.", "d": "Decision making guided by emotion is described by Antonio Damasio's somatic marker hypothesis (1994), in which bodily feelings steer choices; it is about judgement, not about keeping internal conditions stable."}}, {"q": "Identify the option that is not classified as a positive symptom of schizophrenia:", "concept": "The answer is d: a flat or toneless voice is not a positive symptom of schizophrenia; it is a negative symptom. This question repeats Question 43 in slightly different words. Symptoms of schizophrenia are sorted into positive and negative groups. Positive symptoms are additions to normal experience: hallucinations, delusions, disorganised speech and grossly disorganised behaviour, which includes inappropriate affect such as laughing at sad news. Negative symptoms are losses or reductions of normal function. DSM-5 (2013) names two as most prominent: diminished emotional expression (an unmoving face, little eye contact, a monotonous voice) and avolition (loss of goal-directed activity); others are alogia (poverty of speech), anhedonia and asociality. Timothy Crow (1980) linked positive symptoms (Type I) to dopamine overactivity and good drug response, and negative symptoms (Type II) to structural brain changes and poor drug response. Nancy Andreasen (1982) built separate rating scales for each group, the SANS and the SAPS. Peter Liddle (1987) later split the positive symptoms into reality distortion and disorganisation, placing inappropriate affect with disorganisation. Example: Arjun has heard voices commenting on him (a positive symptom) for a year. His sister notices that he now speaks in a dull monotone, shows almost no expression even when his nephew visits, and rarely starts a conversation; this flat, toneless speech is a negative symptom. Option d is right because a flat or toneless voice is a sign of diminished emotional expression (affective flattening), a reduction of normal function, which places it in the negative group. Exam trap: inappropriate affect (option c) and flat affect sound alike but fall in opposite groups. Inappropriate affect is emotion that is present but wrong for the situation, so it counts as positive or disorganised; flat affect is emotion that is absent or reduced, so it is negative.", "traps": {"a": "Hallucinations, perceptions without an external stimulus (most often voices), are a core positive symptom in DSM-5 and among the first-rank symptoms of Kurt Schneider (1959), so this option is a positive symptom and not the answer.", "b": "Disorganised speech (derailment, incoherence, word salad) and delusions (fixed false beliefs) are both positive symptoms in DSM-5, so this option names positive features and is not the answer.", "c": "Inappropriate emotional reactions, such as laughing while describing a death, are grouped with disorganised behaviour among the positive symptoms (Peter Liddle's disorganisation syndrome, 1987), because an abnormal response is present rather than missing."}}, {"q": "The animal instincts that humans inherited in their evolution from lower forms of life is", "concept": "The answer is d: the shadow is the archetype made up of the animal instincts that humans inherited in their evolution from lower forms of life. Carl Gustav Jung (1875 to 1961) divided the unconscious into a personal unconscious of forgotten and repressed experience and, beneath it, a collective unconscious, an inherited layer shared by all humanity. The collective unconscious contains archetypes, universal predispositions to experience the world in certain ways, which appear again and again in myths, dreams and religions. Jung described four major archetypes of personality. The persona is the social mask we show to others. The anima is the feminine side of a man, and the animus the masculine side of a woman. The shadow is the darkest and deepest part: the primitive, animal instincts carried over from our pre-human past, the source of what we see as evil, immoral or shameful, and so usually disowned. Jung did not see the shadow as purely bad. It is also the source of vitality, spontaneity and creativity, and a person who denies it completely becomes flat and lifeless. Unacknowledged, it is projected onto others, as when we hate in someone else what we refuse to see in ourselves. Calvin Hall and Gardner Lindzey (1957) summarised Jung in almost exactly the stem's words. Example: Meera prides herself on being gentle, yet in a dream she savagely attacks a rival; Jung would read the dream figure as her shadow, rage she does not admit when awake. Option d is right because Jung defined the shadow as the archetype that holds humanity's inherited animal instincts. Exam trap: the anima and animus also sound primitive and inherited, and both are archetypes, but they concern the other sex within the psyche, not animal instincts. The persona is a public social role, the opposite of the hidden shadow.", "traps": {"a": "The persona is Jung's archetype of the social mask, the public face a person wears to meet society's expectations; it is shaped by social roles, not by inherited animal instincts.", "b": "The anima is Jung's archetype of the feminine side of a man's psyche, built from men's collective experience of women; it concerns the opposite sex within, not animal instincts.", "c": "The animus is Jung's archetype of the masculine side of a woman's psyche; like the anima it concerns the other sex within, not the primitive animal inheritance."}}, {"q": "Which of the following works on the 'rationality principle '", "concept": "The answer is b: the ego is the part of the personality that works on the rational, reality-based principle the stem calls the \"rationality principle\". Sigmund Freud (1923), in The Ego and the Id, divided the mind into three structures. The id, present at birth, is the reservoir of instinctual energy. It obeys the pleasure principle, demanding immediate gratification, and thinks in primary process (wishes and images, without logic or time). The ego develops out of the id as the child meets the real world. It obeys the reality principle, a term Freud introduced in 1911: it delays gratification until a safe, realistic way to satisfy the need is found. It uses secondary process thinking, which is logical, realistic and problem-solving, and it controls perception, memory and movement. Freud compared it to a rider steering a stronger horse, the id. The superego, the last to form, takes in parents' and society's moral standards through identification at the end of the phallic stage; it works on moral, perfectionist lines through the conscience (guilt) and the ego ideal (pride). The ego mediates between the id, the superego and reality, and uses defence mechanisms when the pressure turns into anxiety. Example: Arjun is hungry during a lecture (id: \"eat now\"). His superego says eating in class is rude. His ego plans: he will wait twenty minutes and buy a samosa at the break. Option b is right because only the ego reasons about reality and chooses realistic, rational ways to meet needs. Exam trap: examiners pair each structure with a principle and then swap them. The id goes with the pleasure principle, the ego with the reality principle, and the superego with morality and perfection. Key note: \"rationality principle\" is the paper's wording; Freud's term is the reality principle, which is the ego's working rule, so the official key stands.", "traps": {"a": "The id, in Sigmund Freud's structural model, is the inborn reservoir of instinctual energy that follows the pleasure principle and primary process thinking; it is irrational and demands immediate gratification.", "c": "The superego is the internalised moral authority of parents and society, working through the conscience and the ego ideal; it follows moral and perfectionist standards, not a realistic or rational principle.", "d": "Libido is not a structure of the mind but the psychic energy of the life (sexual) instincts in Freud's theory; it fuels the id and has no governing principle of its own."}}, {"q": "The prisoner's dilemma is used to study", "concept": "The answer is c: the prisoner's dilemma is used to study cooperation and competition. It is a mixed-motive game, first devised by Merrill Flood and Melvin Dresher (1950) at the RAND Corporation and given its prison story by Albert Tucker (1950). Two suspects are questioned in separate rooms. If both stay silent (cooperate with each other), each gets a light sentence. If both confess (compete), each gets a fairly heavy sentence. If one confesses and the other stays silent, the confessor goes free and the silent one gets the heaviest sentence. Whatever the other does, each person gains by confessing, yet if both reason this way, both end up worse off than if both had trusted each other. The game therefore captures a social dilemma, in which individual self-interest clashes with the shared good. Social psychologists play many rounds for points or money to see when people cooperate. Morton Deutsch (1958) used such games to show that communication and trust raise cooperation, while threats lower it. Robert Axelrod (1984) ran computer tournaments and found that tit-for-tat (cooperate first, then copy the partner's last move) earned the most over repeated games. The tragedy of the commons of Garrett Hardin (1968) is the many-person version. Example: Kabir and Meera run tea stalls side by side near a college. Both can keep prices fair, or one can start a price war. Each is tempted to undercut, but if both do, both lose money. Option c is right because the game is built to pit cooperation against competition and to measure which one people choose, and under what conditions. Exam trap: examiners offer other classic social psychology topics. The autokinetic effect belongs to the norm studies of Muzafer Sherif (1935), and need complementarity to the mate selection theory of Robert Winch (1958); neither uses a payoff matrix.", "traps": {"a": "The autokinetic effect is the illusion that a fixed point of light in a dark room moves; Muzafer Sherif (1935) used it to study how group norms form, not cooperation and competition.", "b": "Affiliation and attraction were studied with other methods, such as Stanley Schachter's (1959) anxiety and affiliation experiments and Theodore Newcomb's (1961) study of student housemates; the prisoner's dilemma measures choices between joint gain and self-gain, not liking.", "d": "Need complementarity is Robert Winch's (1958) theory that people choose partners whose needs complement their own (a dominant person with a submissive one); it is a theory of mate selection, not a game for studying cooperation."}}, {"q": "\"one should not take more love than one can give\" best reflects –", "concept": "The answer is d: \"one should not take more love than one can give\" reflects equity theory. Equity theory began with John Stacy Adams (1963) as a theory of fairness at work: people compare the ratio of their outcomes (pay, praise) to their inputs (effort, skill) with the same ratio for others, and feel distress when the ratios are unequal. Elaine Walster (later Hatfield), G. William Walster and Ellen Berscheid (1978) applied it to close relationships. A relationship feels fair when each partner's benefits are proportional to their contributions. Inputs include love, care, time, money and good looks; outcomes include the love, attention and support received. Inequity has two forms. The under-benefited partner, who gives more than they get, feels angry or resentful. The over-benefited partner, who gets more than they give, feels guilty. Both states are uncomfortable, so people try to restore equity, either by changing what they give or get, or by changing how they see the balance; if that fails, they may leave. Couples who feel equitably treated report more contentment and have more stable relationships. Example: Riya cooks, plans every outing and remembers every birthday, while Arjun gives little back. Riya feels used, and Arjun, if he notices, feels guilty; both feelings push them to rebalance. The stem's maxim tells Arjun not to take more love than he gives, which is exactly the equity rule. Option d is right because equity theory makes fairness, giving in proportion to what one receives, the rule that keeps a relationship satisfying. Exam trap: social exchange theory, from John Thibaut and Harold Kelley (1959), is the close cousin. Exchange theory says people seek the most rewards for the least cost; equity theory adds that the balance must also feel fair, which is what the stem's moral \"should not\" expresses.", "traps": {"a": "The halo effect, named by Edward Thorndike (1920), is the bias by which one good trait (such as attractiveness) leads us to assume other good traits; it is about impression formation, not fair giving and taking.", "b": "Mere exposure theory, from Robert Zajonc (1968), says that repeated exposure to a person or object increases liking; it explains attraction through familiarity, not through balanced giving and receiving.", "c": "The Romeo and Juliet effect, reported by Richard Driscoll, Keith Davis and Milton Lipetz (1972), is the finding that parental interference intensifies romantic love; it concerns opposition from outside, not fairness inside the relationship."}}, {"q": "Which of the given situations involving two people P and O and an object X, will not be in a balanced state according to the balance theory?", "concept": "The answer is b: \"P dislikes O and they both like X\" is not a balanced state in balance theory. Fritz Heider (1946, 1958) proposed that people want their likes and dislikes to fit together consistently. He described a triad of a person (P), another person (O) and an object or issue (X). Each link is positive (likes, agrees) or negative (dislikes, disagrees). The rule is simple: multiply the three signs. If the product is positive, the triad is balanced and stable; if it is negative, it is imbalanced, feels uncomfortable and pushes the person to change one of the links. A quick check: a balanced triad has zero or two negative links, an imbalanced one has one or three. Now apply it. Option a: P likes O (+), P likes X (+), O likes X (+); no negatives, so balanced, like friends who share a taste. Option c: P likes O (+), P dislikes X (minus), O dislikes X (minus); two negatives, so balanced, like friends who share a dislike. Option d: P dislikes O (minus), P likes X (+), O dislikes X (minus); two negatives, so balanced, because disagreeing with someone you dislike feels consistent. Option b: P dislikes O (minus), P likes X (+), O likes X (+); one negative, so the product is negative and the triad is imbalanced. Example: Kabir dislikes his neighbour Arjun, yet they both love the same IPL team. This sits uneasily, so Kabir may warm to Arjun a little, cool on the team, or tell himself Arjun is not a real fan. Option b is right because it is the only triad with a single negative link, so its sign product is negative. Exam trap: students assume any dislike means imbalance, but options c and d contain dislikes and are still balanced. Count the negatives: even means balanced, odd means imbalanced.", "traps": {"a": "P likes O, P likes X and O likes X gives three positive links, so the sign product is positive and the triad is balanced: friends who share a liking, the most stable case in Fritz Heider's theory.", "c": "P likes O while both dislike X has two negative links, so the product is positive and the triad is balanced: friends who share a common dislike feel consistent.", "d": "P dislikes O, P likes X and O dislikes X has two negative links, so the product is positive and the triad is balanced: disagreeing with someone you dislike is psychologically consistent."}}, {"q": "Which of theory assumes that individuals have a need to evaluate their own attitudes and abilities?", "concept": "The answer is a: social comparison theory assumes that individuals have a need to evaluate their own attitudes and abilities. Leon Festinger (1954) proposed that people have a basic drive to know whether their opinions are correct and how good their abilities are. When an objective, physical standard exists (a stopwatch, a fixed pass mark), they use it. When it does not, they compare themselves with other people, and they prefer similar others, because comparison with someone very different tells them little. A mark of 85 percent means little until you know how your classmates scored. Later work added two directions. Upward comparison, with someone better off, can inspire but often brings envy and lower self-esteem. Downward comparison, with someone worse off, described by Thomas Ashby Wills (1981), protects self-esteem, especially under threat. Stanley Schachter (1959) linked the theory to affiliation: frightened participants preferred to wait with others facing the same situation, partly to compare their feelings. Festinger built on the same drive for accurate self-knowledge in his cognitive dissonance theory (1957). Example: Meera, a first-year student, is unsure whether her sketches are good. She does not compare herself with a famous painter or with a child; she looks at the work of others in her art class. Seeing that hers are among the better ones, she feels confident. Option a is right because the need to evaluate one's own opinions and abilities is the opening assumption, the first hypothesis, of Festinger's theory. Exam trap: all four options start with \"social\", so students guess on familiarity. Social facilitation is about performance in the presence of others, social influence is the broad field of conformity and obedience, and social exchange is about costs and rewards in relationships; only social comparison is about self-evaluation.", "traps": {"b": "Social facilitation, found by Norman Triplett (1898) and explained by Robert Zajonc (1965), is the tendency to perform simple or well-learned tasks better in the presence of others; it is about performance, not self-evaluation.", "c": "Social influence is the broad field of how others change our attitudes and behaviour, covering Solomon Asch's conformity studies (1951) and Stanley Milgram's obedience studies (1963); it is not a theory built on a need to evaluate oneself.", "d": "Social exchange theory, from George Homans (1958) and John Thibaut and Harold Kelley (1959), says people stay in relationships when rewards exceed costs; it explains relationships by profit, not by self-evaluation."}}, {"q": "Transferability in qualitative research is", "concept": "The answer is a: transferability is the degree to which the results of qualitative research can be generalised, or transferred, to other contexts. Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba (1985) argued in Naturalistic Inquiry that qualitative research should not be judged by the experimental criteria of internal validity, external validity, reliability and objectivity. They proposed trustworthiness, made of four parallel criteria: credibility (parallel to internal validity), transferability (parallel to external validity), dependability (parallel to reliability) and confirmability (parallel to objectivity). Transferability is the qualitative answer to the question \"does this apply elsewhere?\" Because qualitative studies use small, purposive samples, the researcher cannot claim statistical generalisation. Instead, the researcher provides thick description, a term from the anthropologist Clifford Geertz (1973): a detailed account of the setting, participants, time and processes. The reader, who knows their own setting, then judges how similar the two contexts are and whether the findings fit. The responsibility is shared, but the final judgement lies with the person applying the findings. Example: Riya interviews 12 nurses in a Pune hospital about burnout and describes the ward routines, staffing and shift patterns in depth. A researcher in a Chennai hospital reads the description, sees that her wards are similar, and judges that Riya's themes are likely to transfer. Option a is right because transferability is precisely the extent to which qualitative findings apply to other contexts, the qualitative counterpart of generalisability. Exam trap: the other trustworthiness criteria are often swapped in. Credibility asks whether the findings are believable to participants (checked by member checking), dependability whether the process is consistent (shown by an audit trail), and confirmability whether the findings come from the data rather than the researcher's bias.", "traps": {"b": "Using different qualitative methods interchangeably is not a recognised criterion; studying one question with several methods is methodological triangulation (Norman Denzin, 1970), which strengthens credibility rather than defining transferability.", "c": "Converting qualitative findings into numbers for quantitative analysis is quantitising, a mixed-methods technique described by Abbas Tashakkori and Charles Teddlie (1998); it changes the type of data, not where the findings apply.", "d": "Findings that resonate with readers from another group is closer to Sarah Tracy's (2010) criterion of resonance or Robert Stake's (1978) naturalistic generalisation; it overlaps with transferability, but transferability is defined by fit to other contexts, judged through thick description, which is what option a states."}}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the process of making inference about behaviour", "concept": "The answer is c: attribution is the process of making inferences about the causes of behaviour, our own and other people's. Fritz Heider (1958), in The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations, described ordinary people as naive scientists who try to explain why things happen. His key distinction was between internal (dispositional) attributions, which locate the cause in the person (ability, effort, personality), and external (situational) attributions, which locate it in the environment (luck, task difficulty, pressure from others). Edward Jones and Keith Davis (1965) explained when we infer a trait from an act (correspondent inference): when the act is freely chosen, has distinctive effects and is not socially expected. Harold Kelley (1967) proposed the covariation model, in which we weigh consensus, distinctiveness and consistency. Bernard Weiner (1972) added the dimensions of locus, stability and controllability, especially for success and failure. Attribution is also prone to biases, above all the fundamental attribution error named by Lee Ross (1977): overestimating personal causes and underestimating the situation when explaining others' behaviour. Example: Arjun's classmate arrives late to the exam. Arjun thinks, \"He is careless\", an internal attribution. Later he learns that the metro broke down, an external cause, and Arjun revises his attribution. Option c is right because inferring the causes behind observed behaviour is the very definition of attribution. Exam trap: proxemics also involves watching behaviour, but it is the study of how people use space, not of how they explain behaviour. Cohesiveness and attachment are about bonds, within a group or between child and caregiver, not about causal inference.", "traps": {"a": "Cohesiveness is the strength of the forces that bind members to a group, studied by Leon Festinger, Stanley Schachter and Kurt Back (1950); it is a property of groups, not a process of inferring causes.", "b": "Proxemics, a term coined by Edward T. Hall (1966), is the study of how people use personal space (intimate, personal, social and public zones); it is a nonverbal channel, not causal inference about behaviour.", "d": "Attachment is the enduring emotional bond between infant and caregiver described by John Bowlby (1969) and measured with Mary Ainsworth's Strange Situation (1978); it is a developmental bond, not an inference about behaviour."}}, {"q": "Which theory of emotion is based on the interaction between changes in physiological arousal and cognitive interpretation of that arousal", "concept": "The answer is c: the Schachter-Singer theory explains emotion as the interaction between physiological arousal and the cognitive interpretation of that arousal. Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer (1962) proposed the two-factor theory: arousal gives an emotion its intensity, and a cognitive label, drawn from the situation, gives it its identity. Arousal alone is general and similar across emotions, so without a label we do not know whether we feel fear, anger or joy. In their classic experiment, students were injected with adrenaline (epinephrine), described as a vitamin called Suproxin. Some were told the real side effects (racing heart, trembling); others were misinformed or told nothing. Each then waited with a confederate who acted either euphoric (playing with paper planes) or angry (complaining about an insulting questionnaire). Participants who had no explanation for their arousal tended to catch the confederate's mood; informed participants, who could blame the injection, did not. Donald Dutton and Arthur Aron (1974) found a related misattribution effect: men who crossed a high, swaying bridge were more attracted to a woman interviewer than men on a low, stable bridge. Example: Meera's heart is pounding after she runs up the stairs to a job interview. If she reads it as nerves, she feels anxious; if she reads it as excitement about the opportunity, she feels eager. Option c is right because only Schachter and Singer made emotion the product of arousal plus a cognitive label for that arousal. Exam trap: cognitive appraisal theory (option d) also stresses cognition, but in the version of Richard Lazarus (1966) the appraisal of the situation comes first and produces the emotion; it does not depend on interpreting arousal that is already present.", "traps": {"a": "The James-Lange theory, from William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885), says emotion is the perception of bodily changes (we feel afraid because we tremble); it has arousal but no cognitive labelling step.", "b": "The Cannon-Bard theory, from Walter Cannon (1927) and Philip Bard (1928), says the thalamus signals the cortex and the body at the same time, so feeling and arousal occur simultaneously and independently; it gives no role to interpreting arousal.", "d": "Cognitive appraisal theory, from Magda Arnold (1960) and Richard Lazarus (1966, 1991), says emotion follows our evaluation of what a situation means for our well-being; appraisal of the event, not the labelling of existing arousal, is the trigger."}}]});
